 [:db/cas 17592186054368 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following facts about the \"and\" $(\\land)$ and \"or\" $(\\lor)$ logical connectives:\n\n- The statement $A \\land B$ is true if *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\n- The statement $A \\lor B$ is true if either $A$ is true, or $B$ is true, or *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\nIn our case, $A$ is true, and $B$ is false. Therefore, the result is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{5\\text{ is prime}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{\\frac{1}{3}\\in Z}_{⏟}}_{B} & \\,\\,≡\\,\\text{true}∨\\text{false}≡\\text{true}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the following facts about the \"and\" $(\\land)$ and \"or\" $(\\lor)$ logical connectives:\n\n- The statement $A \\land B$ is true if *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\n- The statement $A \\lor B$ is true if either $A$ is true, or $B$ is true, or *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\nIn our case, $A$ is true, and $B$ is false. Therefore, the result is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{5\\text{ is prime}}_{A}\\,\\underbrace{\\text{or}}_{∨}\\,\\underbrace{\\frac{1}{3}\\in Z}_{B} & \\,\\,≡\\,\\text{true}∨\\text{false}≡\\text{true}\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186054389 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following facts about the \"and\" $(\\land)$ and \"or\" $(\\lor)$ logical connectives:\n\n- The statement $A \\land B$ is true if *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\n- The statement $A \\lor B$ is true if either $A$ is true, or $B$ is true, or *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\nIn our case, $P$ is false, $Q$ is true, and $R$ is false. Therefore, $Q \\lor R$ is true, but the result is false:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P∧(Q∨R) & ≡\\text{false}∧\\overset{\\text{true}}{\\overset{⏞}{(\\text{true}∨\\text{false})}} \\\\  & ≡\\text{false}∧\\text{true} \\\\  & ≡\\text{false}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the following facts about the \"and\" $(\\land)$ and \"or\" $(\\lor)$ logical connectives:\n\n- The statement $A \\land B$ is true if *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\n- The statement $A \\lor B$ is true if either $A$ is true, or $B$ is true, or *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\nIn our case, $P$ is false, $Q$ is true, and $R$ is false. Therefore, $Q \\lor R$ is true, but the result is false:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P∧(Q∨R) & ≡\\text{false}∧\\overbrace{(\\text{true}∨\\text{false})}^{\\text{true}} \\\\  & ≡\\text{false}∧\\text{true} \\\\  & ≡\\text{false}\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186054396 :question/worked-solution "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS. First, we compute the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{(-3)^{3}}_{⏟}}_{-27}÷6=(-27)÷6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-27)÷6 & = \\\\ \\frac{(-27)}{6} & = \\\\ -\\frac{27}{6} & = \\\\ -\\frac{27÷3}{6÷3} & = \\\\ -\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS. First, we compute the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{(-3)^{3}}_{-27}÷6=(-27)÷6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-27)÷6 & = \\\\ \\frac{(-27)}{6} & = \\\\ -\\frac{27}{6} & = \\\\ -\\frac{27÷3}{6÷3} & = \\\\ -\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186054397 :question/worked-solution "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS. First, we perform the operation inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({{1-4}_{⏟}}_{-3})^{2}\\times \\frac{1}{12} & =(-3)^{2}\\times \\frac{1}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{(-3)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{9}\\times \\frac{1}{12} & =9\\times \\frac{1}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\times \\frac{1}{12} & = \\\\ \\frac{9}{1}\\times \\frac{1}{12} & = \\\\ \\frac{9\\times 1}{1\\times 12} & = \\\\ \\frac{9}{12} & = \\\\ \\frac{9÷3}{12÷3} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS. First, we perform the operation inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\underbrace{1-4}_{-3})^{2}\\times \\frac{1}{12} & =(-3)^{2}\\times \\frac{1}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{(-3)^{2}}_{9}\\times \\frac{1}{12} & =9\\times \\frac{1}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\times \\frac{1}{12} & = \\\\ \\frac{9}{1}\\times \\frac{1}{12} & = \\\\ \\frac{9\\times 1}{1\\times 12} & = \\\\ \\frac{9}{12} & = \\\\ \\frac{9÷3}{12÷3} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186054398 :question/worked-solution "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS. First, consider the expression inside the parenthesis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5÷(-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying PEMDAS to this expression, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5÷(-2)^{2} & = \\\\ 5÷4 & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{4} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\overset{\\frac{5}{4}}{\\overset{⏞}{5÷(-2)^{2}}})\\times (-\\frac{4}{5}) & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{4}\\times (-\\frac{4}{5}) & = \\\\ -\\frac{5}{4}\\times \\frac{4}{5} & = \\\\ -\\frac{5\\times 4}{4\\times 5} & = \\\\ -\\frac{\\cancel{5}\\times \\cancel{4}}{\\cancel{4}\\times \\cancel{5}} & = \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS. First, consider the expression inside the parenthesis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5÷(-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying PEMDAS to this expression, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5÷(-2)^{2} & = \\\\ 5÷4 & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{4} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\overbrace{5÷(-2)^{2}}^{\\frac{5}{4}})\\times (-\\frac{4}{5}) & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{4}\\times (-\\frac{4}{5}) & = \\\\ -\\frac{5}{4}\\times \\frac{4}{5} & = \\\\ -\\frac{5\\times 4}{4\\times 5} & = \\\\ -\\frac{\\cancel{5}\\times \\cancel{4}}{\\cancel{4}\\times \\cancel{5}} & = \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186055037 :question/worked-solution "The word \"and\" is represented by the symbol \"$∧$\", while the word \"or\" is represented by the symbol \"$∨$\".\n\nNote that this sentence consists of two parts:\n\n- **Part 1:** $2$ is prime. This corresponds to $R.$\n\n- **Part 2:** $14$ is a multiple of $7$ and $5.$ This corresponds to\n\n$$\n{{14\\text{ is a multiple of }7}_{⏟}}_{S}\\,{{\\text{and}}_{⏟}}_{∧}\\,{{14\\text{ is a multiple of }5}_{⏟}}_{T},\n$$\n\nthat is, $S∧T.$\n\nSo, we can express the sentence as\n\n$$\n{{2\\text{ is prime}}_{⏟}}_{R}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{14\\text{ is a multiple of }7\\text{ and }5}_{⏟}}_{S\\,∧\\,T},\n$$\n\nthat is, $R∨(S∧T).$" "The word \"and\" is represented by the symbol \"$∧$\", while the word \"or\" is represented by the symbol \"$∨$\".\n\nNote that this sentence consists of two parts:\n\n- **Part 1:** $2$ is prime. This corresponds to $R.$\n\n- **Part 2:** $14$ is a multiple of $7$ and $5.$ This corresponds to\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{14\\text{ is a multiple of }7}_{S}\\,\\underbrace{\\text{and}}_{∧}\\,\\underbrace{14\\text{ is a multiple of }5}_{T},\n$$\n\nthat is, $S∧T.$\n\nSo, we can express the sentence as\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{2\\text{ is prime}}_{R}\\,\\underbrace{\\text{or}}_{∨}\\,\\underbrace{14\\text{ is a multiple of }7\\text{ and }5}_{S\\,∧\\,T},\n$$\n\nthat is, $R∨(S∧T).$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186055038 :question/worked-solution "The word \"and\" is represented by the symbol \"$∧$\", while the word \"or\" is represented by the symbol \"$∨$\".\n\nNote that this sentence consists of two parts:\n\n- **Part 1:** $7$ is prime or divisible by $10.$ This corresponds to\n\n$$\n{{7\\text{ is prime}}_{⏟}}_{P}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{7\\text{ is divisible by }10}_{⏟}}_{D}\n$$\n\nthat is, $P∨D.$\n\n- **Part 2:** $5$ is rational or less than $100.$ This corresponds to\n\n$$\n{{5\\text{ is rational}}_{⏟}}_{Q}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{5\\text{ is less than }100}_{⏟}}_{L},\n$$\n\nthat is, $Q∨L.$\n\nSo, we can express the sentence as\n\n$$\n{{7\\text{ is prime }\\text{or}\\text{ divisible by }10}_{⏟}}_{P\\,∨\\,D}\\,{{\\text{and}}_{⏟}}_{∧}\\,{{5\\text{ is rational }\\text{or}\\text{ less than }100}_{⏟}}_{Q\\,∨\\,L},\n$$\n\nthat is, $(P∨D)∧(Q∨L).$" "The word \"and\" is represented by the symbol \"$∧$\", while the word \"or\" is represented by the symbol \"$∨$\".\n\nNote that this sentence consists of two parts:\n\n- **Part 1:** $7$ is prime or divisible by $10.$ This corresponds to\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{7\\text{ is prime}}_{P}\\,\\underbrace{\\text{or}}_{∨}\\,\\underbrace{7\\text{ is divisible by }10}_{D}\n$$\n\nthat is, $P∨D.$\n\n- **Part 2:** $5$ is rational or less than $100.$ This corresponds to\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{5\\text{ is rational}}_{Q}\\,\\underbrace{\\text{or}}_{∨}\\,\\underbrace{5\\text{ is less than }100}_{L},\n$$\n\nthat is, $Q∨L.$\n\nSo, we can express the sentence as\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{7\\text{ is prime }\\text{or}\\text{ divisible by }10}_{P\\,∨\\,D}\\,\\underbrace{\\text{and}}_{∧}\\,\\underbrace{5\\text{ is rational }\\text{or}\\text{ less than }100}_{Q\\,∨\\,L},\n$$\n\nthat is, $(P∨D)∧(Q∨L).$"]
