 [:db/cas 17592186055381 :question/worked-solution "We rearrange the terms so that the like terms, $13x$ and $5x$, are together.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13x-2y+5x+1 & = \\\\ {{13x+5x}_{⏟}}_{x\\text{ terms}}-2y+1 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we combine the like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{13x+5x}_{⏟}-2y+1 & = \\\\ (13+5)x-2y+1 & = \\\\ 18x-2y+1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We rearrange the terms so that the like terms, $13x$ and $5x$, are together.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13x-2y+5x+1 & = \\\\ \\underbrace{13x+5x}_{x\\text{ terms}}-2y+1 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we combine the like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{13x+5x}-2y+1 & = \\\\ (13+5)x-2y+1 & = \\\\ 18x-2y+1\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186056029 :tutorial/content "Let's now consider the truth values of the logical connector \"and\". We have the following key idea:\n\n*The statement $A \\land B$ is true if $A$ is true **and** $B$ is true. Otherwise, it is false.* \n\nLet's determine the truth values of the following compound statement:\n\n$2$ is positive **and** $2$ is even.\n\nWriting this in symbols, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{2\\text{ is positive}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\,{{\\text{and}}_{⏟}}_{∧}\\,{{2\\text{ is even}}_{⏟}}_{B}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor, simply\n\n$$\nA\\land B.\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is true and $B$ is true, this compound statement is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A∧B≡\\text{true}∧\\text{true}≡\\text{true}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this context, the symbol \"$\\equiv$\" means \"is logically equivalent to.\" \n\nLet's now consider a similar compound statement but with the $2$ replaced with a $3$ in the second statement.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{2\\text{ is positive}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\,{{\\text{and}}_{⏟}}_{∧}\\,{{3\\text{ is even}}_{⏟}}_{C}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write this symbolically as follows:\n\n$$\nA\\land C\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is true and $C$ is false, this compound statement is false:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A∧C≡\\text{true}∧\\text{false}≡\\text{false}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's now consider the truth values of the logical connector \"and\". We have the following key idea:\n\n*The statement $A \\land B$ is true if $A$ is true **and** $B$ is true. Otherwise, it is false.* \n\nLet's determine the truth values of the following compound statement:\n\n$2$ is positive **and** $2$ is even.\n\nWriting this in symbols, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{2\\text{ is positive}}_{A}\\,\\underbrace{\\text{and}}_{∧}\\,\\underbrace{2\\text{ is even}}_{B}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor, simply\n\n$$\nA\\land B.\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is true and $B$ is true, this compound statement is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A∧B≡\\text{true}∧\\text{true}≡\\text{true}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this context, the symbol \"$\\equiv$\" means \"is logically equivalent to.\" \n\nLet's now consider a similar compound statement but with the $2$ replaced with a $3$ in the second statement.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{2\\text{ is positive}}_{A}\\,\\underbrace{\\text{and}}_{∧}\\,\\underbrace{3\\text{ is even}}_{C}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write this symbolically as follows:\n\n$$\nA\\land C\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is true and $C$ is false, this compound statement is false:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A∧C≡\\text{true}∧\\text{false}≡\\text{false}\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186056033 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have the indexed family $\\{A_i\\}_{i\\in I}$ where $I$ is an index set. \n\nThe **union** of a family of indexed sets is the set containing all elements belonging to *at least one* set in the indexed family:\n\n$$\n\\bigcup_{i \\in I} A_i = \\big\\{ x\\in U \\: : \\: x \\in A_i \\:\\:\\textrm{for at least one } A_i,\\:\\: i \\in I \\big\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $U$ is a universal set.\n\nFor example, suppose we have the following family of indexed sets:\n\n$$\nX_i = \\{ -i, \\,0, \\, i \\}, \\qquad i\\in \\mathbb N\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the following union:\n\n$$\n\\bigcup_{i \\in \\mathbb N} X_i\n$$\n\nTo calculate our union, let's write down a few of our indexed sets:\n\n- If $i=1,$ we have $X_1 = \\{ -1, 0, 1 \\}.$\n\n- If $i=2,$ we have $X_2 = \\{ -2, 0, 2 \\}.$\n\n- If $i=3,$ we have $X_3 = \\{ -3, 0, 3 \\}.$\n\n- $\\cdots$\n\nand so on. \n\nNow, note the following:\n\n- It's clear that the $X_i$'s contain only integers. Therefore, any union of $X_i$'s will contain only integers. So, \n\n$$\n\\bigcup_{i \\in \\mathbb N} X_i  \\subseteq \\mathbb Z.\n$$\n\n- Notice that *any* integer $n$ belongs to our union. For instance, $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ lies in $X_n=\\{ -n, 0, n \\}$ and, consequently, lies in the union of all $X_i$'s. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\bigcup_{i \\in \\mathbb N} X_i  \\supseteq \\mathbb Z.\n$$\n\nThus, using the fact that $A\\subseteq B$ and $B\\subseteq A$ if and only if $A=B,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{⋃}_{i\\in N}{X}_{i} & =\\overset{{X}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{{-1,0,1}}}\\cup \\overset{{X}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{{-2,0,2}}}\\cup \\overset{{X}_{3}}{\\overset{⏞}{{-3,0,3}}}\\cup ⋯=Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since our index set $I = \\mathbb N$ in this example, we can also use the following notation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{⋃}_{i=1}^{\\infty }{X}_{i}=Z\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Suppose we have the indexed family $\\{A_i\\}_{i\\in I}$ where $I$ is an index set. \n\nThe **union** of a family of indexed sets is the set containing all elements belonging to *at least one* set in the indexed family:\n\n$$\n\\bigcup_{i \\in I} A_i = \\big\\{ x\\in U \\: : \\: x \\in A_i \\:\\:\\textrm{for at least one } A_i,\\:\\: i \\in I \\big\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $U$ is a universal set.\n\nFor example, suppose we have the following family of indexed sets:\n\n$$\nX_i = \\{ -i, \\,0, \\, i \\}, \\qquad i\\in \\mathbb N\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the following union:\n\n$$\n\\bigcup_{i \\in \\mathbb N} X_i\n$$\n\nTo calculate our union, let's write down a few of our indexed sets:\n\n- If $i=1,$ we have $X_1 = \\{ -1, 0, 1 \\}.$\n\n- If $i=2,$ we have $X_2 = \\{ -2, 0, 2 \\}.$\n\n- If $i=3,$ we have $X_3 = \\{ -3, 0, 3 \\}.$\n\n- $\\cdots$\n\nand so on. \n\nNow, note the following:\n\n- It's clear that the $X_i$'s contain only integers. Therefore, any union of $X_i$'s will contain only integers. So, \n\n$$\n\\bigcup_{i \\in \\mathbb N} X_i  \\subseteq \\mathbb Z.\n$$\n\n- Notice that *any* integer $n$ belongs to our union. For instance, $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ lies in $X_n=\\{ -n, 0, n \\}$ and, consequently, lies in the union of all $X_i$'s. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\bigcup_{i \\in \\mathbb N} X_i  \\supseteq \\mathbb Z.\n$$\n\nThus, using the fact that $A\\subseteq B$ and $B\\subseteq A$ if and only if $A=B,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{⋃}_{i\\in N}{X}_{i} & =\\overbrace{{-1,0,1}}^{{X}_{1}}\\cup \\overbrace{{-2,0,2}}^{{X}_{2}}\\cup \\overbrace{{-3,0,3}}^{{X}_{3}}\\cup ⋯=Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since our index set $I = \\mathbb N$ in this example, we can also use the following notation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{⋃}_{i=1}^{\\infty }{X}_{i}=Z\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186056067 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we have the two polynomials $6x^2 + x +1$ and $2x^2 - 3x + 6$, and we want to add them. To do that, we put a plus sign between the two, collect like terms, and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(6x^{2} + x + 1) + (2x^{2} - 3x + 6) &=  \\\\\n6x^{2} + x + 1 + 2x^{2} - 3x + 6 &=  \\\\\n6x^{2} + 2x^{2}_(⏟)_(x^{2}terms) + x - 3x^(⏞)^(xterms) + 1 + 6_(⏟)_(constants) &=  \\\\\n(6 + 2)x^{2} + (1 - 3)x + 7 &=  \\\\\n&= 8x^{2} - 2x + 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Suppose that we have the two polynomials $6x^2 + x +1$ and $2x^2 - 3x + 6$, and we want to add them. To do that, we put a plus sign between the two, collect like terms, and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(6x^{2} + x + 1) + (2x^{2} - 3x + 6) &=  \\\\\n6x^{2} + x + 1 + 2x^{2} - 3x + 6 &=  \\\\\n\\underbrace{6x^{2} + 2x^{2}}_{{x}^{2}\\text{ terms}} + \\overbrace{x - 3x}^{x\\text{ terms}} + \\underbrace{1 + 6}_{\\text{constants}} &=  \\\\\n(6 + 2)x^{2} + (1 - 3)x + 7 &=  \\\\\n&= 8x^{2} - 2x + 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186056069 :tutorial/content "Let's think about how to factor the following trinomial:\n\n$$\nx^2 + 7x + 12\n$$\n\nThis trinomial is neither a perfect square nor a difference of squares. In such cases, the factoring process requires a little trial and error.\n\nWe can factor this trinomial in four steps:\n\n- **Step 1:** Find the factor pairs of the constant term $(12)$:The factor pairs of $12$ are\n$(1, 12), (2, 6), (3, 4)$.\n- **Step 2:** Determine which of these factor pairs add up to the coefficient of the linear term $(7)$:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 + 12|= 13 & \\cdot \\\\ 2 + 6|= 8 & \\cdot \\\\ 3 + 4|= 7 & ✓\\end{bmatrix}$\n- **Step 3:** Using the factor pair identified in the previous step, write the linear term as $7x=3x+4x$:\n$x^{2} + 7x + 12|=; x^{2} + 3x + 4x_(⏟)_(7x) + 12$\n- **Step 4:** Factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately and then factor the expression completely:\n$x^{2} + 3x + 4x + 12|=; (x^{2} + 3x) + (4x + 12)|=; x(x + 3) + 4(x + 3)|=; (x + 4)(x + 3)$\n\nAnd we're done!\n\nBefore we continue, note the following:\n\n- The process carried out in step 4 is sometimes called **factoring by grouping.** This is because we group two sets of terms and factor them individually before factoring the entire expression.\n- We can check that our answer is correct by multiplying out the factors:\n$(x + 4)(x + 3)|= x(x + 3) + 4(x + 3); = x^{2} + 3x + 4x + 12; = x^{2} + 7x + 12✓$" "Let's think about how to factor the following trinomial:\n\n$$\nx^2 + 7x + 12\n$$\n\nThis trinomial is neither a perfect square nor a difference of squares. In such cases, the factoring process requires a little trial and error.\n\nWe can factor this trinomial in four steps:\n\n- **Step 1:** Find the factor pairs of the constant term $(12)$:The factor pairs of $12$ are\n$(1, 12), (2, 6), (3, 4)$.\n- **Step 2:** Determine which of these factor pairs add up to the coefficient of the linear term $(7)$:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 + 12|= 13 & \\cdot \\\\ 2 + 6|= 8 & \\cdot \\\\ 3 + 4|= 7 & ✓\\end{bmatrix}$\n- **Step 3:** Using the factor pair identified in the previous step, write the linear term as $7x=3x+4x$:\n$x^{2} + 7x + 12|=; x^{2} + \\underbrace{3x + 4x}_{7x} + 12$\n- **Step 4:** Factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately and then factor the expression completely:\n$x^{2} + 3x + 4x + 12|=; (x^{2} + 3x) + (4x + 12)|=; x(x + 3) + 4(x + 3)|=; (x + 4)(x + 3)$\n\nAnd we're done!\n\nBefore we continue, note the following:\n\n- The process carried out in step 4 is sometimes called **factoring by grouping.** This is because we group two sets of terms and factor them individually before factoring the entire expression.\n- We can check that our answer is correct by multiplying out the factors:\n$(x + 4)(x + 3)|= x(x + 3) + 4(x + 3); = x^{2} + 3x + 4x + 12; = x^{2} + 7x + 12✓$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186056072 :tutorial/content "Let's now consider the truth values of the logical connector \"or\". We have the following key idea:\n\n*The statement $A \\lor B$ is true if $A$ is true, or $B$ is true, or both $A$ and $B$ are true. Otherwise, it is false.* \n\nLet's consider some examples:\n\n- First, consider the following statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{2\\text{ is positive}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{2\\text{ is even}}_{⏟}}_{B}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is true and $B$ is true, this compound statement is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A∨B≡\\text{true}∨\\text{true}≡\\text{true}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, consider the following statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{2\\text{ is positive}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{3\\text{ is even}}_{⏟}}_{C}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is true, this compound statement is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A∨C≡\\text{true}∨\\text{false}≡\\text{true}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, consider the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{-2\\text{ is positive}}_{⏟}}_{D}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{2\\text{ is even}}_{⏟}}_{B}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $B$ is true, this compound statement is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D∨B≡\\text{false}∨\\text{true}≡\\text{true}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Finally, consider the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{-2\\text{ is positive}}_{⏟}}_{D}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{3\\text{ is even}}_{⏟}}_{C}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D$ and $C$ are both false, this compound statement is false:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D∨C≡\\text{false}∨\\text{false}≡\\text{false}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's now consider the truth values of the logical connector \"or\". We have the following key idea:\n\n*The statement $A \\lor B$ is true if $A$ is true, or $B$ is true, or both $A$ and $B$ are true. Otherwise, it is false.* \n\nLet's consider some examples:\n\n- First, consider the following statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{2\\text{ is positive}}_{A}\\,\\underbrace{\\text{or}}_{∨}\\,\\underbrace{2\\text{ is even}}_{B}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is true and $B$ is true, this compound statement is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A∨B≡\\text{true}∨\\text{true}≡\\text{true}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, consider the following statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{2\\text{ is positive}}_{A}\\,\\underbrace{\\text{or}}_{∨}\\,\\underbrace{3\\text{ is even}}_{C}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is true, this compound statement is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A∨C≡\\text{true}∨\\text{false}≡\\text{true}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, consider the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{-2\\text{ is positive}}_{D}\\,\\underbrace{\\text{or}}_{∨}\\,\\underbrace{2\\text{ is even}}_{B}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $B$ is true, this compound statement is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D∨B≡\\text{false}∨\\text{true}≡\\text{true}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Finally, consider the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{-2\\text{ is positive}}_{D}\\,\\underbrace{\\text{or}}_{∨}\\,\\underbrace{3\\text{ is even}}_{C}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D$ and $C$ are both false, this compound statement is false:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D∨C≡\\text{false}∨\\text{false}≡\\text{false}\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186060894 :tutorial/content "To understand why the derivative of sine is cosine, we need to use the definition of the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}} {\\textrm{d}x} (\\sin x) =  \\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0}\\dfrac{\\sin(x+h) - \\sin x}{h}\n$$\n\nNow, the so-called **addition formula for sine** states that\n\n$$\n\\sin(x+h) = \\sin(x)\\cos(h)+\\cos (x)  \\sin(h).\n$$\n\nBy using the addition formula for sine, we can rewrite our expression for the derivative as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(h)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(h)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(h)-1)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(h)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(h)-1)}{h}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(h)}{h}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){{{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(h)-1)}{h}}_{⏟}}_{0}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x){{{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(h)}{h}}_{⏟}}_{1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the last step, we used two important results for trigonometric limits:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{1 - \\cos(h)}{h}=0 \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad \\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{\\sin(h)}{h}=1.\n$$\n\nDeriving these results requires separate discussions, which we'll leave to future topics." "To understand why the derivative of sine is cosine, we need to use the definition of the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}} {\\textrm{d}x} (\\sin x) =  \\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0}\\dfrac{\\sin(x+h) - \\sin x}{h}\n$$\n\nNow, the so-called **addition formula for sine** states that\n\n$$\n\\sin(x+h) = \\sin(x)\\cos(h)+\\cos (x)  \\sin(h).\n$$\n\nBy using the addition formula for sine, we can rewrite our expression for the derivative as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(h)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(h)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(h)-1)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(h)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(h)-1)}{h}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(h)}{h}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\underbrace{{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(h)-1)}{h}}_{0}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\underbrace{{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(h)}{h}}_{1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the last step, we used two important results for trigonometric limits:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{1 - \\cos(h)}{h}=0 \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad \\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{\\sin(h)}{h}=1.\n$$\n\nDeriving these results requires separate discussions, which we'll leave to future topics."]
 [:db/cas 17592186060895 :tutorial/content "We can follow the same method for the cosine and use the definition of the derivative to show that $(\\cos x)'=-\\sin x.$ First, we start with the definition of the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}} {\\textrm{d}x} (\\cos x) =  \\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0}\\dfrac{\\cos(x+h) - \\cos x}{h}\n$$\n\nNow, the so-called **addition formula for cosine** states that\n\n$$\n\\cos(x+h) = \\cos(x)\\cos(h)- \\sin(x)\\sin(h).\n$$\n\nUsing the addition formula for cosine, we can rewrite our expression for the derivative as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(h)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(h)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(h)-1)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(h)}{h} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x){{{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(h)-1}{h}}_{⏟}}_{0}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){{{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(h)}{h}}_{⏟}}_{1} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnce again, in the last step, we used the special limits for sine and cosine." "We can follow the same method for the cosine and use the definition of the derivative to show that $(\\cos x)'=-\\sin x.$ First, we start with the definition of the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}} {\\textrm{d}x} (\\cos x) =  \\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0}\\dfrac{\\cos(x+h) - \\cos x}{h}\n$$\n\nNow, the so-called **addition formula for cosine** states that\n\n$$\n\\cos(x+h) = \\cos(x)\\cos(h)- \\sin(x)\\sin(h).\n$$\n\nUsing the addition formula for cosine, we can rewrite our expression for the derivative as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(h)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(h)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(h)-1)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(h)}{h} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\underbrace{{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(h)-1}{h}}_{0}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\underbrace{{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(h)}{h}}_{1} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnce again, in the last step, we used the special limits for sine and cosine."]
 [:db/cas 17592186060921 :tutorial/content "To understand the reasoning behind the formula for the dot product of two vectors, it's helpful to write the two vectors in terms of their elementary unit vectors and carry out the computation the long way.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to find the dot product $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b}$ for $\\mathbf{a} = \\langle 3,-5 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 4,6 \\rangle.$\n\nExpressing the vectors in terms of their elementary unit vectors, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=3i-5j,\\,b=4i+6j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using properties of the dot product we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =(3i+(-5)j)\\cdot (4i+6j) \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 4)i\\cdot i+(3\\cdot 6)i\\cdot j+(-5\\cdot 4)j\\cdot i+(-5\\cdot 6)j\\cdot j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that $\\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j}$ are perpendicular, meaning the angle between them is $\\theta=90^\\circ$ and consequently their dot product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}i\\cdot j & =|\\,i\\,|\\cdot |\\,j\\,|\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}{90}^{∘} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, since the angle between a vector and itself is $\\theta=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}i\\cdot i & =|\\,i\\,|\\cdot |\\,i\\,|\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}{0}^{∘} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}j\\cdot j & =|\\,j\\,|\\cdot |\\,j\\,|\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}{0}^{∘} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}i\\cdot j & =j\\cdot i=0, \\\\ i\\cdot i & =j\\cdot j=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand our computation reduces to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =(3\\cdot 4)\\overset{=1}{\\overset{⏞}{i\\cdot i}}+(3\\cdot 6)\\overset{=0}{\\overset{⏞}{i\\cdot j}}+(-5\\cdot 4)\\overset{=0}{\\overset{⏞}{j\\cdot i}}+(-5\\cdot 6)\\overset{=1}{\\overset{⏞}{j\\cdot j}} \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 4)\\cdot 1+(3\\cdot 6)\\cdot 0+(-5\\cdot 4)\\cdot 0+(-5\\cdot 6)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 4)+(-5\\cdot 6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =⟨3,-5⟩\\cdot ⟨4,6⟩ \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 4)+(-5\\cdot 6).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To understand the reasoning behind the formula for the dot product of two vectors, it's helpful to write the two vectors in terms of their elementary unit vectors and carry out the computation the long way.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to find the dot product $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b}$ for $\\mathbf{a} = \\langle 3,-5 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 4,6 \\rangle.$\n\nExpressing the vectors in terms of their elementary unit vectors, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=3i-5j,\\,b=4i+6j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using properties of the dot product we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =(3i+(-5)j)\\cdot (4i+6j) \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 4)i\\cdot i+(3\\cdot 6)i\\cdot j+(-5\\cdot 4)j\\cdot i+(-5\\cdot 6)j\\cdot j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that $\\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j}$ are perpendicular, meaning the angle between them is $\\theta=90^\\circ$ and consequently their dot product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}i\\cdot j & =|\\,i\\,|\\cdot |\\,j\\,|\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}{90}^{∘} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, since the angle between a vector and itself is $\\theta=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}i\\cdot i & =|\\,i\\,|\\cdot |\\,i\\,|\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}{0}^{∘} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}j\\cdot j & =|\\,j\\,|\\cdot |\\,j\\,|\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}{0}^{∘} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}i\\cdot j & =j\\cdot i=0, \\\\ i\\cdot i & =j\\cdot j=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand our computation reduces to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =(3\\cdot 4)\\overbrace{i\\cdot i}^{=1}+(3\\cdot 6)\\overbrace{i\\cdot j}^{=0}+(-5\\cdot 4)\\overbrace{j\\cdot i}^{=0}+(-5\\cdot 6)\\overbrace{j\\cdot j}^{=1} \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 4)\\cdot 1+(3\\cdot 6)\\cdot 0+(-5\\cdot 4)\\cdot 0+(-5\\cdot 6)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 4)+(-5\\cdot 6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =⟨3,-5⟩\\cdot ⟨4,6⟩ \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 4)+(-5\\cdot 6).\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186060923 :tutorial/content "We have been using the following formula to compute the cross product of two vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ {b}_{2} & {b}_{3}\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & {a}_{3} \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{3}\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =({a}_{2}{b}_{3}-{a}_{3}{b}_{2})i-({a}_{1}{b}_{3}-{a}_{3}{b}_{1})j+({a}_{1}{b}_{2}-{a}_{2}{b}_{1})k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBut where does this formula come from? Let's find out.\n\nSuppose we want to find the cross product $\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}$ for $\\mathbf{a}=a_1\\mathbf{i}+a_2\\mathbf{j}+a_3\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=b_1\\mathbf{i}+b_2\\mathbf{j}+b_3\\mathbf{k}.$ Using properties of the cross product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =({a}_{1}i+{a}_{2}j+{a}_{3}k)\\times ({b}_{1}i+{b}_{2}j+{b}_{3}k) \\\\  & =({a}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{1})i\\times i+({a}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{2})i\\times j+({a}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{3})i\\times k \\\\  & +({a}_{2}\\cdot {b}_{1})j\\times i+({a}_{2}\\cdot {b}_{2})j\\times j+({a}_{2}\\cdot {b}_{3})j\\times k \\\\  & +({a}_{3}\\cdot {b}_{1})k\\times i+({a}_{3}\\cdot {b}_{2})k\\times j+({a}_{3}\\cdot {b}_{3})k\\times k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the cross product of two parallel (collinear) vectors is equal to $\\mathbf 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{i}\\times\\mathbf{i} = \\mathbf{j}\\times\\mathbf{j} = \\mathbf{k}\\times\\mathbf{k} = \\mathbf 0.\n$$\n\nAlso, using the definition of the cross product we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}i\\times j=k, & \\,j\\times i=-k, \\\\ j\\times k=i, & \\,k\\times j=-i, \\\\ k\\times i=j, & \\,i\\times k=-j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =({a}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{1}){{i\\times i}_{⏟}}_{=0}+({a}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{2}){{i\\times j}_{⏟}}_{=k}+({a}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{3}){{i\\times k}_{⏟}}_{=-j} \\\\  & +({a}_{2}\\cdot {b}_{1}){{j\\times i}_{⏟}}_{=-k}+({a}_{2}\\cdot {b}_{2}){{j\\times j}_{⏟}}_{=0}+({a}_{2}\\cdot {b}_{3}){{j\\times k}_{⏟}}_{=i} \\\\  & +({a}_{3}\\cdot {b}_{1}){{k\\times i}_{⏟}}_{=j}+({a}_{3}\\cdot {b}_{2}){{k\\times j}_{⏟}}_{=-i}+({a}_{3}\\cdot {b}_{3}){{k\\times k}_{⏟}}_{=0} \\\\  & =({a}_{2}{b}_{3}-{a}_{3}{b}_{2})i-({a}_{1}{b}_{3}-{a}_{3}{b}_{1})j+({a}_{1}{b}_{2}-{a}_{2}{b}_{1})k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression above is equal to the formula given for the cross product of two general vectors using the determinant." "We have been using the following formula to compute the cross product of two vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ {b}_{2} & {b}_{3}\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & {a}_{3} \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{3}\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =({a}_{2}{b}_{3}-{a}_{3}{b}_{2})i-({a}_{1}{b}_{3}-{a}_{3}{b}_{1})j+({a}_{1}{b}_{2}-{a}_{2}{b}_{1})k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBut where does this formula come from? Let's find out.\n\nSuppose we want to find the cross product $\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}$ for $\\mathbf{a}=a_1\\mathbf{i}+a_2\\mathbf{j}+a_3\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=b_1\\mathbf{i}+b_2\\mathbf{j}+b_3\\mathbf{k}.$ Using properties of the cross product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =({a}_{1}i+{a}_{2}j+{a}_{3}k)\\times ({b}_{1}i+{b}_{2}j+{b}_{3}k) \\\\  & =({a}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{1})i\\times i+({a}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{2})i\\times j+({a}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{3})i\\times k \\\\  & +({a}_{2}\\cdot {b}_{1})j\\times i+({a}_{2}\\cdot {b}_{2})j\\times j+({a}_{2}\\cdot {b}_{3})j\\times k \\\\  & +({a}_{3}\\cdot {b}_{1})k\\times i+({a}_{3}\\cdot {b}_{2})k\\times j+({a}_{3}\\cdot {b}_{3})k\\times k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the cross product of two parallel (collinear) vectors is equal to $\\mathbf 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{i}\\times\\mathbf{i} = \\mathbf{j}\\times\\mathbf{j} = \\mathbf{k}\\times\\mathbf{k} = \\mathbf 0.\n$$\n\nAlso, using the definition of the cross product we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}i\\times j=k, & \\,j\\times i=-k, \\\\ j\\times k=i, & \\,k\\times j=-i, \\\\ k\\times i=j, & \\,i\\times k=-j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =({a}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{1})\\underbrace{i\\times i}_{=0}+({a}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{2})\\underbrace{i\\times j}_{=k}+({a}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{3})\\underbrace{i\\times k}_{=-j} \\\\  & +({a}_{2}\\cdot {b}_{1})\\underbrace{j\\times i}_{=-k}+({a}_{2}\\cdot {b}_{2})\\underbrace{j\\times j}_{=0}+({a}_{2}\\cdot {b}_{3})\\underbrace{j\\times k}_{=i} \\\\  & +({a}_{3}\\cdot {b}_{1})\\underbrace{k\\times i}_{=j}+({a}_{3}\\cdot {b}_{2})\\underbrace{k\\times j}_{=-i}+({a}_{3}\\cdot {b}_{3})\\underbrace{k\\times k}_{=0} \\\\  & =({a}_{2}{b}_{3}-{a}_{3}{b}_{2})i-({a}_{1}{b}_{3}-{a}_{3}{b}_{1})j+({a}_{1}{b}_{2}-{a}_{2}{b}_{1})k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression above is equal to the formula given for the cross product of two general vectors using the determinant."]
 [:db/cas 17592186060980 :tutorial/content "Let's recall the table that relates metric units of length.\n\n| Name | Symbol | Conversion |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Kilometer | $\\textrm{km}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{km} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{m}$ |\n| Meter | $\\textrm{m}$ | Base Unit |\n| Centimeter | $\\textrm{cm}$ | $100\\,\\textrm{cm} = 1\\,\\textrm{m}$ |\n| Millimeter | $\\textrm{mm}$ | $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mm} = 1\\,\\textrm{m}$ |\n\n**Important**: Symbols in the metric system follow a consistent set of **prefix rules**, which we explain below. If you find these difficult, *do not worry*! For now, you just need to remember the conversions shown in the table above. \n\nThe prefix rules are as follows:\n\n- The prefix \"$\\textrm k$\" stands for **kilo**, which means $1\\,000$. Therefore, $1\\,\\textrm{km} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{m}.$\n\n- The prefix \"$\\textrm c$\" stands for **centi**, which means $\\dfrac{1}{100}$. Therefore, $1\\,\\textrm{cm} = \\dfrac{1}{100}\\,\\textrm{m}.$ \n\n Notice that if we add $1\\,\\textrm{cm}$ to itself $100$ times, we get exactly $1\\,\\textrm{m}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{100\\text{ times}}{\\overset{⏞}{1\\,\\text{cm}+1\\,\\text{cm}+⋯+1\\,\\text{cm}}} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{100}\\,\\text{m}+\\frac{1}{100}\\,\\text{m}+⋯+\\frac{1}{100}\\,\\text{m} & = \\\\ 100\\times \\frac{1}{100}\\,\\text{m} & = \\\\ \\frac{100\\times 1}{100}\\,\\text{m} & = \\\\ \\frac{100}{100}\\,\\text{m} & = \\\\ 1\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $100\\,\\textrm{cm}$ equals $1\\,\\textrm{m},$ as stated in the table.\n\n- The prefix \"$\\textrm m$\" stands for **milli**, which means $\\dfrac{1}{1\\,000}$. Therefore, $1\\,\\textrm{mm} = \\dfrac{1}{1,000}\\,\\textrm{m}.$ This means that $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mm}$ equals $1\\,\\textrm{m},$ as shown in the table." "Let's recall the table that relates metric units of length.\n\n| Name | Symbol | Conversion |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Kilometer | $\\textrm{km}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{km} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{m}$ |\n| Meter | $\\textrm{m}$ | Base Unit |\n| Centimeter | $\\textrm{cm}$ | $100\\,\\textrm{cm} = 1\\,\\textrm{m}$ |\n| Millimeter | $\\textrm{mm}$ | $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mm} = 1\\,\\textrm{m}$ |\n\n**Important**: Symbols in the metric system follow a consistent set of **prefix rules**, which we explain below. If you find these difficult, *do not worry*! For now, you just need to remember the conversions shown in the table above. \n\nThe prefix rules are as follows:\n\n- The prefix \"$\\textrm k$\" stands for **kilo**, which means $1\\,000$. Therefore, $1\\,\\textrm{km} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{m}.$\n\n- The prefix \"$\\textrm c$\" stands for **centi**, which means $\\dfrac{1}{100}$. Therefore, $1\\,\\textrm{cm} = \\dfrac{1}{100}\\,\\textrm{m}.$ \n\n Notice that if we add $1\\,\\textrm{cm}$ to itself $100$ times, we get exactly $1\\,\\textrm{m}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{1\\,\\text{cm}+1\\,\\text{cm}+⋯+1\\,\\text{cm}}^{100\\text{ times}} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{100}\\,\\text{m}+\\frac{1}{100}\\,\\text{m}+⋯+\\frac{1}{100}\\,\\text{m} & = \\\\ 100\\times \\frac{1}{100}\\,\\text{m} & = \\\\ \\frac{100\\times 1}{100}\\,\\text{m} & = \\\\ \\frac{100}{100}\\,\\text{m} & = \\\\ 1\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $100\\,\\textrm{cm}$ equals $1\\,\\textrm{m},$ as stated in the table.\n\n- The prefix \"$\\textrm m$\" stands for **milli**, which means $\\dfrac{1}{1\\,000}$. Therefore, $1\\,\\textrm{mm} = \\dfrac{1}{1,000}\\,\\textrm{m}.$ This means that $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mm}$ equals $1\\,\\textrm{m},$ as shown in the table."]
 [:db/cas 17592186060989 :tutorial/content "To multiply powers of the same base, we simply *add* the exponents. This is called the **product rule for exponents**.\nFor example, in the expression \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3}^4 \\times {\\color{blue}3}^2,\n$$\n\nboth powers have the same base $({\\color{blue}3}).$ Therefore, we can simplify this expression by adding the exponents:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3}^4 \\times {\\color{blue}3}^2 = {\\color{blue}3}^{4+2} = {\\color{blue}3}^6\n$$\n\n**Note:** The product rule for exponents is really just a quicker alternative to using repeated multiplication, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{4}\\times {3}^{2} & = \\\\ {{3\\times 3\\times 3\\times 3}_{⏟}}_{4\\text{ copies}}\\times {{3\\times 3}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ copies}} & = \\\\ {{3\\times 3\\times 3\\times 3\\times 3\\times 3}_{⏟}}_{6\\text{ copies}} & = \\\\ {3}^{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To multiply powers of the same base, we simply *add* the exponents. This is called the **product rule for exponents**.\nFor example, in the expression \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3}^4 \\times {\\color{blue}3}^2,\n$$\n\nboth powers have the same base $({\\color{blue}3}).$ Therefore, we can simplify this expression by adding the exponents:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3}^4 \\times {\\color{blue}3}^2 = {\\color{blue}3}^{4+2} = {\\color{blue}3}^6\n$$\n\n**Note:** The product rule for exponents is really just a quicker alternative to using repeated multiplication, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{4}\\times {3}^{2} & = \\\\ \\underbrace{3\\times 3\\times 3\\times 3}_{4\\text{ copies}}\\times \\underbrace{3\\times 3}_{2\\text{ copies}} & = \\\\ \\underbrace{3\\times 3\\times 3\\times 3\\times 3\\times 3}_{6\\text{ copies}} & = \\\\ {3}^{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186061006 :tutorial/content "How do we find the determinant of a $3\\times3$ matrix such as\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nOne way to do this is to take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & ={{(\\,7\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{11}}\\cdot \\overset{{M}_{11}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}}}-{{(\\,1\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{12}}\\cdot \\overset{{M}_{12}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{vmatrix}}}+{{(-3)}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{13}}\\cdot \\overset{{M}_{13}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix}}} \\\\  & =7(-2-0)-1(-3-20)-3(0-10) \\\\  & =-14+23+30 \\\\  & =39\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the above, we have denoted $\\color{blue}M_{1j}$ as the minor of the entry ${\\color{red}a_{1j}}.$\n\nIn general, the determinant of a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = {\\color{red}a_{11}}{\\color{blue}M_{11}} - {\\color{red}a_{12}}{\\color{blue}M_{12}} + {\\color{red}a_{13}}{\\color{blue}M_{13}}.\n$$\n\nThat is, if $\\begin{bmatrix}a & b & c \\\\ d & e & f \\\\ g & h & i\\end{bmatrix}$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}a & b & c \\\\ d & e & f \\\\ g & h & i\\end{vmatrix} & =a\\begin{vmatrix}e & f \\\\ h & i\\end{vmatrix}-b\\begin{vmatrix}d & f \\\\ g & i\\end{vmatrix}+c\\begin{vmatrix}d & e \\\\ g & h\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =a(ei-fh)-b(di-fg)+c(dh-eg).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Important:** Do not forget about the *minus sign* in front of the *second term* in the formula!" "How do we find the determinant of a $3\\times3$ matrix such as\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nOne way to do this is to take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & =\\underbrace{(\\,7\\,)}_{{a}_{11}}\\cdot \\overbrace{\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}}^{{M}_{11}}-\\underbrace{(\\,1\\,)}_{{a}_{12}}\\cdot \\overbrace{\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{vmatrix}}^{{M}_{12}}+\\underbrace{(-3)}_{{a}_{13}}\\cdot \\overbrace{\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix}}^{{M}_{13}} \\\\  & =7(-2-0)-1(-3-20)-3(0-10) \\\\  & =-14+23+30 \\\\  & =39\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the above, we have denoted $\\color{blue}M_{1j}$ as the minor of the entry ${\\color{red}a_{1j}}.$\n\nIn general, the determinant of a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = {\\color{red}a_{11}}{\\color{blue}M_{11}} - {\\color{red}a_{12}}{\\color{blue}M_{12}} + {\\color{red}a_{13}}{\\color{blue}M_{13}}.\n$$\n\nThat is, if $\\begin{bmatrix}a & b & c \\\\ d & e & f \\\\ g & h & i\\end{bmatrix}$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}a & b & c \\\\ d & e & f \\\\ g & h & i\\end{vmatrix} & =a\\begin{vmatrix}e & f \\\\ h & i\\end{vmatrix}-b\\begin{vmatrix}d & f \\\\ g & i\\end{vmatrix}+c\\begin{vmatrix}d & e \\\\ g & h\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =a(ei-fh)-b(di-fg)+c(dh-eg).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Important:** Do not forget about the *minus sign* in front of the *second term* in the formula!"]
 [:db/cas 17592186064081 :question/worked-solution "Let $u = x^2+1.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve the integral in terms of $u,$ and then express our final answer in terms of $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{3}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{2}\\cdot x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\overset{u-1}{\\overset{⏞}{{x}^{2}}}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\cdot \\overset{\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u}{\\overset{⏞}{x\\,\\text{d}x}} \\\\  & =\\int (u-1)\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int (u-1){u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int ({u}^{3/2}-{u}^{1/2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{2}{5}{u}^{5/2}-\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2})+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}({x}^{2}+1)^{5/2}-\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{2}+1)^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{5}}-\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $u = x^2+1.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve the integral in terms of $u,$ and then express our final answer in terms of $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{3}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{2}\\cdot x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\overbrace{{x}^{2}}^{u-1}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\cdot \\overbrace{x\\,\\text{d}x}^{\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u} \\\\  & =\\int (u-1)\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int (u-1){u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int ({u}^{3/2}-{u}^{1/2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{2}{5}{u}^{5/2}-\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2})+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}({x}^{2}+1)^{5/2}-\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{2}+1)^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{5}}-\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186064102 :question/worked-solution "Splitting the fraction into separate terms and then simplifying the fractions in the individual terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac{6y − 1}{3} &= \\\n\t\t\\dfrac{6y}{3} - \\dfrac 1 3 &= \\\n\t\t\\dfrac{6}{3} \\cdot y - \\dfrac 1 3 &= \\\t\n\t\t2 \\cdot y - \\dfrac 1 3 &= \\\t\t\n\t\t2y - \\dfrac 1 3 &.\n\t}\n$$\n\nWriting the above in the form $ay+b,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{(2)}_{⏟}}_{a}y+{{(-\\frac{1}{3})}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=2$ and $b=-\\dfrac{1}{3}.$" "Splitting the fraction into separate terms and then simplifying the fractions in the individual terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac{6y − 1}{3} &= \\\n\t\t\\dfrac{6y}{3} - \\dfrac 1 3 &= \\\n\t\t\\dfrac{6}{3} \\cdot y - \\dfrac 1 3 &= \\\t\n\t\t2 \\cdot y - \\dfrac 1 3 &= \\\t\t\n\t\t2y - \\dfrac 1 3 &.\n\t}\n$$\n\nWriting the above in the form $ay+b,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{(2)}_{a}y+\\underbrace{(-\\frac{1}{3})}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=2$ and $b=-\\dfrac{1}{3}.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186064103 :question/worked-solution "Splitting the fraction into separate terms and then simplifying the fractions in the individual terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac{1 − 2a + 16b}{4} &= \\\n\t\t\\dfrac 1 4 − \\dfrac {2a} {4} + \\dfrac {16b}{4} &= \\\n\t\t\\dfrac 1 4 − \\dfrac {2} {4} \\cdot a + \\dfrac {16}{4} \\cdot b &= \\\n\t\t\\dfrac 1 4 − \\dfrac {1} {2} \\cdot a + 4 \\cdot b &= \\\t\t\n\t\t\\dfrac 1 4 − \\dfrac {a} {2} + 4b &= \\\n                \\dfrac{1}{4} - \\dfrac{1}{2}a + 4b &.\n\t}\n$$\n\nWriting the above in the form $p+qa+rb,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{(\\frac{1}{4})}_{⏟}}_{p}+{{(-\\frac{1}{2})}_{⏟}}_{q}a+{{(4)}_{⏟}}_{r}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\dfrac{1}{4},$ $q=-\\dfrac{1}{2},$ and $r=4.$" "Splitting the fraction into separate terms and then simplifying the fractions in the individual terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac{1 − 2a + 16b}{4} &= \\\n\t\t\\dfrac 1 4 − \\dfrac {2a} {4} + \\dfrac {16b}{4} &= \\\n\t\t\\dfrac 1 4 − \\dfrac {2} {4} \\cdot a + \\dfrac {16}{4} \\cdot b &= \\\n\t\t\\dfrac 1 4 − \\dfrac {1} {2} \\cdot a + 4 \\cdot b &= \\\t\t\n\t\t\\dfrac 1 4 − \\dfrac {a} {2} + 4b &= \\\n                \\dfrac{1}{4} - \\dfrac{1}{2}a + 4b &.\n\t}\n$$\n\nWriting the above in the form $p+qa+rb,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{(\\frac{1}{4})}_{p}+\\underbrace{(-\\frac{1}{2})}_{q}a+\\underbrace{(4)}_{r}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\dfrac{1}{4},$ $q=-\\dfrac{1}{2},$ and $r=4.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186064516 :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=3,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](images/b1/b14fbfe336923b44e4e4f3660bef7c6be97ada82ea07c4523a3f25f2905f4fd6.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [-3,0],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a triangle. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [0,3],$  for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a square.  This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [-3,0],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the triangle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [0,3],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the triangle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={3}^{2} \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(3) & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=\\frac{9}{2}+9=\\frac{27}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=3,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](images/b1/b14fbfe336923b44e4e4f3660bef7c6be97ada82ea07c4523a3f25f2905f4fd6.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [-3,0],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a triangle. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [0,3],$  for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a square.  This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\underbrace{{\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{{A}_{1}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [-3,0],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the triangle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [0,3],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the triangle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={3}^{2} \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(3) & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=\\frac{9}{2}+9=\\frac{27}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186064517 :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=5,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(5)={\\int }_{0}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](images/7d/7db30a3be837d9303ce4fca0b5a95466a74fe53f1ce81e6fa0febe2ef00625b5.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [0,2],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a triangle. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [2,5],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a quarter-circle cut out of a rectangle. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(5)={\\int }_{0}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [0,2],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the triangle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [2,5],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the quarter-circle cut out of a rectangle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =bh-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 4-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\  & =12-\\frac{9}{4}\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(5) & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =4+(12-\\frac{9}{4}\\pi ) \\\\  & =16-\\frac{9}{4}\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=5,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(5)={\\int }_{0}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](images/7d/7db30a3be837d9303ce4fca0b5a95466a74fe53f1ce81e6fa0febe2ef00625b5.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [0,2],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a triangle. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [2,5],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a quarter-circle cut out of a rectangle. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(5)={\\int }_{0}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\underbrace{{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{{A}_{1}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [0,2],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the triangle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [2,5],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the quarter-circle cut out of a rectangle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =bh-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 4-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\  & =12-\\frac{9}{4}\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(5) & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =4+(12-\\frac{9}{4}\\pi ) \\\\  & =16-\\frac{9}{4}\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186065344 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to solve the quadratic equation\n\n$$\nx^2+5x+6=0\n$$\n\nThe square root method won't work here, since there is a linear term. We can't factor out the variable, either, since there is a constant term. So, what should we do?\n\nOne way to solve this is to factor the entire trinomial on the left-hand side. To do this, we need to find two numbers that\n\n- multiply to give $6$ (the constant term) and\n- add to give $5$ (the linear coefficient).\n\nThese two numbers are $2$ and $3$. So, we can factor the left-hand-side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 5x + 6 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} + 2x + 3x_(⏟)_(5x) + 6 &= 0 \\\\\n(x^{2} + 2x) + (3x + 6) &= 0 \\\\\nx(x + 2) + 3(x + 2) &= 0 \\\\\n(x + 3)(x + 2) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have\n\n$$\nx+3=0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad x+2=0\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\nx=-3 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad x=-2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-3,-2$." "Suppose that we want to solve the quadratic equation\n\n$$\nx^2+5x+6=0\n$$\n\nThe square root method won't work here, since there is a linear term. We can't factor out the variable, either, since there is a constant term. So, what should we do?\n\nOne way to solve this is to factor the entire trinomial on the left-hand side. To do this, we need to find two numbers that\n\n- multiply to give $6$ (the constant term) and\n- add to give $5$ (the linear coefficient).\n\nThese two numbers are $2$ and $3$. So, we can factor the left-hand-side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 5x + 6 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} + \\underbrace{2x + 3x}_{5x} + 6 &= 0 \\\\\n(x^{2} + 2x) + (3x + 6) &= 0 \\\\\nx(x + 2) + 3(x + 2) &= 0 \\\\\n(x + 3)(x + 2) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have\n\n$$\nx+3=0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad x+2=0\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\nx=-3 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad x=-2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-3,-2$."]
 [:db/cas 17592186065425 :tutorial/content "Subtracting a negative number is the same as adding a positive number. To remember this, just think that whenever you see a minus ($-$) sign followed by a negative ($-$) sign, they combine into a single plus $(+)$ sign, like so:\n![](images/54/54cf3d5630d0f6f43570b1c290019e92d035acaaac13b43ac40b7e3b093f988b.svg)\n\nA minus sign followed by a negative sign can be hard to make out, so it's considered better form to place parentheses around the negative number. For example, $9-(-4)$ is the same thing as $9 + 4.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\,\\overset{+}{\\overset{⏞}{-\\,(-}}\\,4) & = \\\\ 9\\,+\\,4 & = \\\\ 13 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n    \t\t- 16 -(-5) &= - 16 + 5.\n\t}\n$$\n\nTo compute the result of adding $5$ to $-16,$ we start at $-16$ and move $5$ places to the right:\n\n![](images/fa/fa636855c7ec14299ddcb750b21561799f2b76848d52ee492f0a868cd77bccf0.png)\n\n \n\nWhen we do this, we land at $-11$. Therefore, \n\n$$\n-16-(-5)=-16+5=-11.\n$$" "Subtracting a negative number is the same as adding a positive number. To remember this, just think that whenever you see a minus ($-$) sign followed by a negative ($-$) sign, they combine into a single plus $(+)$ sign, like so:\n![](images/54/54cf3d5630d0f6f43570b1c290019e92d035acaaac13b43ac40b7e3b093f988b.svg)\n\nA minus sign followed by a negative sign can be hard to make out, so it's considered better form to place parentheses around the negative number. For example, $9-(-4)$ is the same thing as $9 + 4.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\,\\overbrace{-\\,(-}^{+}\\,4) & = \\\\ 9\\,+\\,4 & = \\\\ 13 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n    \t\t- 16 -(-5) &= - 16 + 5.\n\t}\n$$\n\nTo compute the result of adding $5$ to $-16,$ we start at $-16$ and move $5$ places to the right:\n\n![](images/fa/fa636855c7ec14299ddcb750b21561799f2b76848d52ee492f0a868cd77bccf0.png)\n\n \n\nWhen we do this, we land at $-11$. Therefore, \n\n$$\n-16-(-5)=-16+5=-11.\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186065444 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we introduce a method of integration that can be used in cases where integration by substitution fails. The method is known as **integration by parts**. The so-called **by-parts formula** is \n\n$$\n\\int u\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x}\\,\\textrm{d}x= uv- \\int v\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nor simply\n\n$$\n\\int uv'\\,\\textrm{d}x= uv- \\int vu'\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis method is handy for integrating products of functions where one factor can be easily integrated, and the other factor can easily be differentiated. \n\nLet's see how to apply it to evaluate the integral \n\n$$\n\\int xe^{2x} \\textrm{d}x\\,.\n$$\n\nThe idea is to call one of the factors $u,$ the other factor $v',$ and then apply the by-parts formula. We'll choose the following:\n\n$$\n\\int \\underbrace{\\color{blue}x}_{\\large\\color{blue}u}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\color{red}e^{2x}}_{\\large\\color{red}v'} \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nNow, if ${\\color{blue}u} = {\\color{blue}x}$ then we differentiate to get ${\\color{blue}u'} = {\\color{blue}1}.$ And if ${\\color{red}v'} = {\\color{red}e^{2x}}$ then we integrate to get ${\\color{red}v} = {\\color{red}\\dfrac{1}{2}e^{2x}}.$ Note that we don't bother with the constant of integration here.\n\nWe now have everything we need, so we can substitute into the \"by-parts\" formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {{x\\cdot {e}^{2x}}_{⏟}}_{u{v}^{′}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={{x\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}}_{⏟}}_{uv}-\\int {{\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}\\cdot 1}_{⏟}}_{v{u}^{′}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{2x}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{2x}(2x-1)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done! Notice that integration by parts allows us to take a complicated integral $\\displaystyle \\int xe^{2x}\\,\\textrm{d}x$ and replace it with the simpler $\\displaystyle \\int e^{2x}\\,\\textrm{d}x$ plus some additional terms." "In this lesson, we introduce a method of integration that can be used in cases where integration by substitution fails. The method is known as **integration by parts**. The so-called **by-parts formula** is \n\n$$\n\\int u\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x}\\,\\textrm{d}x= uv- \\int v\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nor simply\n\n$$\n\\int uv'\\,\\textrm{d}x= uv- \\int vu'\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis method is handy for integrating products of functions where one factor can be easily integrated, and the other factor can easily be differentiated. \n\nLet's see how to apply it to evaluate the integral \n\n$$\n\\int xe^{2x} \\textrm{d}x\\,.\n$$\n\nThe idea is to call one of the factors $u,$ the other factor $v',$ and then apply the by-parts formula. We'll choose the following:\n\n$$\n\\int \\underbrace{\\color{blue}x}_{\\large\\color{blue}u}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\color{red}e^{2x}}_{\\large\\color{red}v'} \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nNow, if ${\\color{blue}u} = {\\color{blue}x}$ then we differentiate to get ${\\color{blue}u'} = {\\color{blue}1}.$ And if ${\\color{red}v'} = {\\color{red}e^{2x}}$ then we integrate to get ${\\color{red}v} = {\\color{red}\\dfrac{1}{2}e^{2x}}.$ Note that we don't bother with the constant of integration here.\n\nWe now have everything we need, so we can substitute into the \"by-parts\" formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\underbrace{x\\cdot {e}^{2x}}_{u{v}^{′}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\underbrace{x\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}}_{uv}-\\int \\underbrace{\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}\\cdot 1}_{v{u}^{′}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{2x}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{2x}(2x-1)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done! Notice that integration by parts allows us to take a complicated integral $\\displaystyle \\int xe^{2x}\\,\\textrm{d}x$ and replace it with the simpler $\\displaystyle \\int e^{2x}\\,\\textrm{d}x$ plus some additional terms."]
 [:db/cas 17592186069298 :question/worked-solution "From the figure, we note that the points $A$ and $B$ have coordinates ${{(4,2)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(8,3)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})},$ respectively. \n\nTo find the distance $AB,$ we can use the distance formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(8-4)^{2}+(3-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "From the figure, we note that the points $A$ and $B$ have coordinates $\\underbrace{(4,2)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and $\\underbrace{(8,3)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})},$ respectively. \n\nTo find the distance $AB,$ we can use the distance formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(8-4)^{2}+(3-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17}\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186069299 :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have ${{(-9,2)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(-12,-4)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})}.$\nSo, the distance $d$ can be calculated using the distance formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-12-(-9))^{2}+(-4-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{45} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "In this case, we have $\\underbrace{(-9,2)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and $\\underbrace{(-12,-4)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})}.$\nSo, the distance $d$ can be calculated using the distance formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-12-(-9))^{2}+(-4-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{45} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186069301 :question/worked-solution "From the figure, we note that the points $C$ and $D$ have coordinates ${{(-5,3)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(2,-1)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})},$ respectively.\n\nTo find the length of $\\overset{―}{CD},$ we use the distance formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CD & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2-(-5))^{2}+(-1-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(7)^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{49+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{65}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "From the figure, we note that the points $C$ and $D$ have coordinates $\\underbrace{(-5,3)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and $\\underbrace{(2,-1)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})},$ respectively.\n\nTo find the length of $\\overset{―}{CD},$ we use the distance formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CD & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2-(-5))^{2}+(-1-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(7)^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{49+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{65}\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186070005 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we wish to find the area $\\mathcal{A}$ under the graph of $y=x^2$ between the $x$-values $x=1$ and $x=4,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/99/99cee7824a157e5a08292ebba9e60a5633c7a2f8a5ebb496fc1d5dbe14f858e3.png)\n\nWe can *approximate* the area using, for instance, the area of three rectangles, each with a width equal to $1.$\n\n![](images/8a/8aa7a3f9be8371b1459874cd2cb8c0c4caf1000d76b99d134fb6e806e638c548.png)\n\nSo, we find the area of each rectangle and then add them all together.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\overset{\\text{[math]1[/math]st rectangle}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\overset{\\text{height}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(1)}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{1}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}\\,]}}+\\overset{\\text{[math]2[/math]nd rectangle}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\overset{\\text{height}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(2)}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{1}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}\\,]}}+\\overset{\\text{[math]3[/math]rd rectangle}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\overset{\\text{height}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(3)}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{1}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}\\,]}} \\\\  & =[{1}^{2}\\cdot 1]+[{2}^{2}\\cdot 1]+[{3}^{2}\\cdot 1] \\\\  & =1+4+9 \\\\  & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we approximated the area using a **left Riemann sum**, which means that the top-*left* corner of each rectangle touches the curve. \n\nBecause the function is *strictly increasing* over the interval $[1,4],$ the rectangles are entirely *below* the function, which means our approximation is an *underestimate* of the actual area." "Suppose that we wish to find the area $\\mathcal{A}$ under the graph of $y=x^2$ between the $x$-values $x=1$ and $x=4,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/99/99cee7824a157e5a08292ebba9e60a5633c7a2f8a5ebb496fc1d5dbe14f858e3.png)\n\nWe can *approximate* the area using, for instance, the area of three rectangles, each with a width equal to $1.$\n\n![](images/8a/8aa7a3f9be8371b1459874cd2cb8c0c4caf1000d76b99d134fb6e806e638c548.png)\n\nSo, we find the area of each rectangle and then add them all together.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\overbrace{[\\,\\overbrace{f(1)}^{\\text{height}}\\,\\cdot \\,\\underbrace{1}_{\\text{width}}\\,]}^{\\text{[math]1[/math]st rectangle}}+\\overbrace{[\\,\\overbrace{f(2)}^{\\text{height}}\\,\\cdot \\,\\underbrace{1}_{\\text{width}}\\,]}^{\\text{[math]2[/math]nd rectangle}}+\\overbrace{[\\,\\overbrace{f(3)}^{\\text{height}}\\,\\cdot \\,\\underbrace{1}_{\\text{width}}\\,]}^{\\text{[math]3[/math]rd rectangle}} \\\\  & =[{1}^{2}\\cdot 1]+[{2}^{2}\\cdot 1]+[{3}^{2}\\cdot 1] \\\\  & =1+4+9 \\\\  & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we approximated the area using a **left Riemann sum**, which means that the top-*left* corner of each rectangle touches the curve. \n\nBecause the function is *strictly increasing* over the interval $[1,4],$ the rectangles are entirely *below* the function, which means our approximation is an *underestimate* of the actual area."]
 [:db/cas 17592186074071 :question/worked-solution "Let $({x}_{m},{y}_{m})$ be the midpoint between ${{(p,-3q)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(-p,7q)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})}.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{a+5a}{2},\\frac{2b+7b}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{6a}{2},\\frac{9b}{2}) \\\\  & =(3a,\\frac{9b}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $({x}_{m},{y}_{m})$ be the midpoint between $\\underbrace{(p,-3q)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and $\\underbrace{(-p,7q)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})}.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{a+5a}{2},\\frac{2b+7b}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{6a}{2},\\frac{9b}{2}) \\\\  & =(3a,\\frac{9b}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186074139 :question/worked-solution "The distance $d$ between two points $A({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $B({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $A{{(3,5,-1)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and $B{{(4,3,1)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(4-3)^{2}+(3-5)^{2}+(1-(-1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The distance $d$ between two points $A({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $B({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $A\\underbrace{(3,5,-1)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and $B\\underbrace{(4,3,1)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(4-3)^{2}+(3-5)^{2}+(1-(-1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186074527 :question/worked-solution "The distance $d$ between two points $A({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $B({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1}{)}^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1}{)}^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1}{)}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $A{{(p,2p,3p)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and $B{{(p,-2p,6p)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(p-p{)}^{2}+(-2p-2p{)}^{2}+(6p-3p{)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-4p{)}^{2}+(3p{)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{p}^{2}+9{p}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25{p}^{2}} \\\\  & =5\\,|p|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $d=15,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\,|p| & =15 \\\\ |p| & =3 \\\\ p & =\\pm 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The distance $d$ between two points $A({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $B({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1}{)}^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1}{)}^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1}{)}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $A\\underbrace{(p,2p,3p)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and $B\\underbrace{(p,-2p,6p)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(p-p{)}^{2}+(-2p-2p{)}^{2}+(6p-3p{)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-4p{)}^{2}+(3p{)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{p}^{2}+9{p}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25{p}^{2}} \\\\  & =5\\,|p|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $d=15,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\,|p| & =15 \\\\ |p| & =3 \\\\ p & =\\pm 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186075057 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to add a whole number and a fraction. If we want the final answer to be an improper fraction, then we can proceed by turning the whole number into an improper fraction.\n\nFor example, let's find\n\n$$\n2+\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}5}\n$$\n\nas an improper fraction.\n\nWe start by writing $2$ as an improper fraction with $1$ in the denominator:\n\n$$\n2 =\\dfrac{2}{1}\n$$\n\nThen, we put this fraction over a denominator of $\\color{blue}5$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by ${\\color{blue}5}$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\times {\\color{blue}{5}}}{1\\times {\\color{blue}{5}}} = \\dfrac{10}{\\color{blue}5}\n$$\n\nNow, we can add the two numbers:\n\n$$\n2 + \\frac{1}{5} = \\frac{10}{5}_(⏟)_(2) + \\frac{1}{5} = \\frac{11}{5}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done!\n\nTo use some math jargon, we put the numbers $2$ and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1{\\color{blue}5}\n$$\n\nover a **common denominator** of ${\\color{blue}5}$ before adding them.\n\nNotice that this process is very similar to using fraction models.\n\n![](images/cb/cbe49aa64f7d53911592001c5d93e8cde826ac46dabf2ba9c5e9579738ce6ebd.png)\n\nTo add $2$ to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1{\\color{blue}5}\n$$\n\nusing a model, we split each whole into ${\\color{blue}5}$ equal parts. When we do, we see that there are $11$ shaded parts in total. So we arrive at the same answer:\n\n$$\n2 +\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}5} = \\dfrac{11}{{\\color{blue}5}}\n$$" "Suppose that we want to add a whole number and a fraction. If we want the final answer to be an improper fraction, then we can proceed by turning the whole number into an improper fraction.\n\nFor example, let's find\n\n$$\n2+\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}5}\n$$\n\nas an improper fraction.\n\nWe start by writing $2$ as an improper fraction with $1$ in the denominator:\n\n$$\n2 =\\dfrac{2}{1}\n$$\n\nThen, we put this fraction over a denominator of $\\color{blue}5$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by ${\\color{blue}5}$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\times {\\color{blue}{5}}}{1\\times {\\color{blue}{5}}} = \\dfrac{10}{\\color{blue}5}\n$$\n\nNow, we can add the two numbers:\n\n$$\n2 + \\frac{1}{5} = \\underbrace{\\frac{10}{5}}_{2} + \\frac{1}{5} = \\frac{11}{5}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done!\n\nTo use some math jargon, we put the numbers $2$ and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1{\\color{blue}5}\n$$\n\nover a **common denominator** of ${\\color{blue}5}$ before adding them.\n\nNotice that this process is very similar to using fraction models.\n\n![](images/cb/cbe49aa64f7d53911592001c5d93e8cde826ac46dabf2ba9c5e9579738ce6ebd.png)\n\nTo add $2$ to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1{\\color{blue}5}\n$$\n\nusing a model, we split each whole into ${\\color{blue}5}$ equal parts. When we do, we see that there are $11$ shaded parts in total. So we arrive at the same answer:\n\n$$\n2 +\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}5} = \\dfrac{11}{{\\color{blue}5}}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186078311 :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of four parts: the arc of a semi-circle for $x\\in [-4,0],$ a line segment for $x\\in (0,2),$ a line segment for $x\\in (2,4],$ and the point at $x=2.$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={{{\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+0+{{{\\int }_{2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in [-4,0].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle with a radius of $2,$ so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in (0,2).$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{2} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x = -A_2 = -2.\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in (2,4].$  The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =2\\pi -2+2 \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The graph consists of four parts: the arc of a semi-circle for $x\\in [-4,0],$ a line segment for $x\\in (0,2),$ a line segment for $x\\in (2,4],$ and the point at $x=2.$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\underbrace{{\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{{A}_{1}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{-{A}_{2}}+0+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{{A}_{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in [-4,0].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle with a radius of $2,$ so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in (0,2).$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{2} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x = -A_2 = -2.\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in (2,4].$  The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =2\\pi -2+2 \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186078326 :question/worked-solution "To find the total distance traveled, we first need to find the points where the particle changes its direction, which can only occur when the velocity is zero. Solving $v(t) = 0$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12 - 3t^{2} &= 0 \\\\\n- 3(t^{2} - 4) &= 0 \\\\\nt &= ± 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA sketch of $v(t)$ is shown below:\n\n![](images/ca/caea9bf724b2d9f6abc7434bebe401e28170d669390216e0b4998cf1c5d0481d.png)\n\nNotice that in the interval\n\n$$\n0\\leq t\\leq T\n$$\n\nfor $T > 2$, the curve $v(t)$ crosses the $t$-axis at $t=2$. Therefore, we need to split up the distance integral between the intervals\n\n$$\n0\\leq t\\leq 2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n2\\leq t\\leq T\n$$\n\nIn the interval\n\n$$\n0\\leq t\\leq 2\n$$\n\nwe have $v(t) > 0$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\mid v(t) \\mid = v(t) =12-3t^2\n$$\n\nWe can work out the total distance traveled in the interval $0 < t < 2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= \\int_{0}^{2} \\mid v(t) \\mid dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2}(12 - 3t^{2})dt \\\\\n&= \\left.(12t - t^{3})\\right|_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= 12(2) - 2^{3} \\\\\n&= 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $16 < 32$, the time taken for the particle to travel $32\\,\\textrm{m}$ is greater than $2$ seconds.\n\nNow, in the interval\n\n$$\n2\\leq t\\leq T\n$$\n\nfor $T>2$, we have $v(t)<0$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\mid v(t) \\mid =-v(t) = 3t^2-12\n$$\n\nTherefore, an expression for the total distance $d$ traveled between $t=0$ and $t=T$ for $T >2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd(T) &= \\int_{0}^{T}\\begin{vmatrix}v(t) & dt \\\\ = \\int_{0}^{2} & v(t)\\end{vmatrix}dt_(⏟)_(16) + \\int_{2}^{T} \\mid v(t) \\mid dt \\\\\n&= 16 + \\int_{2}^{T}(3t^{2} - 12)dt \\\\\n&= 16 + \\left.(t^{3} - 12t)\\right|_{2}^{T} \\\\\n&= 16 + [(T^{3} - 12T) - ((2)^{3} - 12(2)] \\\\\n&= 16 + T^{3} - 12T - (-16) \\\\\n&= T^{3} - 12T + 32\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the particle travels $32\\,\\textrm{m}$ when $d = 32$. So, we can solve for $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT^{3} - 12T + 32 &= 32 \\\\\nT^{3} - 12T &= 0 \\\\\nT(T^{2} - 12) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $T=0$ and\n\n$$\nT=\\pm 2\\sqrt 3\n$$\n\nSince we require $T> 2$, the only valid solution is\n\n$$\nT=2\\sqrt 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that it takes $2\\sqrt 3$ seconds for the particle to travel a total of $32\\,\\textrm{m}$." "To find the total distance traveled, we first need to find the points where the particle changes its direction, which can only occur when the velocity is zero. Solving $v(t) = 0$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12 - 3t^{2} &= 0 \\\\\n- 3(t^{2} - 4) &= 0 \\\\\nt &= ± 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA sketch of $v(t)$ is shown below:\n\n![](images/ca/caea9bf724b2d9f6abc7434bebe401e28170d669390216e0b4998cf1c5d0481d.png)\n\nNotice that in the interval\n\n$$\n0\\leq t\\leq T\n$$\n\nfor $T > 2$, the curve $v(t)$ crosses the $t$-axis at $t=2$. Therefore, we need to split up the distance integral between the intervals\n\n$$\n0\\leq t\\leq 2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n2\\leq t\\leq T\n$$\n\nIn the interval\n\n$$\n0\\leq t\\leq 2\n$$\n\nwe have $v(t) > 0$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\mid v(t) \\mid = v(t) =12-3t^2\n$$\n\nWe can work out the total distance traveled in the interval $0 < t < 2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= \\int_{0}^{2} \\mid v(t) \\mid dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2}(12 - 3t^{2})dt \\\\\n&= \\left.(12t - t^{3})\\right|_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= 12(2) - 2^{3} \\\\\n&= 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $16 < 32$, the time taken for the particle to travel $32\\,\\textrm{m}$ is greater than $2$ seconds.\n\nNow, in the interval\n\n$$\n2\\leq t\\leq T\n$$\n\nfor $T>2$, we have $v(t)<0$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\mid v(t) \\mid =-v(t) = 3t^2-12\n$$\n\nTherefore, an expression for the total distance $d$ traveled between $t=0$ and $t=T$ for $T >2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(T) & ={\\int }_{0}^{T}|v(t)|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\underbrace{{\\int }_{0}^{2}|v(t)|\\,\\text{d}t}_{16}+{\\int }_{2}^{T}|v(t)|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\\n&= 16 + \\int_{2}^{T}(3t^{2} - 12)dt \\\\\n&= 16 + \\left.(t^{3} - 12t)\\right|_{2}^{T} \\\\\n&= 16 + [(T^{3} - 12T) - ((2)^{3} - 12(2)] \\\\\n&= 16 + T^{3} - 12T - (-16) \\\\\n&= T^{3} - 12T + 32\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the particle travels $32\\,\\textrm{m}$ when $d = 32$. So, we can solve for $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT^{3} - 12T + 32 &= 32 \\\\\nT^{3} - 12T &= 0 \\\\\nT(T^{2} - 12) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $T=0$ and\n\n$$\nT=\\pm 2\\sqrt 3\n$$\n\nSince we require $T> 2$, the only valid solution is\n\n$$\nT=2\\sqrt 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that it takes $2\\sqrt 3$ seconds for the particle to travel a total of $32\\,\\textrm{m}$."]
 [:db/cas 17592186078742 :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of $3$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-3,1),$ the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in [1,3],$ and the line segment for $x\\in [3,4].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThe $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,1).$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =b\\cdot h \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{1}=-4.\n$$\n\nThe $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,3].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle of radius of $1,$ so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [3,4].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{3}=-\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{1}+{A}_{2}-{A}_{3} \\\\  & =-4+\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{11}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The graph consists of $3$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-3,1),$ the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in [1,3],$ and the line segment for $x\\in [3,4].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\underbrace{{\\int }_{-3}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{-{A}_{1}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{{A}_{2}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{-{A}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThe $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,1).$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =b\\cdot h \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{1}=-4.\n$$\n\nThe $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,3].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle of radius of $1,$ so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [3,4].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{3}=-\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{1}+{A}_{2}-{A}_{3} \\\\  & =-4+\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{11}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186079028 :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-2,3)$ and the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in [3,5].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,3).$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =b\\cdot h \\\\  & =5\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [3,5].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot (1)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =10+\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-2,3)$ and the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in [3,5].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\underbrace{{\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{{A}_{1}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,3).$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =b\\cdot h \\\\  & =5\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [3,5].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot (1)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =10+\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186079031 :question/worked-solution "We divide the interval of integration into $3$ regions: the line segment for $x\\in [-3,-1],$ the line segment for $x\\in [-1,0),$ and the line segment for $x\\in [0,3].$ Therefore, we write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-1}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,-1].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-1,0).$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-0.5.\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,3].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+2)(3) \\\\  & =4.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =2-0.5+4.5 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We divide the interval of integration into $3$ regions: the line segment for $x\\in [-3,-1],$ the line segment for $x\\in [-1,0),$ and the line segment for $x\\in [0,3].$ Therefore, we write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\underbrace{{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{{A}_{1}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{-1}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{-{A}_{2}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{{A}_{3}}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,-1].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-1,0).$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-0.5.\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,3].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+2)(3) \\\\  & =4.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =2-0.5+4.5 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186079709 :tutorial/content "The sum of the first $N$ terms of an arithmetic series with the initial term $a_1$ and the common difference $d$ can be found as\n\n$$\nS_N = \\frac{N}{2}\\big(2a_1+(N-1)d \\big)\n$$\n\nWe'll now prove this result.\n\nFirst, we recall that the $n$th term of the series is given by\n\n$$\na_n = a_1 + (n-1)d\n$$\n\nLet's write down the sum by placing the indices\n\n$$\nn=1,2,3,\\ldots, N\n$$\n\nin ascending order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{N} &= a_{1} + a_{2} + ⋯ + a_{N - 1} + a_{N} \\\\\n&= a_{1} + (a_{1} + d)_(⏟)_(a_{2}) + ⋯ + (a_{1} + (N - 2)d)_(⏟)_(a_{N - 1}) + (a_{1} + (N - 1)d)_(⏟)_(a_{N})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's write down the same sum but place the indices in descending order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{N} &= a_{N} + a_{N - 1} + ⋯ + a_{2} + a_{1} \\\\\n&= (a_{1} + (N - 1)d)_(⏟)_(a_{N}) + (a_{1} + (N - 2)d)_(⏟)_(a_{N - 1}) + ⋯ + (a_{1} + d)_(⏟)_(a_{2}) + a_{1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy adding the two expressions, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}S_{N} &= & a_{1} & + & (a_{1} + d) & + & ⋯ & + & (a_{1} + (N - 1)d) \\\\ S_{N}|= & (a_{1} + (N - 1)d) & + & (a_{1} + (N - 2)d) & + & ⋯ & + & a_{1} \\\\ 2S_{N}|= & (2a_{1} + (N - 1)d) & + & (2a_{1} + (N - 1)d) & + & ⋯ & + & (2a_{1} + (N - 1)d)\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression on the right contains $N$ copies of $2a_1+(N-1)d$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n2S_{N} = N \\cdot (2a_{1} + (N - 1)d)⟹S_{N} = \\frac{N}{2}(2a_{1} + (N - 1)d)\n$$" "The sum of the first $N$ terms of an arithmetic series with the initial term $a_1$ and the common difference $d$ can be found as\n\n$$\nS_N = \\frac{N}{2}\\big(2a_1+(N-1)d \\big)\n$$\n\nWe'll now prove this result.\n\nFirst, we recall that the $n$th term of the series is given by\n\n$$\na_n = a_1 + (n-1)d\n$$\n\nLet's write down the sum by placing the indices\n\n$$\nn=1,2,3,\\ldots, N\n$$\n\nin ascending order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{N} &= a_{1} + a_{2} + ⋯ + a_{N - 1} + a_{N} \\\\\n&= a_{1} + \\underbrace{(a_{1} + d)}_{{a}_{2}} + ⋯ + \\underbrace{(a_{1} + (N - 2)d)}_{{a}_{N-1}} + \\underbrace{(a_{1} + (N - 1)d)}_{{a}_{N}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's write down the same sum but place the indices in descending order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{N} &= a_{N} + a_{N - 1} + ⋯ + a_{2} + a_{1} \\\\\n&= \\underbrace{(a_{1} + (N - 1)d)}_{{a}_{N}} + \\underbrace{(a_{1} + (N - 2)d)}_{{a}_{N-1}} + ⋯ + \\underbrace{(a_{1} + d)}_{{a}_{2}} + a_{1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy adding the two expressions, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}S_{N} &= & a_{1} & + & (a_{1} + d) & + & ⋯ & + & (a_{1} + (N - 1)d) \\\\ S_{N}|= & (a_{1} + (N - 1)d) & + & (a_{1} + (N - 2)d) & + & ⋯ & + & a_{1} \\\\ 2S_{N}|= & (2a_{1} + (N - 1)d) & + & (2a_{1} + (N - 1)d) & + & ⋯ & + & (2a_{1} + (N - 1)d)\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression on the right contains $N$ copies of $2a_1+(N-1)d$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n2S_{N} = N \\cdot (2a_{1} + (N - 1)d)⟹S_{N} = \\frac{N}{2}(2a_{1} + (N - 1)d)\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186084465 :tutorial/content "Here, we'll derive the formula\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^N n = \\dfrac 1 2 N (N+1)\n$$\n\nFirst, let's represent the sum with the letter $S$. This way, it will be easier to manipulate the sum algebraically.\n\n$$\nS = \\sum_{n=1}^N n\n$$\n\nNext, let's write out $S$ twice. The first time, we'll write the numbers in ascending order, and the second time in descending order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}S = 1 & + 2 + ⋯ + (N - 1) + N \\\\ S = N & + (N - 1) + ⋯ + 2 + 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, if we add the two sums above, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2S &= (N + 1) + (2 + N - 1) + ⋯ + (N - 1 + 2) + (N + 1) \\\\\n&= (N + 1) + (N + 1) + ⋯ + (N + 1) + (N + 1)_(⏟)_(Ntimes) \\\\\n&= N(N + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, solving for $S$, we reach the desired result:\n\n$$\nS = \\dfrac 1 2 N (N+1)\n$$" "Here, we'll derive the formula\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^N n = \\dfrac 1 2 N (N+1)\n$$\n\nFirst, let's represent the sum with the letter $S$. This way, it will be easier to manipulate the sum algebraically.\n\n$$\nS = \\sum_{n=1}^N n\n$$\n\nNext, let's write out $S$ twice. The first time, we'll write the numbers in ascending order, and the second time in descending order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}S = 1 & + 2 + ⋯ + (N - 1) + N \\\\ S = N & + (N - 1) + ⋯ + 2 + 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, if we add the two sums above, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2S &= (N + 1) + (2 + N - 1) + ⋯ + (N - 1 + 2) + (N + 1) \\\\\n&= \\underbrace{(N + 1) + (N + 1) + ⋯ + (N + 1) + (N + 1)}_{N\\text{ times}} \\\\\n&= N(N + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, solving for $S$, we reach the desired result:\n\n$$\nS = \\dfrac 1 2 N (N+1)\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186087484 :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify the given expression using the identity\n\n$$\n\\sec^2 x = 1 + \\tan^2 x\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3\\tan 35^{∘}}{2 - \\sec^{2} 35^{∘}} &= (3\\tan 35^{∘})/(2 - (1 + \\tan^{2} 35^{∘})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3\\tan 35^{∘}}{1 - \\tan^{2} 35^{∘}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for tangent:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {2\\tan x}{1 - \\tan^2 x} = \\tan 2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=35^\\circ$ in the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2\\tan 35^{∘}}{1 - \\tan^{2} 35^{∘}} &= \\tan (2 \\cdot 35^{∘}) = \\tan 70^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3\\tan 35^{∘}}{1 - \\tan^{2} 35^{∘}} &= \\frac{3}{2} \\cdot [\\frac{2\\tan 35^{∘}}{1 - \\tan^{2} 35^{∘}}]_(⏟)_(\\tan 70^{∘}) = \\frac{3}{2}\\tan 70^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we simplify the given expression using the identity\n\n$$\n\\sec^2 x = 1 + \\tan^2 x\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3\\tan 35^{∘}}{2 - \\sec^{2} 35^{∘}} &= (3\\tan 35^{∘})/(2 - (1 + \\tan^{2} 35^{∘})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3\\tan 35^{∘}}{1 - \\tan^{2} 35^{∘}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for tangent:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {2\\tan x}{1 - \\tan^2 x} = \\tan 2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=35^\\circ$ in the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2\\tan 35^{∘}}{1 - \\tan^{2} 35^{∘}} &= \\tan (2 \\cdot 35^{∘}) = \\tan 70^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3\\tan 35^{∘}}{1 - \\tan^{2} 35^{∘}} &= \\frac{3}{2} \\cdot \\underbrace{[\\frac{2\\tan 35^{∘}}{1 - \\tan^{2} 35^{∘}}]}_{\\tan {70}^{∘}} = \\frac{3}{2}\\tan 70^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186087599 :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\nP(x)y\n=\nQ(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y & ={{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=\\sin x$ and $Q(x) = \\cos x.$" "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\nP(x)y\n=\nQ(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\underbrace{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}_{P(x)}y & =\\underbrace{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}_{Q(x)}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=\\sin x$ and $Q(x) = \\cos x.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186088032 :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =1-4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ 2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y & ={{\\frac{1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}.$" "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =1-4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ 2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\underbrace{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}_{P(x)}y & =\\underbrace{\\frac{1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}}_{Q(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186088219 :question/worked-solution "The *standard form* equation of a line is \n\n$$\nax+by=c,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ are all integers,\n\n- $a\\ge 0,$ and\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ have no common factors greater than $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equations.\n\n- Equation I is in standard form. Indeed, the variables are on the left-hand side, while the constant term is on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n{{2x}_{⏟}}_{\\text{variables}}\\,={{3}_{⏟}}_{\\text{constant}}\n$$\n\n \nAll coefficients are integers (namely, $2$ and $3$) and don't have common factors greater than $1.$ Also, the coefficient of $x$ is positive ($2$).\n\n- Equation II is *not* in standard form because the coefficient of $x$ is negative ($-1$).\n\n- Equation III is in standard form. Indeed, the variables are on the left-hand side, while the constant term is on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n{{x-2y}_{⏟}}_{\\text{variables}}\\,={{0}_{⏟}}_{\\text{constant}}\n$$\n\n \nAll coefficients are integers (namely, $1,$ $-2,$ and $0$) and don't have common factors greater than $1.$ Also, the coefficient of $x$ is positive ($1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "The *standard form* equation of a line is \n\n$$\nax+by=c,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ are all integers,\n\n- $a\\ge 0,$ and\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ have no common factors greater than $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equations.\n\n- Equation I is in standard form. Indeed, the variables are on the left-hand side, while the constant term is on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{2x}_{\\text{variables}}\\,=\\underbrace{3}_{\\text{constant}}\n$$\n\n \nAll coefficients are integers (namely, $2$ and $3$) and don't have common factors greater than $1.$ Also, the coefficient of $x$ is positive ($2$).\n\n- Equation II is *not* in standard form because the coefficient of $x$ is negative ($-1$).\n\n- Equation III is in standard form. Indeed, the variables are on the left-hand side, while the constant term is on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x-2y}_{\\text{variables}}\\,=\\underbrace{0}_{\\text{constant}}\n$$\n\n \nAll coefficients are integers (namely, $1,$ $-2,$ and $0$) and don't have common factors greater than $1.$ Also, the coefficient of $x$ is positive ($1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""]
 [:db/cas 17592186088420 :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x-y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x}{x}-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & +\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{2x}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & +{{\\frac{1}{x}}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y={{2}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ and $Q(x)=2.$" "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x-y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x}{x}-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & +\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{2x}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & +\\underbrace{\\frac{1}{x}}_{P(x)}y=\\underbrace{2}_{Q(x)}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ and $Q(x)=2.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186088893 :tutorial/content "Given the scalar $a,$ the **multiplicative inverse** of $a$ is defined as the scalar $a^{-1}$ such that\n\n$$\na \\cdot a^{-1} = a^{-1} \\cdot a = 1.\n$$\n\nFor example, the multiplicative inverse of $2$ is the number $2^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ because \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\cdot 2 =  2\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{2} = 1.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, given a square $n \\times n$ matrix $A,$ the **inverse** of $A$ is defined as the $n \\times n$ matrix $A^{-1}$ such that\n\n$$\nA \\cdot A^{-1} = A^{-1} \\cdot A = I_n,\n$$\n\nwhere $I_n$ is the $n\\times n$ identity matrix.\n\nFor example, suppose we're given the following $2\\times 2$ matrix:\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nThe inverse of the above matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo verify that the above matrix is in fact the inverse of $A,$ first observe that if we multiply $A$ by the above matrix $A^{-1},$ then we get the identity matrix $I_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1\\cdot (-2)+(-1)\\cdot 1 & -1\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\ 1\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 1 & 1\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, if we multiply $A^{-1}$ by $A,$ then we also get the identity matrix $I_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A} & ={I}_{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe do not yet know how to find the inverse of a matrix. However, we can verify that one matrix is the inverse of the other by carrying out the same procedure as we did here." "Given the scalar $a,$ the **multiplicative inverse** of $a$ is defined as the scalar $a^{-1}$ such that\n\n$$\na \\cdot a^{-1} = a^{-1} \\cdot a = 1.\n$$\n\nFor example, the multiplicative inverse of $2$ is the number $2^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ because \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\cdot 2 =  2\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{2} = 1.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, given a square $n \\times n$ matrix $A,$ the **inverse** of $A$ is defined as the $n \\times n$ matrix $A^{-1}$ such that\n\n$$\nA \\cdot A^{-1} = A^{-1} \\cdot A = I_n,\n$$\n\nwhere $I_n$ is the $n\\times n$ identity matrix.\n\nFor example, suppose we're given the following $2\\times 2$ matrix:\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nThe inverse of the above matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo verify that the above matrix is in fact the inverse of $A,$ first observe that if we multiply $A$ by the above matrix $A^{-1},$ then we get the identity matrix $I_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{A}^{-1}} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1\\cdot (-2)+(-1)\\cdot 1 & -1\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\ 1\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 1 & 1\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, if we multiply $A^{-1}$ by $A,$ then we also get the identity matrix $I_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{A} & ={I}_{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe do not yet know how to find the inverse of a matrix. However, we can verify that one matrix is the inverse of the other by carrying out the same procedure as we did here."]
 [:db/cas 17592186091949 :question/worked-solution "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1), P(2), P(3),\\ldots,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in\\mathbb N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in\\mathbb N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\nn^2 > 2n+2 \\quad\\text{for}\\quad n \\geq 3.\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called base case. Since we are required to prove the statement for $n \\geq 3,$ the base case is $P(3).$ So, we substitute $n=3$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that the left-hand side is greater than the right-hand side:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=3.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2}=9,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(3)+2=8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=3.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\geq 3.$ \n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\geq 3,$ we have\n\n$$\nk^2 > 2k+2.\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the so-called **inductive step**.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n(k+1)^2 > 2(k+1)+2 . \\qquad\\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this is greater than the right-hand side. We do this as follows:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(k+1)^{2} & ={k}^{2}+2k+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{k}^{2}+2k+1 & ={{({k}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{>2k+2}+2k+1 \\\\  & >2k+2+2k+1 \\\\  & =2k+2+{{2k}_{⏟}}_{>1}+1 \\\\  & >2(k+1)+2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ is greater than the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion. \n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, $n^2 > 2n+2$ for all $n \\geq 3.$" "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1), P(2), P(3),\\ldots,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in\\mathbb N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in\\mathbb N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\nn^2 > 2n+2 \\quad\\text{for}\\quad n \\geq 3.\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called base case. Since we are required to prove the statement for $n \\geq 3,$ the base case is $P(3).$ So, we substitute $n=3$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that the left-hand side is greater than the right-hand side:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=3.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2}=9,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(3)+2=8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=3.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\geq 3.$ \n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\geq 3,$ we have\n\n$$\nk^2 > 2k+2.\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the so-called **inductive step**.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n(k+1)^2 > 2(k+1)+2 . \\qquad\\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this is greater than the right-hand side. We do this as follows:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(k+1)^{2} & ={k}^{2}+2k+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{k}^{2}+2k+1 & =\\underbrace{({k}^{2})}_{>2k+2}+2k+1 \\\\  & >2k+2+2k+1 \\\\  & =2k+2+\\underbrace{2k}_{>1}+1 \\\\  & >2(k+1)+2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ is greater than the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion. \n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, $n^2 > 2n+2$ for all $n \\geq 3.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186091950 :question/worked-solution "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1), P(2), P(3), \\ldots,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k) \\Rightarrow P(k+1)$ for every $k \\in \\mathbb N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in\\mathbb N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n2^n +1 \\leq 3^n \\quad\\text{for} \\quad n \\geq 1.\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called base case, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n = 1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that the left-hand side is less than or equal to the right-hand side:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n = 1.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{1}+1=3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{1}=3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n = 1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n = k \\geq 1.$ \n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n = k \\geq 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n2^k +1 \\leq 3^k.\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k) \\Rightarrow P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the so-called **inductive step**.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n2^{k+1} + 1\\leq 3^{k+1}. \\qquad\\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this is less than or equal to the right-hand side. We do this as follows:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{k+1}+1 & ={2}^{k}\\cdot {2}^{1}+1 \\\\  & =2({2}^{k})+1 \\\\  & ={2}^{k}+{2}^{k}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{k}+{{{2}^{k}+1}_{⏟}}_{\\le {3}^{k}} & \\le {2}^{k}+{3}^{k} \\\\  & ={{{2}^{k}}_{⏟}}_{<{3}^{k}<2\\cdot {3}^{k}}+{3}^{k} \\\\  & <2\\cdot {3}^{k}+{3}^{k} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {3}^{k} \\\\  & ={3}^{k+1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ is less than or equal to the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion. \n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, $2^n +1 \\leq 3^n$ for all $n \\geq 1.$" "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1), P(2), P(3), \\ldots,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k) \\Rightarrow P(k+1)$ for every $k \\in \\mathbb N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in\\mathbb N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n2^n +1 \\leq 3^n \\quad\\text{for} \\quad n \\geq 1.\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called base case, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n = 1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that the left-hand side is less than or equal to the right-hand side:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n = 1.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{1}+1=3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{1}=3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n = 1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n = k \\geq 1.$ \n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n = k \\geq 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n2^k +1 \\leq 3^k.\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k) \\Rightarrow P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the so-called **inductive step**.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n2^{k+1} + 1\\leq 3^{k+1}. \\qquad\\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this is less than or equal to the right-hand side. We do this as follows:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{k+1}+1 & ={2}^{k}\\cdot {2}^{1}+1 \\\\  & =2({2}^{k})+1 \\\\  & ={2}^{k}+{2}^{k}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{k}+\\underbrace{{2}^{k}+1}_{\\le {3}^{k}} & \\le {2}^{k}+{3}^{k} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{{2}^{k}}_{<{3}^{k}<2\\cdot {3}^{k}}+{3}^{k} \\\\  & <2\\cdot {3}^{k}+{3}^{k} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {3}^{k} \\\\  & ={3}^{k+1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ is less than or equal to the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion. \n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, $2^n +1 \\leq 3^n$ for all $n \\geq 1.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186092038 :question/worked-solution "Let\n\n$$\nz = \\cos\\theta + \\textrm{i}\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nWe start with the following identities:\n\n$$\nz + \\frac{1}{z} = 2\\cos θ, z^{n} + \\frac{1}{z^{n}} = 2\\cos nθ\n$$\n\nRaising both sides to the power $3$ in the first identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(z + \\dfrac{1}{z}\\right)^3 = (2\\cos\\theta)^3 =8\\cos^3\\theta\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the binomial using the binomial theorem and simplify the result using our identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(z + \\frac{1}{z})^{3} &= z^{3} + 3z^{2}(\\frac{1}{z}) + 3z(\\frac{1}{z})^{2} + (\\frac{1}{z})^{3} \\\\\n&= z^{3} + 3z + 3(\\frac{1}{z}) + \\frac{1}{z^{3}} \\\\\n&= (z^{3} + \\frac{1}{z^{3}})_(⏟)_(2\\cos 3θ) + 3(z + \\frac{1}{z})_(⏟)_(2\\cos θ) \\\\\n&= 2\\cos 3θ + 3(2\\cos θ) \\\\\n&= 2\\cos 3θ + 6\\cos θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating $8\\cos^3\\theta$ with the above, we get\n\n$$\n8\\cos^3\\theta = 2\\cos3\\theta + 6\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides of the equation by $8$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\cos^3\\theta = \\frac{1}{4}\\cos3\\theta + \\frac{3}{4}\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nB=\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$" "Let\n\n$$\nz = \\cos\\theta + \\textrm{i}\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nWe start with the following identities:\n\n$$\nz + \\frac{1}{z} = 2\\cos θ, z^{n} + \\frac{1}{z^{n}} = 2\\cos nθ\n$$\n\nRaising both sides to the power $3$ in the first identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(z + \\dfrac{1}{z}\\right)^3 = (2\\cos\\theta)^3 =8\\cos^3\\theta\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the binomial using the binomial theorem and simplify the result using our identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(z + \\frac{1}{z})^{3} &= z^{3} + 3z^{2}(\\frac{1}{z}) + 3z(\\frac{1}{z})^{2} + (\\frac{1}{z})^{3} \\\\\n&= z^{3} + 3z + 3(\\frac{1}{z}) + \\frac{1}{z^{3}} \\\\\n&= \\underbrace{(z^{3} + \\frac{1}{z^{3}})}_{2\\cos 3\\theta } + 3\\underbrace{(z + \\frac{1}{z})}_{2\\cos \\theta } \\\\\n&= 2\\cos 3θ + 3(2\\cos θ) \\\\\n&= 2\\cos 3θ + 6\\cos θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating $8\\cos^3\\theta$ with the above, we get\n\n$$\n8\\cos^3\\theta = 2\\cos3\\theta + 6\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides of the equation by $8$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\cos^3\\theta = \\frac{1}{4}\\cos3\\theta + \\frac{3}{4}\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nB=\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186092039 :question/worked-solution "Let\n\n$$\nz = \\cos\\theta + \\textrm{i}\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nWe start with the following identities:\n\n$$\n(z - \\frac{1}{z}) = 2\\textrm{i}\\sin θ(z^{n} + \\frac{1}{z^{n}}) = 2\\cos nθ\n$$\n\nRaising both sides to the power $4$ in the first identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(z - \\frac{1}{z}\\right)^4 \n= (2\\textrm{i}\\sin\\theta)^4\n\n=16\\sin^4\\theta .\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the binomial using the binomial theorem and simplify the result using our identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(z - \\frac{1}{z})^{4} &= z^{4} - 4z^{3}(\\frac{1}{z}) + 6z^{2}(\\frac{1}{z})^{2} - 4z(\\frac{1}{z})^{3} + (\\frac{1}{z})^{4} \\\\\n&= z^{4} - 4z^{2} + 6 - \\frac{4}{z^{2}} + \\frac{1}{z^{4}} \\\\\n&= (z^{4} + \\frac{1}{z^{4}})_(⏟)_(2\\cos 4θ) - 4(z^{2} + \\frac{1}{z^{2}})_(⏟)_(2\\cos 2θ) + 6 \\\\\n&= 2\\cos 4θ - 4(2\\cos 2θ) + 6 \\\\\n&= 2\\cos 4θ - 8\\cos 2θ + 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy equating $16 \\sin^4 \\theta$ with the above, we get\n\n$$\n16\\sin^4\\theta = 2\\cos4\\theta - 8\\cos2\\theta+6\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides of the equation by $16$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\sin^4\\theta = \\frac{1}{8}\\cos4\\theta - \\frac{1}{2}\\cos2\\theta+\\frac{3}{8}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nA=\\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$" "Let\n\n$$\nz = \\cos\\theta + \\textrm{i}\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nWe start with the following identities:\n\n$$\n(z - \\frac{1}{z}) = 2\\textrm{i}\\sin θ(z^{n} + \\frac{1}{z^{n}}) = 2\\cos nθ\n$$\n\nRaising both sides to the power $4$ in the first identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(z - \\frac{1}{z}\\right)^4 \n= (2\\textrm{i}\\sin\\theta)^4\n\n=16\\sin^4\\theta .\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the binomial using the binomial theorem and simplify the result using our identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(z - \\frac{1}{z})^{4} &= z^{4} - 4z^{3}(\\frac{1}{z}) + 6z^{2}(\\frac{1}{z})^{2} - 4z(\\frac{1}{z})^{3} + (\\frac{1}{z})^{4} \\\\\n&= z^{4} - 4z^{2} + 6 - \\frac{4}{z^{2}} + \\frac{1}{z^{4}} \\\\\n&= \\underbrace{(z^{4} + \\frac{1}{z^{4}})}_{2\\cos 4\\theta } - 4\\underbrace{(z^{2} + \\frac{1}{z^{2}})}_{2\\cos 2\\theta } + 6 \\\\\n&= 2\\cos 4θ - 4(2\\cos 2θ) + 6 \\\\\n&= 2\\cos 4θ - 8\\cos 2θ + 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy equating $16 \\sin^4 \\theta$ with the above, we get\n\n$$\n16\\sin^4\\theta = 2\\cos4\\theta - 8\\cos2\\theta+6\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides of the equation by $16$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\sin^4\\theta = \\frac{1}{8}\\cos4\\theta - \\frac{1}{2}\\cos2\\theta+\\frac{3}{8}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nA=\\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186092141 :question/worked-solution "Let\n\n$$\nz = \\cos\\theta + \\textrm{i}\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nWe start with the following identities:\n\n$$\n(z - \\frac{1}{z}) = 2\\textrm{i}\\sin θ(z^{n} - \\frac{1}{z^{n}}) = 2\\textrm{i}\\sin nθ\n$$\n\nRaising both sides to the power $5$ in the first identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(z - \\dfrac{1}{z}\\right)^5 \n= (2\\textrm{i}\\sin\\theta)^5\n\n=32\\textrm{i}\\sin^5\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the binomial using the binomial theorem and simplify the result using our identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(z - \\frac{1}{z})^{5} &= z^{5} - 5z^{4}(\\frac{1}{z}) + 10z^{3}(\\frac{1}{z})^{2} - 10z^{2}(\\frac{1}{z})^{3} + 5z(\\frac{1}{z})^{4} - (\\frac{1}{z})^{5} \\\\\n&= z^{5} - 5z^{3} + 10z - \\frac{10}{z} + \\frac{5}{z^{3}} - \\frac{1}{z^{5}} \\\\\n&= (z^{5} - \\frac{1}{z^{5}})_(⏟)_(2\\textrm{i}\\sin 5θ) - 5(z^{3} - \\frac{1}{z^{3}})_(⏟)_(2\\textrm{i}\\sin 3θ) + 10(z - \\frac{1}{z})_(⏟)_(2\\textrm{i}\\sin θ) \\\\\n&= 2\\textrm{i}\\sin 5θ - 5(2\\textrm{i}\\sin 3θ) + 10(2\\textrm{i}\\sin θ) \\\\\n&= 2\\textrm{i}\\sin 5θ - 10\\textrm{i}\\sin 3θ + 20\\textrm{i}\\sin θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy equating $32\\textrm{i} \\sin^5 \\theta$ with the above, we get\n\n$$\n32\\textrm{i}\\sin^5\\theta = 2\\textrm{i}\\sin5\\theta - 10\\textrm{i}\\sin3\\theta + 20\\textrm{i}\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides of the equation by $32\\textrm{i}$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\sin^5\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{16}\\sin5\\theta - \\dfrac{5}{16}\\sin3\\theta+\\dfrac{5}{8}\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nA=\\dfrac{1}{16}\n$$" "Let\n\n$$\nz = \\cos\\theta + \\textrm{i}\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nWe start with the following identities:\n\n$$\n(z - \\frac{1}{z}) = 2\\textrm{i}\\sin θ(z^{n} - \\frac{1}{z^{n}}) = 2\\textrm{i}\\sin nθ\n$$\n\nRaising both sides to the power $5$ in the first identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(z - \\dfrac{1}{z}\\right)^5 \n= (2\\textrm{i}\\sin\\theta)^5\n\n=32\\textrm{i}\\sin^5\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the binomial using the binomial theorem and simplify the result using our identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(z - \\frac{1}{z})^{5} &= z^{5} - 5z^{4}(\\frac{1}{z}) + 10z^{3}(\\frac{1}{z})^{2} - 10z^{2}(\\frac{1}{z})^{3} + 5z(\\frac{1}{z})^{4} - (\\frac{1}{z})^{5} \\\\\n&= z^{5} - 5z^{3} + 10z - \\frac{10}{z} + \\frac{5}{z^{3}} - \\frac{1}{z^{5}} \\\\\n&= \\underbrace{(z^{5} - \\frac{1}{z^{5}})}_{2\\text{i}\\sin 5\\theta } - 5\\underbrace{(z^{3} - \\frac{1}{z^{3}})}_{2\\text{i}\\sin 3\\theta } + 10\\underbrace{(z - \\frac{1}{z})}_{2\\text{i}\\sin \\theta } \\\\\n&= 2\\textrm{i}\\sin 5θ - 5(2\\textrm{i}\\sin 3θ) + 10(2\\textrm{i}\\sin θ) \\\\\n&= 2\\textrm{i}\\sin 5θ - 10\\textrm{i}\\sin 3θ + 20\\textrm{i}\\sin θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy equating $32\\textrm{i} \\sin^5 \\theta$ with the above, we get\n\n$$\n32\\textrm{i}\\sin^5\\theta = 2\\textrm{i}\\sin5\\theta - 10\\textrm{i}\\sin3\\theta + 20\\textrm{i}\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides of the equation by $32\\textrm{i}$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\sin^5\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{16}\\sin5\\theta - \\dfrac{5}{16}\\sin3\\theta+\\dfrac{5}{8}\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nA=\\dfrac{1}{16}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186092616 :question/worked-solution "A variant of the principle of induction states the following: Let ${n}_{0}$ be an integer. Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P({n}_{0}),P({n}_{0}+1),P({n}_{0}+2),…,$ where\n\n- $P({n}_{0})$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)⇒P(k+1)$ for every integer $k\\ge {n}_{0}.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every integer $n\\ge {n}_{0}.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n6(n+1)<{n}^{3}\\,\\text{for}\\,n\\ge 3.\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called base case, $P(3).$ To do this, we substitute $n=3$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that the left-hand side is less than the right-hand side:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=3.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(3+1)=24,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{3}=27,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=3.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\ge 3.$ \n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 3,$ we have\n\n$$\n6(k+1)<{k}^{3}.\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the so-called **inductive step**.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n6((k+1)+1)=6k+2<(k+1)^{3}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(∗)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this is less than the right-hand side. We do this as follows:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6k+2 & =6(k+1)+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{6(k+1)}_{⏟}}_{<{k}^{3}}+6 & <{k}^{3}+6 \\\\  & ={k}^{3}+{{5}_{⏟}}_{<3{k}^{2}+3k}+1 \\\\  & <{k}^{3}+3{k}^{2}+3k+1 \\\\  & =(k+1)^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(∗)$ is less than the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion. \n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, $6(n+1)<{n}^{3}$ for all $n\\ge 3.$" "A variant of the principle of induction states the following: Let ${n}_{0}$ be an integer. Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P({n}_{0}),P({n}_{0}+1),P({n}_{0}+2),…,$ where\n\n- $P({n}_{0})$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)⇒P(k+1)$ for every integer $k\\ge {n}_{0}.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every integer $n\\ge {n}_{0}.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n6(n+1)<{n}^{3}\\,\\text{for}\\,n\\ge 3.\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called base case, $P(3).$ To do this, we substitute $n=3$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that the left-hand side is less than the right-hand side:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=3.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(3+1)=24,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{3}=27,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=3.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\ge 3.$ \n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 3,$ we have\n\n$$\n6(k+1)<{k}^{3}.\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the so-called **inductive step**.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n6((k+1)+1)=6k+2<(k+1)^{3}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(∗)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this is less than the right-hand side. We do this as follows:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6k+2 & =6(k+1)+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{6(k+1)}_{<{k}^{3}}+6 & <{k}^{3}+6 \\\\  & ={k}^{3}+\\underbrace{5}_{<3{k}^{2}+3k}+1 \\\\  & <{k}^{3}+3{k}^{2}+3k+1 \\\\  & =(k+1)^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(∗)$ is less than the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion. \n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, $6(n+1)<{n}^{3}$ for all $n\\ge 3.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186093416 :tutorial/content "Now that we've had some practice using the power-reducing formulas, let's see where they come from.\n\nFirst, let\n\n$$\nz = \\cos \\theta + \\textrm{i} \\sin \\theta\n$$\n\nUsing De Moivre's theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz^{n} &= (\\cos θ + \\textrm{i}\\sin θ)^{n} \\\\\n&= \\cos (nθ) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (nθ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's simplify the expression for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{z^n}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{z^{n}} &= (1)/(\\cos (nθ) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (nθ)) \\\\\n&= (1)/(\\cos (nθ) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (nθ)) \\cdot (\\cos (nθ) - \\textrm{i}\\sin (nθ))/(\\cos (nθ) - \\textrm{i}\\sin (nθ)) \\\\\n&= (\\cos (nθ) - \\textrm{i}\\sin (nθ))/(\\cos^{2} (nθ) + \\sin^{2} (nθ)) \\\\\n&= (\\cos (nθ) - \\textrm{i}\\sin (nθ))/(1) \\\\\n&= \\cos (nθ) - \\textrm{i}\\sin (nθ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write down the power-reducing formulas. The power-reducing formula for cosine is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(z^{n} + \\frac{1}{z^{n}}) &= [\\cos (nθ) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (nθ)]^(⏞)^(z^{n}) + [\\cos (nθ) - \\textrm{i}\\sin (nθ)]^(⏞)^(\\frac{1}{z^{n}}) \\\\\n&= 2\\cos (nθ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the power-reducing formula for sine is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(z^{n} - \\frac{1}{z^{n}}) &= [\\cos (nθ) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (nθ)]^(⏞)^(z^{n}) - [\\cos (nθ) - \\textrm{i}\\sin (nθ)]^(⏞)^(\\frac{1}{z^{n}}) \\\\\n&= 2\\textrm{i}\\sin (nθ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Now that we've had some practice using the power-reducing formulas, let's see where they come from.\n\nFirst, let\n\n$$\nz = \\cos \\theta + \\textrm{i} \\sin \\theta\n$$\n\nUsing De Moivre's theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz^{n} &= (\\cos θ + \\textrm{i}\\sin θ)^{n} \\\\\n&= \\cos (nθ) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (nθ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's simplify the expression for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{z^n}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{z^{n}} &= (1)/(\\cos (nθ) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (nθ)) \\\\\n&= (1)/(\\cos (nθ) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (nθ)) \\cdot (\\cos (nθ) - \\textrm{i}\\sin (nθ))/(\\cos (nθ) - \\textrm{i}\\sin (nθ)) \\\\\n&= (\\cos (nθ) - \\textrm{i}\\sin (nθ))/(\\cos^{2} (nθ) + \\sin^{2} (nθ)) \\\\\n&= (\\cos (nθ) - \\textrm{i}\\sin (nθ))/(1) \\\\\n&= \\cos (nθ) - \\textrm{i}\\sin (nθ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write down the power-reducing formulas. The power-reducing formula for cosine is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(z^{n} + \\frac{1}{z^{n}}) &= \\overbrace{[\\cos (nθ) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (nθ)]}^{{z}^{n}} + \\overbrace{[\\cos (nθ) - \\textrm{i}\\sin (nθ)]}^{\\frac{1}{{z}^{n}}} \\\\\n&= 2\\cos (nθ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the power-reducing formula for sine is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(z^{n} - \\frac{1}{z^{n}}) &= \\overbrace{[\\cos (nθ) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (nθ)]}^{{z}^{n}} - \\overbrace{[\\cos (nθ) - \\textrm{i}\\sin (nθ)]}^{\\frac{1}{{z}^{n}}} \\\\\n&= 2\\textrm{i}\\sin (nθ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186093461 :tutorial/content "One particular type of geometric series modeling problem involves bouncing balls. Let's walk though an example of this type of problem.\n\nA ball is dropped from a height of $16$ meters. Then, it bounces to a height of $8$ meters and continues to bounce to heights that follow a geometric sequence. Assuming that the ball's motion is vertical throughout, what is the total distance the ball travels between when it is dropped and when it hits the ground for the fourth time?\n\nLet's start by visualizing how the height of the ball changes with time $t$:\n\n![](images/76/7626cb0067ab591a64fc95fcd19fbc277ed4287bd694e937e395c1ad9618ca72.png)\n\nLet $a_n$ denote the maximum height of the ball between the $(n-1)$th and $n$th bounces. Then, we have\n\n$$\na_1 = 16, \\qquad a_2 = 8, \\qquad \\ldots\n$$\n\nWe're told that $a_n$ is geometric. The common ratio is\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac{a_2}{a_1} = \\dfrac{8}{16} = \\dfrac12\n$$\n\nWe break down the motion of the ball as follows:\n\n- Before the first bounce, the ball travels $a_1$ meters.\n- Between the $1$st and $2$nd bounce, the ball travels $a_2 + a_2 = 2a_2$ meters.\n- Between the $2$nd and $3$rd bounce, the ball travels $a_3 + a_3 = 2a_3$ meters.\n- Between the $3$rd and $4$th bounce, the ball travels $a_4 + a_4 = 2a_4$ meters.\n\nTherefore, the total distance $d$ traveled between when the ball is dropped and when it hits the ground for the fourth time is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= a_{1} + 2a_{2} + 2a_{3} + 2a_{4} \\\\\n&= 2a_{1} - a_{1}_(⏟)_(= a_{1}) + 2a_{2} + 2a_{3} + 2a_{4} \\\\\n&= 2a_{1} + 2a_{2} + 2a_{3} + 2a_{4} - a_{1} \\\\\n&= 2(a_{1} + a_{2} + a_{3} + a_{4}) - a_{1} \\\\\n&= 2S_{4} - a_{1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S_4$ is the sum of the first $4$ terms of the geometric sequence.\n\nTo calculate $S_4$, we use the formula\n\n$$\nS_N = a_1\\left(\\dfrac{1-r^N}{1-r} \\right)\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\na_1 = 16, r = \\dfrac12\n$$\n\nand $N=4$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{4} &= 16((1 - (\\frac{1}{2})^{4})/((1 - \\frac{1}{2}))) \\\\\n&= 16 \\cdot \\frac{15}{8} \\\\\n&= 30\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ball travels a total distance of\n\n$$\nd = 2 \\cdot 30 - 16 = 44 \\,\\textrm m\n$$" "One particular type of geometric series modeling problem involves bouncing balls. Let's walk though an example of this type of problem.\n\nA ball is dropped from a height of $16$ meters. Then, it bounces to a height of $8$ meters and continues to bounce to heights that follow a geometric sequence. Assuming that the ball's motion is vertical throughout, what is the total distance the ball travels between when it is dropped and when it hits the ground for the fourth time?\n\nLet's start by visualizing how the height of the ball changes with time $t$:\n\n![](images/76/7626cb0067ab591a64fc95fcd19fbc277ed4287bd694e937e395c1ad9618ca72.png)\n\nLet $a_n$ denote the maximum height of the ball between the $(n-1)$th and $n$th bounces. Then, we have\n\n$$\na_1 = 16, \\qquad a_2 = 8, \\qquad \\ldots\n$$\n\nWe're told that $a_n$ is geometric. The common ratio is\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac{a_2}{a_1} = \\dfrac{8}{16} = \\dfrac12\n$$\n\nWe break down the motion of the ball as follows:\n\n- Before the first bounce, the ball travels $a_1$ meters.\n- Between the $1$st and $2$nd bounce, the ball travels $a_2 + a_2 = 2a_2$ meters.\n- Between the $2$nd and $3$rd bounce, the ball travels $a_3 + a_3 = 2a_3$ meters.\n- Between the $3$rd and $4$th bounce, the ball travels $a_4 + a_4 = 2a_4$ meters.\n\nTherefore, the total distance $d$ traveled between when the ball is dropped and when it hits the ground for the fourth time is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= a_{1} + 2a_{2} + 2a_{3} + 2a_{4} \\\\\n&= \\underbrace{2a_{1} - a_{1}}_{={a}_{1}} + 2a_{2} + 2a_{3} + 2a_{4} \\\\\n&= 2a_{1} + 2a_{2} + 2a_{3} + 2a_{4} - a_{1} \\\\\n&= 2(a_{1} + a_{2} + a_{3} + a_{4}) - a_{1} \\\\\n&= 2S_{4} - a_{1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S_4$ is the sum of the first $4$ terms of the geometric sequence.\n\nTo calculate $S_4$, we use the formula\n\n$$\nS_N = a_1\\left(\\dfrac{1-r^N}{1-r} \\right)\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\na_1 = 16, r = \\dfrac12\n$$\n\nand $N=4$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{4} &= 16((1 - (\\frac{1}{2})^{4})/((1 - \\frac{1}{2}))) \\\\\n&= 16 \\cdot \\frac{15}{8} \\\\\n&= 30\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ball travels a total distance of\n\n$$\nd = 2 \\cdot 30 - 16 = 44 \\,\\textrm m\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186093486 :tutorial/content "Suppose we wish to approximate the area $\\mathcal{A}$ under the curve\n\n$$\ny=1+x^2\n$$\n\nover the interval $[1,3]$.\n\n![](images/51/51961229e4e31dc9cac2e12e90b965898ab8887fc922d5efddbd60a21de9cecf.png)\n\nWe could use the left or right Riemann sum. However, there is a method that usually gives us an even better approximation.\n\nThis method is called the **midpoint Riemann sum**, which means that the curve passes through the top *midpoint* of each rectangle.\n\nLet's approximate the area under the curve using a midpoint Riemann sum with two rectangles, as shown below.\n\n![](images/d0/d0b87f94eafabc820c6e7b60e393448c3135f3fba30f381e09b8c3c7926fb98d.png)\n\nAdding the areas of the two rectangles, we get\n\n$$\nA \\mid \\approx [f(1.5)^(⏞)^(height) \\cdot 1_(⏟)_(width)]^(⏞)^([math]1[/math]st rectangle) + [f(2.5)^(⏞)^(height) \\cdot 1_(⏟)_(width)]^(⏞)^([math]2[/math]nd rectangle); = [(1 + 1.5^{2}) \\cdot 1] + [(1 + 2.5^{2}) \\cdot 1]; = 3.25 + 7.25; = 10.5\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *concave up* over the interval $[1,3]$, the \"missing\" area between the rectangles and the function is greater than the \"extra\" area, which means our approximation is an *underestimate* of the actual area." "Suppose we wish to approximate the area $\\mathcal{A}$ under the curve\n\n$$\ny=1+x^2\n$$\n\nover the interval $[1,3]$.\n\n![](images/51/51961229e4e31dc9cac2e12e90b965898ab8887fc922d5efddbd60a21de9cecf.png)\n\nWe could use the left or right Riemann sum. However, there is a method that usually gives us an even better approximation.\n\nThis method is called the **midpoint Riemann sum**, which means that the curve passes through the top *midpoint* of each rectangle.\n\nLet's approximate the area under the curve using a midpoint Riemann sum with two rectangles, as shown below.\n\n![](images/d0/d0b87f94eafabc820c6e7b60e393448c3135f3fba30f381e09b8c3c7926fb98d.png)\n\nAdding the areas of the two rectangles, we get\n\n$$\nA \\mid \\approx \\overbrace{[\\overbrace{f(1.5)}^{\\text{height}} \\cdot \\underbrace{1}_{\\text{width}}]}^{{1}\\text{st rectangle}} + \\overbrace{[\\overbrace{f(2.5)}^{\\text{height}} \\cdot \\underbrace{1}_{\\text{width}}]}^{{2}\\text{nd rectangle}}; = [(1 + 1.5^{2}) \\cdot 1] + [(1 + 2.5^{2}) \\cdot 1]; = 3.25 + 7.25; = 10.5\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *concave up* over the interval $[1,3]$, the \"missing\" area between the rectangles and the function is greater than the \"extra\" area, which means our approximation is an *underestimate* of the actual area."]
 [:db/cas 17592186093497 :tutorial/content "Let's prove that the following statement is true using mathematical induction:\n\n$n^2+3n > 2n-1$ for all $n\\in\\mathbb N$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$P(n): \\quad n^2+3n > 2n-1$ for all $n \\in\\mathbb N$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called base case. Since we are required to prove the statement for $n \\geq 1,$ the base case is $P(1).$ So, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that the left-hand side is greater than the right-hand side:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2}+3(1)=4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(1)-1=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, since $4 > 1,$ our statement is true for $n=1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\geq 1.$ \n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\geq 1,$ we have\n\n$$\nk^2+3k > 2k-1.\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the so-called **inductive step**.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n(k+1)^2+ 3(k+1) > 2(k+1)-1 = 2k+1 \\qquad\\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this is greater than the right-hand side. We do this as follows:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(k+1)^{2}+3(k+1) & ={k}^{2}+2k+1+3k+3 \\\\  & ={k}^{2}+3k+2k+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{k}^{2}+3k+2k+4 & ={{({k}^{2}+3k)}_{⏟}}_{>2k-1}+2k+4 \\\\  & >2k-1+2k+4 \\\\  & =(2k+2)+(2k+1) \\\\  & ={{2k+2}_{⏟}}_{>0}+2k+1 \\\\  & >2k+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ is greater than the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion. \n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, $n^2+3n > 2n-1$ for all $n \\in \\mathbb N.$" "Let's prove that the following statement is true using mathematical induction:\n\n$n^2+3n > 2n-1$ for all $n\\in\\mathbb N$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$P(n): \\quad n^2+3n > 2n-1$ for all $n \\in\\mathbb N$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called base case. Since we are required to prove the statement for $n \\geq 1,$ the base case is $P(1).$ So, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that the left-hand side is greater than the right-hand side:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2}+3(1)=4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(1)-1=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, since $4 > 1,$ our statement is true for $n=1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\geq 1.$ \n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\geq 1,$ we have\n\n$$\nk^2+3k > 2k-1.\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the so-called **inductive step**.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n(k+1)^2+ 3(k+1) > 2(k+1)-1 = 2k+1 \\qquad\\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this is greater than the right-hand side. We do this as follows:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(k+1)^{2}+3(k+1) & ={k}^{2}+2k+1+3k+3 \\\\  & ={k}^{2}+3k+2k+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{k}^{2}+3k+2k+4 & =\\underbrace{({k}^{2}+3k)}_{>2k-1}+2k+4 \\\\  & >2k-1+2k+4 \\\\  & =(2k+2)+(2k+1) \\\\  & =\\underbrace{2k+2}_{>0}+2k+1 \\\\  & >2k+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ is greater than the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion. \n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, $n^2+3n > 2n-1$ for all $n \\in \\mathbb N.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186096577 :question/worked-solution "To write a number in scientific notation, we need to express it in the form\n\n$$\na\\times 10^n\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n1 \\leq a < 10\n$$\n\nand $n$ is an integer.\n\nLet's start by writing $7\\,800$ in scientific notation.\n\nTo find $a$, we place the decimal point directly after the first non-zero digit. In this case, we have the following:\n$a = 7.8$\n\nTo find $n$, we take the number $a$ that we just found and count the number of spaces we need to move the decimal point to retrieve the original number.\n\n$$\n7.8 \\rightarrow 7\\,800\n$$\n\nWe can see that the decimal point must move $3$ spaces to the *right*, so $n=3$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n7\\,800 = 7.8 \\times 10^{3}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can express our original number in scientific notation using the laws of exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7800 \\cdot 10^{3} &= 7.8 \\cdot 10^{3}_(⏟)_(7800) \\cdot 10^{3} \\\\\n&= 7.8 \\cdot 10^{3} \\cdot 10^{3} \\\\\n&= 7.8 \\cdot 10^{3 + 3} \\\\\n&= 7.8 \\cdot 10^{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To write a number in scientific notation, we need to express it in the form\n\n$$\na\\times 10^n\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n1 \\leq a < 10\n$$\n\nand $n$ is an integer.\n\nLet's start by writing $7\\,800$ in scientific notation.\n\nTo find $a$, we place the decimal point directly after the first non-zero digit. In this case, we have the following:\n$a = 7.8$\n\nTo find $n$, we take the number $a$ that we just found and count the number of spaces we need to move the decimal point to retrieve the original number.\n\n$$\n7.8 \\rightarrow 7\\,800\n$$\n\nWe can see that the decimal point must move $3$ spaces to the *right*, so $n=3$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n7\\,800 = 7.8 \\times 10^{3}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can express our original number in scientific notation using the laws of exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7800 \\cdot 10^{3} &= \\underbrace{7.8 \\cdot 10^{3}}_{7\\,800} \\cdot 10^{3} \\\\\n&= 7.8 \\cdot 10^{3} \\cdot 10^{3} \\\\\n&= 7.8 \\cdot 10^{3 + 3} \\\\\n&= 7.8 \\cdot 10^{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186097927 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n\\int x\\ln{x}\\, \\textrm{d}x \\,.\n$$\n\nThe method of integration by parts can help us solve this problem. Let's remind ourselves of the \"by-parts\" formula,\n\n$$\n\\int u v' \\textrm{d}x = uv - \\int v u'\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe usually let $u = x,$ but that would leave us with $v' = \\ln(x)$ which is hard to integrate. So instead, we're going to choose our $u$ and $v'$ slightly differently, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow the formula of integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}_{⏟}}_{u}\\cdot {{x}_{⏟}}_{{v}^{′}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,{x}^{2}-\\int \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\frac{1}{2}\\int x\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen we apply integration by parts to integrals containing logarithmic functions, we should *always* identify the logarithmic function with $u,$ not with $v'.$" "Suppose that we want to evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n\\int x\\ln{x}\\, \\textrm{d}x \\,.\n$$\n\nThe method of integration by parts can help us solve this problem. Let's remind ourselves of the \"by-parts\" formula,\n\n$$\n\\int u v' \\textrm{d}x = uv - \\int v u'\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe usually let $u = x,$ but that would leave us with $v' = \\ln(x)$ which is hard to integrate. So instead, we're going to choose our $u$ and $v'$ slightly differently, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow the formula of integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\underbrace{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}_{u}\\cdot \\underbrace{x}_{{v}^{′}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,{x}^{2}-\\int \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\frac{1}{2}\\int x\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen we apply integration by parts to integrals containing logarithmic functions, we should *always* identify the logarithmic function with $u,$ not with $v'.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186100589 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the situation.\n\n![](images/a2/a22e7e19b360c91bcdae8eba8a3230943bd69270eac9dd158652d1648e24de51.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The dotted line is the *perpendicular bisector* of the segment $\\overline{AB}.$\n\n- All the points on this perpendicular bisector are equidistant to $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Our task is to find the point $Q$ on this line that's closest to $P.$\n\nFirst, we create a function $S$ which represents the *squared* distance between the point $P(3,-2)$ and a general point $Q(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-3)^{2}+(y-(-2))^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+(y+2)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-6x+9+{y}^{2}+4y+4 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6x+4y+13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required point $Q(x,y)$ is equidistant to the points $A(-1,1)$ and $B(2,0).$ So, by writing down $(QA)^2 = (QB)^2,$ we have the following constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{(x-(-1))^{2}+(y-1)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QA)^{2}} & ={{(x-2)^{2}+(y-0)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QB)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $y$ the subject in the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+1+{y}^{2}-2y+1 & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+{y}^{2} \\\\ \\cancel{{x}^{2}}+2x+1+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}-2y+1 & =\\cancel{{x}^{2}}-4x+4+\\cancel{{y}^{2}} \\\\ 2x-2y+2 & =-4x+4 \\\\ 6x-2 & =2y \\\\ y & =3x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $S(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & ={x}^{2}+(3x-1)^{2}-6x+4(3x-1)+13 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+9{x}^{2}-6x+1-6x+12x-4+13 \\\\  & =10{x}^{2}+10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the first derivative,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =20x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve $S'(x)=0$ to find the stationary point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 20x & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify that this stationary point is a minimum. Taking the second derivative, we find\n\n$$\nS''(0) =20>0.\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the stationary point $x=0$ indeed minimizes the squared distance. \n\nFinally, the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\ny = 3(0)-1 = -1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required point is $\\left(0,-1\\right).$" "Let's sketch the situation.\n\n![](images/a2/a22e7e19b360c91bcdae8eba8a3230943bd69270eac9dd158652d1648e24de51.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The dotted line is the *perpendicular bisector* of the segment $\\overline{AB}.$\n\n- All the points on this perpendicular bisector are equidistant to $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Our task is to find the point $Q$ on this line that's closest to $P.$\n\nFirst, we create a function $S$ which represents the *squared* distance between the point $P(3,-2)$ and a general point $Q(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-3)^{2}+(y-(-2))^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+(y+2)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-6x+9+{y}^{2}+4y+4 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6x+4y+13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required point $Q(x,y)$ is equidistant to the points $A(-1,1)$ and $B(2,0).$ So, by writing down $(QA)^2 = (QB)^2,$ we have the following constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{(x-(-1))^{2}+(y-1)^{2}}_{(QA)^{2}} & =\\underbrace{(x-2)^{2}+(y-0)^{2}}_{(QB)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $y$ the subject in the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+1+{y}^{2}-2y+1 & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+{y}^{2} \\\\ \\cancel{{x}^{2}}+2x+1+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}-2y+1 & =\\cancel{{x}^{2}}-4x+4+\\cancel{{y}^{2}} \\\\ 2x-2y+2 & =-4x+4 \\\\ 6x-2 & =2y \\\\ y & =3x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $S(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & ={x}^{2}+(3x-1)^{2}-6x+4(3x-1)+13 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+9{x}^{2}-6x+1-6x+12x-4+13 \\\\  & =10{x}^{2}+10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the first derivative,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =20x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve $S'(x)=0$ to find the stationary point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 20x & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify that this stationary point is a minimum. Taking the second derivative, we find\n\n$$\nS''(0) =20>0.\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the stationary point $x=0$ indeed minimizes the squared distance. \n\nFinally, the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\ny = 3(0)-1 = -1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required point is $\\left(0,-1\\right).$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186100784 :question/worked-solution "First, note that if there are $x$ *vacant* rooms, the hotel has $(21-x)$ occupied rooms, and the rate per room is $(12+x)$ dollars. So, the total revenue (in dollars) is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & ={{(21-x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{occupied rooms}}\\times \\,\\,\\,{{(12+x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{rate per room}} \\\\  & =252+21x-12x-{x}^{2} \\\\  & =252+9x-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, for having $(21-x)$ rooms rented out, the hotel has a cost (in dollars) of \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(x) & =3(21-x) \\\\  & =63-3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\ P(x) & =(252+9x-{x}^{2})-(63-3x) \\\\  & =189+12x-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince there are $21$ rooms, the permitted domain is $0 \\leq x\\leq 21.$ \n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0 \\lt x\\lt 21.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\nP'(x) = 12 - 2x \\,.\n$$\n\nand solving $P'(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 12-2x & =0 \\\\ 2x & =12 \\\\ x & =6\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=6$ lies in the interval $0 <  x < 21.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=6$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\nP''(x) = -2 <             0\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=6$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x).$ And since $P(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=6$ is a global maximum of $P(x).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that the hotel will have a maximum profit when there are $x=6$ vacant rooms.  \n\nWe know that the rate per room is $(12+x)$ dollars. If there are $6$ vacant rooms, then the rate per room is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12+6 & =\\text{\\[}math]18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the manager should charge a room rate of $\\[/math]18$ per night." "First, note that if there are $x$ *vacant* rooms, the hotel has $(21-x)$ occupied rooms, and the rate per room is $(12+x)$ dollars. So, the total revenue (in dollars) is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & =\\underbrace{(21-x)}_{\\text{occupied rooms}}\\times \\,\\,\\,\\underbrace{(12+x)}_{\\text{rate per room}} \\\\  & =252+21x-12x-{x}^{2} \\\\  & =252+9x-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, for having $(21-x)$ rooms rented out, the hotel has a cost (in dollars) of \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(x) & =3(21-x) \\\\  & =63-3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\ P(x) & =(252+9x-{x}^{2})-(63-3x) \\\\  & =189+12x-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince there are $21$ rooms, the permitted domain is $0 \\leq x\\leq 21.$ \n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0 \\lt x\\lt 21.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\nP'(x) = 12 - 2x \\,.\n$$\n\nand solving $P'(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 12-2x & =0 \\\\ 2x & =12 \\\\ x & =6\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=6$ lies in the interval $0 <  x < 21.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=6$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\nP''(x) = -2 <             0\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=6$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x).$ And since $P(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=6$ is a global maximum of $P(x).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that the hotel will have a maximum profit when there are $x=6$ vacant rooms.  \n\nWe know that the rate per room is $(12+x)$ dollars. If there are $6$ vacant rooms, then the rate per room is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12+6 & =\\text{\\[}math]18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the manager should charge a room rate of $\\[/math]18$ per night."]
 [:db/cas 17592186101113 :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for $x$ kids attending, the summer camp has a revenue (in dollars) of \n\n$$\nR(x)=300\\cdot x=300x\n$$\n\nand a cost (in dollars) of \n\n$$\nC(x)={{2\\,000}_{⏟}}_{\\text{fixed cost}}\\,\\,+{{100x.}_{⏟}}_{\\text{variable cost}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =300x-(2\\,000+100x) \\\\  & =200x-2\\,000.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 200.$ Therefore, $x=0$ and $x=200$ are candidates for the global maxima of $P(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<200.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=200.\n$$\n\nWe can see that the equation ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ has no solutions.\n\nThus, our candidates are $x=0$ and $x=200.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =200(0)-2\\,000=-2\\,000 \\\\ P(200) & =200(200)-2\\,000=38\\,000\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the summer camp will have a maximum profit level when $200$ kids attend." "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for $x$ kids attending, the summer camp has a revenue (in dollars) of \n\n$$\nR(x)=300\\cdot x=300x\n$$\n\nand a cost (in dollars) of \n\n$$\nC(x)=\\underbrace{2\\,000}_{\\text{fixed cost}}\\,\\,+\\underbrace{100x.}_{\\text{variable cost}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =300x-(2\\,000+100x) \\\\  & =200x-2\\,000.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 200.$ Therefore, $x=0$ and $x=200$ are candidates for the global maxima of $P(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<200.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=200.\n$$\n\nWe can see that the equation ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ has no solutions.\n\nThus, our candidates are $x=0$ and $x=200.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =200(0)-2\\,000=-2\\,000 \\\\ P(200) & =200(200)-2\\,000=38\\,000\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the summer camp will have a maximum profit level when $200$ kids attend."]
 [:db/cas 17592186101196 :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ tickets, the cinema has a revenue (in dollars) of \n\n$$\nR(x)=10\\cdot x=10x\n$$\n\nand a cost (in dollars) of \n\n$$\nC(x)={{400}_{⏟}}_{\\text{fixed cost}}\\,\\,+{{0.25x.}_{⏟}}_{\\text{variable cost}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =10x-(400+0.25x) \\\\  & =9.75x-400.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 120.$ Therefore, $x=0$ and $x=120$ are candidates for the global maxima of $P(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<120.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=9.75.\n$$\n\nWe can see that the equation ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ has no solutions.\n\nOur candidates are $x=0$ and $x=120.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =9.75(0)-400=-400 \\\\ P(120) & =9.75(120)-400=770\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the cinema will have a maximum profit level when it sells $120$ tickets." "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ tickets, the cinema has a revenue (in dollars) of \n\n$$\nR(x)=10\\cdot x=10x\n$$\n\nand a cost (in dollars) of \n\n$$\nC(x)=\\underbrace{400}_{\\text{fixed cost}}\\,\\,+\\underbrace{0.25x.}_{\\text{variable cost}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =10x-(400+0.25x) \\\\  & =9.75x-400.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 120.$ Therefore, $x=0$ and $x=120$ are candidates for the global maxima of $P(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<120.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=9.75.\n$$\n\nWe can see that the equation ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ has no solutions.\n\nOur candidates are $x=0$ and $x=120.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =9.75(0)-400=-400 \\\\ P(120) & =9.75(120)-400=770\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the cinema will have a maximum profit level when it sells $120$ tickets."]
 [:db/cas 17592186101537 :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-3,0]$  and the line segment for $x\\in [0,4].$ Therefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nThe $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,0].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4+1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,4].$ The corresponding region is rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =lw \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}+4 \\\\  & =\\frac{23}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-3,0]$  and the line segment for $x\\in [0,4].$ Therefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\underbrace{{\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{{A}_{1}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nThe $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,0].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4+1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,4].$ The corresponding region is rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =lw \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}+4 \\\\  & =\\frac{23}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186101969 :tutorial/content "To raise a power to another power, we multiply the exponents. This is the **power rule for exponents**. \n\nTo demonstrate, let's simplify the expression $\\left( 3^5\\right)^4$ by applying the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\left( 3^5\\right)^4 = 3^{5\\cdot 4} = 3^{20}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The power rule for exponents is just a quicker alternative to using repeated multiplication, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({3}^{5})}^{4} & = \\\\ {{{3}^{5}\\times {3}^{5}\\times {3}^{5}\\times {3}^{5}}_{⏟}}_{4\\text{ copies}} & = \\\\ {3}^{5+5+5+5} & = \\\\ {3}^{5\\cdot 4} & = \\\\ {3}^{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To raise a power to another power, we multiply the exponents. This is the **power rule for exponents**. \n\nTo demonstrate, let's simplify the expression $\\left( 3^5\\right)^4$ by applying the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\left( 3^5\\right)^4 = 3^{5\\cdot 4} = 3^{20}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The power rule for exponents is just a quicker alternative to using repeated multiplication, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({3}^{5})}^{4} & = \\\\ \\underbrace{{3}^{5}\\times {3}^{5}\\times {3}^{5}\\times {3}^{5}}_{4\\text{ copies}} & = \\\\ {3}^{5+5+5+5} & = \\\\ {3}^{5\\cdot 4} & = \\\\ {3}^{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186101981 :tutorial/content "To compute the quotient of two complex numbers\n\n$$\nz_1 = r_1 \\left[ \\cos(\\theta_1) + \\textrm{i} \\sin(\\theta_1) \\right]\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nz_2 = r_2 \\left[ \\cos(\\theta_2) + \\textrm{i} \\sin(\\theta_2) \\right]\n$$\n\nexpressed in polar form, we have been using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{z_{1}}{z_{2}} = \\frac{r_{1}}{r_{2}}[\\cos (θ_{1} - θ_{2}) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (θ_{1} - θ_{2})]\n$$\n\nTo see why the formula works, let's compute the quotient manually:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{z_{1}}{z_{2}} &= (r_{1}[\\cos (θ_{1}) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (θ_{1})])/(r_{2}[\\cos (θ_{2}) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (θ_{2})]) \\\\\n&= \\frac{r_{1}}{r_{2}} \\cdot (\\cos (θ_{1}) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (θ_{1}))/(\\cos (θ_{2}) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (θ_{2})) \\cdot (\\cos (θ_{2}) - \\textrm{i}\\sin (θ_{2}))/(\\cos (θ_{2}) - \\textrm{i}\\sin (θ_{2})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{r_{1}}{r_{2}} \\cdot ([\\cos (θ_{1})\\cos (θ_{2}) + \\sin (θ_{1})\\sin (θ_{2})]^(⏞)^(\\cos (θ_{1} - θ_{2})) + \\textrm{i}[\\sin (θ_{1})\\cos (θ_{2}) - \\cos (θ_{1})\\sin (θ_{2})]^(⏞)^(\\sin (θ_{1} - θ_{2})))/(\\cos^{2} (θ_{2}) + \\sin^{2} (θ_{2})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{r_{1}}{r_{2}} \\cdot (\\cos (θ_{1} - θ_{2}) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (θ_{1} - θ_{2}))/(1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{r_{1}}{r_{2}}[\\cos (θ_{1} - θ_{2}) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (θ_{1} - θ_{2})]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, this formula is a consequence of the difference formulas for cosine and sine!\n\nBy working out the product manually, we get the same result, but with a lot more effort and calculations. The formula is the quickest way to go." "To compute the quotient of two complex numbers\n\n$$\nz_1 = r_1 \\left[ \\cos(\\theta_1) + \\textrm{i} \\sin(\\theta_1) \\right]\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nz_2 = r_2 \\left[ \\cos(\\theta_2) + \\textrm{i} \\sin(\\theta_2) \\right]\n$$\n\nexpressed in polar form, we have been using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{z_{1}}{z_{2}} = \\frac{r_{1}}{r_{2}}[\\cos (θ_{1} - θ_{2}) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (θ_{1} - θ_{2})]\n$$\n\nTo see why the formula works, let's compute the quotient manually:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{z_{1}}{z_{2}} &= (r_{1}[\\cos (θ_{1}) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (θ_{1})])/(r_{2}[\\cos (θ_{2}) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (θ_{2})]) \\\\\n&= \\frac{r_{1}}{r_{2}} \\cdot (\\cos (θ_{1}) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (θ_{1}))/(\\cos (θ_{2}) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (θ_{2})) \\cdot (\\cos (θ_{2}) - \\textrm{i}\\sin (θ_{2}))/(\\cos (θ_{2}) - \\textrm{i}\\sin (θ_{2})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{r_{1}}{r_{2}} \\cdot (\\overbrace{[\\cos (θ_{1})\\cos (θ_{2}) + \\sin (θ_{1})\\sin (θ_{2})]}^{\\cos ({\\theta }_{1}-{\\theta }_{2})} + \\textrm{i}\\overbrace{[\\sin (θ_{1})\\cos (θ_{2}) - \\cos (θ_{1})\\sin (θ_{2})]}^{\\sin ({\\theta }_{1}-{\\theta }_{2})})/(\\cos^{2} (θ_{2}) + \\sin^{2} (θ_{2})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{r_{1}}{r_{2}} \\cdot (\\cos (θ_{1} - θ_{2}) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (θ_{1} - θ_{2}))/(1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{r_{1}}{r_{2}}[\\cos (θ_{1} - θ_{2}) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (θ_{1} - θ_{2})]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, this formula is a consequence of the difference formulas for cosine and sine!\n\nBy working out the product manually, we get the same result, but with a lot more effort and calculations. The formula is the quickest way to go."]
 [:db/cas 17592186102016 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have the points $A(-1,0)$ and $B(3,0)$ in the plane. \n\nRecall that the *perpendicular bisector* of the segment $\\overline{AB}$ is the line perpendicular to $\\overline{AB}$ that bisects it.  \n\nThe points $A, B,$ and the perpendicular bisector of $\\overline{AB}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/52/52fb46efe17119f3299831a8947777b4528d8a677ced46d49e8084d1efab3c1c.png)\n\nThe *perpendicular bisector theorem* tells us that every point on this line is *equidistant* to $A$ and $B.$ In other words, if we pick a point $Q(x,y)$ on the line, then $AQ = BQ$ for every point $Q.$\n\n![](images/05/054d51662ad486b97bbacacc6dc8eac4389788480c2e1a08e9c2769490170527.png)\n\nNow, suppose we pick the point $P(2,3)$ that does not lie on the perpendicular bisector. What point on our line is closest to $P?$\n\n![](images/3c/3cb1c0280c74ba3b347764b493ae438b04c6812c6bdd9498024d3f99860af012.png)\n\nTo answer this, we first create a function $S$ representing the squared distance between the point $P(2,3)$ and a general point $Q(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+{y}^{2}-6y+9 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4x-6y+13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe cannot differentiate this function because it contains two variables. However, we can eliminate one by noting that $Q$ lies on the perpendicular bisector.\n\nThe required point $Q(x,y)$ is equidistant to the points $A(-1,0)$ and $B(3,0).$ So, by writing down $(QA)^2 = (QB)^2,$ we have the following constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{(x-(-1))^{2}+(y-0)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QA)^{2}} & ={{(x-3)^{2}+(y-0)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QB)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $x$ the subject in the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x+1 & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6x+9 \\\\ \\cancel{{x}^{2}}+2x+1+\\cancel{{y}^{2}} & =\\cancel{{x}^{2}}-6x+9+\\cancel{{y}^{2}} \\\\ 2x+6x & =9-1 \\\\ 8x & =8 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $S(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(y) & ={1}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4(1)-6y+13 \\\\  & =1+{y}^{2}-4-6y+13 \\\\  & ={y}^{2}-6y+10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the first derivative,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{'}(y) & =2y-6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve $S'(y)=0$ to find the stationary point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{'}(y) & =0 \\\\ 2y-6 & =0 \\\\ y & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify that this stationary point is a minimum. Taking the second derivative, we find\n\n$$\nS''(3) =2>0.\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the stationary point $y=3$ indeed minimizes the squared distance. \n\nTherefore, the required point is $\\left(1,3\\right).$" "Suppose we have the points $A(-1,0)$ and $B(3,0)$ in the plane. \n\nRecall that the *perpendicular bisector* of the segment $\\overline{AB}$ is the line perpendicular to $\\overline{AB}$ that bisects it.  \n\nThe points $A, B,$ and the perpendicular bisector of $\\overline{AB}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/52/52fb46efe17119f3299831a8947777b4528d8a677ced46d49e8084d1efab3c1c.png)\n\nThe *perpendicular bisector theorem* tells us that every point on this line is *equidistant* to $A$ and $B.$ In other words, if we pick a point $Q(x,y)$ on the line, then $AQ = BQ$ for every point $Q.$\n\n![](images/05/054d51662ad486b97bbacacc6dc8eac4389788480c2e1a08e9c2769490170527.png)\n\nNow, suppose we pick the point $P(2,3)$ that does not lie on the perpendicular bisector. What point on our line is closest to $P?$\n\n![](images/3c/3cb1c0280c74ba3b347764b493ae438b04c6812c6bdd9498024d3f99860af012.png)\n\nTo answer this, we first create a function $S$ representing the squared distance between the point $P(2,3)$ and a general point $Q(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+{y}^{2}-6y+9 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4x-6y+13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe cannot differentiate this function because it contains two variables. However, we can eliminate one by noting that $Q$ lies on the perpendicular bisector.\n\nThe required point $Q(x,y)$ is equidistant to the points $A(-1,0)$ and $B(3,0).$ So, by writing down $(QA)^2 = (QB)^2,$ we have the following constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{(x-(-1))^{2}+(y-0)^{2}}_{(QA)^{2}} & =\\underbrace{(x-3)^{2}+(y-0)^{2}}_{(QB)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $x$ the subject in the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x+1 & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6x+9 \\\\ \\cancel{{x}^{2}}+2x+1+\\cancel{{y}^{2}} & =\\cancel{{x}^{2}}-6x+9+\\cancel{{y}^{2}} \\\\ 2x+6x & =9-1 \\\\ 8x & =8 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $S(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(y) & ={1}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4(1)-6y+13 \\\\  & =1+{y}^{2}-4-6y+13 \\\\  & ={y}^{2}-6y+10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the first derivative,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{'}(y) & =2y-6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve $S'(y)=0$ to find the stationary point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{'}(y) & =0 \\\\ 2y-6 & =0 \\\\ y & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify that this stationary point is a minimum. Taking the second derivative, we find\n\n$$\nS''(3) =2>0.\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the stationary point $y=3$ indeed minimizes the squared distance. \n\nTherefore, the required point is $\\left(1,3\\right).$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186102023 :tutorial/content "To divide powers of the same base, we simply *subtract* the exponents. This is known as the **quotient rule for exponents**. For instance, in the expression \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3}^5 \\div {\\color{blue}3}^2,\n$$\n\nboth powers have the same base $({\\color{blue}3})$. Therefore, we can simplify this expression by subtracting the exponents:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3}^5 \\div {\\color{blue}3}^2 = {\\color{blue}3}^{5-2} = {\\color{blue}3}^3.\n$$\n\n**Note:** The quotient rule for exponents is really just a quicker alternative to the following process:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{5}÷{3}^{2} & = \\\\ {3}^{3+2}÷{3}^{2} & = \\\\ {3}^{3}\\times {{{3}^{2}÷{3}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{=1} & = \\\\ {3}^{3}\\times 1 & = \\\\ {3}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To divide powers of the same base, we simply *subtract* the exponents. This is known as the **quotient rule for exponents**. For instance, in the expression \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3}^5 \\div {\\color{blue}3}^2,\n$$\n\nboth powers have the same base $({\\color{blue}3})$. Therefore, we can simplify this expression by subtracting the exponents:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3}^5 \\div {\\color{blue}3}^2 = {\\color{blue}3}^{5-2} = {\\color{blue}3}^3.\n$$\n\n**Note:** The quotient rule for exponents is really just a quicker alternative to the following process:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{5}÷{3}^{2} & = \\\\ {3}^{3+2}÷{3}^{2} & = \\\\ {3}^{3}\\times \\underbrace{{3}^{2}÷{3}^{2}}_{=1} & = \\\\ {3}^{3}\\times 1 & = \\\\ {3}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186102031 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 2y.\n$$\n\nHow can we verify whether a given function is a **solution** of this equation? For example, is $y=e^{2x}$ a solution?\n\nTo check whether a given function $y$ satisfies a differential equation, we calculate the derivative and plug the result into the equation.\n\nFor $y=e^{2x},$ the derivative is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 2e^{2x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe now substitute the above and our expression for $y,$ into the original equation and see if we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2y \\\\ {{2{e}^{2x}}_{⏟}}_{{y}^{'}} & =2\\cdot {{{e}^{2x}}_{⏟}}_{y} \\\\ 2{e}^{2x} & =2{e}^{2x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe get a true statement! Therefore, $y=e^{2x}$ is a solution to the original equation.\n\n**Note:** There are often infinitely many solutions to a given differential equation! We will learn how to solve differential equations like the one shown above in future lessons." "Suppose we have the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 2y.\n$$\n\nHow can we verify whether a given function is a **solution** of this equation? For example, is $y=e^{2x}$ a solution?\n\nTo check whether a given function $y$ satisfies a differential equation, we calculate the derivative and plug the result into the equation.\n\nFor $y=e^{2x},$ the derivative is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 2e^{2x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe now substitute the above and our expression for $y,$ into the original equation and see if we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2y \\\\ \\underbrace{2{e}^{2x}}_{{y}^{'}} & =2\\cdot \\underbrace{{e}^{2x}}_{y} \\\\ 2{e}^{2x} & =2{e}^{2x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe get a true statement! Therefore, $y=e^{2x}$ is a solution to the original equation.\n\n**Note:** There are often infinitely many solutions to a given differential equation! We will learn how to solve differential equations like the one shown above in future lessons."]
 [:db/cas 17592186106485 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](images/c4/c478dd4988aaf23348c2453e1f9fca137ddd33284cde7cb7eae1775982bd4b2e.png)\n\nThe area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0.25)+f(0.5)+f(0.75)+f(1)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0.25$ | $0.5$ | $0.75$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3.5$ | $3$ | $2.5$ | $2$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(3.5+3+2.5+2)\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =11\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =2.75\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](images/c4/c478dd4988aaf23348c2453e1f9fca137ddd33284cde7cb7eae1775982bd4b2e.png)\n\nThe area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overbrace{f(0.25)+f(0.5)+f(0.75)+f(1)}^{\\text{heights}})\\cdot \\underbrace{Δx}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0.25$ | $0.5$ | $0.75$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3.5$ | $3$ | $2.5$ | $2$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(3.5+3+2.5+2)\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =11\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =2.75\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186106541 :question/worked-solution "In this case, the expression for $w(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)={e}^{x}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{w}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({e}^{x})^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x-{e}^{x}\\cdot (\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x)^{′}}{(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x-{e}^{x}\\cdot 2{sech}^{2}⁡2x}{{tanh}^{2}⁡2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}\\overset{g(x)}{\\overset{⏞}{(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x-2{sech}^{2}⁡2x)}}}{{tanh}^{2}⁡2x}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x-2{sech}^{2}⁡2x.$" "In this case, the expression for $w(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)={e}^{x}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{w}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({e}^{x})^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x-{e}^{x}\\cdot (\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x)^{′}}{(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x-{e}^{x}\\cdot 2{sech}^{2}⁡2x}{{tanh}^{2}⁡2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}\\overbrace{(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x-2{sech}^{2}⁡2x)}^{g(x)}}{{tanh}^{2}⁡2x}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x-2{sech}^{2}⁡2x.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186107150 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we wish to find the area $\\mathcal{A}$ under the curve $y=x^2$ between the $x$-values $x=1$ and $x=4,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/35/3538fb94de9aac062e7ed65b9496085d478be911e5da2bf8480b260cec70ae4f.png)\n\n \n\nWe can approximate the area using, for instance, the area of three rectangles, each with a width equal to $1.$\n\n![](images/cb/cbb7b5af27aa5ebd3b5bbb51026017454999682dcf7e172411df79278564a5ae.png)\n\nSo, we find the area of each rectangle and then add them all together.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\overset{\\text{[math]1[/math]st rectangle}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\overset{\\text{height}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(2)}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{1}_{⏟}}_{\\text{base}}\\,]}}+\\overset{\\text{[math]2[/math]nd rectangle}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\overset{\\text{height}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(3)}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{1}_{⏟}}_{\\text{base}}\\,]}}+\\overset{\\text{[math]3[/math]rd rectangle}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\overset{\\text{height}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(4)}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{1}_{⏟}}_{\\text{base}}\\,]}} \\\\  & =[{2}^{2}\\cdot 1]+[{3}^{2}\\cdot 1]+[{4}^{2}\\cdot 1] \\\\  & =4+9+16 \\\\  & =29\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, we approximated the area using a **right Riemann sum**, which means that the top-*right* corner of each rectangle touches the curve.\n\nBecause the function is *strictly increasing* over the interval $[1,4],$ the rectangles reach *above* the function, which means our approximation is an *overestimate* of the actual area (see the picture above)." "Suppose that we wish to find the area $\\mathcal{A}$ under the curve $y=x^2$ between the $x$-values $x=1$ and $x=4,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/35/3538fb94de9aac062e7ed65b9496085d478be911e5da2bf8480b260cec70ae4f.png)\n\n \n\nWe can approximate the area using, for instance, the area of three rectangles, each with a width equal to $1.$\n\n![](images/cb/cbb7b5af27aa5ebd3b5bbb51026017454999682dcf7e172411df79278564a5ae.png)\n\nSo, we find the area of each rectangle and then add them all together.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\overbrace{[\\,\\overbrace{f(2)}^{\\text{height}}\\,\\cdot \\,\\underbrace{1}_{\\text{base}}\\,]}^{\\text{[math]1[/math]st rectangle}}+\\overbrace{[\\,\\overbrace{f(3)}^{\\text{height}}\\,\\cdot \\,\\underbrace{1}_{\\text{base}}\\,]}^{\\text{[math]2[/math]nd rectangle}}+\\overbrace{[\\,\\overbrace{f(4)}^{\\text{height}}\\,\\cdot \\,\\underbrace{1}_{\\text{base}}\\,]}^{\\text{[math]3[/math]rd rectangle}} \\\\  & =[{2}^{2}\\cdot 1]+[{3}^{2}\\cdot 1]+[{4}^{2}\\cdot 1] \\\\  & =4+9+16 \\\\  & =29\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, we approximated the area using a **right Riemann sum**, which means that the top-*right* corner of each rectangle touches the curve.\n\nBecause the function is *strictly increasing* over the interval $[1,4],$ the rectangles reach *above* the function, which means our approximation is an *overestimate* of the actual area (see the picture above)."]
 [:db/cas 17592186110400 :question/worked-solution "*Method 1*\n\nLet's start by finding the value of the missing interior angle at $B.$ We use the fact that the interior and exterior angles are supplementary:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{73}^{∘}+m∠B & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠B & ={180}^{∘}-{73}^{∘} \\\\ m∠B & ={107}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate $x$ using the fact that the interior angles of a triangle sum to $180^\\circ\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {41}^{∘}+{107}^{∘}+2x & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {148}^{∘}+2x & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 2x & ={32}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={16}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\n*Method 2*\n\nWe could rewrite the equation $m\\angle{A} +m\\angle{B} +m\\angle{C} = 180^\\circ$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{{180}^{∘}-m∠B}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Exterior angle at [math]B[/math]}}\\,\\,\\,=m∠A+m∠C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting, we can solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{{180}^{∘}-m∠B}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Exterior angle at [math]B[/math]}}\\,\\,\\, & =m∠A+m∠C \\\\ {73}^{∘} & ={41}^{∘}+2x \\\\ {32}^{∘} & =2x \\\\ {16}^{∘} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$" "*Method 1*\n\nLet's start by finding the value of the missing interior angle at $B.$ We use the fact that the interior and exterior angles are supplementary:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{73}^{∘}+m∠B & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠B & ={180}^{∘}-{73}^{∘} \\\\ m∠B & ={107}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate $x$ using the fact that the interior angles of a triangle sum to $180^\\circ\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {41}^{∘}+{107}^{∘}+2x & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {148}^{∘}+2x & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 2x & ={32}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={16}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\n*Method 2*\n\nWe could rewrite the equation $m\\angle{A} +m\\angle{B} +m\\angle{C} = 180^\\circ$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{{180}^{∘}-m∠B}_{\\text{Exterior angle at [math]B[/math]}}\\,\\,\\,=m∠A+m∠C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting, we can solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{{180}^{∘}-m∠B}_{\\text{Exterior angle at [math]B[/math]}}\\,\\,\\, & =m∠A+m∠C \\\\ {73}^{∘} & ={41}^{∘}+2x \\\\ {32}^{∘} & =2x \\\\ {16}^{∘} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186111830 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to solve the quadratic equation\n\n$$\n2x^2-3x+1=0\n$$\n\nTo do this, we need to factor the trinomial on the left-hand side. We'll need to find two numbers such that\n\n- their product is equal to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant (which is $2 \\times 1 = 2$), and\n- their sum is equal to the linear coefficient (which is $-3$).\n\nThese two numbers are $-1$ and $-2$. So, we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x^{2} - 3x + 1 &= 0 \\\\\n2x^{2}- 1x - 2x_(⏟)_(-3x) + 1 &= 0 \\\\\n(2x^{2} - x) + (-2x + 1) &= 0 \\\\\nx(2x - 1) - 1(2x - 1) &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 1)(2x - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have\n\n$$\nx-1=0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad 2x-1=0\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\nx=1 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad x=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{1}{2},1\n$$" "Suppose that we want to solve the quadratic equation\n\n$$\n2x^2-3x+1=0\n$$\n\nTo do this, we need to factor the trinomial on the left-hand side. We'll need to find two numbers such that\n\n- their product is equal to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant (which is $2 \\times 1 = 2$), and\n- their sum is equal to the linear coefficient (which is $-3$).\n\nThese two numbers are $-1$ and $-2$. So, we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x^{2} - 3x + 1 &= 0 \\\\\n2x^{2}\\underbrace{-1x - 2x}_{-3x} + 1 &= 0 \\\\\n(2x^{2} - x) + (-2x + 1) &= 0 \\\\\nx(2x - 1) - 1(2x - 1) &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 1)(2x - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have\n\n$$\nx-1=0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad 2x-1=0\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\nx=1 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad x=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{1}{2},1\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186111836 :tutorial/content "When two angles share a common side, they are called **adjacent** angles. When two angles are adjacent, we can add their measures to find the measure of the larger angle. \n\nFor example, consider the diagram below.\n\n![](images/f5/f562f3505de0dd2345b29e2baabaf51a7885cbccb5d95e6201b8bc9aec1b1986.png)\n\nThe angles $\\angle PSR$ and $\\angle RST$ are adjacent because they both have the side $\\overrightarrow{SR}$ in common.\n\nSince the angles $\\angle PSR$ and $\\angle RST$ are adjacent, we can find the measure of the combined angle $\\angle PST$ by adding the two angles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠PST & ={{m∠PSR}_{⏟}}_{\\text{orange angle}}+{{m∠RST}_{⏟}}_{\\text{blue angle}} \\\\  & ={95}^{∘}+{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={140}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "When two angles share a common side, they are called **adjacent** angles. When two angles are adjacent, we can add their measures to find the measure of the larger angle. \n\nFor example, consider the diagram below.\n\n![](images/f5/f562f3505de0dd2345b29e2baabaf51a7885cbccb5d95e6201b8bc9aec1b1986.png)\n\nThe angles $\\angle PSR$ and $\\angle RST$ are adjacent because they both have the side $\\overrightarrow{SR}$ in common.\n\nSince the angles $\\angle PSR$ and $\\angle RST$ are adjacent, we can find the measure of the combined angle $\\angle PST$ by adding the two angles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠PST & =\\underbrace{m∠PSR}_{\\text{orange angle}}+\\underbrace{m∠RST}_{\\text{blue angle}} \\\\  & ={95}^{∘}+{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={140}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186111889 :tutorial/content "Let's recall the multinomial theorem:\n\n> *For any positive integer $n$, the terms of $(a+b+c)^n$ can be written as*\n> $\\binom{n}{k_1,\\,k_2,\\,k_3} a^{k_1} b^{k_2} c^{k_3}$,\n> *where $k_1 + k_2 + k_3 = n$ and the general formula for the multinomial coefficient is*\n> $\\binom{n}{k_1,\\,k_2,\\,k_3} = \\dfrac{n!}{k_1!\\, k_2! \\, k_3!}$.\n\nFor example, using the multinomial theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle ( a + b + c {)}^{2} & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{2 , \\hspace{0.167em} 0 , \\hspace{0.167em} 0} \\right) {a}^{2} {b}^{0} {c}^{0} + \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{0 , \\hspace{0.167em} 2 , \\hspace{0.167em} 0} \\right) {a}^{0} {b}^{2} {c}^{0} + \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{0 , \\hspace{0.167em} 0 , \\hspace{0.167em} 2} \\right) {a}^{0} {b}^{0} {c}^{2} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\phantom{==} + \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{1 , \\hspace{0.167em} 1 , \\hspace{0.167em} 0} \\right) {a}^{1} {b}^{1} {c}^{0} + \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{1 , \\hspace{0.167em} 0 , \\hspace{0.167em} 1} \\right) {a}^{1} {b}^{0} {c}^{1} + \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{0 , \\hspace{0.167em} 1 , \\hspace{0.167em} 1} \\right) {a}^{0} {b}^{1} {c}^{1} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 !}{2 ! \\hspace{0.167em} 0 ! \\hspace{0.167em} 0 !}} {a}^{2} + {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 !}{0 ! \\hspace{0.167em} 2 ! \\hspace{0.167em} 0 !}} {b}^{2} + {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 !}{0 ! \\hspace{0.167em} 0 ! \\hspace{0.167em} 2 !}} {c}^{2} + {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 !}{1 ! \\hspace{0.167em} 1 ! \\hspace{0.167em} 0 !}} a b + {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 !}{1 ! \\hspace{0.167em} 0 ! \\hspace{0.167em} 1 !}} a c + {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 !}{0 ! \\hspace{0.167em} 1 ! \\hspace{0.167em} 1 !}} b c \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {a}^{2} + {b}^{2} + {c}^{2} + 2 a b + 2 a c + 2 b c .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have $6$ terms in the above expansion:\n\n$$\n(a + b + c)^{2} = a^{2} + b^{2} + c^{2} + 2ab + 2ac + 2bc_(⏟)_(6terms)\n$$\n\nIn general, the number of distinct terms in the multinomial expansion with $t$ variables raised to the $n$th power is given by\n$\\binom{n+t-1}{t-1}$.\n\nIn the case of $(a+b+c)^2$, we have a multinomial expansion with $t=3$ variables raised to the $n=2$nd power. Therefore, the number of distinct terms in the expansion of $(a+b+c)^2$ is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2 + 3 - 1}{3 - 1} \\right) & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2} \\right) = 6 \\hspace{0.167em} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nIndeed, this matches up with what we saw in our expansion.\n\nNote that one of the variables can actually be a nonzero constant and the number of terms will still be the same. For example, we could take $c = 1$ above and we'd still get an expansion with $6$ terms:\n\n$$\n(a + b + 1)^{2} = a^{2} + b^{2} + 1 + 2ab + 2a + 2b_(⏟)_(6terms)\n$$\n\nHowever, at most one variable can be a nonzero constant; the rest must be distinct variables." "Let's recall the multinomial theorem:\n\n> *For any positive integer $n$, the terms of $(a+b+c)^n$ can be written as*\n> $\\binom{n}{k_1,\\,k_2,\\,k_3} a^{k_1} b^{k_2} c^{k_3}$,\n> *where $k_1 + k_2 + k_3 = n$ and the general formula for the multinomial coefficient is*\n> $\\binom{n}{k_1,\\,k_2,\\,k_3} = \\dfrac{n!}{k_1!\\, k_2! \\, k_3!}$.\n\nFor example, using the multinomial theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle ( a + b + c {)}^{2} & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{2 , \\hspace{0.167em} 0 , \\hspace{0.167em} 0} \\right) {a}^{2} {b}^{0} {c}^{0} + \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{0 , \\hspace{0.167em} 2 , \\hspace{0.167em} 0} \\right) {a}^{0} {b}^{2} {c}^{0} + \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{0 , \\hspace{0.167em} 0 , \\hspace{0.167em} 2} \\right) {a}^{0} {b}^{0} {c}^{2} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\phantom{==} + \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{1 , \\hspace{0.167em} 1 , \\hspace{0.167em} 0} \\right) {a}^{1} {b}^{1} {c}^{0} + \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{1 , \\hspace{0.167em} 0 , \\hspace{0.167em} 1} \\right) {a}^{1} {b}^{0} {c}^{1} + \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{0 , \\hspace{0.167em} 1 , \\hspace{0.167em} 1} \\right) {a}^{0} {b}^{1} {c}^{1} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 !}{2 ! \\hspace{0.167em} 0 ! \\hspace{0.167em} 0 !}} {a}^{2} + {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 !}{0 ! \\hspace{0.167em} 2 ! \\hspace{0.167em} 0 !}} {b}^{2} + {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 !}{0 ! \\hspace{0.167em} 0 ! \\hspace{0.167em} 2 !}} {c}^{2} + {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 !}{1 ! \\hspace{0.167em} 1 ! \\hspace{0.167em} 0 !}} a b + {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 !}{1 ! \\hspace{0.167em} 0 ! \\hspace{0.167em} 1 !}} a c + {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 !}{0 ! \\hspace{0.167em} 1 ! \\hspace{0.167em} 1 !}} b c \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {a}^{2} + {b}^{2} + {c}^{2} + 2 a b + 2 a c + 2 b c .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have $6$ terms in the above expansion:\n\n$$\n(a + b + c)^{2} = \\underbrace{a^{2} + b^{2} + c^{2} + 2ab + 2ac + 2bc}_{6\\text{ terms}}\n$$\n\nIn general, the number of distinct terms in the multinomial expansion with $t$ variables raised to the $n$th power is given by\n$\\binom{n+t-1}{t-1}$.\n\nIn the case of $(a+b+c)^2$, we have a multinomial expansion with $t=3$ variables raised to the $n=2$nd power. Therefore, the number of distinct terms in the expansion of $(a+b+c)^2$ is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2 + 3 - 1}{3 - 1} \\right) & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2} \\right) = 6 \\hspace{0.167em} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nIndeed, this matches up with what we saw in our expansion.\n\nNote that one of the variables can actually be a nonzero constant and the number of terms will still be the same. For example, we could take $c = 1$ above and we'd still get an expansion with $6$ terms:\n\n$$\n(a + b + 1)^{2} = \\underbrace{a^{2} + b^{2} + 1 + 2ab + 2a + 2b}_{6\\text{ terms}}\n$$\n\nHowever, at most one variable can be a nonzero constant; the rest must be distinct variables."]
 [:db/cas 17592186111899 :tutorial/content "We can extend our rule relating sums of adjacent angles to more than two angles.\n\nFor example, let's consider the angles shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/32/3264d0ab1a40b02915a737dad57e9f26820193d9bdc6b42431ec1ab6508cc523.png)\n\nAccording to our rule, the measure of the reflex angle $\\angle QST$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠QST & ={{m∠QSP}_{⏟}}_{\\text{green angle}}+{{m∠PSR}_{⏟}}_{\\text{orange angle}}+{{m∠RST}_{⏟}}_{\\text{blue angle}} \\\\  & ={100}^{∘}+{95}^{∘}+{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={240}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can also use this rule to determine the size of a smaller angle when we know a larger one. Let's see an example." "We can extend our rule relating sums of adjacent angles to more than two angles.\n\nFor example, let's consider the angles shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/32/3264d0ab1a40b02915a737dad57e9f26820193d9bdc6b42431ec1ab6508cc523.png)\n\nAccording to our rule, the measure of the reflex angle $\\angle QST$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠QST & =\\underbrace{m∠QSP}_{\\text{green angle}}+\\underbrace{m∠PSR}_{\\text{orange angle}}+\\underbrace{m∠RST}_{\\text{blue angle}} \\\\  & ={100}^{∘}+{95}^{∘}+{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={240}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can also use this rule to determine the size of a smaller angle when we know a larger one. Let's see an example."]
 [:db/cas 17592186114975 :question/worked-solution "Using the constant multiple rule and the addition rule for limits, and the fact that both functions are continuous, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}[2f(t)-g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 1}2f(t)+{lim}_{t\\to 1}[-g(t)] \\\\  & =2{lim}_{t\\to 1}f(t)-{lim}_{t\\to 1}g(t) \\\\  & =2f(1)-g(1) \\\\  & =2{{⟨1,1,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{f(1)}-g(1) \\\\  & =⟨2,2,-2⟩-g(1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we are told that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{t\\rightarrow 1} \\big[ 2\\mathbf f(t)-\\mathbf g(t) \\big] = \\left\\langle 2, 1, -1 \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following equation which we can solve for $\\mathbf{g} (1)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}[2f(t)-g(t)] & =⟨2,1,-1⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,2,-2⟩-g(1) & =⟨2,1,-1⟩ \\\\ g(1) & =⟨2,2,-2⟩-⟨2,1,-1⟩ \\\\ g(1) & =⟨0,1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Using the constant multiple rule and the addition rule for limits, and the fact that both functions are continuous, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}[2f(t)-g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 1}2f(t)+{lim}_{t\\to 1}[-g(t)] \\\\  & =2{lim}_{t\\to 1}f(t)-{lim}_{t\\to 1}g(t) \\\\  & =2f(1)-g(1) \\\\  & =2\\underbrace{⟨1,1,-1⟩}_{f(1)}-g(1) \\\\  & =⟨2,2,-2⟩-g(1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we are told that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{t\\rightarrow 1} \\big[ 2\\mathbf f(t)-\\mathbf g(t) \\big] = \\left\\langle 2, 1, -1 \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following equation which we can solve for $\\mathbf{g} (1)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}[2f(t)-g(t)] & =⟨2,1,-1⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,2,-2⟩-g(1) & =⟨2,1,-1⟩ \\\\ g(1) & =⟨2,2,-2⟩-⟨2,1,-1⟩ \\\\ g(1) & =⟨0,1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186115804 :question/worked-solution "Using the constant multiple, and the addition rule for limits, and the fact that both functions are continuous, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 2}[f(t)+2g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 2}f(t)+{lim}_{t\\to 2}2g(t) \\\\  & ={lim}_{t\\to 2}f(t)+2{lim}_{t\\to 2}g(t) \\\\  & =f(2)+2g(2) \\\\  & ={{⟨1,1,2⟩}_{⏟}}_{f(2)}+2g(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we are told that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 2}[f(t)+2g(t)]=⟨-1,7,0⟩\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following equation which we can solve for $g(2):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 2}[f(t)+2g(t)] & =⟨-1,7,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1,2⟩+2g(2) & =⟨-1,7,0⟩ \\\\ 2g(2) & =⟨-1,7,0⟩-⟨1,1,2⟩ \\\\ 2g(2) & =⟨-2,6,-2⟩ \\\\ g(2) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ⟨-2,6,-2⟩ \\\\ g(2) & =⟨-1,3,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Using the constant multiple, and the addition rule for limits, and the fact that both functions are continuous, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 2}[f(t)+2g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 2}f(t)+{lim}_{t\\to 2}2g(t) \\\\  & ={lim}_{t\\to 2}f(t)+2{lim}_{t\\to 2}g(t) \\\\  & =f(2)+2g(2) \\\\  & =\\underbrace{⟨1,1,2⟩}_{f(2)}+2g(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we are told that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 2}[f(t)+2g(t)]=⟨-1,7,0⟩\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following equation which we can solve for $g(2):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 2}[f(t)+2g(t)] & =⟨-1,7,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1,2⟩+2g(2) & =⟨-1,7,0⟩ \\\\ 2g(2) & =⟨-1,7,0⟩-⟨1,1,2⟩ \\\\ 2g(2) & =⟨-2,6,-2⟩ \\\\ g(2) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ⟨-2,6,-2⟩ \\\\ g(2) & =⟨-1,3,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186116437 :tutorial/content "To find the limit of a vector function, we compute the limit of each component individually. So, if $\\mathbf f (t) = x(t) \\ \\mathbf {i} + y(t) \\ \\mathbf {j} +z(t) \\ \\mathbf {k}$ is our vector function, then the limit is computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{t\\to a} \\mathbf f (t) = \\bigg[ \\lim_{t \\to a} x(t) \\bigg] \\mathbf {i} + \\bigg[ \\lim_{t \\to a}y(t) \\bigg] \\mathbf {j} +\\bigg[ \\lim_{t \\to a}z(t) \\bigg] \\mathbf {k}\n$$\n\nThe limit on the left exists *if and only if* all the limits on the right exist too.\n\nFor example, suppose we are given the vector function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf f (t) = \\left( 5+t \\right) \\ \\mathbf {i} +  (t^{2}-1) \\ \\mathbf {j} + \\sin{t} \\ \\mathbf {k},\n$$\n\nand we want to compute the limit $\\lim\\limits_{t \\to 0} \\mathbf f (t).$ To do this, we find the limit of each component function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t) & =[\\,{{{lim}_{t\\to 0}(5+t)}_{⏟}}_{=\\,5}\\,]i+[\\,{{{lim}_{t\\to 0}({t}^{2}-1)\\,}_{⏟}}_{=\\,-1}]j+[\\,{{{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,}_{⏟}}_{=\\,0}]k \\\\  & =5\\text{ }i+(-1)\\text{ }j+0\\text{ }k \\\\  & =⟨5,\\,-1,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find the limit of a vector function, we compute the limit of each component individually. So, if $\\mathbf f (t) = x(t) \\ \\mathbf {i} + y(t) \\ \\mathbf {j} +z(t) \\ \\mathbf {k}$ is our vector function, then the limit is computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{t\\to a} \\mathbf f (t) = \\bigg[ \\lim_{t \\to a} x(t) \\bigg] \\mathbf {i} + \\bigg[ \\lim_{t \\to a}y(t) \\bigg] \\mathbf {j} +\\bigg[ \\lim_{t \\to a}z(t) \\bigg] \\mathbf {k}\n$$\n\nThe limit on the left exists *if and only if* all the limits on the right exist too.\n\nFor example, suppose we are given the vector function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf f (t) = \\left( 5+t \\right) \\ \\mathbf {i} +  (t^{2}-1) \\ \\mathbf {j} + \\sin{t} \\ \\mathbf {k},\n$$\n\nand we want to compute the limit $\\lim\\limits_{t \\to 0} \\mathbf f (t).$ To do this, we find the limit of each component function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t) & =[\\,\\underbrace{{lim}_{t\\to 0}(5+t)}_{=\\,5}\\,]i+[\\,\\underbrace{{lim}_{t\\to 0}({t}^{2}-1)\\,}_{=\\,-1}]j+[\\,\\underbrace{{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,}_{=\\,0}]k \\\\  & =5\\text{ }i+(-1)\\text{ }j+0\\text{ }k \\\\  & =⟨5,\\,-1,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186116474 :tutorial/content "To see why the derivative of $\\sec x$ is $\\sec x \\tan x,$ we start by remembering that\n\n$$\n\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}.\n$$\n\nIf we now differentiate the above relation using the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡x]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & ={{\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}_{⏟}}_{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}{{\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}_{⏟}}_{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the same method to find the derivative of the other two reciprocal trigonometric functions,\n\n$$\n\\csc x = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin x} \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad \\cot x = \\dfrac{1}{\\tan x}.\n$$" "To see why the derivative of $\\sec x$ is $\\sec x \\tan x,$ we start by remembering that\n\n$$\n\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}.\n$$\n\nIf we now differentiate the above relation using the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡x]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}_{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\underbrace{\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}_{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the same method to find the derivative of the other two reciprocal trigonometric functions,\n\n$$\n\\csc x = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin x} \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad \\cot x = \\dfrac{1}{\\tan x}.\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186116531 :tutorial/content "Recall the definition of variance:\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 =  \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2\n$$\n\nYou may wonder why the variance uses the *squared* difference, $(x_i - \\overline{x})^2,$ instead of just using the non-squared difference $x_i - \\overline{x}.$\n\nIt turns out that if we add up all the non-squared differences $x_i - \\overline{x},$ then the negatives will cancel out the positives, and the result will always come out to zero no matter how spread out or concentrated the distribution is.\n\nFor example, consider the dataset\n\n$$\n-2, \\: -2, \\: 0, \\: 2, \\: 2.\n$$\n\nWe have $\\overline{x}=0,$ and if we compute $\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x}),$ we get a result of $0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}) & =\\frac{1}{5}[(-2-0)+(-2-0)+(0-0)+(2-0)+(2-0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[-2-2+0+2+2] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis will happen every time, because the quantity $\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})$ always simplifies to $0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}) & ={{\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{―}{x}}-\\frac{1}{n}{{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\overset{―}{x}}_{⏟}}_{n\\overset{―}{x}} \\\\  & =\\overset{―}{x}-\\frac{1}{n}\\cdot n\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =\\overset{―}{x}-\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need to perform some operation to the distances $x_i - \\overline{x}$ to make them non-negative. Squaring the distances, $(x_i - \\overline{x})^2,$ will accomplish this.\n\nYou might also wonder why we square the differences instead of taking the absolute value. The reason is that the sum of absolute values of the distances isn't guaranteed to increase if the data points are more spread out.\n\nFor example, consider the data set\n\n\\[\n-3, \\: -1, \\: 0, \\: 1, \\: 3,\n\\]\n\nwhich is more spread out than the earlier data set.\n\nEven though this data set is more spread out, the sum of absolute values of the distances is the same, which is not what we want. We want the variance to increase whenever the data set becomes more spread out.\n\n\\[\\begin{align*}\n-2, \\: -2, \\: 0, \\: 2, \\: 2 &\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{1}{5}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^5 \\left| x_i - \\overline{x} \\right| = \\dfrac{1}{5} \\left( 2+2+0+2+2 \\right) = \\dfrac{8}{5} \\quad \\color{red}\\times \\\\[5pt]\n-3, \\: -1, \\: 0, \\: 1, \\: 3 &\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{1}{5}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^5 \\left| x_i - \\overline{x} \\right| = \\dfrac{1}{5} \\left( 3+1+0+1+3 \\right) = \\dfrac{8}{5} \\quad \\color{red}\\times \\\\[5pt]\n\\end{align*}\\]\n\nIf we square the distances, though, we'll get a quantity that increases whenever the data set becomes more spread out.\n\n\\[\\begin{align*}\n-2, \\: -2, \\: 0, \\: 2, \\: 2 &\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{1}{5}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^5 \\left( x_i - \\overline{x} \\right)^2 = \\dfrac{1}{5} \\left( 2^2+2^2+0^2+2^2+2^2 \\right) = \\dfrac{16}{5} \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark \\\\[5pt]\n-3, \\: -1, \\: 0, \\: 1, \\: 3 &\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{1}{5}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^5 \\left( x_i - \\overline{x} \\right)^2 = \\dfrac{1}{5} \\left( 3^2+1^2+0^2+1^2+3^2 \\right) = \\dfrac{20}{5} \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark \\\\[5pt]\n\\end{align*}\\]" "Recall the definition of variance:\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 =  \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2\n$$\n\nYou may wonder why the variance uses the *squared* difference, $(x_i - \\overline{x})^2,$ instead of just using the non-squared difference $x_i - \\overline{x}.$\n\nIt turns out that if we add up all the non-squared differences $x_i - \\overline{x},$ then the negatives will cancel out the positives, and the result will always come out to zero no matter how spread out or concentrated the distribution is.\n\nFor example, consider the dataset\n\n$$\n-2, \\: -2, \\: 0, \\: 2, \\: 2.\n$$\n\nWe have $\\overline{x}=0,$ and if we compute $\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x}),$ we get a result of $0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}) & =\\frac{1}{5}[(-2-0)+(-2-0)+(0-0)+(2-0)+(2-0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[-2-2+0+2+2] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis will happen every time, because the quantity $\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})$ always simplifies to $0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}) & =\\underbrace{\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}}_{\\overset{―}{x}}-\\frac{1}{n}\\underbrace{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\overset{―}{x}}_{n\\overset{―}{x}} \\\\  & =\\overset{―}{x}-\\frac{1}{n}\\cdot n\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =\\overset{―}{x}-\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need to perform some operation to the distances $x_i - \\overline{x}$ to make them non-negative. Squaring the distances, $(x_i - \\overline{x})^2,$ will accomplish this.\n\nYou might also wonder why we square the differences instead of taking the absolute value. The reason is that the sum of absolute values of the distances isn't guaranteed to increase if the data points are more spread out.\n\nFor example, consider the data set\n\n\\[\n-3, \\: -1, \\: 0, \\: 1, \\: 3,\n\\]\n\nwhich is more spread out than the earlier data set.\n\nEven though this data set is more spread out, the sum of absolute values of the distances is the same, which is not what we want. We want the variance to increase whenever the data set becomes more spread out.\n\n\\[\\begin{align*}\n-2, \\: -2, \\: 0, \\: 2, \\: 2 &\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{1}{5}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^5 \\left| x_i - \\overline{x} \\right| = \\dfrac{1}{5} \\left( 2+2+0+2+2 \\right) = \\dfrac{8}{5} \\quad \\color{red}\\times \\\\[5pt]\n-3, \\: -1, \\: 0, \\: 1, \\: 3 &\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{1}{5}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^5 \\left| x_i - \\overline{x} \\right| = \\dfrac{1}{5} \\left( 3+1+0+1+3 \\right) = \\dfrac{8}{5} \\quad \\color{red}\\times \\\\[5pt]\n\\end{align*}\\]\n\nIf we square the distances, though, we'll get a quantity that increases whenever the data set becomes more spread out.\n\n\\[\\begin{align*}\n-2, \\: -2, \\: 0, \\: 2, \\: 2 &\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{1}{5}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^5 \\left( x_i - \\overline{x} \\right)^2 = \\dfrac{1}{5} \\left( 2^2+2^2+0^2+2^2+2^2 \\right) = \\dfrac{16}{5} \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark \\\\[5pt]\n-3, \\: -1, \\: 0, \\: 1, \\: 3 &\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{1}{5}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^5 \\left( x_i - \\overline{x} \\right)^2 = \\dfrac{1}{5} \\left( 3^2+1^2+0^2+1^2+3^2 \\right) = \\dfrac{20}{5} \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark \\\\[5pt]\n\\end{align*}\\]"]
 [:db/cas 17592186116538 :tutorial/content "Consider the $3 \\times 4$ matrix $M,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can subdivide $M$ using horizontal and vertical lines, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen partitioned in this way, we say that the matrix $M$ consists of four **blocks**, where each block is itself a matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{{M}_{11}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}} & \\,\\,\\overset{{M}_{12}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\ {{\\begin{bmatrix}4\\, & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{M}_{21}}\\, & \\,\\,\\,{{\\begin{bmatrix}0\\, & -5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{M}_{22}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nQuite often, a matrix can be partitioned in various ways. Another partition of $M$ is shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUnder this partition, we obtain different blocks." "Consider the $3 \\times 4$ matrix $M,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can subdivide $M$ using horizontal and vertical lines, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen partitioned in this way, we say that the matrix $M$ consists of four **blocks**, where each block is itself a matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}^{{M}_{11}} & \\,\\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}^{{M}_{12}} \\\\ \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}4\\, & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{M}_{21}}\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0\\, & -5\\end{bmatrix}}_{{M}_{22}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nQuite often, a matrix can be partitioned in various ways. Another partition of $M$ is shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUnder this partition, we obtain different blocks."]
 [:db/cas 17592186119594 :question/worked-solution "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a & b & c+1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}a+0 & b+0 & c+1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants and expanding the second determinant across the $1$st row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a+0 & b+0 & c+1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & ={{|\\begin{aligned}a & b & c \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|}_{⏟}}_{-10}+|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-10+1(-1)^{1+3}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-10-1 \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a & b & c+1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}a+0 & b+0 & c+1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants and expanding the second determinant across the $1$st row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a+0 & b+0 & c+1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =\\underbrace{|\\begin{aligned}a & b & c \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|}_{-10}+|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-10+1(-1)^{1+3}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-10-1 \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186119603 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ -7 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+1\\cdot {R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ -7 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+1\\cdot {R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186119604 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 12 & 9\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 12 & 9\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{U}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186119605 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186119606 :question/worked-solution "Applying $R_2:=R_2 + {\\color{red}1} \\cdot R_1$ and then $R_3:=R_3+ {\\color{red}2} \\cdot R_1$ is equivalent to multiplying $F$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n![](images/a9/a99ebe6a4021d9124320439383f1e5351f134a321a353c2dafaa7bbf8555aae7.svg)\n\nNow, since $E_2E_1F=U,$ we can invert $E_2$ and $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}F & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}F & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ F & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ F & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ F & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ F & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Applying $R_2:=R_2 + {\\color{red}1} \\cdot R_1$ and then $R_3:=R_3+ {\\color{red}2} \\cdot R_1$ is equivalent to multiplying $F$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n![](images/a9/a99ebe6a4021d9124320439383f1e5351f134a321a353c2dafaa7bbf8555aae7.svg)\n\nNow, since $E_2E_1F=U,$ we can invert $E_2$ and $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}F & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}F & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ F & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ F & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ F & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ F & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186119607 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 11 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 16\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{5}{4}){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 16\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_2E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_2$ and $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{5}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{5}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 11 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 16\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{5}{4}){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 16\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_2E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_2$ and $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{5}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{5}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186119610 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $U\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{y},$ we obtain\n\n![](images/a1/a185a6fd91c78ed3e291b3ac8b99f9430290e5d8c1060a7d97317efc230aeb40.svg)\n\nNow, we solve the two systems in turn.\n\n- First, we solve $L\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{b}.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n![](images/1e/1e780d384ca1fd957d742bbd047399c558ab016d187ce4cf71682546673aba35.svg)\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=6 \\\\ -2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=-8.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is $y_1=6$ and $y_2=4.$ Therefore, \n![](images/58/58da62903247cf6dfdf33be86293c181ef5184048ca7cd6c921360e5d202f353.svg)\n\n- Next, we solve $U\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{y}.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n![](images/e0/e0563dde4944ecbe2ccb98bc91246fa18c7a808df96ab77f9f8efb5510084677.svg)\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}7{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} &=6 \\\\ -2{x}_{2} &=4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is $x_2=-2$ and $x_1=2.$ Therefore, \n![](images/a8/a84088cff2a3589652ef7cabf09e3d8429fad7797ea20d44a416f85dcc7f9b65.svg)\n\nFinally, $x_1 \\cdot y_2 = 2 \\cdot 4 = 8.$" "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,\\underbrace{(LU)}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $U\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{y},$ we obtain\n\n![](images/a1/a185a6fd91c78ed3e291b3ac8b99f9430290e5d8c1060a7d97317efc230aeb40.svg)\n\nNow, we solve the two systems in turn.\n\n- First, we solve $L\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{b}.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n![](images/1e/1e780d384ca1fd957d742bbd047399c558ab016d187ce4cf71682546673aba35.svg)\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=6 \\\\ -2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=-8.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is $y_1=6$ and $y_2=4.$ Therefore, \n![](images/58/58da62903247cf6dfdf33be86293c181ef5184048ca7cd6c921360e5d202f353.svg)\n\n- Next, we solve $U\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{y}.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n![](images/e0/e0563dde4944ecbe2ccb98bc91246fa18c7a808df96ab77f9f8efb5510084677.svg)\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}7{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} &=6 \\\\ -2{x}_{2} &=4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is $x_2=-2$ and $x_1=2.$ Therefore, \n![](images/a8/a84088cff2a3589652ef7cabf09e3d8429fad7797ea20d44a416f85dcc7f9b65.svg)\n\nFinally, $x_1 \\cdot y_2 = 2 \\cdot 4 = 8.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186119611 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $U\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{y},$ we obtain\n\n![](images/60/605f569e0b4a54d2ca68d7b3330a2a0ebb06dcaba2b7efa911f58dbffd796fb0.svg)\n\nWe now solve $L\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{b}.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n![](images/d2/d259a6740aa32e0e577756375fd85589b815232314511c74dc434a88d0146a3b.svg)\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=2 \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=4 \\\\ {y}_{1}-2{y}_{2}+{y}_{3} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the solution to this system using forward substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =2, \\\\ {y}_{2} & =4-{y}_{1} \\\\  & =4-(2) \\\\  & =2, \\\\ {y}_{3} & =1-{y}_{1}+2{y}_{2} \\\\  & =1-(2)+2(2) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n![](images/c5/c5198aa83d3976d992b200f63941261f952282fedb241db780c20c12c97e3ba9.svg)" "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,\\underbrace{(LU)}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $U\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{y},$ we obtain\n\n![](images/60/605f569e0b4a54d2ca68d7b3330a2a0ebb06dcaba2b7efa911f58dbffd796fb0.svg)\n\nWe now solve $L\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{b}.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n![](images/d2/d259a6740aa32e0e577756375fd85589b815232314511c74dc434a88d0146a3b.svg)\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=2 \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=4 \\\\ {y}_{1}-2{y}_{2}+{y}_{3} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the solution to this system using forward substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =2, \\\\ {y}_{2} & =4-{y}_{1} \\\\  & =4-(2) \\\\  & =2, \\\\ {y}_{3} & =1-{y}_{1}+2{y}_{2} \\\\  & =1-(2)+2(2) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n![](images/c5/c5198aa83d3976d992b200f63941261f952282fedb241db780c20c12c97e3ba9.svg)"]
 [:db/cas 17592186119631 :question/worked-solution "We know that any vector in  $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ has the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the coordinates relative to the basis $\\,\\mathcal{B}.$ Since we are told that ![](images/de/de91d9b4cd4eabed0a27696d7bf0512c19f6c66eb3c81ab6dc6a73fc0a1c15a5.svg) we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={{(-1)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{{(\\,3\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We know that any vector in  $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ has the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the coordinates relative to the basis $\\,\\mathcal{B}.$ Since we are told that ![](images/de/de91d9b4cd4eabed0a27696d7bf0512c19f6c66eb3c81ab6dc6a73fc0a1c15a5.svg) we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\underbrace{(-1)}_{{x}_{1}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\underbrace{(\\,3\\,)}_{{x}_{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186119706 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $U\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{y},$ we obtain\n\n![](images/42/4298fe244fd6c66c7623ad508c97b96464321387506aa1e4be5e2399a5c9a862.svg)\n\nWe now solve $L\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{b}.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 15 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 66\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=5 \\\\ 15{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=66.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is $y_1=5$ and $y_2=-9.$ \n\nTherefore, ![](images/a8/a86dfd0e07b8c415e526764cf9f7ba8f020609bf7b13cc90536deba868ec4aa6.svg)" "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,\\underbrace{(LU)}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $U\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{y},$ we obtain\n\n![](images/42/4298fe244fd6c66c7623ad508c97b96464321387506aa1e4be5e2399a5c9a862.svg)\n\nWe now solve $L\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{b}.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 15 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 66\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=5 \\\\ 15{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=66.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is $y_1=5$ and $y_2=-9.$ \n\nTherefore, ![](images/a8/a86dfd0e07b8c415e526764cf9f7ba8f020609bf7b13cc90536deba868ec4aa6.svg)"]
 [:db/cas 17592186120080 :question/problem "Consider the LU decomposition of the matrix $A$ and the vector $b,$ given by\n\n$$\nA={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $⟨{x}_{1},{x}_{2}⟩$ and $⟨{y}_{1},{y}_{2}⟩$ are the solutions to the systems $Ax=b$ and $Ly=b$ respectively, what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {y}_{1}?$" "Consider the LU decomposition of the matrix $A$ and the vector $b,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{U},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $⟨{x}_{1},{x}_{2}⟩$ and $⟨{y}_{1},{y}_{2}⟩$ are the solutions to the systems $Ax=b$ and $Ly=b$ respectively, what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {y}_{1}?$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186120080 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly &=b \\\\ Ux &=y.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the two systems in turn.\n\n- First, we solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=b=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=-1 \\\\ -{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${y}_{1}=-1$ and ${y}_{2}=2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\ny=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $Ux=y.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=y=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=-1 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${x}_{2}=2$ and ${x}_{1}=-1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {y}_{1}=(-1)\\cdot (-1)=1.$" "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,\\underbrace{(LU)}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL\\underbrace{(Ux)}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly &=b \\\\ Ux &=y.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the two systems in turn.\n\n- First, we solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=b=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=-1 \\\\ -{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${y}_{1}=-1$ and ${y}_{2}=2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\ny=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $Ux=y.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=y=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=-1 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${x}_{2}=2$ and ${x}_{1}=-1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {y}_{1}=(-1)\\cdot (-1)=1.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186120182 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=3,{u}_{22}=-1,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{22}=-3.$" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=3,{u}_{22}=-1,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{22}=-3.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186120183 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
