ligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-2,$ ${u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=-2.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186120187 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -16 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & \\,{R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -16 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & \\,{R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186120188 :question/worked-solution "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $A,$ we write $A=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nA=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the right is not an upper triangular matrix, as it has a non-zero entry below the diagonal.\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although we have a product of an upper triangular matrix on the left and a unit lower triangular matrix on the right, the order of multiplication is not correct (instead, we need a unit lower triangular matrix on the left and an upper triangular matrix on the right).\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The third product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $A,$ we write $A=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nA=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{L}\\cdot \\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}}^{U}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the right is not an upper triangular matrix, as it has a non-zero entry below the diagonal.\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although we have a product of an upper triangular matrix on the left and a unit lower triangular matrix on the right, the order of multiplication is not correct (instead, we need a unit lower triangular matrix on the left and an upper triangular matrix on the right).\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The third product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."]
 [:db/cas 17592186120190 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 9 & -8\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 9 & -8\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{U}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186120193 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 5 & 10 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-5){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{31}=5,{u}_{33}=-6,$ and ${l}_{31}\\cdot {u}_{33}=-30.$" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 5 & 10 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-5){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{L}\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{31}=5,{u}_{33}=-6,$ and ${l}_{31}\\cdot {u}_{33}=-30.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186120200 :question/worked-solution "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+4\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $C$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot C=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}C=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}C & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}C & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ C & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ C & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+4\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $C$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot C=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}C=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}C & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}C & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ C & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ C & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
