 [:db/cas 17592186120247 :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vector of $Q$ is $q=⟨-2,2,0⟩.$ Converting the parametric equations of the line to vector form, we get\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=3-4t \\\\ y &=1+6t \\\\ z &=-1+2t\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,⟨x,y,z⟩=⟨3,1,-1⟩+t\\text{ }{{⟨-4,6,2⟩}_{⏟}}_{v}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe direction vector of the line is $v=⟨-4,6,2⟩,$ so a normal vector to the plane is \n\n$$\nn=v=⟨-4,6,2⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-4,6,2⟩ & =⟨-2,2,0⟩\\cdot ⟨-4,6,2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-4,6,2⟩ & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Note that the position vector of $Q$ is $q=⟨-2,2,0⟩.$ Converting the parametric equations of the line to vector form, we get\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=3-4t \\\\ y &=1+6t \\\\ z &=-1+2t\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,⟨x,y,z⟩=⟨3,1,-1⟩+t\\text{ }\\underbrace{⟨-4,6,2⟩}_{v}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe direction vector of the line is $v=⟨-4,6,2⟩,$ so a normal vector to the plane is \n\n$$\nn=v=⟨-4,6,2⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-4,6,2⟩ & =⟨-2,2,0⟩\\cdot ⟨-4,6,2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-4,6,2⟩ & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186120379 :question/worked-solution "We know that any vector in  $\\text{Span}\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ has the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the coordinates relative to the basis $\\,B.$ Since we are told that $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={{(\\,-3\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+{{(\\,5\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}25 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ 21 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We know that any vector in  $\\text{Span}\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ has the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the coordinates relative to the basis $\\,B.$ Since we are told that $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\underbrace{(\\,-3\\,)}_{{x}_{1}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+\\underbrace{(\\,5\\,)}_{{x}_{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}25 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ 21 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186120435 :question/worked-solution "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a+2 & -5 \\\\ b+4 & 6\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}(a+3)-1 & -5 \\\\ b+4 & 6\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}(a+3)-1 & -5 \\\\ b+4 & 6\\end{aligned}| & ={{|\\begin{aligned}a+3 & -5 \\\\ b & 6\\end{aligned}|}_{⏟}}_{-7}+|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -5 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-7+(-6+20) \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a+2 & -5 \\\\ b+4 & 6\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}(a+3)-1 & -5 \\\\ b+4 & 6\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}(a+3)-1 & -5 \\\\ b+4 & 6\\end{aligned}| & =\\underbrace{|\\begin{aligned}a+3 & -5 \\\\ b & 6\\end{aligned}|}_{-7}+|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -5 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-7+(-6+20) \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186120519 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=2,{u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=2.$" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=2,{u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=2.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186120524 :question/worked-solution "We want to determine if $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}\\}.$ So, we want to find ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}+{x}_{3}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}={{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{4}}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=-1 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=2 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3} &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the augmented matrix of the system to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 6 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 6 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the reduced matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd columns. So,  $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}\\}.$\nAlso, the matrix tells us that ${x}_{1}=-8,$ ${x}_{2}=0,$ and ${x}_{3}=7,$ meaning that\n\n$$\n(-8)\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+0\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}+7\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}={{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{4}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=-8+0+7=-1.$" "We want to determine if $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}\\}.$ So, we want to find ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{1}}+{x}_{2}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{2}}+{x}_{3}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{3}}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{4}}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=-1 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=2 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3} &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the augmented matrix of the system to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 6 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 6 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the reduced matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd columns. So,  $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}\\}.$\nAlso, the matrix tells us that ${x}_{1}=-8,$ ${x}_{2}=0,$ and ${x}_{3}=7,$ meaning that\n\n$$\n(-8)\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{1}}+0\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{2}}+7\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{3}}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{4}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=-8+0+7=-1.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186120525 :question/worked-solution "Remember, the order of the vectors in a basis is important.\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}={{(\\,2\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{{(\\,-4\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx=2\\cdot {b}_{1}+(-4)\\cdot {b}_{2}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that ${x}_{2}=-4.$" "Remember, the order of the vectors in a basis is important.\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}=\\underbrace{(\\,2\\,)}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{b}_{1}}+\\underbrace{(\\,-4\\,)}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{b}_{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx=2\\cdot {b}_{1}+(-4)\\cdot {b}_{2}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that ${x}_{2}=-4.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186121024 :tutorial/content "One final important property of the determinant is that it is **linear in each row and column**. For rows, for example, this means \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & det\\begin{bmatrix}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {◻}_{1}+{△}_{1} & {◻}_{2}+{△}_{2} & … & {◻}_{n}+{△}_{n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\,\\,=det\\begin{bmatrix}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {◻}_{1} & {◻}_{2} & … & {◻}_{n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{bmatrix}+det\\begin{bmatrix}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {△}_{1} & {△}_{2} & … & {△}_{n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor instance, it's easy to check that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{-2}{\\overset{⏞}{det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}} & =det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0+3 & 4+0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{4}+{{det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{-6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this property also holds for columns:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det\\begin{bmatrix}⋯ & {◻}_{1}+{△}_{1} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋯ & {◻}_{2}+{△}_{2} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ ⋯ & {◻}_{n}+{△}_{n} & ⋯\\end{bmatrix}=det\\begin{bmatrix}⋯ & {◻}_{1} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋯ & {◻}_{2} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ ⋯ & {◻}_{n} & ⋯\\end{bmatrix}+det\\begin{bmatrix}⋯ & {△}_{1} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋯ & {△}_{2} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ ⋯ & {△}_{n} & ⋯\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{-2}{\\overset{⏞}{det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}} & =det\\begin{bmatrix}1+0 & 2 \\\\ 0+3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{4}+{{det\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{-6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "One final important property of the determinant is that it is **linear in each row and column**. For rows, for example, this means \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & det\\begin{bmatrix}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {◻}_{1}+{△}_{1} & {◻}_{2}+{△}_{2} & … & {◻}_{n}+{△}_{n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\,\\,=det\\begin{bmatrix}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {◻}_{1} & {◻}_{2} & … & {◻}_{n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{bmatrix}+det\\begin{bmatrix}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {△}_{1} & {△}_{2} & … & {△}_{n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor instance, it's easy to check that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-2} & =det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0+3 & 4+0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{4}+\\underbrace{det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{-6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this property also holds for columns:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det\\begin{bmatrix}⋯ & {◻}_{1}+{△}_{1} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋯ & {◻}_{2}+{△}_{2} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ ⋯ & {◻}_{n}+{△}_{n} & ⋯\\end{bmatrix}=det\\begin{bmatrix}⋯ & {◻}_{1} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋯ & {◻}_{2} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ ⋯ & {◻}_{n} & ⋯\\end{bmatrix}+det\\begin{bmatrix}⋯ & {△}_{1} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋯ & {△}_{2} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ ⋯ & {△}_{n} & ⋯\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-2} & =det\\begin{bmatrix}1+0 & 2 \\\\ 0+3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{4}+\\underbrace{det\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{-6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186121028 :tutorial/content "In the same way that we can factor numbers or polynomials, we can factor a matrix by writing it as a product of two or more matrices. In fact, there is one factorization technique that works for *all* matrices, called **LU factorization** (or **LU decomposition**).\n\nLU factorization involves writing a matrix $A$ as a product of two matrices $L$ and $U,$ where\n\n- ![](images/ad/adca162da15cab5989e5726acb495fc12533231c2e38b0392d68388aeb18493b.svg) is a *unit lower triangular matrix* (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal), and\n\n- ![](images/93/93338df4d1ee1791dc5b1679dbddff34d94d05677c362b1a41dcb925ca83a1dc.svg) is an *upper triangular matrix*.\n\nFor example, we can write the matrix ![](images/83/835b8ad372998cbb35be4c1998d236fe96f1d1b03ee1ad7062bdbbb77e254bf6.svg) in LU factored form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nYou may be wondering how we arrived at this factorization. Shortly, we'll discuss an algorithm that explains how to compute $L$ and $U$ for any $2\\times2$ matrix $A.$ But for now, let's focus on recognizing LU factored forms." "In the same way that we can factor numbers or polynomials, we can factor a matrix by writing it as a product of two or more matrices. In fact, there is one factorization technique that works for *all* matrices, called **LU factorization** (or **LU decomposition**).\n\nLU factorization involves writing a matrix $A$ as a product of two matrices $L$ and $U,$ where\n\n- ![](images/ad/adca162da15cab5989e5726acb495fc12533231c2e38b0392d68388aeb18493b.svg) is a *unit lower triangular matrix* (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal), and\n\n- ![](images/93/93338df4d1ee1791dc5b1679dbddff34d94d05677c362b1a41dcb925ca83a1dc.svg) is an *upper triangular matrix*.\n\nFor example, we can write the matrix ![](images/83/835b8ad372998cbb35be4c1998d236fe96f1d1b03ee1ad7062bdbbb77e254bf6.svg) in LU factored form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{aligned}]=\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{L}\\cdot \\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nYou may be wondering how we arrived at this factorization. Shortly, we'll discuss an algorithm that explains how to compute $L$ and $U$ for any $2\\times2$ matrix $A.$ But for now, let's focus on recognizing LU factored forms."]
 [:db/cas 17592186121029 :tutorial/content "Recall that LU factorization involves writing a matrix $A$ as a product of two matrices $L$ and $U,$ where\n\n- ![](images/2e/2e6c0b6fdaf90e3a3e23ed91ffe09cb360bb39325b9a630f1b7219b3188a25db.svg) is a *unit lower triangular matrix* (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal), and\n\n- ![](images/0c/0c5d7337e47a53dfa5bdce8c6f4a96bb1e52ef6054349011a6cc38f29701cc61.svg) is an *upper triangular matrix*.\n\nFor example, suppose we're given the matrix ![](images/22/22dba4fe66ac5478f5d81a68342a01809a360de8e722e809998655e7740bafe8.svg) To perform LU factorization, we need to write $A$ as \n\n![](images/d4/d4c92b2e8252619f9a1511d166be884b9a2bdb4311eea8e408e8fe75eed6f36c.svg)\n\nAs usual, to find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we can reduce the matrix $A$ to *echelon form* while simultaneously performing the same elementary row operations on the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} & ⇔ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]=U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Caution:** In order to get a unit lower triangular matrix at the end, we have the following restriction while reducing a matrix $A$ to its *echelon form*:\n\n- we can only add multiples of higher rows to lower rows ($R_j:=R_j+k R_i$ where $i < j$), and\n\n- the other elementary row operations (swapping or multiplying a row by a number) are not allowed." "Recall that LU factorization involves writing a matrix $A$ as a product of two matrices $L$ and $U,$ where\n\n- ![](images/2e/2e6c0b6fdaf90e3a3e23ed91ffe09cb360bb39325b9a630f1b7219b3188a25db.svg) is a *unit lower triangular matrix* (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal), and\n\n- ![](images/0c/0c5d7337e47a53dfa5bdce8c6f4a96bb1e52ef6054349011a6cc38f29701cc61.svg) is an *upper triangular matrix*.\n\nFor example, suppose we're given the matrix ![](images/22/22dba4fe66ac5478f5d81a68342a01809a360de8e722e809998655e7740bafe8.svg) To perform LU factorization, we need to write $A$ as \n\n![](images/d4/d4c92b2e8252619f9a1511d166be884b9a2bdb4311eea8e408e8fe75eed6f36c.svg)\n\nAs usual, to find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we can reduce the matrix $A$ to *echelon form* while simultaneously performing the same elementary row operations on the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} & ⇔ & \\,\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]=U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{L}\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Caution:** In order to get a unit lower triangular matrix at the end, we have the following restriction while reducing a matrix $A$ to its *echelon form*:\n\n- we can only add multiples of higher rows to lower rows ($R_j:=R_j+k R_i$ where $i < j$), and\n\n- the other elementary row operations (swapping or multiplying a row by a number) are not allowed."]
 [:db/cas 17592186121037 :tutorial/content "The **column space** of a matrix $A$ is denoted $\\text{Col}(A)$ and is the span of the columns of $A$ (i.e., the set of all possible linear combinations of columns of $A$). For example, consider the matrix\n\n![](images/6c/6c03ab3dfd7b75c9a89defe102f33946da200fb88d26139e54c889a90467068a.svg)\nwhere we denote its columns by $\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_1$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2},$ respectively. The column space of this matrix is given by\n![](images/5f/5faad23bc77f821d42223beaca06364e38c7f0ba8a4869cb138e0e923e90b8f7.svg)\n\nNow, suppose we would like to determine whether the vector ![](images/45/45349edee356334c54ad85a6d60757a6a7833a392962086815a2436446e323dc.svg) lies in $\\textrm{Col}(A).$ In other words, we need to check if $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Span}\\{ {\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2} \\}.$ So, we need to determine whether $\\mathbf{b}$ can be written as some linear combination of $\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_1$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the equation above can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} & {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =b \\\\ {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}{{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{x}} & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{b}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation is equivalent to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}.$ To solve the equation, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[A \\,|\\, \\mathbf{b}]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,b] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -7 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=2 \\\\ -7{x}_{2} &=-7.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x_2=1.$ Substituting into the first equation, we get $x_1=-1.$\n\nSince we have a solution, the equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is consistent, which implies that $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Col}(A).$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $\\mathbf{b}$ as a linear combination of $\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_1$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/a3/a3965a53b3b3cfda2e11c4a68ba882631dc2763f32de67de30b4751aca76f2e5.svg)" "The **column space** of a matrix $A$ is denoted $\\text{Col}(A)$ and is the span of the columns of $A$ (i.e., the set of all possible linear combinations of columns of $A$). For example, consider the matrix\n\n![](images/6c/6c03ab3dfd7b75c9a89defe102f33946da200fb88d26139e54c889a90467068a.svg)\nwhere we denote its columns by $\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_1$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2},$ respectively. The column space of this matrix is given by\n![](images/5f/5faad23bc77f821d42223beaca06364e38c7f0ba8a4869cb138e0e923e90b8f7.svg)\n\nNow, suppose we would like to determine whether the vector ![](images/45/45349edee356334c54ad85a6d60757a6a7833a392962086815a2436446e323dc.svg) lies in $\\textrm{Col}(A).$ In other words, we need to check if $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Span}\\{ {\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2} \\}.$ So, we need to determine whether $\\mathbf{b}$ can be written as some linear combination of $\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_1$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the equation above can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} & {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =b \\\\ \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\text{x}} & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\text{b}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation is equivalent to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}.$ To solve the equation, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[A \\,|\\, \\mathbf{b}]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,b] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -7 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=2 \\\\ -7{x}_{2} &=-7.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x_2=1.$ Substituting into the first equation, we get $x_1=-1.$\n\nSince we have a solution, the equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is consistent, which implies that $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Col}(A).$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $\\mathbf{b}$ as a linear combination of $\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_1$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/a3/a3965a53b3b3cfda2e11c4a68ba882631dc2763f32de67de30b4751aca76f2e5.svg)"]
 [:db/cas 17592186121067 :tutorial/content "Let ![](images/ff/ff7b3f4bca1676cbe3fb78ae1c2a47d8fd0bc71dd98dee4c7b6fea4cc94ec7bd.svg) To perform LU factorization, we need to write $A$ as \n\n![](images/3f/3f74fdde04340f6a4dbd86962f5a31f8af22b5fb8053c66fa3e8be41629b12a1.svg)\n\nTo find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Caution:** To get a unit lower triangular matrix at the end, we have the following restriction while reducing a matrix $A$ to row echelon form:\n\n- we can only add a multiple of the $1$st row to the $2$nd row ($R_2:=R_2+k R_1$), and\n\n- the other elementary row operations (swapping or multiplying a row by a number) are not allowed." "Let ![](images/ff/ff7b3f4bca1676cbe3fb78ae1c2a47d8fd0bc71dd98dee4c7b6fea4cc94ec7bd.svg) To perform LU factorization, we need to write $A$ as \n\n![](images/3f/3f74fdde04340f6a4dbd86962f5a31f8af22b5fb8053c66fa3e8be41629b12a1.svg)\n\nTo find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{L}\\cdot \\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Caution:** To get a unit lower triangular matrix at the end, we have the following restriction while reducing a matrix $A$ to row echelon form:\n\n- we can only add a multiple of the $1$st row to the $2$nd row ($R_2:=R_2+k R_1$), and\n\n- the other elementary row operations (swapping or multiplying a row by a number) are not allowed."]
 [:db/cas 17592186124359 :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each property in turn.\n\n- The domain of $f(x)=a\\sqrt{b-x}+c\\,$ is determined by the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b-x & \\ge 0 \\\\ x & \\le b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that the domain is $x\\in \\left(-\\infty, 2\\right],$ which is equivalent to $x\\le 2.$ Hence, we obtain\n\n$$\nb=2.\n$$\n\n- Since the range is unbounded on the right-hand side, we know that $a>0.$ Furthermore, since we are given that the lowest point in the range is $2,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{a\\sqrt{2-x}}_{⏟}}_{\\ge 0}+c\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $c=2.$\n\n- Finally, since the point $(1,4)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =a\\sqrt{2-1}+2 \\\\ 4 & =a+2 \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our function is $f(x)=2\\sqrt{2-x}+2,$ and \n\n$$\na+b+c = 2 + 2 + 2 = 6.\n$$" "Let's consider each property in turn.\n\n- The domain of $f(x)=a\\sqrt{b-x}+c\\,$ is determined by the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b-x & \\ge 0 \\\\ x & \\le b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that the domain is $x\\in \\left(-\\infty, 2\\right],$ which is equivalent to $x\\le 2.$ Hence, we obtain\n\n$$\nb=2.\n$$\n\n- Since the range is unbounded on the right-hand side, we know that $a>0.$ Furthermore, since we are given that the lowest point in the range is $2,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{a\\sqrt{2-x}}_{\\ge 0}+c\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $c=2.$\n\n- Finally, since the point $(1,4)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =a\\sqrt{2-1}+2 \\\\ 4 & =a+2 \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our function is $f(x)=2\\sqrt{2-x}+2,$ and \n\n$$\na+b+c = 2 + 2 + 2 = 6.\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186125045 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have been given the following diagonalization of $A:$\n\n$$\nA={{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSince $A=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${A}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{A}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{101} & =P{D}^{101}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}^{101}(\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{1}^{101} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (-1)^{101}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, notice that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have been given the following diagonalization of $A:$\n\n$$\nA=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{P}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{D}\\underbrace{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSince $A=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${A}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{A}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{101} & =P{D}^{101}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}^{101}(\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{1}^{101} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (-1)^{101}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186125046 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), right-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $B:$\n\n$$\nB={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $B$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P:$\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$" "Notice that the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), right-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $B:$\n\n$$\nB=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{P}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{D}\\underbrace{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $B$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P:$\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186125047 :question/worked-solution "Since $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $C,$ then $C$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $C=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $C,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nC={{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}(-\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 15 \\\\ 20 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 11\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $C,$ then $C$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $C=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $C,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nC=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}_{P}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{D}\\underbrace{{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}(-\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 15 \\\\ 20 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 11\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186125048 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), left-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $E:$\n\n$$\nE={{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $E$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $E$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P.$ Computing $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the corresponding eigenvectors are: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$" "Notice that the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), left-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $E:$\n\n$$\nE=\\underbrace{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{P}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{D}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $E$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $E$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P.$ Computing $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the corresponding eigenvectors are: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186125058 :question/worked-solution "Since $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $F,$ then $F$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $F=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $F,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nF={{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.$ Then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -5 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -10 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-26 & -30 \\\\ 24 & 28\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $F,$ then $F$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $F=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $F,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nF=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}_{P}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{D}\\underbrace{{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.$ Then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -5 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -10 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-26 & -30 \\\\ 24 & 28\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186125085 :question/worked-solution "We have been given the following diagonalization of $A.$\n\n$$\nA={{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSo, since $A=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${A}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{A}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the inverse of the matrix $P.$ We get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{97} & =P{D}^{97}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{97}{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}(-1)^{97} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {1}^{97}\\end{bmatrix}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We have been given the following diagonalization of $A.$\n\n$$\nA=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{P}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{D}\\underbrace{{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSo, since $A=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${A}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{A}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the inverse of the matrix $P.$ We get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{97} & =P{D}^{97}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{97}{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}(-1)^{97} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {1}^{97}\\end{bmatrix}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186125149 :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each property in turn.\n\n- The domain of $f(x)=a\\sqrt{x+b}+c\\,$ is determined by the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+b & \\ge 0 \\\\ x & \\ge -b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that the domain is $x\\in [1,\\infty ),$ which is equivalent to $x\\ge 1.$ Hence, we obtain\n\n$$\n-b=1\\,⟹\\,b=-1.\n$$\n\n- Since the range is unbounded on the right-hand side, we know that $a>0.$ Furthermore, since we are given that the lowest point in the range is $2,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{a\\sqrt{x-1}}_{⏟}}_{\\ge 0}+c\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $c=2.$\n\n- Finally, since the point $(5,4)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(5) & =a\\sqrt{5-1}+2 \\\\ 4 & =2a+2 \\\\ 2a & =2 \\\\ a & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our function is $f(x)=\\sqrt{x-1}+2$ and \n\n$$\na+b+c=1+(-1)+2=2.\n$$" "Let's consider each property in turn.\n\n- The domain of $f(x)=a\\sqrt{x+b}+c\\,$ is determined by the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+b & \\ge 0 \\\\ x & \\ge -b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that the domain is $x\\in [1,\\infty ),$ which is equivalent to $x\\ge 1.$ Hence, we obtain\n\n$$\n-b=1\\,⟹\\,b=-1.\n$$\n\n- Since the range is unbounded on the right-hand side, we know that $a>0.$ Furthermore, since we are given that the lowest point in the range is $2,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{a\\sqrt{x-1}}_{\\ge 0}+c\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $c=2.$\n\n- Finally, since the point $(5,4)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(5) & =a\\sqrt{5-1}+2 \\\\ 4 & =2a+2 \\\\ 2a & =2 \\\\ a & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our function is $f(x)=\\sqrt{x-1}+2$ and \n\n$$\na+b+c=1+(-1)+2=2.\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186125762 :tutorial/content "Given a vector space $V$, any subset $H$ of $V,$ which is itself a vector space, is called a **subspace** of $V$. \n\nFortunately, we do not need to check all vector space axioms to determine whether a subset of some vector space is a subspace. Instead, we only need to check the **subspace criterion**, which states the following:\n\n*A subset $H$ of a vector space $V$ is a subspace of $V$ if and only if $H$ is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication. That is to say, both of the following conditions hold:*\n\n- *If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ lie in $H$, then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ also lies in $H$. Formally,*\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u},\\mathbf{v} \\in H \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad  \\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v} \\in H.\n$$\n\n- *If $\\mathbf{u}$ lies in $H$, then $k \\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ also lies in $H$ for any scalar  $k$. Formally,* \n\n$$\nk \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\,\\mathbf{u} \\in H \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad  k\\cdot\\mathbf{u} \\in H.\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the vector space $\\mathbb{R}_3[t]$ containing all polynomials of degree at most $3,$ along with the subset \n\n$$\nA = \\{ at^2 \\:|\\: a \\in \\mathbb{R} \\}.\n$$\n\nIs $A$ a subspace? Let's check the two subspace criterion conditions: \n\n- Suppose $p_1(t)=a_1 t^2$ and $p_2(t)=a_2 t^2$ are two polynomials from $A$. Summing the two polynomials, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}_{1}(t)+{p}_{2}(t) & =({a}_{1}{t}^{2})+({a}_{2}{t}^{2}) \\\\  & ={{({a}_{1}+{a}_{2})}_{⏟}}_{a\\in R}{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThus, $p_1(t)+p_2(t)$ is a polynomial of the form $at^2,$ which belongs to $A$.\n\n- If $p_1(t)=a_1 t^2 \\in A$ and $k \\in \\mathbb{R},$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{p}_{1}(t) & =k({a}_{1}{t}^{2}) \\\\  & ={{(k{a}_{1})}_{⏟}}_{a\\in R}{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThus, $kp_1(t)$ is a polynomial of the form $at^2,$ which belongs to $A$.\n\nSince both conditions hold, we conclude that $A$ is a subspace $\\mathbb{R}_3[t].$" "Given a vector space $V$, any subset $H$ of $V,$ which is itself a vector space, is called a **subspace** of $V$. \n\nFortunately, we do not need to check all vector space axioms to determine whether a subset of some vector space is a subspace. Instead, we only need to check the **subspace criterion**, which states the following:\n\n*A subset $H$ of a vector space $V$ is a subspace of $V$ if and only if $H$ is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication. That is to say, both of the following conditions hold:*\n\n- *If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ lie in $H$, then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ also lies in $H$. Formally,*\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u},\\mathbf{v} \\in H \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad  \\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v} \\in H.\n$$\n\n- *If $\\mathbf{u}$ lies in $H$, then $k \\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ also lies in $H$ for any scalar  $k$. Formally,* \n\n$$\nk \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\,\\mathbf{u} \\in H \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad  k\\cdot\\mathbf{u} \\in H.\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the vector space $\\mathbb{R}_3[t]$ containing all polynomials of degree at most $3,$ along with the subset \n\n$$\nA = \\{ at^2 \\:|\\: a \\in \\mathbb{R} \\}.\n$$\n\nIs $A$ a subspace? Let's check the two subspace criterion conditions: \n\n- Suppose $p_1(t)=a_1 t^2$ and $p_2(t)=a_2 t^2$ are two polynomials from $A$. Summing the two polynomials, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}_{1}(t)+{p}_{2}(t) & =({a}_{1}{t}^{2})+({a}_{2}{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\underbrace{({a}_{1}+{a}_{2})}_{a\\in R}{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThus, $p_1(t)+p_2(t)$ is a polynomial of the form $at^2,$ which belongs to $A$.\n\n- If $p_1(t)=a_1 t^2 \\in A$ and $k \\in \\mathbb{R},$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{p}_{1}(t) & =k({a}_{1}{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\underbrace{(k{a}_{1})}_{a\\in R}{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThus, $kp_1(t)$ is a polynomial of the form $at^2,$ which belongs to $A$.\n\nSince both conditions hold, we conclude that $A$ is a subspace $\\mathbb{R}_3[t].$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186125768 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $\\mathbf x \\in V$ for some vector space $V,$ and that $\\mathcal B$ and $\\mathcal C$ are two bases of $V.$ \n\nTo find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to $\\mathcal{C}$ given its coordinates relative to $\\mathcal B,$ we can use the **change-of-basis formula**\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [\\,x\\,]_{B},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we recall that\n\n- $[\\,\\mathbf x\\,]_{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}$ and $[\\,\\mathbf x\\,]_{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}$ are the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the bases $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ and ${\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}},$ respectively, and\n\n- $P_{\\small{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ to ${\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}},$ whose columns are the coordinates of vectors of the basis $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis ${\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}.$\n\nAs an example, suppose we are given two bases $\\mathcal{B}=\\left\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\right\\}$ and $\\mathcal{C}=\\left\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1, \\mathbf{c}_2 \\right\\}$ of a vector space $V,$ and we already know that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}$ is given by\n![](images/d9/d9388f46f84cc0c6b1d7e5cf43ee973085366ae4f44cfe74515c59bfc1994662.svg)\n\nLet $\\mathbf{v} \\in V$ and ![](images/f9/f99f37621c20057077eb7c6e23690a45159cfe1b665c6b88b4a604f8687728bf.svg) Then we can easily find $[\\,\\mathbf{v}\\,]_{\\mathcal{C}},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,v\\,]_{C} & =\\overset{{P}_{B\\to C}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot \\overset{[v]_{B}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Suppose that $\\mathbf x \\in V$ for some vector space $V,$ and that $\\mathcal B$ and $\\mathcal C$ are two bases of $V.$ \n\nTo find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to $\\mathcal{C}$ given its coordinates relative to $\\mathcal B,$ we can use the **change-of-basis formula**\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [\\,x\\,]_{B},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we recall that\n\n- $[\\,\\mathbf x\\,]_{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}$ and $[\\,\\mathbf x\\,]_{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}$ are the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the bases $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ and ${\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}},$ respectively, and\n\n- $P_{\\small{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ to ${\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}},$ whose columns are the coordinates of vectors of the basis $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis ${\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}.$\n\nAs an example, suppose we are given two bases $\\mathcal{B}=\\left\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\right\\}$ and $\\mathcal{C}=\\left\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1, \\mathbf{c}_2 \\right\\}$ of a vector space $V,$ and we already know that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}$ is given by\n![](images/d9/d9388f46f84cc0c6b1d7e5cf43ee973085366ae4f44cfe74515c59bfc1994662.svg)\n\nLet $\\mathbf{v} \\in V$ and ![](images/f9/f99f37621c20057077eb7c6e23690a45159cfe1b665c6b88b4a604f8687728bf.svg) Then we can easily find $[\\,\\mathbf{v}\\,]_{\\mathcal{C}},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,v\\,]_{C} & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{P}_{B\\to C}}\\cdot \\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{[v]_{B}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186125781 :tutorial/content "Every linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ that acts on 2D-vectors has a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $T$ associated with it. The matrix is called the **standard matrix** of the linear transformation. \nFor any vector $\\mathbf{v},$ the standard matrix $T$ of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ must have the property\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}) = T \\cdot \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nwhere $T \\cdot \\mathbf{v}$ is the matrix product of the standard matrix $T$ and the vector $\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nTo illustrate, consider the following linear transformation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe standard matrix for the linear transformation $\\mathbf{S}$ is <div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$S=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 7 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div> since it has the following property:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{S}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 7 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\cdot x+7\\cdot y \\\\ 1\\cdot x+(-5)\\cdot y\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3x+7y \\\\ x-5y\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =S\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice here that $\\mathbf{S}$ (using a **bold** letter) denotes the transformation (function) itself, while $S$ (big *italic* letter) is a $2 \\times 2$ matrix that corresponds to the linear transformation.\n\nIn general, the standard matrix of the linear transformation $\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can learn a lot about linear transformations by looking at their standard matrices." "Every linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ that acts on 2D-vectors has a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $T$ associated with it. The matrix is called the **standard matrix** of the linear transformation. \nFor any vector $\\mathbf{v},$ the standard matrix $T$ of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ must have the property\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}) = T \\cdot \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nwhere $T \\cdot \\mathbf{v}$ is the matrix product of the standard matrix $T$ and the vector $\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nTo illustrate, consider the following linear transformation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe standard matrix for the linear transformation $\\mathbf{S}$ is <div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$S=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 7 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div> since it has the following property:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 7 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}}^{S}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\cdot x+7\\cdot y \\\\ 1\\cdot x+(-5)\\cdot y\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3x+7y \\\\ x-5y\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =S\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice here that $\\mathbf{S}$ (using a **bold** letter) denotes the transformation (function) itself, while $S$ (big *italic* letter) is a $2 \\times 2$ matrix that corresponds to the linear transformation.\n\nIn general, the standard matrix of the linear transformation $\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can learn a lot about linear transformations by looking at their standard matrices."]
 [:db/cas 17592186125796 :tutorial/content "What conclusions can we make regarding the characteristic polynomial of the general $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/8e/8e26937ef1981804a548e92a4041bd8672cc75c7c5cc6efa0c0c2694c0736791.svg)\n\nFirst, let's find the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\left|\\begin{aligned}a-\\lambda  & b \\\\ c & d-\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\right| & =0 \\\\ (a-\\lambda )(d-\\lambda )-bc & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-(a+d)\\lambda +(ad-bc) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the constant term is the determinant of the matrix: \n\n$$\n\\det(A) = ad-bc\n$$\n\n \n\nThe coefficient of $\\lambda$ is the sum of the diagonal entries of $A$ taken with a negative sign. The sum of the diagonal entries of a matrix $A$ is called the **trace** of $A,$ and we denote it as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{tr}(A) = a+d.\n$$\n\nSo, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$, the characteristic polynomial is\n\n$$\np(\\lambda) = \\lambda^2 -\\mathrm{tr}(A)\\lambda + \\det(A).\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result can be extended to the general case of $n \\times n$ matrices. If $A$ is an $n \\times n$ matrix, then its characteristic polynomial takes the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =det(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =(-1)^{n}{\\lambda }^{n}+{c}_{n-1}{\\lambda }^{n-1}+⋯+{c}_{1}\\lambda +{c}_{0} \\\\  & =(-1)^{n}{\\lambda }^{n}+{{(-1)^{n-1}\\text{tr}(A)}_{⏟}}_{{c}_{n-1}}{\\lambda }^{n-1}+⋯+{c}_{1}\\lambda +{{det(A)}_{⏟}}_{{c}_{0}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{n-1}=(-1)^{n-1}\\text{tr}(A)\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{0}=det(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "What conclusions can we make regarding the characteristic polynomial of the general $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/8e/8e26937ef1981804a548e92a4041bd8672cc75c7c5cc6efa0c0c2694c0736791.svg)\n\nFirst, let's find the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\left|\\begin{aligned}a-\\lambda  & b \\\\ c & d-\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\right| & =0 \\\\ (a-\\lambda )(d-\\lambda )-bc & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-(a+d)\\lambda +(ad-bc) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the constant term is the determinant of the matrix: \n\n$$\n\\det(A) = ad-bc\n$$\n\n \n\nThe coefficient of $\\lambda$ is the sum of the diagonal entries of $A$ taken with a negative sign. The sum of the diagonal entries of a matrix $A$ is called the **trace** of $A,$ and we denote it as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{tr}(A) = a+d.\n$$\n\nSo, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$, the characteristic polynomial is\n\n$$\np(\\lambda) = \\lambda^2 -\\mathrm{tr}(A)\\lambda + \\det(A).\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result can be extended to the general case of $n \\times n$ matrices. If $A$ is an $n \\times n$ matrix, then its characteristic polynomial takes the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =det(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =(-1)^{n}{\\lambda }^{n}+{c}_{n-1}{\\lambda }^{n-1}+⋯+{c}_{1}\\lambda +{c}_{0} \\\\  & =(-1)^{n}{\\lambda }^{n}+\\underbrace{(-1)^{n-1}\\text{tr}(A)}_{{c}_{n-1}}{\\lambda }^{n-1}+⋯+{c}_{1}\\lambda +\\underbrace{det(A)}_{{c}_{0}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{n-1}=(-1)^{n-1}\\text{tr}(A)\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{0}=det(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186128943 :question/worked-solution "For the given matrix $A,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2-3\\text{i} & -5-7\\text{i} \\\\ 1-6\\text{i} & 5-9\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(A)}+\\text{i}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -7 \\\\ -6 & -9\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(A)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $a=2,d=5,e=-3$ and $h=-9.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nad+eh=2 \\cdot 5 + (-3) \\cdot (-9) = 37.\n$$" "For the given matrix $A,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2-3\\text{i} & -5-7\\text{i} \\\\ 1-6\\text{i} & 5-9\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\text{Re}(A)}+\\text{i}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -7 \\\\ -6 & -9\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\text{Im}(A)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $a=2,d=5,e=-3$ and $h=-9.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nad+eh=2 \\cdot 5 + (-3) \\cdot (-9) = 37.\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186128945 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2 \\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PCP^{-1},$ where \n\n![](images/9f/9fb15e52a1df912c9e5fb4238f45d705cd0a56fb7190620c0f0fd597f9a2b8c0.png)\n\nand $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda.$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n![](images/80/80bd36915851e6ebeb67befe8af41e5496fa5491b9d87c294d8b937eb586319f.png)\n\nNext, we can find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1+2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(v)}+\\text{i}{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(v)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that for any $2 \\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PCP^{-1},$ where \n\n![](images/9f/9fb15e52a1df912c9e5fb4238f45d705cd0a56fb7190620c0f0fd597f9a2b8c0.png)\n\nand $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda.$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n![](images/80/80bd36915851e6ebeb67befe8af41e5496fa5491b9d87c294d8b937eb586319f.png)\n\nNext, we can find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1+2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\text{Re}(v)}+\\text{i}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\text{Im}(v)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186129033 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2 \\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PCP^{-1},$ where \n\n![](images/4a/4a53548a17d9b4fd870ce70285bfc2c57cf6c8d352f75149e5aa7511ef4af853.png)\n\nand $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda.$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n![](images/aa/aa5738b600440bf8327670fc21cd8005c660def232119b2b4808237d988367bd.png)\n\nwhile\n\n![](images/fc/fc91fcabd47720075061e670f62be9ea9b636070a05cba70752bb7060608b764.png)\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overline{\\lambda}=2-4\\textrm{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda.$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overline{\\mathbf{v}},$ the complex conjugate of $\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\text{i} \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that for any $2 \\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PCP^{-1},$ where \n\n![](images/4a/4a53548a17d9b4fd870ce70285bfc2c57cf6c8d352f75149e5aa7511ef4af853.png)\n\nand $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda.$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n![](images/aa/aa5738b600440bf8327670fc21cd8005c660def232119b2b4808237d988367bd.png)\n\nwhile\n\n![](images/fc/fc91fcabd47720075061e670f62be9ea9b636070a05cba70752bb7060608b764.png)\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overline{\\lambda}=2-4\\textrm{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda.$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overline{\\mathbf{v}},$ the complex conjugate of $\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\text{i} \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186129525 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot {{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{⏟}}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u\\cdot (-3{v}^{2})-1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-6u{v}^{2}-2 \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(1,2)} & =(-6u{v}^{2}-2)\\,{|}_{u=1\\,v=2} \\\\  & =-6(1)(2)^{2}-2 \\\\  & =-26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|-26|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =26\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot \\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u\\cdot (-3{v}^{2})-1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-6u{v}^{2}-2 \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(1,2)} & =(-6u{v}^{2}-2)\\,{|}_{u=1\\,v=2} \\\\  & =-6(1)(2)^{2}-2 \\\\  & =-26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|-26|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =26\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186129527 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot {{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{⏟}}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{u} & \\frac{1}{v} \\\\ {v}^{2} & 2uv\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot 2uv-\\frac{1}{v}\\cdot {v}^{2} \\\\  & =2v-v \\\\  & =v \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(1,5)} & =v\\,{|}_{u=1\\,v=5} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|5|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =5\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot \\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{u} & \\frac{1}{v} \\\\ {v}^{2} & 2uv\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot 2uv-\\frac{1}{v}\\cdot {v}^{2} \\\\  & =2v-v \\\\  & =v \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(1,5)} & =v\\,{|}_{u=1\\,v=5} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|5|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =5\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186129644 :question/worked-solution "For the given matrix $A,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}3+5\\text{i} & -2-4\\text{i} \\\\ 2-3\\text{i} & 1-6\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(A)}+\\text{i}{{\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -4 \\\\ -3 & -6\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(A)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $a=3,d=1,e=5$ and $h=-6.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nad+eh=3\\cdot 1+5\\cdot (-6)=-27.\n$$" "For the given matrix $A,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}3+5\\text{i} & -2-4\\text{i} \\\\ 2-3\\text{i} & 1-6\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\text{Re}(A)}+\\text{i}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -4 \\\\ -3 & -6\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\text{Im}(A)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $a=3,d=1,e=5$ and $h=-6.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nad+eh=3\\cdot 1+5\\cdot (-6)=-27.\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186129648 :question/worked-solution "For the given matrix $A,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2+3\\text{i} & -5+2\\text{i} \\\\ 6-7\\text{i} & 4-9\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -5 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(A)}+\\text{i}{{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -7 & -9\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(A)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{Re}(A)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -5 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$" "For the given matrix $A,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2+3\\text{i} & -5+2\\text{i} \\\\ 6-7\\text{i} & 4-9\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -5 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\text{Re}(A)}+\\text{i}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -7 & -9\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\text{Im}(A)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{Re}(A)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -5 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186129662 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=C.\n$$\n\nNext, we can find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(v)}+\\text{i}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(v)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=C.\n$$\n\nNext, we can find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\text{Re}(v)}+\\text{i}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\text{Im}(v)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186129666 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -6 \\\\ 6 & -7\\end{bmatrix}=C.\n$$\n\nNext, we can find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\begin{bmatrix}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(v)}+\\text{i}{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(v)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -6 \\\\ 6 & -7\\end{bmatrix}=C.\n$$\n\nNext, we can find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\begin{bmatrix}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\text{Re}(v)}+\\text{i}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\text{Im}(v)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186129717 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\ne C,\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda })\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{―}{\\lambda }=1-6\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{―}{v},$ the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i} \\\\ 2-3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\ne C,\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda })\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{―}{\\lambda }=1-6\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{―}{v},$ the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i} \\\\ 2-3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186130340 :tutorial/content "To raise the product of two numbers to a power, we can raise each number to that power, and then multiply. This is the **power of product rule for exponents**. \n\nTo demonstrate, let's simplify the expression $(5 \\times 3)^4$ by applying the power of product rule:\n\n$$\n(5\\times 3)^4=5^4\\times 3^4\n$$\n\n**Note:** The power of product rule for exponents is really just a quicker alternative to using repeated multiplication, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5\\times 3)^{4} & = \\\\ {{(5\\times 3)\\times (5\\times 3)\\times (5\\times 3)\\times (5\\times 3)}_{⏟}}_{4\\text{ copies}} & = \\\\ {{(5\\times 5\\times 5\\times 5)}_{⏟}}_{4\\text{ copies}}\\times {{(3\\times 3\\times 3\\times 3)}_{⏟}}_{4\\text{ copies}} & = \\\\ {5}^{4}\\times {3}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To raise the product of two numbers to a power, we can raise each number to that power, and then multiply. This is the **power of product rule for exponents**. \n\nTo demonstrate, let's simplify the expression $(5 \\times 3)^4$ by applying the power of product rule:\n\n$$\n(5\\times 3)^4=5^4\\times 3^4\n$$\n\n**Note:** The power of product rule for exponents is really just a quicker alternative to using repeated multiplication, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5\\times 3)^{4} & = \\\\ \\underbrace{(5\\times 3)\\times (5\\times 3)\\times (5\\times 3)\\times (5\\times 3)}_{4\\text{ copies}} & = \\\\ \\underbrace{(5\\times 5\\times 5\\times 5)}_{4\\text{ copies}}\\times \\underbrace{(3\\times 3\\times 3\\times 3)}_{4\\text{ copies}} & = \\\\ {5}^{4}\\times {3}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186133487 :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}\n= \\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the paraboloid $z=x^2+y^2$ and the plane $z=1.$ One such surface would be the plane $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz = g(x,y) = 1.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨-xz,\\,-yz,\\,{z}^{2}+x⟩{|}_{z=1} \\\\  & =⟨{{-x}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{-y}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{1+x}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(-x)\\cdot 0-(-y)\\cdot 0+1+x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1+x)\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNow, note that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle $x^2+y^2=1$ of radius $1$ centered at the origin. Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$\nx = r \\cos\\theta, \\qquad\ny = r \\sin\\theta, \\qquad\n\\textrm{d}A = r \\, \\textrm{d}r \\textrm{d}\\theta,\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(1+x)\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(1+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }r+{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[r\\theta +{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2\\pi r\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\pi [{r}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}\n= \\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the paraboloid $z=x^2+y^2$ and the plane $z=1.$ One such surface would be the plane $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz = g(x,y) = 1.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨-xz,\\,-yz,\\,{z}^{2}+x⟩{|}_{z=1} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{-x}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{-y}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{1+x}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(-x)\\cdot 0-(-y)\\cdot 0+1+x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1+x)\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNow, note that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle $x^2+y^2=1$ of radius $1$ centered at the origin. Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$\nx = r \\cos\\theta, \\qquad\ny = r \\sin\\theta, \\qquad\n\\textrm{d}A = r \\, \\textrm{d}r \\textrm{d}\\theta,\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(1+x)\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(1+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }r+{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[r\\theta +{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2\\pi r\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\pi [{r}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186133512 :question/worked-solution "To work out $\\left(-13\\right)^3,$ we multiply $-13$ by itself and then multiply the result by $-13$ again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-13)^{3} & =\\overset{169}{\\overset{⏞}{(-13)\\cdot (-13)}}\\cdot (-13) \\\\  & =169\\cdot (-13) \\\\  & =-2\\,197\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To work out $\\left(-13\\right)^3,$ we multiply $-13$ by itself and then multiply the result by $-13$ again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-13)^{3} & =\\overbrace{(-13)\\cdot (-13)}^{169}\\cdot (-13) \\\\  & =169\\cdot (-13) \\\\  & =-2\\,197\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186133514 :question/worked-solution "To work out $\\left(-0.5\\right)^3,$ we multiply $-0.5$ by itself and then multiply the result by $-0.5$ once more:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-0.5)^{3} & =\\overset{0.25}{\\overset{⏞}{(-0.5)\\cdot (-0.5)}}\\cdot (-0.5) \\\\  & =0.25\\cdot (-0.5) \\\\  & =-0.125\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To work out $\\left(-0.5\\right)^3,$ we multiply $-0.5$ by itself and then multiply the result by $-0.5$ once more:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-0.5)^{3} & =\\overbrace{(-0.5)\\cdot (-0.5)}^{0.25}\\cdot (-0.5) \\\\  & =0.25\\cdot (-0.5) \\\\  & =-0.125\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186133612 :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\nA^T A\\mathbf{x} = A^T\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, it satisfies $Q^T Q = I,$ and we get\n\n$$\nR^T R \\mathbf{x} = R^T \\! Q^T \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $R^T$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $(R^T)^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x & =\\begin{bmatrix}28 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}7{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2} &=28 \\\\ 2{x}_{2} &=14.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x_2=7.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{x}_{1}-3(7) & =28 \\\\ 7{x}_{1} & =49 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is ![](images/c3/c3f08e2cdc175b34030857c03d0301e5b734975355d09b2016559b4f28b6f5e4.svg)" "The least-squares solution of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\nA^T A\\mathbf{x} = A^T\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, it satisfies $Q^T Q = I,$ and we get\n\n$$\nR^T R \\mathbf{x} = R^T \\! Q^T \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $R^T$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $(R^T)^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}x & =\\overbrace{\\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{{Q}^{T}}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{b} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x & =\\begin{bmatrix}28 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}7{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2} &=28 \\\\ 2{x}_{2} &=14.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x_2=7.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{x}_{1}-3(7) & =28 \\\\ 7{x}_{1} & =49 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is ![](images/c3/c3f08e2cdc175b34030857c03d0301e5b734975355d09b2016559b4f28b6f5e4.svg)"]
 [:db/cas 17592186133613 :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X^T} X )^{-1}$ and $X^T \\mathbf{y}$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{104}\\begin{bmatrix}26 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}27 \\\\ -26\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6.75 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $y = 6.75 - x.$" "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{X}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X^T} X )^{-1}$ and $X^T \\mathbf{y}$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{104}\\begin{bmatrix}26 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}27 \\\\ -26\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6.75 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $y = 6.75 - x.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186133614 :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}0.8 \\\\ 1.8 \\\\ 3.2 \\\\ 4.2\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X^T} X )^{-1}$ and $X^T \\mathbf{y}$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}\\begin{bmatrix}23 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 38.2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.04 \\\\ 0.82\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y = 0.04+ 0.82x.$\n\nFinally, to predict how much the spring will stretch if an $8$-ounce mass is hung from it,  we substitute $x=8$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.04+0.82(8) \\\\  & =6.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, according to the model, if an $8$ ounce mass is hung on the spring, the spring will stretch $6.6$ inches. \n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=8$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable." "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to 