 [:db/cas 17592186134072 :question/worked-solution "First,  notice that ${A}^{T}$ is a $n\\times m$ matrix.\n\nNow, pre-multiplying a vector $x\\in {R}^{m}$ by ${A}^{T}$ gives a linear combination of the columns of ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & ⋯ & | \\\\ {a}_{1}^{T} & {a}_{2}^{T} & ⋯ & {a}_{m}^{T} \\\\ | & | & ⋯ & |\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{T}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{m}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}={x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ {a}_{1}^{T} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ {a}_{2}^{T} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}+⋯+{x}_{m}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ {a}_{m}^{T} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${R}^{m}$ under the action of ${A}^{T}$ must be $\\text{Col}({A}^{T}),$ the column space of ${A}^{T},$ or equivalently, the row space of $A.$\n\nFurthermore, since $\\text{Null}({A}^{T})$ is mapped to the zero vector $0$ by the definition of the null subspace, the image of its orthogonal complement $\\text{Col}(({A}^{T})^{T})=\\text{Col}(A)$ must be $\\text{Col}({A}^{T}).$\n\nThis can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/8c/8ca8508d05e28e4c91b5a7f3c65826ce68b5dacd382bfb1a76f09c09d1d004bf.png)" "First,  notice that ${A}^{T}$ is a $n\\times m$ matrix.\n\nNow, pre-multiplying a vector $x\\in {R}^{m}$ by ${A}^{T}$ gives a linear combination of the columns of ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & ⋯ & | \\\\ {a}_{1}^{T} & {a}_{2}^{T} & ⋯ & {a}_{m}^{T} \\\\ | & | & ⋯ & |\\end{bmatrix}}_{{A}^{T}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{m}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}={x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ {a}_{1}^{T} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ {a}_{2}^{T} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}+⋯+{x}_{m}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ {a}_{m}^{T} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${R}^{m}$ under the action of ${A}^{T}$ must be $\\text{Col}({A}^{T}),$ the column space of ${A}^{T},$ or equivalently, the row space of $A.$\n\nFurthermore, since $\\text{Null}({A}^{T})$ is mapped to the zero vector $0$ by the definition of the null subspace, the image of its orthogonal complement $\\text{Col}(({A}^{T})^{T})=\\text{Col}(A)$ must be $\\text{Col}({A}^{T}).$\n\nThis can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/8c/8ca8508d05e28e4c91b5a7f3c65826ce68b5dacd382bfb1a76f09c09d1d004bf.png)"]
 [:db/cas 17592186134073 :question/worked-solution "Pre-multiplying a vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ by $A$ gives a linear combination of the columns of $A:$\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & ⋯ & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & ⋯ & {a}_{n} \\\\ | & | & ⋯ & |\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}={x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ {a}_{1} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}+⋯+{x}_{n}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ {a}_{n} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${R}^{n}$ under the action of $A$ must be $\\text{Col}(A),$ the column space of $A.$" "Pre-multiplying a vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ by $A$ gives a linear combination of the columns of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & ⋯ & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & ⋯ & {a}_{n} \\\\ | & | & ⋯ & |\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}={x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ {a}_{1} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}+⋯+{x}_{n}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ {a}_{n} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${R}^{n}$ under the action of $A$ must be $\\text{Col}(A),$ the column space of $A.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186134106 :question/worked-solution "| $x$ | **$-2$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-6$ | $-5$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector." "| $x$ | **$-2$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-6$ | $-5$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{X}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."]
 [:db/cas 17592186134108 :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -1.7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=6-1.7x.$" "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{X}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -1.7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=6-1.7x.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186134111 :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1.2 \\\\ 2.5 \\\\ 3.6 \\\\ 4.5\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}15 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}11.8 \\\\ 35\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.2 \\\\ 1.1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=0.2+1.1x.$\n\nFinally, to predict the  height after $8$ years, we substitute $x=8$ into the equation of the line: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.2+1.1(8) \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, according to the model, the height of a pine tree that is $8$ years old will be $9$ feet. \n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=8$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable." "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{X}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1.2 \\\\ 2.5 \\\\ 3.6 \\\\ 4.5\\end{bmatrix}}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}15 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}11.8 \\\\ 35\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.2 \\\\ 1.1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=0.2+1.1x.$\n\nFinally, to predict the  height after $8$ years, we substitute $x=8$ into the equation of the line: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.2+1.1(8) \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, according to the model, the height of a pine tree that is $8$ years old will be $9$ feet. \n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=8$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable."]
