 [:db/cas 17592186134125 :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional region of type II can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/e4/e4c9cbbad2f13e9cc125ddbb89125ed2d734eee3f80737125961f4af1846a972.png)\n\n \n\nLet's find a type II representation of our region.\n\n**Step 1.** We start by finding the surfaces ${f}_{1}(y,z)$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z).$ Since all $x$ in the region are bounded *below* by $x={y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and *above* by $x=2y,$ we have\n\n$$\n{{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{1}(y,z)}\\le x\\le {{2y}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{2}(y,z)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true.\n\n**Step 2.** We find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $x={y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and the plane $x=2y$ onto the $yz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $x.$ Substituting this expression for $x$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y & ={y}^{2}+{z}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2}-2y+{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ ({y}^{2}-2y+1)+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle $(y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ centered at $(1,0)$ in the $yz$-plane. \n\n![](images/01/01cb7f25e98c8607258f673a5c5be6a1e55869b379c4484c05a04cebbe5dc5f6.png)\n\nThe region is bounded *below* by $z=-\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}$ and bounded *above* by $z=\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{-\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{1}(y)}\\le z\\le {{\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{2}(y)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false.\n\n**Step 3.** We find the bounds for $y.$ From the diagram, $y$ is bounded *below* by $y=0$ and bounded *above* by $y=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{0}_{⏟}}_{a}\\le y\\le {{2}_{⏟}}_{b}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement III is false.\n\nFinally, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "A typical three-dimensional region of type II can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/e4/e4c9cbbad2f13e9cc125ddbb89125ed2d734eee3f80737125961f4af1846a972.png)\n\n \n\nLet's find a type II representation of our region.\n\n**Step 1.** We start by finding the surfaces ${f}_{1}(y,z)$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z).$ Since all $x$ in the region are bounded *below* by $x={y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and *above* by $x=2y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}_{{f}_{1}(y,z)}\\le x\\le \\underbrace{2y}_{{f}_{2}(y,z)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true.\n\n**Step 2.** We find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $x={y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and the plane $x=2y$ onto the $yz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $x.$ Substituting this expression for $x$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y & ={y}^{2}+{z}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2}-2y+{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ ({y}^{2}-2y+1)+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle $(y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ centered at $(1,0)$ in the $yz$-plane. \n\n![](images/01/01cb7f25e98c8607258f673a5c5be6a1e55869b379c4484c05a04cebbe5dc5f6.png)\n\nThe region is bounded *below* by $z=-\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}$ and bounded *above* by $z=\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{-\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}}_{{g}_{1}(y)}\\le z\\le \\underbrace{\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}}_{{g}_{2}(y)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false.\n\n**Step 3.** We find the bounds for $y.$ From the diagram, $y$ is bounded *below* by $y=0$ and bounded *above* by $y=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{0}_{a}\\le y\\le \\underbrace{2}_{b}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement III is false.\n\nFinally, the correct answer is \"I only.\""]
 [:db/cas 17592186134130 :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the plane $z=4-x-2y.$ One such surface would be the plane $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=4-x-2y.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-x-2y)=-1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-x-2y)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,-2y⟩{|}_{z=4-x-2y} \\\\  & =⟨{{0}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{0}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{-2y}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot (-2)+(-2y))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-2y\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-palne is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ with radius $1$ centered at the origin. Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}-2y\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}2r\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi }\\cdot [{r}^{2}]_{r=0}^{r=1} \\\\  & =(1-1)\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the plane $z=4-x-2y.$ One such surface would be the plane $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=4-x-2y.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-x-2y)=-1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-x-2y)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,-2y⟩{|}_{z=4-x-2y} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{0}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{0}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{-2y}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot (-2)+(-2y))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-2y\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-palne is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ with radius $1$ centered at the origin. Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}-2y\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}2r\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi }\\cdot [{r}^{2}]_{r=0}^{r=1} \\\\  & =(1-1)\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186134131 :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the paraboloid $z=10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the boundary of our infinite vertical column. One such surface is our paraboloid $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨-2z,\\,0,\\,0⟩{|}_{z=10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨-2(10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}),\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{{2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-20}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{0}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{0}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-20)\\cdot (-2x)-0\\cdot (-2y)+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{2}-40x)\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/c1/c181e209cca01a1d6e372caa7f7810a5fa4aa9f177595bd92f937a7cba8b8a75.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(4{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{2}-40x)\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}\\,4{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{2}-40x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the paraboloid $z=10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the boundary of our infinite vertical column. One such surface is our paraboloid $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨-2z,\\,0,\\,0⟩{|}_{z=10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨-2(10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}),\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-20}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{0}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{0}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-20)\\cdot (-2x)-0\\cdot (-2y)+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{2}-40x)\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/c1/c181e209cca01a1d6e372caa7f7810a5fa4aa9f177595bd92f937a7cba8b8a75.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(4{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{2}-40x)\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}\\,4{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{2}-40x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186134213 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={{\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+{{\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-\\frac{2xy}{(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{1}^{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡2)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+{ln}^{2}⁡2)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+{ln}^{2}⁡2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\underbrace{\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+\\underbrace{\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-\\frac{2xy}{(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{1}^{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡2)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+{ln}^{2}⁡2)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+{ln}^{2}⁡2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186134324 :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{12}.$" "$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{12}.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186134324 :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}-(-\\frac{12}{6})\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{6} & -2\\sqrt{6} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{6} & -2\\sqrt{6} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{12}=(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})\\cdot (-2\\sqrt{6})=2\\sqrt{2}.$" "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}-(-\\frac{12}{6})\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{6} & -2\\sqrt{6} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{6} & -2\\sqrt{6} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{12}=(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})\\cdot (-2\\sqrt{6})=2\\sqrt{2}.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186134326 :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of ${q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{12}.$" "$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of ${q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{12}.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186134326 :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**. We We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-(-\\frac{9}{9})\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{12}=(-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}})\\cdot (-3)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.$" "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**. We We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-(-\\frac{9}{9})\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{12}=(-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}})\\cdot (-3)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186134329 :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 24\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}^{x} & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 24\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186134330 :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{A}\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}^{x} & =\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}^{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186134335 :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is orthogonal, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}x & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+7{x}_{2} &=5 \\\\ 5{x}_{2} &=5.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=1.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+7(1) & =5 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$" "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is orthogonal, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}x & =\\overbrace{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{Q}^{T}}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{b} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}x & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+7{x}_{2} &=5 \\\\ 5{x}_{2} &=5.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=1.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+7(1) & =5 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186134352 :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. You're given that the system of equations given below connects the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$  \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} &=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2} &=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{2}+2{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3} &=\\frac{1}{6}{a}_{3}-4{q}_{1}+{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of ${r}_{12}+{r}_{22}+{r}_{23}?$" "$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. You're given that the system of equations given below connects the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$  \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} &=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2} &=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{2}+2{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3} &=\\frac{1}{6}{a}_{3}-4{q}_{1}+{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of ${r}_{12}+{r}_{22}+{r}_{23}?$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186134352 :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} &=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2} &=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{2}+2{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3} &=\\frac{1}{6}{a}_{3}-4{q}_{1}+{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} &=2{q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} &=-8{q}_{1}+4{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3} &=24{q}_{1}-6{q}_{2}+6{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, this system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -8 & 24 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{12}+{r}_{22}+{r}_{23}=-8+4+(-6)=-10.\n$$" "Let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} &=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2} &=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{2}+2{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3} &=\\frac{1}{6}{a}_{3}-4{q}_{1}+{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} &=2{q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} &=-8{q}_{1}+4{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3} &=24{q}_{1}-6{q}_{2}+6{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, this system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -8 & 24 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{12}+{r}_{22}+{r}_{23}=-8+4+(-6)=-10.\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186134354 :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ \\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖} & \\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖} & \\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above, where $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is an orthogonal set of vectors. You're given that $‖{v}_{1}‖=4,$ $‖{v}_{2}‖=2,$ $‖{v}_{3}‖=6,$ and that the system of equations below describes the relationship between the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$ \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} &={a}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} &={a}_{2}-2{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{3} &={a}_{3}-4{v}_{1}+3{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of ${r}_{11}+{r}_{13}+{r}_{33}?$" "$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ \\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖} & \\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖} & \\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above, where $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is an orthogonal set of vectors. You're given that $‖{v}_{1}‖=4,$ $‖{v}_{2}‖=2,$ $‖{v}_{3}‖=6,$ and that the system of equations below describes the relationship between the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$ \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} &={a}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} &={a}_{2}-2{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{3} &={a}_{3}-4{v}_{1}+3{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of ${r}_{11}+{r}_{13}+{r}_{33}?$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186134354 :question/worked-solution "First, we normalize the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{4}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{1}=4{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{2}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{2}=2{q}_{2} \\\\ {q}_{3} & =\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{3}}{6}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{3}=6{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the set $\\{{q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}\\}$ will be both orthogonal and normalized.\n\nNext, let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} &={a}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} &={a}_{2}-2{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{3} &={a}_{3}-4{v}_{1}+3{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} &={v}_{1}=4{q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} &=2{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}=8{q}_{1}+2{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3} &=4{v}_{1}-3{v}_{2}+{v}_{3}=16{q}_{1}-6{q}_{2}+6{q}_{3}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe final system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 16 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{11}+{r}_{13}+{r}_{33}=4+16+6=26.\n$$" "First, we normalize the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{4}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{1}=4{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{2}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{2}=2{q}_{2} \\\\ {q}_{3} & =\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{3}}{6}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{3}=6{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the set $\\{{q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}\\}$ will be both orthogonal and normalized.\n\nNext, let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} &={a}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} &={a}_{2}-2{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{3} &={a}_{3}-4{v}_{1}+3{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} &={v}_{1}=4{q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} &=2{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}=8{q}_{1}+2{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3} &=4{v}_{1}-3{v}_{2}+{v}_{3}=16{q}_{1}-6{q}_{2}+6{q}_{3}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe final system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 16 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{11}+{r}_{13}+{r}_{33}=4+16+6=26.\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186134356 :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ of $A$ are orthogonal, and that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained by normalizing the columns of $A,$ what is the value of ${q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{12}?$" "$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ of $A$ are orthogonal, and that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained by normalizing the columns of $A,$ what is the value of ${q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{12}?$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186134356 :question/worked-solution "Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct an orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we simply normalize ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{14} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} & 0\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{14} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{12}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\cdot 0=0.$" "Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct an orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we simply normalize ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{14} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} & 0\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{14} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{12}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\cdot 0=0.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186134362 :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}8.3 \\\\ 5.1 \\\\ 10.9 \\\\ 10.7 \\\\ 9.5\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}44.5 \\\\ 141.5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6.5 \\\\ 0.8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=6.5+0.8x.$\n\nFinally, to predict the profit in the $6$th month, we substitute $x=6$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =6.5+0.8(6) \\\\  & =11.3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the profit in the $6$th month will be $$11\\,300.$\n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=6$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable." "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}^{X}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}8.3 \\\\ 5.1 \\\\ 10.9 \\\\ 10.7 \\\\ 9.5\\end{bmatrix}}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}44.5 \\\\ 141.5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6.5 \\\\ 0.8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=6.5+0.8x.$\n\nFinally, to predict the profit in the $6$th month, we substitute $x=6$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =6.5+0.8(6) \\\\  & =11.3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the profit in the $6$th month will be $$11\\,300.$\n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=6$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable."]
 [:db/cas 17592186134363 :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\,\\,\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 18 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}36 \\\\ 72 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{36}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 18\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}36 \\\\ 72 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}^{x} & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}^{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\,\\,\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 18 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}36 \\\\ 72 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{36}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 18\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}36 \\\\ 72 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186134364 :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}14 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{35}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -7 \\\\ -7 & 14\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}^{x} & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}14 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{35}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -7 \\\\ -7 & 14\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186134366 :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖$ is attained when $x=\\hat{x},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $Ax=b.$ In this case, $A\\hat{x}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $‖A\\hat{x}-b‖$ is the distance between the vector $b$ and the column space. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\hat{x}-b & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n‖A\\hat{x}-b‖=\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=\\sqrt{30}.\n$$" "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{x} & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}}^{b} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖$ is attained when $x=\\hat{x},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $Ax=b.$ In this case, $A\\hat{x}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $‖A\\hat{x}-b‖$ is the distance between the vector $b$ and the column space. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\hat{x}-b & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n‖A\\hat{x}-b‖=\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=\\sqrt{30}.\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186134387 :question/worked-solution "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$10$** | **$25$** | **$32$** | **$42$** | **$15$** |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $-5$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $7$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the plane \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 25 \\\\ 3 & 32 \\\\ 4 & 42 \\\\ 5 & 15\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector." "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$10$** | **$25$** | **$32$** | **$42$** | **$15$** |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $-5$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $7$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the plane \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 25 \\\\ 3 & 32 \\\\ 4 & 42 \\\\ 5 & 15\\end{aligned}]}^{X}\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."]
 [:db/cas 17592186134389 :question/worked-solution "| $x$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the curve \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}^{3}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & {1}^{2} & {1}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} & {3}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 5 & {5}^{2} & {5}^{3}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector." "| $x$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the curve \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}^{3}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & {1}^{2} & {1}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} & {3}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 5 & {5}^{2} & {5}^{3}\\end{aligned}]}^{X}\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{aligned}]}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."]
 [:db/cas 17592186134390 :question/worked-solution "| $x$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the curve \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & {1}^{2} \\\\ 1 & {2}^{2} \\\\ 1 & {3}^{2} \\\\ 1 & {4}^{2} \\\\ 1 & {5}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector." "| $x$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the curve \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & {1}^{2} \\\\ 1 & {2}^{2} \\\\ 1 & {3}^{2} \\\\ 1 & {4}^{2} \\\\ 1 & {5}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{X}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."]
 [:db/cas 17592186134392 :question/worked-solution "| $x$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the curve \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}^{3}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & {1}^{2} & {1}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} & {3}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 5 & {5}^{2} & {5}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector." "| $x$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the curve \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}^{3}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & {1}^{2} & {1}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} & {3}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 5 & {5}^{2} & {5}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}}^{X}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."]
 [:db/cas 17592186135064 :tutorial/content "When carrying out a linear regression in two variables, say $x$ and $y,$ our task is to find a straight line that best fits a set of data points $(x_i,y_i).$ We can easily extend this technique to situations involving three variables. \n\nThe following table shows the number of units $x_1$ and $x_2$ (in hundreds) of two goods produced by a small company in several production runs and the respective profits $y$ (in thousands of dollars) obtained by the company.   \n\n| Good Type 1 $(x_1)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Good Type 2 $(x_2)$ | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$6$** |\n| Profit $(y)$ | $6$ | $15$ | $18$ | $24$ |\n\nSince we now have three variables, $x_1, x_2,$ and $y,$ our task is to find a *plane* of the form\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0+ \\beta_1x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data.\n\n![](images/ce/ced34ebf098fd20194fafd74f4f75bf855707ad1457e706264c9e922a7efa706.png)\n\n  \n\nSubstituting the values of $x_1$ and $x_2$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}+2{\\beta }_{2}=6 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+2{\\beta }_{1}+3{\\beta }_{2}=15 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+3{\\beta }_{1}+4{\\beta }_{2}=18 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+4{\\beta }_{1}+6{\\beta }_{2}=24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system can be expressed in matrix notation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 6\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAs usual, $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the coefficient vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. \n\nSince our points do not all lie on a plane, there will be no parameters $\\beta_0,$ $\\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_0,$ $\\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ that best approximate the solution.\n\nIn other words, we seek to find a column vector $\\boldsymbol\\beta$ such that\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\boldsymbol{\\beta}\\|\n$$\n\nis as small as possible.\n\nNotice that this is exactly the least-squares problem for the system $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}.$ Therefore, the coefficients of the plane that best fits the data can be found as the solution to the corresponding least-squares problem! \n\nPre-multiplying the equation $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}$ by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible, and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ into the formula above and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta }=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the plane of best fit (regression plane) is \n\n$$\ny = 2+ 7x_1-x_2.\n$$\n\n    \n\nNote that we can use the same technique to fit data involving more variables. For example, if we have four variables $x_1, x_2, x_3,$ and $y,$ then we can find a four-dimensional **hyperplane** of the form\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0+ \\beta_1x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2 + \\beta_3 x_3\n$$\n\nthat best fits a set of data points $(x_{1i},x_{2i}, x_{3i}, y).$  The coefficients of this hyperplane are found by solving the corresponding least-squares problem. \n\nFinally, applying linear regression to situations involving three or more variables is called **multiple linear regression**." "When carrying out a linear regression in two variables, say $x$ and $y,$ our task is to find a straight line that best fits a set of data points $(x_i,y_i).$ We can easily extend this technique to situations involving three variables. \n\nThe following table shows the number of units $x_1$ and $x_2$ (in hundreds) of two goods produced by a small company in several production runs and the respective profits $y$ (in thousands of dollars) obtained by the company.   \n\n| Good Type 1 $(x_1)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Good Type 2 $(x_2)$ | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$6$** |\n| Profit $(y)$ | $6$ | $15$ | $18$ | $24$ |\n\nSince we now have three variables, $x_1, x_2,$ and $y,$ our task is to find a *plane* of the form\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0+ \\beta_1x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data.\n\n![](images/ce/ced34ebf098fd20194fafd74f4f75bf855707ad1457e706264c9e922a7efa706.png)\n\n  \n\nSubstituting the values of $x_1$ and $x_2$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}+2{\\beta }_{2}=6 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+2{\\beta }_{1}+3{\\beta }_{2}=15 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+3{\\beta }_{1}+4{\\beta }_{2}=18 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+4{\\beta }_{1}+6{\\beta }_{2}=24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system can be expressed in matrix notation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{X}\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]}^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAs usual, $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the coefficient vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. \n\nSince our points do not all lie on a plane, there will be no parameters $\\beta_0,$ $\\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_0,$ $\\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ that best approximate the solution.\n\nIn other words, we seek to find a column vector $\\boldsymbol\\beta$ such that\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\boldsymbol{\\beta}\\|\n$$\n\nis as small as possible.\n\nNotice that this is exactly the least-squares problem for the system $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}.$ Therefore, the coefficients of the plane that best fits the data can be found as the solution to the corresponding least-squares problem! \n\nPre-multiplying the equation $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}$ by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible, and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ into the formula above and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta }=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the plane of best fit (regression plane) is \n\n$$\ny = 2+ 7x_1-x_2.\n$$\n\n    \n\nNote that we can use the same technique to fit data involving more variables. For example, if we have four variables $x_1, x_2, x_3,$ and $y,$ then we can find a four-dimensional **hyperplane** of the form\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0+ \\beta_1x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2 + \\beta_3 x_3\n$$\n\nthat best fits a set of data points $(x_{1i},x_{2i}, x_{3i}, y).$  The coefficients of this hyperplane are found by solving the corresponding least-squares problem. \n\nFinally, applying linear regression to situations involving three or more variables is called **multiple linear regression**."]
 [:db/cas 17592186135098 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown below:\n\n![](images/ee/ee731d95c111101559ad135429198dd7e0b14313272f0933b1c706006da2fbd2.svg)\n\nSuppose also that we are given the following system of equations that connects the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A{:}$  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{2}+3{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3}=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{3}-2{q}_{1}+{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHow do we find $R?$\nFirst, let's rewrite the vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3$ as a linear combination of the vectors $\\mathbf{q}_1, \\mathbf{q}_2, \\mathbf{q}_3 \\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/82/824c39c97bb115768856d672b39be8f65d8b4ecbc2ee5d6ccc9f4943594cbacd.svg)\n\nIn this new system, the coefficients of $\\mathbf{q}_1$ correspond to the first-row entries of $R,$ the coefficients of $\\mathbf{q}_2$ correspond to the second-row entries of $R,$ and the coefficients of $\\mathbf{q}_3$ correspond to the third-row entries of $R.$ Therefore,\n\n![](images/5b/5ba9887a4d9b47963af171e9275319a2564eca5a4ef8b3ef44978e2ff4b111d9.svg)\n\nTo see why this is, we can take the transpose of both sides of $A=QR{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =QR \\\\ {A}^{T} & =(QR)^{T} \\\\ {A}^{T} & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T} \\\\ \\overset{{A}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-\\,\\,\\,\\, & {a}_{1} & \\,\\,\\,\\,- \\\\ -\\,\\,\\,\\, & {a}_{2} & \\,\\,\\,\\,- \\\\ -\\,\\,\\,\\, & {a}_{3} & \\,\\,\\,\\,-\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{{R}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -4 & 4\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-\\,\\,\\,\\, & {q}_{1} & \\,\\,\\,\\,- \\\\ -\\,\\,\\,\\, & {q}_{2} & \\,\\,\\,\\,- \\\\ -\\,\\,\\,\\, & {q}_{3} & \\,\\,\\,\\,-\\end{aligned}]}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we were to expand this matrix product, we would obtain our system of equations." "Let's consider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown below:\n\n![](images/ee/ee731d95c111101559ad135429198dd7e0b14313272f0933b1c706006da2fbd2.svg)\n\nSuppose also that we are given the following system of equations that connects the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A{:}$  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{2}+3{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3}=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{3}-2{q}_{1}+{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHow do we find $R?$\nFirst, let's rewrite the vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3$ as a linear combination of the vectors $\\mathbf{q}_1, \\mathbf{q}_2, \\mathbf{q}_3 \\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/82/824c39c97bb115768856d672b39be8f65d8b4ecbc2ee5d6ccc9f4943594cbacd.svg)\n\nIn this new system, the coefficients of $\\mathbf{q}_1$ correspond to the first-row entries of $R,$ the coefficients of $\\mathbf{q}_2$ correspond to the second-row entries of $R,$ and the coefficients of $\\mathbf{q}_3$ correspond to the third-row entries of $R.$ Therefore,\n\n![](images/5b/5ba9887a4d9b47963af171e9275319a2564eca5a4ef8b3ef44978e2ff4b111d9.svg)\n\nTo see why this is, we can take the transpose of both sides of $A=QR{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =QR \\\\ {A}^{T} & =(QR)^{T} \\\\ {A}^{T} & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T} \\\\ \\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}-\\,\\,\\,\\, & {a}_{1} & \\,\\,\\,\\,- \\\\ -\\,\\,\\,\\, & {a}_{2} & \\,\\,\\,\\,- \\\\ -\\,\\,\\,\\, & {a}_{3} & \\,\\,\\,\\,-\\end{aligned}]}^{{A}^{T}} & =\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -4 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{{R}^{T}}\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}-\\,\\,\\,\\, & {q}_{1} & \\,\\,\\,\\,- \\\\ -\\,\\,\\,\\, & {q}_{2} & \\,\\,\\,\\,- \\\\ -\\,\\,\\,\\, & {q}_{3} & \\,\\,\\,\\,-\\end{aligned}]}^{{Q}^{T}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we were to expand this matrix product, we would obtain our system of equations."]
 [:db/cas 17592186135140 :tutorial/content "When proving Green's theorem, we typically start by assuming that $D$ is a so-called **simple region** (that is, a region that is both type I and type II). Then, Green's theorem is *extended* to include regions that are not simple.\n\nLet's suppose that we've proved that Green's theorem is true for type I and type II regions. How can we use this to show that Green's theorem is also true for regions containing holes?\n\nTo illustrate how this is done, let's consider the vector field $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j,$ and the line integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_{\\partial D} \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $D$ is the annular region below.\n\n![](images/57/57c22c3d7a92ce4a1a1a798f7e6a861ded2e9a6be4d8d98c36869fe29e2783d0.png)\n\nNote that $\\partial D= C_1\\cup C_2$ is positively oriented. \n\nNow, imagine splitting $D$ into two regions $D_1$ and $D_2$ by means of *cuts* along the segments $[-4, -2]$ and $[2,4]$ on the $x$-axis, as shown below \n\n![](images/e7/e7f0d41c3fb909073cdaf4ea23f4ab617032e0d24bdc8d9b9e3724e7d56abe3b.png)\n\nNotice that $D_1$ and $D_2$ are positively oriented type I regions. In addition, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1} & ={C}_{3}\\cup {C}_{8} \\\\ {C}_{2} & ={C}_{5}\\cup {C}_{10} \\\\ ∂{D}_{1} & ={C}_{3}\\cup {C}_{4}\\cup {C}_{5}\\cup {C}_{6}, \\\\ ∂{D}_{2} & ={C}_{8}\\cup (-{C}_{6})\\cup {C}_{10}\\cup (-{C}_{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the circulation of $\\mathbf F$ over $\\partial D$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}\\cup \\,{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{8}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{5}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{10}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & +{{{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{4}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{6}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{-{C}_{6}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r}_{⏟}}_{0} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{5}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{6}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & +{\\int }_{{C}_{8}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{6}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{10}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{-{C}_{4}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={∮}_{∂{D}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{∂{D}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={∮}_{∂{D}_{1}}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y+{∮}_{∂{D}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo summarize,\n\n$$\n\\oint \\limits_{\\partial D}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = \\iint_\\limits{D}\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is just Green's theorem for the region $D.$" "When proving Green's theorem, we typically start by assuming that $D$ is a so-called **simple region** (that is, a region that is both type I and type II). Then, Green's theorem is *extended* to include regions that are not simple.\n\nLet's suppose that we've proved that Green's theorem is true for type I and type II regions. How can we use this to show that Green's theorem is also true for regions containing holes?\n\nTo illustrate how this is done, let's consider the vector field $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j,$ and the line integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_{\\partial D} \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $D$ is the annular region below.\n\n![](images/57/57c22c3d7a92ce4a1a1a798f7e6a861ded2e9a6be4d8d98c36869fe29e2783d0.png)\n\nNote that $\\partial D= C_1\\cup C_2$ is positively oriented. \n\nNow, imagine splitting $D$ into two regions $D_1$ and $D_2$ by means of *cuts* along the segments $[-4, -2]$ and $[2,4]$ on the $x$-axis, as shown below \n\n![](images/e7/e7f0d41c3fb909073cdaf4ea23f4ab617032e0d24bdc8d9b9e3724e7d56abe3b.png)\n\nNotice that $D_1$ and $D_2$ are positively oriented type I regions. In addition, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1} & ={C}_{3}\\cup {C}_{8} \\\\ {C}_{2} & ={C}_{5}\\cup {C}_{10} \\\\ ∂{D}_{1} & ={C}_{3}\\cup {C}_{4}\\cup {C}_{5}\\cup {C}_{6}, \\\\ ∂{D}_{2} & ={C}_{8}\\cup (-{C}_{6})\\cup {C}_{10}\\cup (-{C}_{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the circulation of $\\mathbf F$ over $\\partial D$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}\\cup \\,{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{8}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{5}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{10}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & +\\underbrace{{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{4}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{6}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{-{C}_{6}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r}_{0} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{5}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{6}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & +{\\int }_{{C}_{8}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{6}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{10}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{-{C}_{4}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={∮}_{∂{D}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{∂{D}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={∮}_{∂{D}_{1}}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y+{∮}_{∂{D}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo summarize,\n\n$$\n\\oint \\limits_{\\partial D}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = \\iint_\\limits{D}\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is just Green's theorem for the region $D.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186135143 :tutorial/content "Let's find the least-squares solution to $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b},$ where $A = QR,$ and\n![](images/4e/4e2433312f3a424aa161d5aeec63df38787c43435a9c2af44ba6b3d63f9ef065.svg)\n\nPreviously, we showed that the normal equation corresponding to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b},$ where $A=QR,$ can be reduced to\n\n$$\nR \\mathbf{x} = Q^T \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our matrices into this equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x_2=2.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4(2) & =2 \\\\ 2{x}_{1} & =10 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}$ is ![](images/72/723835f5ecb612e7d79c29ada9cc05241ffd4f233e29c9ed9a643994580a8c36.svg)" "Let's find the least-squares solution to $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b},$ where $A = QR,$ and\n![](images/4e/4e2433312f3a424aa161d5aeec63df38787c43435a9c2af44ba6b3d63f9ef065.svg)\n\nPreviously, we showed that the normal equation corresponding to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b},$ where $A=QR,$ can be reduced to\n\n$$\nR \\mathbf{x} = Q^T \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our matrices into this equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{R}x & =\\overbrace{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{{Q}^{T}}\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}^{b} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x_2=2.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4(2) & =2 \\\\ 2{x}_{1} & =10 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}$ is ![](images/72/723835f5ecb612e7d79c29ada9cc05241ffd4f233e29c9ed9a643994580a8c36.svg)"]
 [:db/cas 17592186135145 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, we might need to place a constraint on the regression plane that forces it to pass through the origin. \n\nLet's once again consider our small company. The following table shows the number of units $x_1$ and $x_2$ (in hundreds) of two goods produced by the company in several production runs and the respective profits $y$ (in thousands of dollars) obtained by the company.   \n\n| Good Type 1 $(x_1)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Good Type 2 $(x_2)$ | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$6$** |\n| Profit $(y)$ | $6$ | $15$ | $18$ | $24$ |\n\nIt's known that the profit equals zero when the company does not produce any goods, so $y=0$ when $x_1=x_2 = 0.$ The company wishes to find a plane that best fits the data *subject to* the constraint that it should pass through the point $(0,0, 0).$\n\nTherefore, we want to find the plane\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_1 x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2\n$$\n\nthat best fits the given data points. \n\nSubstituting the values of $x_1$ and $x_2$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. \n\nThe rest of the arguments are the same as before. Since not all points lie on a plane, there will be no parameters $\\beta_1$ and $\\beta_2$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_1$ and $\\beta_2$ that best approximate the solution." "Sometimes, we might need to place a constraint on the regression plane that forces it to pass through the origin. \n\nLet's once again consider our small company. The following table shows the number of units $x_1$ and $x_2$ (in hundreds) of two goods produced by the company in several production runs and the respective profits $y$ (in thousands of dollars) obtained by the company.   \n\n| Good Type 1 $(x_1)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Good Type 2 $(x_2)$ | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$6$** |\n| Profit $(y)$ | $6$ | $15$ | $18$ | $24$ |\n\nIt's known that the profit equals zero when the company does not produce any goods, so $y=0$ when $x_1=x_2 = 0.$ The company wishes to find a plane that best fits the data *subject to* the constraint that it should pass through the point $(0,0, 0).$\n\nTherefore, we want to find the plane\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_1 x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2\n$$\n\nthat best fits the given data points. \n\nSubstituting the values of $x_1$ and $x_2$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{X}\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. \n\nThe rest of the arguments are the same as before. Since not all points lie on a plane, there will be no parameters $\\beta_1$ and $\\beta_2$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_1$ and $\\beta_2$ that best approximate the solution."]
 [:db/cas 17592186135162 :tutorial/content "Calculating the circulation of a vector field along a closed, piecewise smooth curve $C$ can be time-consuming because it often requires splitting $C$ into several parts, evaluating a line integral along each one, and calculating their sum. \n\nHowever, in such cases, we can often use Stokes' theorem to reduce the amount of work required by converting this sum of line integrals to a single surface integral. \n\nFor example, suppose that $C$ is the intersection of the paraboloid $z=x^2 + y^2$ and the boundary of the infinite vertical column enclosed by the planes $x = \\pm 1$ and $y=\\pm 1.$ Suppose also that $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above, and \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F} = \\bigg\\langle 0, \\: \\dfrac{1}{2}z^2, \\: xz \\bigg\\rangle.\n$$\n\nLet's use Stokes' theorem to find the circulation of $\\mathbf{F}$ along $C$.\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}\n= \\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy computing the curl of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n![](images/fd/fd25c80fd757477b724b320d1941c57630ec1c64f36bdb44d4c1c70c526fa2a9.svg)\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨-z,\\,-z,\\,0⟩{|}_{z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨{{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{0}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr & ={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot 2x+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot 2y+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2({x}^{3}+{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}+{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/dc/dcf7ba14ac2c0f3da12efed6d539817ab1708273f5e8e665fdde14fee28bb4e3.png)\n\nEvaluating this integral using the usual methods, we get\n\n$$\n2\\int_{-1}^{1} \\int_{-1}^{1} x^3 + x^2 y + x y^2 + y^3 \\: \\textrm{d}y  \\: \\textrm{d}x = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = 0.\n$$\n\n**Note**: Stokes theorem tells us that \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\n depends only on the circulation of $\\mathbf F$ at the boundary of $S.$ Therefore, we could have evaluated our line integral by selecting *any* surface with $C$ as its bounding curve and integrating $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F$ over this surface." "Calculating the circulation of a vector field along a closed, piecewise smooth curve $C$ can be time-consuming because it often requires splitting $C$ into several parts, evaluating a line integral along each one, and calculating their sum. \n\nHowever, in such cases, we can often use Stokes' theorem to reduce the amount of work required by converting this sum of line integrals to a single surface integral. \n\nFor example, suppose that $C$ is the intersection of the paraboloid $z=x^2 + y^2$ and the boundary of the infinite vertical column enclosed by the planes $x = \\pm 1$ and $y=\\pm 1.$ Suppose also that $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above, and \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F} = \\bigg\\langle 0, \\: \\dfrac{1}{2}z^2, \\: xz \\bigg\\rangle.\n$$\n\nLet's use Stokes' theorem to find the circulation of $\\mathbf{F}$ along $C$.\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}\n= \\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy computing the curl of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n![](images/fd/fd25c80fd757477b724b320d1941c57630ec1c64f36bdb44d4c1c70c526fa2a9.svg)\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨-z,\\,-z,\\,0⟩{|}_{z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{0}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr & ={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot 2x+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot 2y+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2({x}^{3}+{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}+{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/dc/dcf7ba14ac2c0f3da12efed6d539817ab1708273f5e8e665fdde14fee28bb4e3.png)\n\nEvaluating this integral using the usual methods, we get\n\n$$\n2\\int_{-1}^{1} \\int_{-1}^{1} x^3 + x^2 y + x y^2 + y^3 \\: \\textrm{d}y  \\: \\textrm{d}x = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = 0.\n$$\n\n**Note**: Stokes theorem tells us that \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\n depends only on the circulation of $\\mathbf F$ at the boundary of $S.$ Therefore, we could have evaluated our line integral by selecting *any* surface with $C$ as its bounding curve and integrating $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F$ over this surface."]
 [:db/cas 17592186135184 :tutorial/content "Cubing a negative number *always* results in another negative number.\n\nThis is because a negative times a negative gives a positive value, and then a positive times a negative is a negative again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-)\\,\\cdot \\,(-)\\,\\cdot \\,(-)\\,=\\,{{(-)\\,\\cdot \\,(-)}_{⏟}}_{(+)}\\,\\cdot \\,(-)\\,=\\,(+)\\,\\cdot \\,(-)\\,=\\,(-)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n({\\color{red}-5})^3 = \\underbrace{({\\color{red}-5}) \\cdot ({\\color{red}-5})}_{\\large {\\color{blue}25}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}-5}) = {\\color{blue}25} \\cdot ({\\color{red}-5}) = {\\color{red}-125}.\n$$" "Cubing a negative number *always* results in another negative number.\n\nThis is because a negative times a negative gives a positive value, and then a positive times a negative is a negative again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-)\\,\\cdot \\,(-)\\,\\cdot \\,(-)\\,=\\,\\underbrace{(-)\\,\\cdot \\,(-)}_{(+)}\\,\\cdot \\,(-)\\,=\\,(+)\\,\\cdot \\,(-)\\,=\\,(-)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n({\\color{red}-5})^3 = \\underbrace{({\\color{red}-5}) \\cdot ({\\color{red}-5})}_{\\large {\\color{blue}25}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}-5}) = {\\color{blue}25} \\cdot ({\\color{red}-5}) = {\\color{red}-125}.\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186138135 :question/worked-solution "We need to evaluate $4^2.$\n\nTo evaluate $4^2,$ we write it out with multiplication signs and then compute the product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{2}={{4\\times 4}_{⏟}}_{\\text{[math]2[/math] times}}=16\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We need to evaluate $4^2.$\n\nTo evaluate $4^2,$ we write it out with multiplication signs and then compute the product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{2}=\\underbrace{4\\times 4}_{\\text{[math]2[/math] times}}=16\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186138136 :question/worked-solution "Here, $5$ raised to the power of $3$ means $5^3.$\n\nTo compute this power, we start by writing it out with multiplication signs:\n![](images/e7/e7beecad2379c3be9452eb111496ed8c65a5d4f747874658167169bba6ff11c9.svg)\n\nNow, we compute the product. Since $5 \\times 5 = 25,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{5\\times 5}_{⏟}}_{25}\\times 5 & =25\\times 5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we work out ${\\color{blue}25} \\times 5.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,{}_{2}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $5^3=125.$" "Here, $5$ raised to the power of $3$ means $5^3.$\n\nTo compute this power, we start by writing it out with multiplication signs:\n![](images/e7/e7beecad2379c3be9452eb111496ed8c65a5d4f747874658167169bba6ff11c9.svg)\n\nNow, we compute the product. Since $5 \\times 5 = 25,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{5\\times 5}_{25}\\times 5 & =25\\times 5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we work out ${\\color{blue}25} \\times 5.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,{}_{2}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $5^3=125.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186138714 :question/worked-solution "Here, ${0}^{4}$ is in exponent form. The base is $0$ and the exponent is $4.$\n\nThis means that $0$ is taken as a factor $4$ times. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{0}^{4}={{0\\times 0\\times 0\\times 0}_{⏟}}_{4\\text{ times}}=0.\n$$" "Here, ${0}^{4}$ is in exponent form. The base is $0$ and the exponent is $4.$\n\nThis means that $0$ is taken as a factor $4$ times. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{0}^{4}=\\underbrace{0\\times 0\\times 0\\times 0}_{4\\text{ times}}=0.\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186139743 :tutorial/content "Let's evaluate the following exponent:\n\n$$\n2^{\\color{red}{3}}\n$$\n\nWe start by writing our exponent as repeated multiplication:\n![](images/fe/fea572ac021fd3eabd4d5b1ade6692cbb1507d993b16616f63b8aa78259d3ade.svg)\n\nNow, we compute the product. \n\nSince $2 \\times 2 = 4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{2\\times 2}_{⏟}}_{4}\\times 2 & =4\\times 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${\\color{blue}{4}}\\times 2=8,$ we have $2^3=8.$\n\nRaising a number to the power of $3$ is known as **cubing** a number. Therefore, the following are equivalent.\n\n- $2^3 = 8$\n\n- The **cube** of $2$ is $8.$\n\n- $2$ raised to the *third* power equals $8.$" "Let's evaluate the following exponent:\n\n$$\n2^{\\color{red}{3}}\n$$\n\nWe start by writing our exponent as repeated multiplication:\n![](images/fe/fea572ac021fd3eabd4d5b1ade6692cbb1507d993b16616f63b8aa78259d3ade.svg)\n\nNow, we compute the product. \n\nSince $2 \\times 2 = 4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{2\\times 2}_{4}\\times 2 & =4\\times 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${\\color{blue}{4}}\\times 2=8,$ we have $2^3=8.$\n\nRaising a number to the power of $3$ is known as **cubing** a number. Therefore, the following are equivalent.\n\n- $2^3 = 8$\n\n- The **cube** of $2$ is $8.$\n\n- $2$ raised to the *third* power equals $8.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186142712 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that the volume of a rectangular package can be calculated as\n\n$$\nV = \\text{length} \\times \\text{width} \\times \\text{height}\n$$\n\nand we are told that this volume is equal to $75 \\, \\text{in}^3$.\n\n![](images/32/32798500f85c9bc6dfc97b588d787ec9befd8a4f5b52aa0bc482588f71ca989f.png)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n$75 = \\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}\\times 6 \\times 2.5$\n\nSo, since\n\n$$\n6 \\times 2.5 = 15\n$$\n\n, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n75 &= [math]\\,\\phantom{0}\\,[/math] \\cdot 6 \\cdot 2.5_(⏟)_(15) \\\\\n75 &= [math]\\,\\phantom{0}\\,[/math] \\cdot 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the relationship between multiplication and division, we obtain that the length of the package must be\n$\\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}} = 75 \\div 15 = 5 \\, \\text{in}.$" "First, recall that the volume of a rectangular package can be calculated as\n\n$$\nV = \\text{length} \\times \\text{width} \\times \\text{height}\n$$\n\nand we are told that this volume is equal to $75 \\, \\text{in}^3$.\n\n![](images/32/32798500f85c9bc6dfc97b588d787ec9befd8a4f5b52aa0bc482588f71ca989f.png)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n$75 = \\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}\\times 6 \\times 2.5$\n\nSo, since\n\n$$\n6 \\times 2.5 = 15\n$$\n\n, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n75 &= [math]\\,\\phantom{0}\\,[/math] \\cdot \\underbrace{6 \\cdot 2.5}_{15} \\\\\n75 &= [math]\\,\\phantom{0}\\,[/math] \\cdot 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the relationship between multiplication and division, we obtain that the length of the package must be\n$\\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}} = 75 \\div 15 = 5 \\, \\text{in}.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186144088 :tutorial/content "When an expression contains a mix of addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division, the order of operations tells us:\n\n- First, we *multiply* and *divide* from left to right.\n- Then, we *add* and *subtract* from left to right.\n\nLet's use the order of operations to evaluate the expression $18 - 9 \\div 3$.\n\nFirst, we multiply and divide, from left to right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n18 - 9 \\div 3_(⏟)_(1st) &= 18 - 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we add and subtract from left to right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n18 - 3 &= 15✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** In this question, it's really important that we use the order of operations. If we only solve from left to right, we get an incorrect result:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {\\color{red}18 - 9} \\div  3 & \\displaystyle  = \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\color{red}9} \\div  3 & \\displaystyle  = \\\\ \\displaystyle 3 & \\displaystyle  \\phantom{=} {\\color{red}\\times }\\end{array}}\n$$" "When an expression contains a mix of addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division, the order of operations tells us:\n\n- First, we *multiply* and *divide* from left to right.\n- Then, we *add* and *subtract* from left to right.\n\nLet's use the order of operations to evaluate the expression $18 - 9 \\div 3$.\n\nFirst, we multiply and divide, from left to right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n18 - \\underbrace{9 \\div 3}_{1\\text{st}} &= 18 - 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we add and subtract from left to right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n18 - 3 &= 15✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** In this question, it's really important that we use the order of operations. If we only solve from left to right, we get an incorrect result:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {\\color{red}18 - 9} \\div  3 & \\displaystyle  = \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\color{red}9} \\div  3 & \\displaystyle  = \\\\ \\displaystyle 3 & \\displaystyle  \\phantom{=} {\\color{red}\\times }\\end{array}}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186147170 :question/worked-solution "Interpreting the fractions as division, we get\n\n$$\n(12\\,{\\color{red}{\\div\\, 7}})\\times(21\\,{\\color{blue}{\\div\\, 4}}).\n$$\n\n \n\nSince our expression contains multiplication and division only, we can create an equivalent expression by swapping the ${\\color{red}{\\div\\, 7}}$ and ${\\color{blue}{\\div\\, 4}}$:\n\n$$\n(12\\,{\\color{blue}{\\div\\, 4}})\\times(21\\,{\\color{red}{\\div\\, 7}})\n$$\n\n \n\nInterpreting the divisions as fractions, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{12}{4}\\times\\dfrac{21}{7}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the divisions, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\frac{12}{4}}_{⏟}}_{3}\\times {{\\frac{21}{7}}_{⏟}}_{3}=3\\times 3=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Interpreting the fractions as division, we get\n\n$$\n(12\\,{\\color{red}{\\div\\, 7}})\\times(21\\,{\\color{blue}{\\div\\, 4}}).\n$$\n\n \n\nSince our expression contains multiplication and division only, we can create an equivalent expression by swapping the ${\\color{red}{\\div\\, 7}}$ and ${\\color{blue}{\\div\\, 4}}$:\n\n$$\n(12\\,{\\color{blue}{\\div\\, 4}})\\times(21\\,{\\color{red}{\\div\\, 7}})\n$$\n\n \n\nInterpreting the divisions as fractions, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{12}{4}\\times\\dfrac{21}{7}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the divisions, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{\\frac{12}{4}}_{3}\\times \\underbrace{\\frac{21}{7}}_{3}=3\\times 3=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186147171 :question/worked-solution "We proceed in two steps:\n\n- First, we write $\\left(\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right)^2$ with multiplication signs:\n$(\\frac{4}{5})^{2}|= \\frac{4}{5} \\cdot \\frac{4}{5}_(⏟)_([math]{\\color{blue}{2}}[/math] times)$\n- Then, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n$(\\frac{4}{5})^{2}|= \\frac{4}{5} \\cdot \\frac{4}{5}; = \\frac{4 \\cdot 4}{5 \\cdot 5}; = \\frac{16}{25}$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right)^2= \\dfrac{16}{25}\n$$" "We proceed in two steps:\n\n- First, we write $\\left(\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right)^2$ with multiplication signs:\n$(\\frac{4}{5})^{2}|= \\underbrace{\\frac{4}{5} \\cdot \\frac{4}{5}}_{{{\\color{blue}{2}}}\\text{ times}}$\n- Then, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n$(\\frac{4}{5})^{2}|= \\frac{4}{5} \\cdot \\frac{4}{5}; = \\frac{4 \\cdot 4}{5 \\cdot 5}; = \\frac{16}{25}$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right)^2= \\dfrac{16}{25}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186147172 :question/worked-solution "We proceed in two steps:\n\n- First, we write $\\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\right)^3$ with multiplication signs:\n$(\\frac{3}{5})^{3}|= \\frac{3}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{5}_(⏟)_([math]{\\color{blue}{3}}[/math] times)$\n- Then, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n$(\\frac{3}{5})^{3}|= \\frac{3}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{5}; = \\frac{3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3}{5 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 5}; = \\frac{9 \\cdot 3}{25 \\cdot 5}; = \\frac{27}{125}$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\right)^3=\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue} \\dfrac{27}{125}}\n$$" "We proceed in two steps:\n\n- First, we write $\\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\right)^3$ with multiplication signs:\n$(\\frac{3}{5})^{3}|= \\underbrace{\\frac{3}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{5}}_{{{\\color{blue}{3}}}\\text{ times}}$\n- Then, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n$(\\frac{3}{5})^{3}|= \\frac{3}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{5}; = \\frac{3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3}{5 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 5}; = \\frac{9 \\cdot 3}{25 \\cdot 5}; = \\frac{27}{125}$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\right)^3=\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue} \\dfrac{27}{125}}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186147183 :question/worked-solution "First, by writing the expression with multiplication signs, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(0.5)^{3} &= 0.5 \\cdot 0.5 \\cdot 0.5_(⏟)_([math]{\\color{blue}{3}}[/math] times)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute this product, we first ignore any decimal points and multiply the factors just as we would with whole numbers. We also ignore any leading zeros during the multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{5 \\times 5 \\times 5}_{\\large { { {\\color{blue}3}} \\text{ times}}} = 125\n$$\n\nWe now count the number of decimal places in the $\\color{blue}3$ factors.\n\nThere is ${\\color{purple}{1}}$ decimal place in $0.5$.\nTherefore, the exponent $(0.5)^{\\color{blue}3}$ will have\n\n$$\n{\\color{purple}{1}}\\times {\\color{blue}{3}} = \\color{red}3\n$$\n\ndecimal places.\n\nSo, we take our value of $125$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $\\color{red}3$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n0\\,\\overset{\\color{red}\\downarrow}{\\color{red}\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{.}}\\!\\!\\!\\underbrace{125}_{\\large{ { {\\color{red}3}} \\text{ digits}}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n(0.5)^3 = 0.125\n$$" "First, by writing the expression with multiplication signs, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(0.5)^{3} &= \\underbrace{0.5 \\cdot 0.5 \\cdot 0.5}_{{{\\color{blue}{3}}}\\text{ times}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute this product, we first ignore any decimal points and multiply the factors just as we would with whole numbers. We also ignore any leading zeros during the multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{5 \\times 5 \\times 5}_{\\large { { {\\color{blue}3}} \\text{ times}}} = 125\n$$\n\nWe now count the number of decimal places in the $\\color{blue}3$ factors.\n\nThere is ${\\color{purple}{1}}$ decimal place in $0.5$.\nTherefore, the exponent $(0.5)^{\\color{blue}3}$ will have\n\n$$\n{\\color{purple}{1}}\\times {\\color{blue}{3}} = \\color{red}3\n$$\n\ndecimal places.\n\nSo, we take our value of $125$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $\\color{red}3$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n0\\,\\overset{\\color{red}\\downarrow}{\\color{red}\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{.}}\\!\\!\\!\\underbrace{125}_{\\large{ { {\\color{red}3}} \\text{ digits}}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n(0.5)^3 = 0.125\n$$"]
