 [:db/cas 17592186198859 :question/worked-solution "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nWe have the following grammatical constructions for $S⇒N:$\n\n- $S$ is sufficient for $N$\n\n- For $N,$ it is sufficient that $S$\n\n- $N$ is necessary for $S$\n\n- For $S,$ it is necessary that $N$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option I reads\n\n$$\n{{6\\,|\\,x}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\\,\\text{is sufficient for}\\,{{3\\,|\\,x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $P(x)⇒Q(x).$ $\\,✓$\n\n- Option II reads\n\n$$\n\\text{for}\\,{{3\\,|\\,x,}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,\\text{it is sufficient that}\\,{{6\\,|\\,x}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $P(x)⇒Q(x).$ $\\,✓$\n\n- Option III reads\n\n$$\n{{6\\,|\\,x}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\\,\\text{is necessary for}\\,{{3\\,|\\,x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $Q(x)⇒P(x).$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- Option IV reads\n\n$$\n\\text{for}\\,{{6\\,|\\,x,}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\\,\\text{it is necessary that}\\,{{3\\,|\\,x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $P(x)⇒Q(x).$ $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and IV only.\"" "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nWe have the following grammatical constructions for $S⇒N:$\n\n- $S$ is sufficient for $N$\n\n- For $N,$ it is sufficient that $S$\n\n- $N$ is necessary for $S$\n\n- For $S,$ it is necessary that $N$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option I reads\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{6\\,|\\,x}_{P(x)}\\,\\text{is sufficient for}\\,\\underbrace{3\\,|\\,x}_{Q(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $P(x)⇒Q(x).$ $\\,✓$\n\n- Option II reads\n\n$$\n\\text{for}\\,\\underbrace{3\\,|\\,x,}_{Q(x)}\\,\\text{it is sufficient that}\\,\\underbrace{6\\,|\\,x}_{P(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $P(x)⇒Q(x).$ $\\,✓$\n\n- Option III reads\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{6\\,|\\,x}_{P(x)}\\,\\text{is necessary for}\\,\\underbrace{3\\,|\\,x}_{Q(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $Q(x)⇒P(x).$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- Option IV reads\n\n$$\n\\text{for}\\,\\underbrace{6\\,|\\,x,}_{P(x)}\\,\\text{it is necessary that}\\,\\underbrace{3\\,|\\,x}_{Q(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $P(x)⇒Q(x).$ $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and IV only.\""]
 [:db/cas 17592186199493 :tutorial/content "**Measuring** means finding a number that describes the size of something. In particular, we determine the duration between two events by measuring the **time** between those events. We use **time units** for this purpose.\n\nThe most popular measurement system within the scientific community is the **metric system**. Within the metric system, the most common units of measurement are called **base units**.\n\nThe base unit for time is the **second**, which we denote using the symbol \"$\\textrm{s}.$\" We measure the time between two events using clocks like the ones shown below.\n\n![](images/93/93026f119d483006ac3e2a58c4b31d68f6a25618afc0b998da74f4914ca1c1c7.png)\n\nWe use **milliseconds** to measure smaller time intervals. The symbol for milliseconds is \"$\\textrm{ms}.$\" The prefix $\\textrm m$ stands for **milli,** which means $\\dfrac{1}{1\\,000}.$ Therefore, $1\\,\\textrm{ms}$ equals $\\dfrac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\textrm s.$\n\nNotice that if we add $1\\,\\textrm{ms}$ to itself $1\\,000$ times, we get exactly $1\\,\\textrm{s}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{1\\,000\\text{ times}}{\\overset{⏞}{1\\,\\text{ms}+1\\,\\text{ms}+⋯+1\\,\\text{ms}}} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{s}+\\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{s}+⋯+\\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{s} & = \\\\ 1\\,000\\times \\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{s} & = \\\\ \\frac{1\\,000\\times 1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{s} & = \\\\ \\frac{1\\,000}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{s} & = \\\\ 1\\,\\text{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{ms}$ equals $1\\,\\textrm{s},$ as stated in the diagram.\n\nOne millisecond is a very short time interval. To put this into context, a single blink of a human eye is around $200$ milliseconds on average." "**Measuring** means finding a number that describes the size of something. In particular, we determine the duration between two events by measuring the **time** between those events. We use **time units** for this purpose.\n\nThe most popular measurement system within the scientific community is the **metric system**. Within the metric system, the most common units of measurement are called **base units**.\n\nThe base unit for time is the **second**, which we denote using the symbol \"$\\textrm{s}.$\" We measure the time between two events using clocks like the ones shown below.\n\n![](images/93/93026f119d483006ac3e2a58c4b31d68f6a25618afc0b998da74f4914ca1c1c7.png)\n\nWe use **milliseconds** to measure smaller time intervals. The symbol for milliseconds is \"$\\textrm{ms}.$\" The prefix $\\textrm m$ stands for **milli,** which means $\\dfrac{1}{1\\,000}.$ Therefore, $1\\,\\textrm{ms}$ equals $\\dfrac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\textrm s.$\n\nNotice that if we add $1\\,\\textrm{ms}$ to itself $1\\,000$ times, we get exactly $1\\,\\textrm{s}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{1\\,\\text{ms}+1\\,\\text{ms}+⋯+1\\,\\text{ms}}^{1\\,000\\text{ times}} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{s}+\\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{s}+⋯+\\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{s} & = \\\\ 1\\,000\\times \\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{s} & = \\\\ \\frac{1\\,000\\times 1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{s} & = \\\\ \\frac{1\\,000}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{s} & = \\\\ 1\\,\\text{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{ms}$ equals $1\\,\\textrm{s},$ as stated in the diagram.\n\nOne millisecond is a very short time interval. To put this into context, a single blink of a human eye is around $200$ milliseconds on average."]
 [:db/cas 17592186199494 :tutorial/content "**Measuring** means finding a number that describes the size of something. The most popular measurement system within the scientific community is the **metric system**. \n\nWe determine how much space an object occupies by measuring its **volume**. We use **volume units** to specify an object's volume. \n\nA common metric unit of volume is the **liter,** which we denote using the symbol \"$\\textrm{L}$.\" We can use **measuring jugs** like the ones below to measure the volume of a liquid.\n\n![](images/fa/fa15e2d9b446412876732435aa06b8117fbb4903ab179e4cc64c50da4868fee7.png)\n\nWe use **milliliters** to measure smaller volumes. The symbol for milliliters is \"$\\textrm{mL}.$\" The prefix $\\textrm m$ stands for **milli,** which means $\\dfrac{1}{1\\,000}.$ Therefore, $1\\,\\textrm{mL}$ equals $\\dfrac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\textrm L.$\n\nNotice that if we add $1\\,\\textrm{mL}$ to itself $1\\,000$ times, we get exactly $1\\,\\textrm{L}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{1\\,000\\text{ times}}{\\overset{⏞}{1\\,\\text{mL}+1\\,\\text{mL}+⋯+1\\,\\text{mL}}} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{L}+\\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{L}+⋯+\\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{L} & = \\\\ 1\\,000\\times \\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{L} & = \\\\ \\frac{1\\,000\\times 1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{L} & = \\\\ \\frac{1\\,000}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{L} & = \\\\ 1\\,\\text{L}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mL}$ equals $1\\,\\textrm{L},$ as stated in the diagram." "**Measuring** means finding a number that describes the size of something. The most popular measurement system within the scientific community is the **metric system**. \n\nWe determine how much space an object occupies by measuring its **volume**. We use **volume units** to specify an object's volume. \n\nA common metric unit of volume is the **liter,** which we denote using the symbol \"$\\textrm{L}$.\" We can use **measuring jugs** like the ones below to measure the volume of a liquid.\n\n![](images/fa/fa15e2d9b446412876732435aa06b8117fbb4903ab179e4cc64c50da4868fee7.png)\n\nWe use **milliliters** to measure smaller volumes. The symbol for milliliters is \"$\\textrm{mL}.$\" The prefix $\\textrm m$ stands for **milli,** which means $\\dfrac{1}{1\\,000}.$ Therefore, $1\\,\\textrm{mL}$ equals $\\dfrac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\textrm L.$\n\nNotice that if we add $1\\,\\textrm{mL}$ to itself $1\\,000$ times, we get exactly $1\\,\\textrm{L}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{1\\,\\text{mL}+1\\,\\text{mL}+⋯+1\\,\\text{mL}}^{1\\,000\\text{ times}} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{L}+\\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{L}+⋯+\\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{L} & = \\\\ 1\\,000\\times \\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{L} & = \\\\ \\frac{1\\,000\\times 1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{L} & = \\\\ \\frac{1\\,000}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{L} & = \\\\ 1\\,\\text{L}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mL}$ equals $1\\,\\textrm{L},$ as stated in the diagram."]
 [:db/cas 17592186199501 :tutorial/content "Recall that the quadratic Taylor polynomial of a function $f(x)$ at the point $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\approx  \\overbrace{f(a) + f'(a)(x-a)}^{\\text{Linear approximation}} + \\underbrace{\\dfrac{1}{2}f''(a)(x-a)^2}_{\\textrm{Quadratic part}}.\n$$\n\nWe can extend the concept of Taylor polynomials to multivariable functions. \n\nGiven a twice-differentiable function $f(x,y),$ the **quadratic Taylor polynomial** of $f$ about the point $(a,b)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(a,b)+{f}_{x}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)+{f}_{y}(a,b)\\cdot (y-b)}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)(y-b)+{f}_{yy}(a,b)\\cdot (y-b)^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn particular, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f(x,y)$ about the origin $O$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y \\\\  & \\,\\,+\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLater in the lesson, we will develop some intuition for why the quadratic part looks the way it does. But before we do that, let's get some practice working with this formula." "Recall that the quadratic Taylor polynomial of a function $f(x)$ at the point $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\approx  \\overbrace{f(a) + f'(a)(x-a)}^{\\text{Linear approximation}} + \\underbrace{\\dfrac{1}{2}f''(a)(x-a)^2}_{\\textrm{Quadratic part}}.\n$$\n\nWe can extend the concept of Taylor polynomials to multivariable functions. \n\nGiven a twice-differentiable function $f(x,y),$ the **quadratic Taylor polynomial** of $f$ about the point $(a,b)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overbrace{f(a,b)+{f}_{x}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)+{f}_{y}(a,b)\\cdot (y-b)}^{\\text{Linear approximation}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+\\underbrace{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)(y-b)+{f}_{yy}(a,b)\\cdot (y-b)^{2})}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn particular, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f(x,y)$ about the origin $O$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y \\\\  & \\,\\,+\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLater in the lesson, we will develop some intuition for why the quadratic part looks the way it does. But before we do that, let's get some practice working with this formula."]
 [:db/cas 17592186199502 :tutorial/content "It's possible to derive Taylor polynomials for functions of more than two variables. However, increasing the number of variables significantly increases the number of terms in the Taylor polynomial. \n\nIdeally, we'd like a compact expression for the quadratic Taylor polynomial that works for all twice-differentiable functions $f:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R.$ This can be achieved using matrix notation. \n\nTo see how, let's consider the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f(x,y)$ about $(a,b){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(a,b)+{f}_{x}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)+{f}_{y}(a,b)\\cdot (y-b)}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)(y-b)+{f}_{yy}(a,b)\\cdot (y-b)^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote ![](images/6f/6fe1832d2f4ae2b36446386e5d0ec7c41f898f4e575af9238695c37f7c427250.svg) and ![](images/49/496eacb98651e3fab4c0d0a7b44ab283fadad1c07243da485aeb0c4a059d5712.svg) then the total derivative of $f(x,y)=f(\\mathbf{x})$ at $(a,b)=\\mathbf{a}$ is given by\n\n![](images/d6/d696700e295d9f43111ac53602b936a991b7766af654cedaa5afdae21b088a59.svg)\n\nWith that in mind, we can rewrite the linear part of the approximation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Linear approximation} & =f(a,b)+{f}_{x}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)+{f}_{y}(a,b)\\cdot (y-b) \\\\  & =f(a)+[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(a,b) & {f}_{y}(a,b)\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x-a \\\\ y-b\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =f(a)+[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(a,b) & {f}_{y}(a,b)\\end{aligned}]\\cdot ([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this is just our usual linear approximation of a function $f.$\n\nThe second derivative of $f$ at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a$ is given by\n\n![](images/de/de2a75a6681e1e393b21b60de9d7aeee748a8e6d04010ab713b40f0f41f239cf.svg)\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $f_{xy} = f_{yx},$ we can rewrite the quadratic part of the approximation using matrix multiplication as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Quadratic part} & =\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)(y-b)+{f}_{yy}(a,b)\\cdot (y-b)^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}x-a & y-b\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(a) & {f}_{xy}(a) \\\\ {f}_{yx}(a) & {f}_{yy}(a)\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x-a \\\\ y-b\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}([\\begin{aligned}x & y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}a & b\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(a) & {f}_{xy}(a) \\\\ {f}_{yx}(a) & {f}_{yy}(a)\\end{aligned}]\\cdot ([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our quadratic Taylor polynomial can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)}}+{{\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this formula is more compact and has clear similarity with the corresponding formula for functions with one variable:\n\n$$\nf(x) \\approx  \\overbrace{f(a) + f'(a)(x-a)}^{\\text{Linear approximation}} + \\underbrace{\\dfrac{1}{2}f''(a)(x-a)^2}_{\\textrm{Quadratic part}}\n$$" "It's possible to derive Taylor polynomials for functions of more than two variables. However, increasing the number of variables significantly increases the number of terms in the Taylor polynomial. \n\nIdeally, we'd like a compact expression for the quadratic Taylor polynomial that works for all twice-differentiable functions $f:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R.$ This can be achieved using matrix notation. \n\nTo see how, let's consider the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f(x,y)$ about $(a,b){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overbrace{f(a,b)+{f}_{x}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)+{f}_{y}(a,b)\\cdot (y-b)}^{\\text{Linear approximation}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+\\underbrace{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)(y-b)+{f}_{yy}(a,b)\\cdot (y-b)^{2})}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote ![](images/6f/6fe1832d2f4ae2b36446386e5d0ec7c41f898f4e575af9238695c37f7c427250.svg) and ![](images/49/496eacb98651e3fab4c0d0a7b44ab283fadad1c07243da485aeb0c4a059d5712.svg) then the total derivative of $f(x,y)=f(\\mathbf{x})$ at $(a,b)=\\mathbf{a}$ is given by\n\n![](images/d6/d696700e295d9f43111ac53602b936a991b7766af654cedaa5afdae21b088a59.svg)\n\nWith that in mind, we can rewrite the linear part of the approximation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Linear approximation} & =f(a,b)+{f}_{x}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)+{f}_{y}(a,b)\\cdot (y-b) \\\\  & =f(a)+[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(a,b) & {f}_{y}(a,b)\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x-a \\\\ y-b\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =f(a)+[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(a,b) & {f}_{y}(a,b)\\end{aligned}]\\cdot ([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this is just our usual linear approximation of a function $f.$\n\nThe second derivative of $f$ at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a$ is given by\n\n![](images/de/de2a75a6681e1e393b21b60de9d7aeee748a8e6d04010ab713b40f0f41f239cf.svg)\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $f_{xy} = f_{yx},$ we can rewrite the quadratic part of the approximation using matrix multiplication as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Quadratic part} & =\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)(y-b)+{f}_{yy}(a,b)\\cdot (y-b)^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}x-a & y-b\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(a) & {f}_{xy}(a) \\\\ {f}_{yx}(a) & {f}_{yy}(a)\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x-a \\\\ y-b\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}([\\begin{aligned}x & y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}a & b\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(a) & {f}_{xy}(a) \\\\ {f}_{yx}(a) & {f}_{yy}(a)\\end{aligned}]\\cdot ([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our quadratic Taylor polynomial can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈\\overbrace{f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)}^{\\text{Linear approximation}}+\\underbrace{\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a)}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this formula is more compact and has clear similarity with the corresponding formula for functions with one variable:\n\n$$\nf(x) \\approx  \\overbrace{f(a) + f'(a)(x-a)}^{\\text{Linear approximation}} + \\underbrace{\\dfrac{1}{2}f''(a)(x-a)^2}_{\\textrm{Quadratic part}}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186199514 :tutorial/content "Let's recall the table that relates metric units of mass.\n\n| Name | Symbol | Conversion |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Kilogram | $\\textrm{kg}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{kg} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{g}$ |\n| Gram | $\\textrm{g}$ | Base Unit |\n| Milligram | $\\textrm{mg}$ | $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mg} = 1\\,\\textrm{g}$ |\n\n**Important**: Symbols in the metric system follow a consistent set of **prefix rules**, which we explain below. If you find these difficult, *do not worry*! For now, you just need to remember the conversions shown in the table above. \n\nThe prefix rules are as follows:\n\n- The prefix \"$\\textrm k$\" stands for **kilo**, which means $1\\,000$. Therefore, $1\\,\\textrm{kg} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{g}.$\n\n- The prefix \"$\\textrm m$\" stands for **milli**, which means $\\dfrac{1}{1,000}$. Therefore, $1\\,\\textrm{mg}=\\dfrac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\textrm{g}.$ \n\nNotice that if we add $1\\,\\textrm{mg}$ to itself $1\\,000$ times, we get exactly $1\\,\\textrm{g}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{1\\,000\\text{ times}}{\\overset{⏞}{1\\,\\text{mg}+1\\,\\text{mg}+⋯+1\\,\\text{mg}}} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{g}+\\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{g}+⋯+\\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{g} & = \\\\ 1\\,000\\times \\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{g} & = \\\\ \\frac{1\\,000\\times 1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{g} & = \\\\ \\frac{1\\,000}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{g} & = \\\\ 1\\,\\text{g}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mg}$ equals $1\\,\\textrm{g},$ as stated in the table." "Let's recall the table that relates metric units of mass.\n\n| Name | Symbol | Conversion |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Kilogram | $\\textrm{kg}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{kg} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{g}$ |\n| Gram | $\\textrm{g}$ | Base Unit |\n| Milligram | $\\textrm{mg}$ | $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mg} = 1\\,\\textrm{g}$ |\n\n**Important**: Symbols in the metric system follow a consistent set of **prefix rules**, which we explain below. If you find these difficult, *do not worry*! For now, you just need to remember the conversions shown in the table above. \n\nThe prefix rules are as follows:\n\n- The prefix \"$\\textrm k$\" stands for **kilo**, which means $1\\,000$. Therefore, $1\\,\\textrm{kg} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{g}.$\n\n- The prefix \"$\\textrm m$\" stands for **milli**, which means $\\dfrac{1}{1,000}$. Therefore, $1\\,\\textrm{mg}=\\dfrac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\textrm{g}.$ \n\nNotice that if we add $1\\,\\textrm{mg}$ to itself $1\\,000$ times, we get exactly $1\\,\\textrm{g}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{1\\,\\text{mg}+1\\,\\text{mg}+⋯+1\\,\\text{mg}}^{1\\,000\\text{ times}} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{g}+\\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{g}+⋯+\\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{g} & = \\\\ 1\\,000\\times \\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{g} & = \\\\ \\frac{1\\,000\\times 1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{g} & = \\\\ \\frac{1\\,000}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{g} & = \\\\ 1\\,\\text{g}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mg}$ equals $1\\,\\textrm{g},$ as stated in the table."]
 [:db/cas 17592186199588 :tutorial/content "Let's prove the following proposition.\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{mn \\text{is even}}_{P(m,n)} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\underbrace{m \\text{is even or} n \\text{is even}}_{Q(m,n)}\n$$\n\nTo prove $P \\Rightarrow Q$, it suffices to prove the contrapositive\n\n$$\n\\neg Q \\Rightarrow \\neg P\n$$\n\n$$\n\\neg\\left(m \\text{is even or} n \\text{is even}\\right) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\neg\\left(mn \\text{is even}\\right)\n$$\n\nNegating the right-hand-side of our implication is straightforward:\n\n$$\n\\neg\\left(m \\text{is even or} n \\text{is even}\\right) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad mn \\text{is odd}\n$$\n\nTo negate the statement \"$m$ is even or $n$ is even,\" we use De Morgan's law for disjunctions.\n\n$$\n¬(mis even ornis even) \\mid ⇔¬(mis even∨nis even); ⇔¬(mis even)∧¬(nis even); ⇔mis odd∧nis odd; ⇔mis odd andnis odd\n$$\n\nSo, let's write down the contrapositive of our original statement.\n\n> *The contrapositive form of the given statement is as follows:*\n> $m \\text{is odd and} n \\text{is odd} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad mn \\text{is odd}$\n\nWe start by using the fact that $m$ and $n$ are odd.\n\n> *If $m$ and $n$ are odd, then*\n> $m = 2a+1 \\quad\\text{and}\\quad n = 2b+1$,\n> *where $a$ and $b$ are integers.*\n\nWe substitute\n\n$$\nm=2a+1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn=2b+1\n$$\n\ninto the expression $mn$ and show it is odd by writing it as one larger than a multiple of $2$.\n\n> *Therefore,*\n> $mn|= (2a + 1)(2b + 1); = 4ab + 2a + 2b + 1; = 2(2ab + a + b)_(⏟)_(c) + 1$.\n\nNow, if we define another integer\n\n$$\nc=2ab+a+b\n$$\n\n(highlighted above), we have that\n\n$$\nmn = 2c+1\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $mn$ is indeed one larger than a multiple of $2$.\n\n> *Let $c=2ab+a+b$. Since $a$ and $b$ are integers, we have that $c$ is an integer.*\n> *Hence, we have*\n> $mn|= 2(2ab + a + b)_(⏟)_(c) + 1; = 2c + 1$,\n> *which is one larger than a multiple of $2$.*\n\nFinally, we write our conclusion.\n\n> *Therefore, $mn$ is odd.*\n\nFor clarity, let's restate the proposition and its full proof." "Let's prove the following proposition.\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{mn \\text{is even}}_{P(m,n)} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\underbrace{m \\text{is even or} n \\text{is even}}_{Q(m,n)}\n$$\n\nTo prove $P \\Rightarrow Q$, it suffices to prove the contrapositive\n\n$$\n\\neg Q \\Rightarrow \\neg P\n$$\n\n$$\n\\neg\\left(m \\text{is even or} n \\text{is even}\\right) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\neg\\left(mn \\text{is even}\\right)\n$$\n\nNegating the right-hand-side of our implication is straightforward:\n\n$$\n\\neg\\left(m \\text{is even or} n \\text{is even}\\right) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad mn \\text{is odd}\n$$\n\nTo negate the statement \"$m$ is even or $n$ is even,\" we use De Morgan's law for disjunctions.\n\n$$\n¬(mis even ornis even) \\mid ⇔¬(mis even∨nis even); ⇔¬(mis even)∧¬(nis even); ⇔mis odd∧nis odd; ⇔mis odd andnis odd\n$$\n\nSo, let's write down the contrapositive of our original statement.\n\n> *The contrapositive form of the given statement is as follows:*\n> $m \\text{is odd and} n \\text{is odd} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad mn \\text{is odd}$\n\nWe start by using the fact that $m$ and $n$ are odd.\n\n> *If $m$ and $n$ are odd, then*\n> $m = 2a+1 \\quad\\text{and}\\quad n = 2b+1$,\n> *where $a$ and $b$ are integers.*\n\nWe substitute\n\n$$\nm=2a+1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn=2b+1\n$$\n\ninto the expression $mn$ and show it is odd by writing it as one larger than a multiple of $2$.\n\n> *Therefore,*\n> $mn|= (2a + 1)(2b + 1); = 4ab + 2a + 2b + 1; = 2\\underbrace{(2ab + a + b)}_{c} + 1$.\n\nNow, if we define another integer\n\n$$\nc=2ab+a+b\n$$\n\n(highlighted above), we have that\n\n$$\nmn = 2c+1\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $mn$ is indeed one larger than a multiple of $2$.\n\n> *Let $c=2ab+a+b$. Since $a$ and $b$ are integers, we have that $c$ is an integer.*\n> *Hence, we have*\n> $mn|= 2\\underbrace{(2ab + a + b)}_{c} + 1; = 2c + 1$,\n> *which is one larger than a multiple of $2$.*\n\nFinally, we write our conclusion.\n\n> *Therefore, $mn$ is odd.*\n\nFor clarity, let's restate the proposition and its full proof."]
 [:db/cas 17592186199620 :tutorial/content "We wish to prove the following statement:\n\n$$\nm \\text{is even and} n \\text{is odd} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad m + 2n \\text{is even}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the proof step-by-step. We start by assuming that the antecedent is true:\n\n> **Step 1:** *Let $m$ and $n$ be integers, where $m$ is even and $n$ is odd.*\n\nNow, we use the definitions of evenness and oddness.\n\n> **Step 2:** *Then, $m=2a$ and $n=2b+1$ for some integers $a$ and $b$.*\n\n**Watch Out**! We must use two *different* integers to represent the evenness of $m$ and the oddness of $n$. In this case, we use $a$ and $b$.\n\nThe idea is to substitute $m=2a$ and\n\n$$\nn=2b+1\n$$\n\ninto our expression $m + 2n$ and show it is even by writing it as a multiple of $2$.\n\n> **Step 3:** *Substituting $m=2a$ and $n=2b+1$ into the expression, we get*\n> $m + 2n|= (2a) + 2(2b + 1); = 2a + 4b + 2; = 2(a + 2b + 1)_(⏟)_(c)$.\n\nNow, if we define another integer\n\n$$\nc=a + 2b + 1\n$$\n\n(highlighted above), we have that\n\n$$\nm + 2n = 2c\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $m + 2n$ is indeed a multiple of $2$.\n\n> *Let $c=a + 2b + 1$. Since $a$ and $b$ are integers, $c$ is an integer. As a result, we have*\n> $m + 2n|= 2(a + 2b + 1)_(⏟)_(c); = 2c$\n> *which is a multiple of $2$.*\n\nFinally, we write our conclusion:\n\n> **Step 4:** *Therefore, $m + 2n$ is even.*\n\nNow that we've figured out all the details, let's write down the full proof." "We wish to prove the following statement:\n\n$$\nm \\text{is even and} n \\text{is odd} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad m + 2n \\text{is even}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the proof step-by-step. We start by assuming that the antecedent is true:\n\n> **Step 1:** *Let $m$ and $n$ be integers, where $m$ is even and $n$ is odd.*\n\nNow, we use the definitions of evenness and oddness.\n\n> **Step 2:** *Then, $m=2a$ and $n=2b+1$ for some integers $a$ and $b$.*\n\n**Watch Out**! We must use two *different* integers to represent the evenness of $m$ and the oddness of $n$. In this case, we use $a$ and $b$.\n\nThe idea is to substitute $m=2a$ and\n\n$$\nn=2b+1\n$$\n\ninto our expression $m + 2n$ and show it is even by writing it as a multiple of $2$.\n\n> **Step 3:** *Substituting $m=2a$ and $n=2b+1$ into the expression, we get*\n> $m + 2n|= (2a) + 2(2b + 1); = 2a + 4b + 2; = 2\\underbrace{(a + 2b + 1)}_{c}$.\n\nNow, if we define another integer\n\n$$\nc=a + 2b + 1\n$$\n\n(highlighted above), we have that\n\n$$\nm + 2n = 2c\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $m + 2n$ is indeed a multiple of $2$.\n\n> *Let $c=a + 2b + 1$. Since $a$ and $b$ are integers, $c$ is an integer. As a result, we have*\n> $m + 2n|= 2\\underbrace{(a + 2b + 1)}_{c}; = 2c$\n> *which is a multiple of $2$.*\n\nFinally, we write our conclusion:\n\n> **Step 4:** *Therefore, $m + 2n$ is even.*\n\nNow that we've figured out all the details, let's write down the full proof."]
 [:db/cas 17592186203675 :question/worked-solution "Let's write down the formal statement $P$ that we wish to prove:\n\n$$\nP{:} \\quad 3-\\sqrt{2} \\: \\textrm{is irrational}\n$$\n\nTo prove that $P$ is true, we assume that $\\lnot P$ is true and show this leads to a contradiction.\n\n$$\n\\lnot P{:} \\quad 3-\\sqrt{2} \\: \\textrm{is rational}\n$$\n\nWe begin our proof as follows:\n\n> Assume, for a contradiction, that $3-\\sqrt{2}$ is rational.\n\nSince we're assuming $3-\\sqrt{2}$ is rational, we can express it as the ratio of two non-zero integers:\n\n> Then $3-\\sqrt{2} = \\dfrac{a}{b}$ for some non-zero integers $a$ and $b$.\n\nThe idea is to rearrange the equation, leaving a root on one side and a fraction on the other.\n\n> So, we have\n> $3 - \\sqrt{2}|= \\frac{a}{b}; - \\sqrt{2}|= \\frac{a}{b} - 3; \\sqrt{2}|= 3 - \\frac{a}{b}; \\sqrt{2}|= \\frac{3b}{b} - \\frac{a}{b}; \\sqrt{2}|= \\frac{3b - a}{b}^(⏞)^(c)$\n\nNow, if we define $c=3b-a$ (highlighted above), we have that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2}=\\dfrac{c}{b}\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $\\sqrt{2}$ is rational.\n\n> Let $c=3b-a$. Since $a$ and $b$ are integers, we have that $c$ is an integer.\n> Therefore, we have\n> $\\sqrt{2} = \\frac{c}{b}$,\n> which is rational.\n> But this is a contradiction since $\\sqrt{2}$ is irrational.\n\nSince we have a contradiction, our original assumption must be incorrect. Hence, we write our conclusion as follows:\n\n> Therefore, $3-\\sqrt{2}$ is irrational." "Let's write down the formal statement $P$ that we wish to prove:\n\n$$\nP{:} \\quad 3-\\sqrt{2} \\: \\textrm{is irrational}\n$$\n\nTo prove that $P$ is true, we assume that $\\lnot P$ is true and show this leads to a contradiction.\n\n$$\n\\lnot P{:} \\quad 3-\\sqrt{2} \\: \\textrm{is rational}\n$$\n\nWe begin our proof as follows:\n\n> Assume, for a contradiction, that $3-\\sqrt{2}$ is rational.\n\nSince we're assuming $3-\\sqrt{2}$ is rational, we can express it as the ratio of two non-zero integers:\n\n> Then $3-\\sqrt{2} = \\dfrac{a}{b}$ for some non-zero integers $a$ and $b$.\n\nThe idea is to rearrange the equation, leaving a root on one side and a fraction on the other.\n\n> So, we have\n> $3 - \\sqrt{2}|= \\frac{a}{b}; - \\sqrt{2}|= \\frac{a}{b} - 3; \\sqrt{2}|= 3 - \\frac{a}{b}; \\sqrt{2}|= \\frac{3b}{b} - \\frac{a}{b}; \\sqrt{2}|= \\overbrace{\\frac{3b - a}{b}}^{c}$\n\nNow, if we define $c=3b-a$ (highlighted above), we have that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2}=\\dfrac{c}{b}\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $\\sqrt{2}$ is rational.\n\n> Let $c=3b-a$. Since $a$ and $b$ are integers, we have that $c$ is an integer.\n> Therefore, we have\n> $\\sqrt{2} = \\frac{c}{b}$,\n> which is rational.\n> But this is a contradiction since $\\sqrt{2}$ is irrational.\n\nSince we have a contradiction, our original assumption must be incorrect. Hence, we write our conclusion as follows:\n\n> Therefore, $3-\\sqrt{2}$ is irrational."]
 [:db/cas 17592186203724 :question/worked-solution "Let's write down the formal statement $P$ that we wish to prove:\n\n$$\nP{:} \\quad \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\: \\textrm{is irrational}\n$$\n\nTo prove that $P$ is true, we assume that $\\lnot P$ is true and show this leads to a contradiction.\n\n$$\n\\lnot P{:} \\quad \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\: \\textrm{is rational}\n$$\n\nWe begin our proof as follows:\n\n> Assume, for a contradiction, that $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ is rational.\n\nSince we're assuming\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n$$\n\nis rational, we can express it as the ratio of two non-zero integers:\n\n> Then $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} = \\dfrac{a}{b}$ for some non-zero integers $a$ and $b$.\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the equation to give a radical on one side and a fraction on the other.\n\n> So, we have\n> $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}|= \\frac{a}{b}; \\sqrt{3}|= (2a^(⏞)^(c))/(b)$.\n\nNow, if we define the integer $c=2a$ (highlighted above), we have that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}=\\dfrac{c}{b}\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $\\sqrt{3}$ is rational.\n\n> Let $c=2a$. Since $2$ and $a$ are integers, we have that $c$ is an integer.\n> Therefore, we have\n> $\\sqrt{3} = \\frac{c}{b}$,\n> which is rational.\n> But this is a contradiction since $\\sqrt{3}$ is irrational.\n\nSince we have a contradiction, our original assumption must be incorrect. Hence, we write our conclusion as follows:\n\n> Therefore, $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ is irrational." "Let's write down the formal statement $P$ that we wish to prove:\n\n$$\nP{:} \\quad \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\: \\textrm{is irrational}\n$$\n\nTo prove that $P$ is true, we assume that $\\lnot P$ is true and show this leads to a contradiction.\n\n$$\n\\lnot P{:} \\quad \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\: \\textrm{is rational}\n$$\n\nWe begin our proof as follows:\n\n> Assume, for a contradiction, that $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ is rational.\n\nSince we're assuming\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n$$\n\nis rational, we can express it as the ratio of two non-zero integers:\n\n> Then $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} = \\dfrac{a}{b}$ for some non-zero integers $a$ and $b$.\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the equation to give a radical on one side and a fraction on the other.\n\n> So, we have\n> $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}|= \\frac{a}{b}; \\sqrt{3}|= (\\overbrace{2a}^{c})/(b)$.\n\nNow, if we define the integer $c=2a$ (highlighted above), we have that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}=\\dfrac{c}{b}\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $\\sqrt{3}$ is rational.\n\n> Let $c=2a$. Since $2$ and $a$ are integers, we have that $c$ is an integer.\n> Therefore, we have\n> $\\sqrt{3} = \\frac{c}{b}$,\n> which is rational.\n> But this is a contradiction since $\\sqrt{3}$ is irrational.\n\nSince we have a contradiction, our original assumption must be incorrect. Hence, we write our conclusion as follows:\n\n> Therefore, $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ is irrational."]
 [:db/cas 17592186204214 :question/problem "Disprove the following statement:\n\nFor any $a,b\\in Z,$ if $5a≡5b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20),$ then $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20).$\n\nTo {{field-1}} a universal statement, it's sufficient to find {{field-2}}. \nIn other words, we need to show that {{field-3}}\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}(\\text{mod}\\,20)\\,\\text{and}\\,{{field-5}}(\\text{mod}\\,20).\n$$\n\nLet's choose\n\n$$\na={{field-6}},\\,b={{field-7}}.\n$$\n\nThen, $5a=$ {{field-8}} and $5b=$ {{field-9}}. \n\nHence, we have that \n\n$$\n{{20}_{⏟}}_{5a}{{field-10}}{{40}_{⏟}}_{5b}\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20)\\,\\text{and}\\,{{4}_{⏟}}_{a}{{field-11}}{{8}_{⏟}}_{b}\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20).\n$$\n\nSo, the pair $a=$ {{field-12}} and $b=$ {{field-13}} is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false." "Disprove the following statement:\n\nFor any $a,b\\in Z,$ if $5a≡5b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20),$ then $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20).$\n\nTo {{field-1}} a universal statement, it's sufficient to find {{field-2}}. \nIn other words, we need to show that {{field-3}}\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}(\\text{mod}\\,20)\\,\\text{and}\\,{{field-5}}(\\text{mod}\\,20).\n$$\n\nLet's choose\n\n$$\na={{field-6}},\\,b={{field-7}}.\n$$\n\nThen, $5a=$ {{field-8}} and $5b=$ {{field-9}}. \n\nHence, we have that \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{20}_{5a}{{field-10}}\\underbrace{40}_{5b}\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20)\\,\\text{and}\\,\\underbrace{4}_{a}{{field-11}}\\underbrace{8}_{b}\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20).\n$$\n\nSo, the pair $a=$ {{field-12}} and $b=$ {{field-13}} is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false."]
 [:db/cas 17592186204214 :question/worked-solution "By denoting $P(a,b)$ and $Q(a,b)$ as\n\n- $P(a,b):\\,5a≡5b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20)$\n\n- $Q(a,b):\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20)$\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∀a,b\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q.\n$$\n\nWe want to disprove this statement. In other words, we must show that its negation is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∀a,b\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q) & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,¬(P⇒Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,¬(¬P∨Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,P∧¬Q \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,(5a≡5b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20))\\,∧\\,(a≢b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we begin as follows:\n\nTo disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. \nIn other words, we need to show that there exist integers $a$ and $b$ such that \n\n$$\n5a≡5b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20)\\,\\text{and}\\,a≢b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20)\n$$\n\nFinding a counterexample is sometimes a nontrivial task, but we only need one. In this case, substituting $(a,b)=(4,8)$ suffices. \n\nLet's choose\n\n$$\na=4,\\,b=8.\n$$\n\nThen, $5a=20$ and $5b=40.$ \n\nTo check that this is a required counterexample, we proceed as follows:\n\nHence, we have that \n\n$$\n{{20}_{⏟}}_{5a}≡{{40}_{⏟}}_{5b}\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20)\\,\\text{and}\\,{{4}_{⏟}}_{a}≢{{8}_{⏟}}_{b}\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20).\n$$\n\nFinally, we state our conclusion:\n\nSo, the pair $a=4$ and $b=8$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false." "By denoting $P(a,b)$ and $Q(a,b)$ as\n\n- $P(a,b):\\,5a≡5b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20)$\n\n- $Q(a,b):\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20)$\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∀a,b\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q.\n$$\n\nWe want to disprove this statement. In other words, we must show that its negation is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∀a,b\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q) & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,¬(P⇒Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,¬(¬P∨Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,P∧¬Q \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,(5a≡5b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20))\\,∧\\,(a≢b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we begin as follows:\n\nTo disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. \nIn other words, we need to show that there exist integers $a$ and $b$ such that \n\n$$\n5a≡5b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20)\\,\\text{and}\\,a≢b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20)\n$$\n\nFinding a counterexample is sometimes a nontrivial task, but we only need one. In this case, substituting $(a,b)=(4,8)$ suffices. \n\nLet's choose\n\n$$\na=4,\\,b=8.\n$$\n\nThen, $5a=20$ and $5b=40.$ \n\nTo check that this is a required counterexample, we proceed as follows:\n\nHence, we have that \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{20}_{5a}≡\\underbrace{40}_{5b}\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20)\\,\\text{and}\\,\\underbrace{4}_{a}≢\\underbrace{8}_{b}\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20).\n$$\n\nFinally, we state our conclusion:\n\nSo, the pair $a=4$ and $b=8$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false."]
 [:db/cas 17592186205135 :tutorial/content "Let's now prove that the $k$th sample moment is an unbiased estimator of the $k$th theoretical moment.\n\nSuppose we have an I.I.D. random sample of size $n{:}$\n$X_1,\\quad X_2,\\quad X_3, \\quad \\ldots,\\quad X_n$\n\nThe $k$th theoretical moment, denoted $\\mu_k$, is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mu_k = \\textrm{E}[X_i^k] = \\sum_{x\\in S} x^k \\cdot p(x)\n$$\n\nand the $k$th sample moment is\n\n$$\nM_k = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^k\n$$\n\nRecall that $M_k$ is an unbiased estimate of $\\mu_k$ if\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[M_k] = \\mu_k\n$$\n\nWe can show this is true using the properties of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[M_{k}] &= E[\\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)X_{i}^{k}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n} \\cdot E[∑_(i = 1)^(n)X_{i}^{k}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n} \\cdot (E[X_{1}^{k}] + E[X_{2}^{k}] + ⋯ + E[X_{n}^{k}]) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n} \\cdot (μ_{k} + μ_{k} + ⋯ + μ_{k})_(⏟)_(ntimes) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n} \\cdot nμ_{k} \\\\\n&= μ_{k}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required." "Let's now prove that the $k$th sample moment is an unbiased estimator of the $k$th theoretical moment.\n\nSuppose we have an I.I.D. random sample of size $n{:}$\n$X_1,\\quad X_2,\\quad X_3, \\quad \\ldots,\\quad X_n$\n\nThe $k$th theoretical moment, denoted $\\mu_k$, is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mu_k = \\textrm{E}[X_i^k] = \\sum_{x\\in S} x^k \\cdot p(x)\n$$\n\nand the $k$th sample moment is\n\n$$\nM_k = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^k\n$$\n\nRecall that $M_k$ is an unbiased estimate of $\\mu_k$ if\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[M_k] = \\mu_k\n$$\n\nWe can show this is true using the properties of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[M_{k}] &= E[\\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)X_{i}^{k}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n} \\cdot E[∑_(i = 1)^(n)X_{i}^{k}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n} \\cdot (E[X_{1}^{k}] + E[X_{2}^{k}] + ⋯ + E[X_{n}^{k}]) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n} \\cdot \\underbrace{(μ_{k} + μ_{k} + ⋯ + μ_{k})}_{n\\text{ times}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n} \\cdot nμ_{k} \\\\\n&= μ_{k}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required."]
 [:db/cas 17592186205262 :tutorial/content "Some relations are *neither symmetric nor antisymmetric*.\n\nFor example, $x \\mid y,$ defined on the set of integers, is \n\n- not symmetric since $2 \\mid 4$ but $4 \\not\\mid 2,$ and\n\n- not antisymmetric since $(-1) \\mid 1$ and $1 \\mid (-1),$ but $-1 \\neq 1.$\n\nIn contrast, some relations are *both symmetric and antisymmetric!*\n\nFor example, consider the relation of equality ($=$) defined over $\\mathbb{R}.$ This relation contains no pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x \\neq y.$ As a result, the antecedents in the implications from the definitions of symmetricity and antisymmetricity are always false. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\text{false}}{\\overset{⏞}{(x,y)\\in R}} & \\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R \\\\ {{(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R}_{⏟}}_{\\text{false}} & \\,⟹\\,x=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the implications are always true. So, equality over $\\mathbb R$ is vacuously symmetric and antisymmetric at the same time!\n\nWe can summarize everything we've discussed so far in the following diagram:\n\n![](images/75/75e7daabc982abcb6677b372229cbf59a506a3286f49693260098bb3407db857.png)" "Some relations are *neither symmetric nor antisymmetric*.\n\nFor example, $x \\mid y,$ defined on the set of integers, is \n\n- not symmetric since $2 \\mid 4$ but $4 \\not\\mid 2,$ and\n\n- not antisymmetric since $(-1) \\mid 1$ and $1 \\mid (-1),$ but $-1 \\neq 1.$\n\nIn contrast, some relations are *both symmetric and antisymmetric!*\n\nFor example, consider the relation of equality ($=$) defined over $\\mathbb{R}.$ This relation contains no pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x \\neq y.$ As a result, the antecedents in the implications from the definitions of symmetricity and antisymmetricity are always false. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{(x,y)\\in R}^{\\text{false}} & \\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R \\\\ \\underbrace{(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R}_{\\text{false}} & \\,⟹\\,x=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the implications are always true. So, equality over $\\mathbb R$ is vacuously symmetric and antisymmetric at the same time!\n\nWe can summarize everything we've discussed so far in the following diagram:\n\n![](images/75/75e7daabc982abcb6677b372229cbf59a506a3286f49693260098bb3407db857.png)"]
 [:db/cas 17592186205269 :tutorial/content "We can use proof by contradiction to prove the following properties about rational and irrational numbers:\n\n- The sum of a rational and an irrational number is always irrational.\n- The product of a nonzero rational number and an irrational number is always irrational.\n- The quotient of an irrational number and a nonzero rational number is always irrational.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn how to prove these properties in particular cases.\n\nFor example, assume we've proved that $\\sqrt{3}$ is irrational. Let's use this result to prove that $2\\sqrt{3}$ is also irrational.\n\nFirst, we write down the formal statement $P$ that we wish to prove:\n\n$$\n\\phantom{\\lnot}\\:\\: P{:} \\quad 2\\sqrt{3} \\: \\textrm{is irrational}\n$$\n\nTo prove that $P$ is true, we assume that $\\lnot P$ is true and show this leads to a contradiction.\n\n$$\n\\lnot P{:} \\quad 2\\sqrt{3} \\: \\textrm{is rational}\n$$\n\nWe begin our proof as follows:\n\n> *Assume, for a contradiction, that $2\\sqrt{3}$ is rational.*\n\nSince we're assuming $2\\sqrt{3}$ is rational, we can express it as the ratio of two nonzero integers:\n\n> *Then $2\\sqrt{3} = \\dfrac{a}{b}$ for some non-zero integers $a$ and $b$.*\n\nThe idea is to rewrite this equation to give a radical $(\\sqrt 3)$ on one side and a fraction on the other. This will provide us with a contradiction since $\\sqrt 3$ is irrational and, therefore, cannot equal a ratio of two integers.\n\n> *So, we have*\n> $2\\sqrt{3}|= \\frac{a}{b}; \\sqrt{3}|= (a)/(2b_(⏟)_(c))$.\n\nNow, if we define the integer $c=2b$ (highlighted above), we have that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}=\\dfrac{a}{c}\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $\\sqrt{3}$ is rational.\n\n> *Let $c=2b$. Since $2$ and $b$ are integers, we have that $c$ is an integer.*\n> *Therefore, we have*\n> $\\sqrt{3} = \\frac{a}{c}$,\n> *which is rational.*\n> *But this is a contradiction since $\\sqrt{3}$ is irrational.*\n\nSince we have a contradiction, our original assumption must be incorrect. Hence, we write our conclusion as follows:\n\n> *Therefore, $2\\sqrt{3}$ is irrational.*\n\nNow that we've figured out the details, let's state the full proof." "We can use proof by contradiction to prove the following properties about rational and irrational numbers:\n\n- The sum of a rational and an irrational number is always irrational.\n- The product of a nonzero rational number and an irrational number is always irrational.\n- The quotient of an irrational number and a nonzero rational number is always irrational.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn how to prove these properties in particular cases.\n\nFor example, assume we've proved that $\\sqrt{3}$ is irrational. Let's use this result to prove that $2\\sqrt{3}$ is also irrational.\n\nFirst, we write down the formal statement $P$ that we wish to prove:\n\n$$\n\\phantom{\\lnot}\\:\\: P{:} \\quad 2\\sqrt{3} \\: \\textrm{is irrational}\n$$\n\nTo prove that $P$ is true, we assume that $\\lnot P$ is true and show this leads to a contradiction.\n\n$$\n\\lnot P{:} \\quad 2\\sqrt{3} \\: \\textrm{is rational}\n$$\n\nWe begin our proof as follows:\n\n> *Assume, for a contradiction, that $2\\sqrt{3}$ is rational.*\n\nSince we're assuming $2\\sqrt{3}$ is rational, we can express it as the ratio of two nonzero integers:\n\n> *Then $2\\sqrt{3} = \\dfrac{a}{b}$ for some non-zero integers $a$ and $b$.*\n\nThe idea is to rewrite this equation to give a radical $(\\sqrt 3)$ on one side and a fraction on the other. This will provide us with a contradiction since $\\sqrt 3$ is irrational and, therefore, cannot equal a ratio of two integers.\n\n> *So, we have*\n> $2\\sqrt{3}|= \\frac{a}{b}; \\sqrt{3}|= (a)/(\\underbrace{2b}_{c})$.\n\nNow, if we define the integer $c=2b$ (highlighted above), we have that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}=\\dfrac{a}{c}\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $\\sqrt{3}$ is rational.\n\n> *Let $c=2b$. Since $2$ and $b$ are integers, we have that $c$ is an integer.*\n> *Therefore, we have*\n> $\\sqrt{3} = \\frac{a}{c}$,\n> *which is rational.*\n> *But this is a contradiction since $\\sqrt{3}$ is irrational.*\n\nSince we have a contradiction, our original assumption must be incorrect. Hence, we write our conclusion as follows:\n\n> *Therefore, $2\\sqrt{3}$ is irrational.*\n\nNow that we've figured out the details, let's state the full proof."]
 [:db/cas 17592186205274 :tutorial/content "Recall that the biconditional statement $P\\Leftrightarrow Q$ is true if and only if $P$ and $Q$ have the same truth value.\n\nThe relation \"$\\Leftrightarrow$\" between two statements is an *equivalence relation* because it's reflexive, symmetric, and transitive:\n\n- $P\\Leftrightarrow P$ is always true because $P$ has the same truth value as itself.\n- If $P\\Leftrightarrow Q$ is true, then $Q\\Leftrightarrow P$ is also true. If $P$ and $Q$ have the same truth value, then $Q$ and $P$ also have the same truth value.\n- If $P\\Leftrightarrow Q$ and $Q\\Leftrightarrow R$ are both true, then $P\\Leftrightarrow R$ is also true.\n\nSuppose have the following chain of equivalent statements:\n\n$$\nP \\quad \\Leftrightarrow\\quad Q \\quad \\Leftrightarrow\\quad R\n$$\n\nThere are only two ways in which this chain of equivalences is true:\n\n- $P,Q$, and $R$ are *all* true\n- $P,Q$, and $R$ are *all* false\n\nConsider the following predicate for $x\\in\\mathbb Z$.\n\n$$\nP(x): \\quad 2x-3 = 5\n$$\n\nThis is *equivalent* to the statement $x=4$, and this can be proved using a *chain of equivalent statements* as follows:\n\n$$\n2x - 3 = 5_(⏟)_(P(x))⇔2x = 8_(⏟)_(Q(x))⇔x = 4_(⏟)_(R(x))\n$$\n\nNote that substituting\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{x}}={\\color{blue}{4}}\n$$\n\ninto $P,Q$, and $R$ gives a truth value of \"true\" $(\\textrm{T})$ in each case:\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot 4 - 3 = 5_(⏟)_(T)⇔2 \\cdot 4 = 8_(⏟)_(T)⇔4 = 4_(⏟)_(T)\n$$\n\nHowever, substituting\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{x}}={\\color{red}{3}}\n$$\n\ninto $P,Q$, and $R$ gives a truth value of \"false\" $(\\textrm{F})$ in each case:\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot 3 - 3 = 5_(⏟)_(F)⇔2 \\cdot 3 = 8_(⏟)_(F)⇔3 = 4_(⏟)_(F)\n$$\n\nIn general, we can prove a biconditional $P\\Leftrightarrow Q$ by finding a set of statements $R_1, R_2, \\ldots, R_n$ and using them to form a chain of equivalent statements connecting $P$ and $Q{:}$\n\n$$\nP \\quad \\Leftrightarrow\\quad R_1 \\quad \\Leftrightarrow\\quad R_2 \\quad \\Leftrightarrow\\quad \\cdots \\quad \\Leftrightarrow\\quad R_n \\quad \\Leftrightarrow\\quad Q\n$$\n\nIn fact, you've been implicitly using this type of argument throughout your mathematical career! Solving linear equations is just one example." "Recall that the biconditional statement $P\\Leftrightarrow Q$ is true if and only if $P$ and $Q$ have the same truth value.\n\nThe relation \"$\\Leftrightarrow$\" between two statements is an *equivalence relation* because it's reflexive, symmetric, and transitive:\n\n- $P\\Leftrightarrow P$ is always true because $P$ has the same truth value as itself.\n- If $P\\Leftrightarrow Q$ is true, then $Q\\Leftrightarrow P$ is also true. If $P$ and $Q$ have the same truth value, then $Q$ and $P$ also have the same truth value.\n- If $P\\Leftrightarrow Q$ and $Q\\Leftrightarrow R$ are both true, then $P\\Leftrightarrow R$ is also true.\n\nSuppose have the following chain of equivalent statements:\n\n$$\nP \\quad \\Leftrightarrow\\quad Q \\quad \\Leftrightarrow\\quad R\n$$\n\nThere are only two ways in which this chain of equivalences is true:\n\n- $P,Q$, and $R$ are *all* true\n- $P,Q$, and $R$ are *all* false\n\nConsider the following predicate for $x\\in\\mathbb Z$.\n\n$$\nP(x): \\quad 2x-3 = 5\n$$\n\nThis is *equivalent* to the statement $x=4$, and this can be proved using a *chain of equivalent statements* as follows:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{2x - 3 = 5}_{P(x)}⇔\\underbrace{2x = 8}_{Q(x)}⇔\\underbrace{x = 4}_{R(x)}\n$$\n\nNote that substituting\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{x}}={\\color{blue}{4}}\n$$\n\ninto $P,Q$, and $R$ gives a truth value of \"true\" $(\\textrm{T})$ in each case:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{2 \\cdot 4 - 3 = 5}_{\\text{T}}⇔\\underbrace{2 \\cdot 4 = 8}_{\\text{T}}⇔\\underbrace{4 = 4}_{\\text{T}}\n$$\n\nHowever, substituting\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{x}}={\\color{red}{3}}\n$$\n\ninto $P,Q$, and $R$ gives a truth value of \"false\" $(\\textrm{F})$ in each case:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{2 \\cdot 3 - 3 = 5}_{\\text{F}}⇔\\underbrace{2 \\cdot 3 = 8}_{\\text{F}}⇔\\underbrace{3 = 4}_{\\text{F}}\n$$\n\nIn general, we can prove a biconditional $P\\Leftrightarrow Q$ by finding a set of statements $R_1, R_2, \\ldots, R_n$ and using them to form a chain of equivalent statements connecting $P$ and $Q{:}$\n\n$$\nP \\quad \\Leftrightarrow\\quad R_1 \\quad \\Leftrightarrow\\quad R_2 \\quad \\Leftrightarrow\\quad \\cdots \\quad \\Leftrightarrow\\quad R_n \\quad \\Leftrightarrow\\quad Q\n$$\n\nIn fact, you've been implicitly using this type of argument throughout your mathematical career! Solving linear equations is just one example."]
 [:db/cas 17592186205275 :tutorial/content "Proposition:\n\n> *Given that $\\sqrt{3}$ is irrational, prove that $2\\sqrt{3}$ is irrational.*\n\nProof:\n\n> *Assume, for a contradiction, that $2\\sqrt{3}$ is rational.*\n> *Then $2\\sqrt{3} = \\dfrac{a}{b}$ for some non-zero integers $a$ and $b$.*\n> *So, we have*\n> $2\\sqrt{3}|= \\frac{a}{b}; \\sqrt{3}|= (a)/(2b_(⏟)_(c))$.\n> *Let $c=2b$. Since $2$ and $b$ are integers, we have that $c$ is an integer.*\n> *Therefore, we have*\n> $\\sqrt{3} = \\frac{a}{c}$,\n> *which is rational.*\n> *But this is a contradiction since $\\sqrt{3}$ is irrational.*\n> *Therefore, $2\\sqrt{3}$ is irrational.*\n\nThe whole proof rests on the assumption that $\\sqrt 3$ is irrational. You may ask, \"But how do we know that $\\sqrt 3$ is irrational?\"\n\nThe irrationality of $\\sqrt 3$ can be proved using proof by contradiction! However, the proof methodology is slightly different, so we'll save that discussion for a separate lesson." "Proposition:\n\n> *Given that $\\sqrt{3}$ is irrational, prove that $2\\sqrt{3}$ is irrational.*\n\nProof:\n\n> *Assume, for a contradiction, that $2\\sqrt{3}$ is rational.*\n> *Then $2\\sqrt{3} = \\dfrac{a}{b}$ for some non-zero integers $a$ and $b$.*\n> *So, we have*\n> $2\\sqrt{3}|= \\frac{a}{b}; \\sqrt{3}|= (a)/(\\underbrace{2b}_{c})$.\n> *Let $c=2b$. Since $2$ and $b$ are integers, we have that $c$ is an integer.*\n> *Therefore, we have*\n> $\\sqrt{3} = \\frac{a}{c}$,\n> *which is rational.*\n> *But this is a contradiction since $\\sqrt{3}$ is irrational.*\n> *Therefore, $2\\sqrt{3}$ is irrational.*\n\nThe whole proof rests on the assumption that $\\sqrt 3$ is irrational. You may ask, \"But how do we know that $\\sqrt 3$ is irrational?\"\n\nThe irrationality of $\\sqrt 3$ can be proved using proof by contradiction! However, the proof methodology is slightly different, so we'll save that discussion for a separate lesson."]
 [:db/cas 17592186208402 :question/worked-solution "We are given the following system:\n\n$$\n{y = 49; y = x^{2} - 2x + 1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y = 49$ into the second equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 2x + 1 &= 49 \\\\\nx^{2} - 2x + 1 - 49 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 2x - 48 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve this quadratic equation by factoring. We begin by finding two numbers that multiply to $-48$ and add to $-2$. These are $-8$ and $6{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 2x - 48 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2}- 8x + 6x_(⏟)_(-2x) - 48 &= 0 \\\\\nx(x - 8) + 6(x - 8) &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 8)(x + 6) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x - 8 = 0 & orx + 6 = 0 \\\\ x = 8 & orx =-6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, among the given options, the possible value of $x$ is $8$." "We are given the following system:\n\n$$\n{y = 49; y = x^{2} - 2x + 1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y = 49$ into the second equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 2x + 1 &= 49 \\\\\nx^{2} - 2x + 1 - 49 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 2x - 48 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve this quadratic equation by factoring. We begin by finding two numbers that multiply to $-48$ and add to $-2$. These are $-8$ and $6{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 2x - 48 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2}\\underbrace{-8x + 6x}_{-2x} - 48 &= 0 \\\\\nx(x - 8) + 6(x - 8) &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 8)(x + 6) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x - 8 = 0 & orx + 6 = 0 \\\\ x = 8 & orx =-6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, among the given options, the possible value of $x$ is $8$."]
 [:db/cas 17592186209877 :tutorial/content "Now, let $\\mathbf{y}: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^q$ and $f: \\mathbb{R}^q \\to \\mathbb{R}$. Suppose we want to compute the gradient \n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} f(\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf{x})).\n$$\n\nTo do this, we need to apply the chain rule. The chain rule for total derivatives tells us that\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\frac{\\textrm{d} f}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}}}_{1 \\times n}\n= \n\\underbrace{\\frac{\\textrm{d} f}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{y}}}_{1 \\times q}\n\\cdot \n\\underbrace{\\frac{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{y}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}}}_{q \\times n}.\n$$\n\nNow, recall that the gradient of a scalar function is the transpose of the total derivative:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} f}_{n \\times 1} = \n\\left( \\frac{\\textrm{d} f}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}} \\right)^T\n$$\n\nTo derive the chain rule in terms of gradients, we apply the identity $(AB)^T = B^T A^T$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x})}^{T} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T} \\\\  & ={{{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}}_{⏟}}_{n\\times q}\\cdot {{{(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y})}^{T}}_{⏟}}_{q\\times 1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient chain rule is\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} f = \n\\left( \\frac{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{y}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}} \\right)^T\n\\cdot \n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{y}} f.\n$$\n\nNote that the order matters; when expressed in the gradient form, the derivatives are applied *from right to left!*" "Now, let $\\mathbf{y}: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^q$ and $f: \\mathbb{R}^q \\to \\mathbb{R}$. Suppose we want to compute the gradient \n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} f(\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf{x})).\n$$\n\nTo do this, we need to apply the chain rule. The chain rule for total derivatives tells us that\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\frac{\\textrm{d} f}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}}}_{1 \\times n}\n= \n\\underbrace{\\frac{\\textrm{d} f}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{y}}}_{1 \\times q}\n\\cdot \n\\underbrace{\\frac{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{y}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}}}_{q \\times n}.\n$$\n\nNow, recall that the gradient of a scalar function is the transpose of the total derivative:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} f}_{n \\times 1} = \n\\left( \\frac{\\textrm{d} f}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}} \\right)^T\n$$\n\nTo derive the chain rule in terms of gradients, we apply the identity $(AB)^T = B^T A^T$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x})}^{T} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}}_{n\\times q}\\cdot \\underbrace{{(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y})}^{T}}_{q\\times 1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient chain rule is\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} f = \n\\left( \\frac{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{y}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}} \\right)^T\n\\cdot \n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{y}} f.\n$$\n\nNote that the order matters; when expressed in the gradient form, the derivatives are applied *from right to left!*"]
 [:db/cas 17592186212765 :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the denominator in the first term, $2x^2-11x+5$.\n\nTo factor this quadratic expression, we need to find two numbers that multiply to\n\n$$\n2\\cdot 5=10\n$$\n\nand add to $-11$. These are $-10$ and $-1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x^{2} - 11x + 5 &= 2x^{2}- 10x - x_(⏟)_(-11x) + 5 \\\\\n&= (2x^{2} - 10x) + (-x + 5) \\\\\n&= 2x(x - 5) - 1(x - 5) \\\\\n&= (x - 5)(2x - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, we can rewrite our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{2x^2-11x+5}+\\dfrac{7x}{x-5} = \\dfrac{5}{(x-5)(2x-1)}+\\dfrac{7x}{x-5}\n$$\n\nNow, we can put each rational expression over a common denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of the second fraction by $(2x-1)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(5)/((x - 5)(2x - 1)) + \\frac{7x}{x - 5} &= (5)/((x - 5)(2x - 1)) + \\frac{2x - 1}{2x - 1} \\cdot \\frac{7x}{x - 5} \\\\\n&= (5)/((x - 5)(2x - 1)) + (7x(2x - 1))/((x - 5)(2x - 1))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we add the rational expressions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(5)/((x - 5)(2x - 1)) + (7x(2x - 1))/((x - 5)(2x - 1)) &= (5 + 7x(2x - 1))/((x - 5)(2x - 1)) \\\\\n&= (5 + 14x^{2} - 7x)/((x - 5)(2x - 1)) \\\\\n&= (14x^{2} - 7x + 5)/((x - 5)(2x - 1))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we factor the denominator in the first term, $2x^2-11x+5$.\n\nTo factor this quadratic expression, we need to find two numbers that multiply to\n\n$$\n2\\cdot 5=10\n$$\n\nand add to $-11$. These are $-10$ and $-1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x^{2} - 11x + 5 &= 2x^{2}\\underbrace{-10x - x}_{-11x} + 5 \\\\\n&= (2x^{2} - 10x) + (-x + 5) \\\\\n&= 2x(x - 5) - 1(x - 5) \\\\\n&= (x - 5)(2x - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, we can rewrite our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{2x^2-11x+5}+\\dfrac{7x}{x-5} = \\dfrac{5}{(x-5)(2x-1)}+\\dfrac{7x}{x-5}\n$$\n\nNow, we can put each rational expression over a common denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of the second fraction by $(2x-1)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(5)/((x - 5)(2x - 1)) + \\frac{7x}{x - 5} &= (5)/((x - 5)(2x - 1)) + \\frac{2x - 1}{2x - 1} \\cdot \\frac{7x}{x - 5} \\\\\n&= (5)/((x - 5)(2x - 1)) + (7x(2x - 1))/((x - 5)(2x - 1))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we add the rational expressions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(5)/((x - 5)(2x - 1)) + (7x(2x - 1))/((x - 5)(2x - 1)) &= (5 + 7x(2x - 1))/((x - 5)(2x - 1)) \\\\\n&= (5 + 14x^{2} - 7x)/((x - 5)(2x - 1)) \\\\\n&= (14x^{2} - 7x + 5)/((x - 5)(2x - 1))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186212835 :question/worked-solution "We're asked to factor the expression\n$(2a-7)^3(a+2) + (3a+4)(7-2a)^2$.\n\nNotice that\n\n- the first term in this expression contains a factor of $(2a-7)^2$, and\n- the second term contains a factor of $(7-2a)^2$.\n\nThese factors differ only by the sign:\n\n$$\n7-2a = -(2a-7)\n$$\n\nHowever, the squaring eliminates the minus sign:\n\n$$\n(7-2a)^2 = (2a-7)^2\n$$\n\nSo, we can simply rewrite the second term:\n$(2a-7)^3(a+2) + (3a+4)(2a-7)^2$\n\nNow, we can factor the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n(2a-7)^2 \\left[(2a-7)(a+2) + (3a+4)\\right]\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the expression inside the square brackets. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2a - 7)^{2}[(2a - 7)(a + 2) + (3a + 4)] &=  \\\\\n(2a - 7)^{2}[2a^{2} + 4a - 7a - 14 + 3a + 4] &=  \\\\\n&= (2a - 7)^{2}(2a^{2} - 10)_(⏟)_(g(a))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\ng(a) = 2a^2 - 10\n$$" "We're asked to factor the expression\n$(2a-7)^3(a+2) + (3a+4)(7-2a)^2$.\n\nNotice that\n\n- the first term in this expression contains a factor of $(2a-7)^2$, and\n- the second term contains a factor of $(7-2a)^2$.\n\nThese factors differ only by the sign:\n\n$$\n7-2a = -(2a-7)\n$$\n\nHowever, the squaring eliminates the minus sign:\n\n$$\n(7-2a)^2 = (2a-7)^2\n$$\n\nSo, we can simply rewrite the second term:\n$(2a-7)^3(a+2) + (3a+4)(2a-7)^2$\n\nNow, we can factor the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n(2a-7)^2 \\left[(2a-7)(a+2) + (3a+4)\\right]\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the expression inside the square brackets. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2a - 7)^{2}[(2a - 7)(a + 2) + (3a + 4)] &=  \\\\\n(2a - 7)^{2}[2a^{2} + 4a - 7a - 14 + 3a + 4] &=  \\\\\n&= (2a - 7)^{2}\\underbrace{(2a^{2} - 10)}_{g(a)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\ng(a) = 2a^2 - 10\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186216892 :question/worked-solution "When a transversal intersects two parallel lines, it creates eight angles in total: four acute angles and four obtuse angles. All acute angles are congruent, and all obtuse angles are congruent, as shown below.\n\n![](images/1b/1b463497712cb58cf8ad57e55a177ca0d9c645207348232d5875f6e6976c49c3.png)\n\nLet $\\angle A$ be an acute angle and $\\angle O$ be an obtuse angle.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nm\\angle A = (96 - 5t)^\\circ\n$$\n\nWe are told that the sum of one obtuse angle and two acute angles is $(k-5t)^\\circ$. Writing as an equation, we have\n\n$$\nm\\angle O + 2m\\angle A = (k-5t)^\\circ\n$$\n\nWe know that $\\angle A$ and $\\angle O$ are supplementary. Therefore, the left-hand side of the above equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠O + 2m∠A &= m∠O + m∠A_(⏟)_(180^{∘}) + m∠A \\\\\n&= 180^{∘} + (96 - 5t)^{∘} \\\\\n&= 276^{∘} - 5t^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can equate this to $(k-5t)^\\circ$ and solve for $k$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n276^{∘} - 5t^{∘} &= k^{∘} - 5t^{∘} \\\\\n276 &= k \\\\\nk &= 276\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "When a transversal intersects two parallel lines, it creates eight angles in total: four acute angles and four obtuse angles. All acute angles are congruent, and all obtuse angles are congruent, as shown below.\n\n![](images/1b/1b463497712cb58cf8ad57e55a177ca0d9c645207348232d5875f6e6976c49c3.png)\n\nLet $\\angle A$ be an acute angle and $\\angle O$ be an obtuse angle.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nm\\angle A = (96 - 5t)^\\circ\n$$\n\nWe are told that the sum of one obtuse angle and two acute angles is $(k-5t)^\\circ$. Writing as an equation, we have\n\n$$\nm\\angle O + 2m\\angle A = (k-5t)^\\circ\n$$\n\nWe know that $\\angle A$ and $\\angle O$ are supplementary. Therefore, the left-hand side of the above equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠O + 2m∠A &= \\underbrace{m∠O + m∠A}_{{180}^{∘}} + m∠A \\\\\n&= 180^{∘} + (96 - 5t)^{∘} \\\\\n&= 276^{∘} - 5t^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can equate this to $(k-5t)^\\circ$ and solve for $k$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n276^{∘} - 5t^{∘} &= k^{∘} - 5t^{∘} \\\\\n276 &= k \\\\\nk &= 276\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186216980 :question/worked-solution "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{Q}\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\exp\\left(\\int g(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\exp(x)$ denotes the exponential function $e^x.$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{Q}\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\dfrac{1}{x}$ is a function of $x$ only, we can conclude that the integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int {x}^{-1}\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our equation by the integrating factor, we arrive at the following exact differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x[(x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x+(xy+\\frac{2}{x})\\,\\text{d}y] & =0 \\\\ {{({x}^{2}+x{y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{P}}\\,\\text{d}x+{{({x}^{2}y+2)}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{Q}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor this exact equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\widetilde{P}(x,y)=x^2+xy^2,\\qquad\n\\widetilde{Q}(x,y)=x^2y+2.\n$$\n\nTo solve the equation, let's first integrate $\\widetilde{P}(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $y,$ which we'll call $F(y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{P}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\int ({x}^{2}+x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{2}+F(y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's integrate $\\widetilde{Q}(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $x,$ which we'll call $G(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\int ({x}^{2}y+2)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{2}+2y+G(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we must have\n\n$$\nF(y)=2y,\\qquad G(x)=\\frac{x^3}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^3}{3}+\\frac{x^2y^2}{2}+2y=c.\n$$" "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{Q}\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\exp\\left(\\int g(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\exp(x)$ denotes the exponential function $e^x.$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{Q}\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\dfrac{1}{x}$ is a function of $x$ only, we can conclude that the integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int {x}^{-1}\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our equation by the integrating factor, we arrive at the following exact differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x[(x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x+(xy+\\frac{2}{x})\\,\\text{d}y] & =0 \\\\ \\underbrace{({x}^{2}+x{y}^{2})}_{\\overset{~}{P}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\underbrace{({x}^{2}y+2)}_{\\overset{~}{Q}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor this exact equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\widetilde{P}(x,y)=x^2+xy^2,\\qquad\n\\widetilde{Q}(x,y)=x^2y+2.\n$$\n\nTo solve the equation, let's first integrate $\\widetilde{P}(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $y,$ which we'll call $F(y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{P}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\int ({x}^{2}+x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{2}+F(y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's integrate $\\widetilde{Q}(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $x,$ which we'll call $G(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\int ({x}^{2}y+2)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{2}+2y+G(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we must have\n\n$$\nF(y)=2y,\\qquad G(x)=\\frac{x^3}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^3}{3}+\\frac{x^2y^2}{2}+2y=c.\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186217792 :question/worked-solution "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(x)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{x}.$\n\nWe are given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{3}{x}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{3}{x}$ is a function of $x$ only, we can conclude that the integrating factor for the equation is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int 3{x}^{-1}\\text{ }\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our equation by the integrating factor, we arrive at the following exact differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}[(\\frac{6{y}^{2}}{x}+4{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+(4y+3x{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})\\,\\text{d}y] & =0 \\\\ {{(6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4{x}^{3}{y}^{3})}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{P}}\\,\\text{d}x+{{(4{x}^{3}y+3{x}^{4}{y}^{2}+1)}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{Q}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor this exact equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)=6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4{x}^{3}{y}^{3},\\,\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)=4{x}^{3}y+3{x}^{4}{y}^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nTo solve the equation, let's first integrate $\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $y,$ which we'll call $F(y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{P}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\int (6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4{x}^{3}{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+{x}^{4}{y}^{3}+F(y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's integrate $\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $x,$ which we'll call $G(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\int (4{x}^{3}y+3{x}^{4}{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+{x}^{4}{y}^{3}+y+G(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we must have\n\n$$\nF(y)=y,\\,G(x)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+{x}^{4}{y}^{3}+y=c.\n$$" "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(x)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{x}.$\n\nWe are given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{3}{x}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{3}{x}$ is a function of $x$ only, we can conclude that the integrating factor for the equation is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int 3{x}^{-1}\\text{ }\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our equation by the integrating factor, we arrive at the following exact differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}[(\\frac{6{y}^{2}}{x}+4{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+(4y+3x{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})\\,\\text{d}y] & =0 \\\\ \\underbrace{(6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4{x}^{3}{y}^{3})}_{\\overset{~}{P}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\underbrace{(4{x}^{3}y+3{x}^{4}{y}^{2}+1)}_{\\overset{~}{Q}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor this exact equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)=6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4{x}^{3}{y}^{3},\\,\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)=4{x}^{3}y+3{x}^{4}{y}^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nTo solve the equation, let's first integrate $\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $y,$ which we'll call $F(y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{P}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\int (6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4{x}^{3}{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+{x}^{4}{y}^{3}+F(y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's integrate $\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $x,$ which we'll call $G(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\int (4{x}^{3}y+3{x}^{4}{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+{x}^{4}{y}^{3}+y+G(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we must have\n\n$$\nF(y)=y,\\,G(x)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+{x}^{4}{y}^{3}+y=c.\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186221091 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n$ax = b$\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and\n\n$$\nb\\neq 0\n$$\n\nIf $a = 0$, then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $az = b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(r - 9)z + 2 &= -1 \\\\\n(r - 9)z + 2 - 2 &= -1 - 2 \\\\\n(r - 9)_(⏟)_(a)z &= -3_(⏟)_(b)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n$r - 9 = 0$.\n\nSolving this for $r$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr - 9 &= 0 \\\\\nr &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n$ax = b$\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and\n\n$$\nb\\neq 0\n$$\n\nIf $a = 0$, then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $az = b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(r - 9)z + 2 &= -1 \\\\\n(r - 9)z + 2 - 2 &= -1 - 2 \\\\\n\\underbrace{(r - 9)}_{a}z &= \\underbrace{-3}_{b}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n$r - 9 = 0$.\n\nSolving this for $r$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr - 9 &= 0 \\\\\nr &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186224846 :question/worked-solution "Let's solve the first two equations:\n\n- To factor the left-hand side of $m^2 + 9m + 14 = 0$, we need to find two numbers that multiply to $14$ and add to $9$. These two numbers are $7$ and $2$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n$m^{2} + 9m + 14|= 0; m^{2}+ 7m + 2m_(⏟)_(9m) + 14|= 0; m(m + 7) + 2(m + 7)|= 0; (m + 7)(m + 2)|= 0$\nApplying the zero product rule, we have the following:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}m + 7 = 0 & ⇒m =-7 \\\\ m + 2 = 0 & ⇒m =-2\\end{bmatrix}$\nThus, the solutions $r$ and $s$, with $r < s$, are the following:\n$r =-7ands =-2$\n- To factor the left-hand side of $m^2 + 5m + 4 = 0$, we need to find two numbers that multiply to $4$ and add to $5$. These two numbers are $1$ and $4$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n$m^{2} + 5m + 4|= 0; m^{2}+ m + 4m_(⏟)_(5m) + 4|= 0; m(m + 1) + 4(m + 1)|= 0; (m + 1)(m + 4)|= 0$\nApplying the zero product rule, we have the following:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}m + 1 = 0 & ⇒m =-1 \\\\ m + 4 = 0 & ⇒m =-4\\end{bmatrix}$\nThus, the solutions $t$ and $u$, with $t < u$, are the following:\n$t =-4andu =-1$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr + t &= -7 + (-4) =-11, \\\\\ns + u &= -2 + (-1) =-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we are given that both $r+t$ and $s+u$ are solutions to the equation\n\n$$\nm^2 + 14m + k = 0\n$$\n\nSo, substituting\n\n$$\nm = s+u = -3\n$$\n\ninto this equation (it also works to substitute\n\n$$\nm=r+t=-11\n$$\n\n) and solving for $k$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(-3)^{2} + 14(-3) + k &= 0 \\\\\n9 - 42 + k &= 0 \\\\\n- 33 + k &= 0 \\\\\nk &= 33\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's solve the first two equations:\n\n- To factor the left-hand side of $m^2 + 9m + 14 = 0$, we need to find two numbers that multiply to $14$ and add to $9$. These two numbers are $7$ and $2$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n$m^{2} + 9m + 14|= 0; m^{2}\\underbrace{+ 7m + 2m}_{9m} + 14|= 0; m(m + 7) + 2(m + 7)|= 0; (m + 7)(m + 2)|= 0$\nApplying the zero product rule, we have the following:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}m + 7 = 0 & ⇒m =-7 \\\\ m + 2 = 0 & ⇒m =-2\\end{bmatrix}$\nThus, the solutions $r$ and $s$, with $r < s$, are the following:\n$r =-7ands =-2$\n- To factor the left-hand side of $m^2 + 5m + 4 = 0$, we need to find two numbers that multiply to $4$ and add to $5$. These two numbers are $1$ and $4$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n$m^{2} + 5m + 4|= 0; m^{2}\\underbrace{+ m + 4m}_{5m} + 4|= 0; m(m + 1) + 4(m + 1)|= 0; (m + 1)(m + 4)|= 0$\nApplying the zero product rule, we have the following:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}m + 1 = 0 & ⇒m =-1 \\\\ m + 4 = 0 & ⇒m =-4\\end{bmatrix}$\nThus, the solutions $t$ and $u$, with $t < u$, are the following:\n$t =-4andu =-1$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr + t &= -7 + (-4) =-11, \\\\\ns + u &= -2 + (-1) =-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we are given that both $r+t$ and $s+u$ are solutions to the equation\n\n$$\nm^2 + 14m + k = 0\n$$\n\nSo, substituting\n\n$$\nm = s+u = -3\n$$\n\ninto this equation (it also works to substitute\n\n$$\nm=r+t=-11\n$$\n\n) and solving for $k$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(-3)^{2} + 14(-3) + k &= 0 \\\\\n9 - 42 + k &= 0 \\\\\n- 33 + k &= 0 \\\\\nk &= 33\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186224898 :question/worked-solution "We are given a system of equations and asked to find $y_1 + y_2$, the sum of the $y$-coordinates of the two points where the graphs intersect.\n\nFirst, we substitute the expression for $y$ into the second equation and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - y &= 2 \\\\\nx - (x^{2} - 5x + 3) &= 2 \\\\\n- x^{2} + 6x - 3 &= 2 \\\\\n- x^{2} + 6x - 5 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 6x + 5 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve this quadratic equation by factoring. We begin by finding two numbers that multiply to $5$ and add to $-6$. These are $-1$ and $-5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 6x + 5 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 5x - x_(⏟)_(-6x) + 5 &= 0 \\\\\nx(x - 5) - 1(x - 5) &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 5)(x - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\nx = 5 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x = 1\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $y$ using\n\n$$\ny = x^2 - 5x + 3{:}\n$$\n\n- If $x_1 = 5$, then\n$y_1 = 5^2 - 5(5) + 3 = 25 - 25 + 3 = 3$\n- If $x_2 = 1$, then\n$y_2 = 1^2 - 5(1)+3=1 - 5 + 3 = -1$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\ny_1 + y_2 = 3 + (-1) = 2\n$$" "We are given a system of equations and asked to find $y_1 + y_2$, the sum of the $y$-coordinates of the two points where the graphs intersect.\n\nFirst, we substitute the expression for $y$ into the second equation and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - y &= 2 \\\\\nx - (x^{2} - 5x + 3) &= 2 \\\\\n- x^{2} + 6x - 3 &= 2 \\\\\n- x^{2} + 6x - 5 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 6x + 5 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve this quadratic equation by factoring. We begin by finding two numbers that multiply to $5$ and add to $-6$. These are $-1$ and $-5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 6x + 5 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} \\underbrace{-5x - x}_{-6x} + 5 &= 0 \\\\\nx(x - 5) - 1(x - 5) &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 5)(x - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\nx = 5 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x = 1\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $y$ using\n\n$$\ny = x^2 - 5x + 3{:}\n$$\n\n- If $x_1 = 5$, then\n$y_1 = 5^2 - 5(5) + 3 = 25 - 25 + 3 = 3$\n- If $x_2 = 1$, then\n$y_2 = 1^2 - 5(1)+3=1 - 5 + 3 = -1$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\ny_1 + y_2 = 3 + (-1) = 2\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186224993 :question/worked-solution "To factor the left-hand side, we need to find two numbers that multiply to $-30$ and add to $-1$. These two numbers are $-6$ and $5$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt^{2} - t - 30 &= 0 \\\\\nt^{2}- 6t + 5t_(⏟)_(-t) - 30 &= 0 \\\\\nt(t - 6) + 5(t - 6) &= 0 \\\\\n(t - 6)(t + 5) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}t - 6 = 0 & ⇒t = 6 \\\\ t + 5 = 0 & ⇒t =-5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, we are given that $k$ is a solution to our equation, with $k \\gt 0$. Therefore, we conclude that $k = 6$." "To factor the left-hand side, we need to find two numbers that multiply to $-30$ and add to $-1$. These two numbers are $-6$ and $5$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt^{2} - t - 30 &= 0 \\\\\nt^{2}\\underbrace{-6t + 5t}_{-t} - 30 &= 0 \\\\\nt(t - 6) + 5(t - 6) &= 0 \\\\\n(t - 6)(t + 5) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}t - 6 = 0 & ⇒t = 6 \\\\ t + 5 = 0 & ⇒t =-5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, we are given that $k$ is a solution to our equation, with $k \\gt 0$. Therefore, we conclude that $k = 6$."]
 [:db/cas 17592186224994 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite our equation in standard form and with a positive leading coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-p^{2} - 3p + 4 &= 0 \\\\\np^{2} + 3p - 4 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo factor the left-hand side, we need to find two numbers that multiply to $-4$ and add to $3$. These two numbers are $4$ and $-1$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np^{2} + 3p - 4 &= 0 \\\\\np^{2}+ 4p - p_(⏟)_(3p) - 4 &= 0 \\\\\np(p + 4) - 1(p + 4) &= 0 \\\\\n(p + 4)(p - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}p + 4 = 0 & ⇒p =-4 \\\\ p - 1 = 0 & ⇒p = 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the **positive** solution to the given equation is $p = 1$." "First, we rewrite our equation in standard form and with a positive leading coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-p^{2} - 3p + 4 &= 0 \\\\\np^{2} + 3p - 4 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo factor the left-hand side, we need to find two numbers that multiply to $-4$ and add to $3$. These two numbers are $4$ and $-1$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np^{2} + 3p - 4 &= 0 \\\\\np^{2}\\underbrace{+ 4p - p}_{3p} - 4 &= 0 \\\\\np(p + 4) - 1(p + 4) &= 0 \\\\\n(p + 4)(p - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}p + 4 = 0 & ⇒p =-4 \\\\ p - 1 = 0 & ⇒p = 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the **positive** solution to the given equation is $p = 1$."]
 [:db/cas 17592186224995 :question/problem "What is the positive solution to the equation $4y^2 - 17y - 15 = 0$?\n\nExplanation:\n\nTo factor the left-hand side, we need two numbers that multiply to $4 \\cdot (-15) = -60$ and add to $-17$. These two numbers are $-20$ and $3$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n\n$4y^{2} - 17y - 15|= 0; 4y^{2}- 20y + 3y_(⏟)_(-17y) - 15|= 0; 4y(y - 5) + 3(y - 5)|= 0; (y - 5)(4y + 3)|= 0$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have the following:\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}y - 5 = 0 & ⇒y = 5 \\\\ 4y + 3 = 0 & ⇒y =-\\frac{3}{4}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the **positive** solution to the given equation is $y = 5$." "What is the positive solution to the equation $4y^2 - 17y - 15 = 0$?\n\nExplanation:\n\nTo factor the left-hand side, we need two numbers that multiply to $4 \\cdot (-15) = -60$ and add to $-17$. These two numbers are $-20$ and $3$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n\n$4y^{2} - 17y - 15|= 0; 4y^{2}\\underbrace{-20y + 3y}_{-17y} - 15|= 0; 4y(y - 5) + 3(y - 5)|= 0; (y - 5)(4y + 3)|= 0$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have the following:\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}y - 5 = 0 & ⇒y = 5 \\\\ 4y + 3 = 0 & ⇒y =-\\frac{3}{4}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the **positive** solution to the given equation is $y = 5$."]
