 [:db/cas 17592186225062 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite our equation in standard form and with a positive leading coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-12p^{2} &= -17p - 5 \\\\\n- 12p^{2} + 17p + 5 &= 0 \\\\\n12p^{2} - 17p - 5 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo factor the left-hand side, we need two numbers that multiply to\n\n$$\n12 \\cdot (-5) = -60\n$$\n\nand add to $-17$. These two numbers are $-20$ and $3$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12p^{2} - 17p - 5 &= 0 \\\\\n12p^{2}- 20p + 3p_(⏟)_(-17p) - 5 &= 0 \\\\\n4p(3p - 5) + 1(3p - 5) &= 0 \\\\\n(4p + 1)(3p - 5) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4p + 1 = 0 & ⇒p =-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 3p - 5 = 0 & ⇒p = \\frac{5}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the **positive** solution to the given equation is\n\n$$\np = \\dfrac{5}{3}\n$$" "First, we rewrite our equation in standard form and with a positive leading coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-12p^{2} &= -17p - 5 \\\\\n- 12p^{2} + 17p + 5 &= 0 \\\\\n12p^{2} - 17p - 5 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo factor the left-hand side, we need two numbers that multiply to\n\n$$\n12 \\cdot (-5) = -60\n$$\n\nand add to $-17$. These two numbers are $-20$ and $3$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12p^{2} - 17p - 5 &= 0 \\\\\n12p^{2}\\underbrace{-20p + 3p}_{-17p} - 5 &= 0 \\\\\n4p(3p - 5) + 1(3p - 5) &= 0 \\\\\n(4p + 1)(3p - 5) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4p + 1 = 0 & ⇒p =-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 3p - 5 = 0 & ⇒p = \\frac{5}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the **positive** solution to the given equation is\n\n$$\np = \\dfrac{5}{3}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186228831 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = y'_\\text{new} \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwith $y'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn general, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g(y_\\text{new}){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {{{y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}-y}_{⏟}}_{g({y}_{\\text{new}})} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $\\Delta x = 1$ is\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^3 + x_\\text{new}y_\\text{new} - y.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is $x_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 1 + 1 = 2.$ Therefore, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+2{y}_{\\text{new}}-1 \\\\  & =3{y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = y'_\\text{new} \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwith $y'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn general, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g(y_\\text{new}){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ \\underbrace{{y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}-y}_{g({y}_{\\text{new}})} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $\\Delta x = 1$ is\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^3 + x_\\text{new}y_\\text{new} - y.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is $x_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 1 + 1 = 2.$ Therefore, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+2{y}_{\\text{new}}-1 \\\\  & =3{y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186228953 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution $y_p$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $u_1$ and $u_2$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\nu_1(x)=-\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{f(x)y_2(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\n\\qquad\nu_2(x)=\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{f(x)y_1(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $W(y_1,y_2)$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)=\\tan x.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c(x)=A\\cos x + B\\sin x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\ny_1(x)=\\cos x,\n\\qquad\ny_2(x)=\\sin x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are linearly independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(x){y}_{2}^{′}(x)-{y}_{1}^{′}(x){y}_{2}(x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are independent.\n\nLet’s compute $u_1(x)$ and $u_2(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-ln\\,|\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x| \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-ln\\,|\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x|)+(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡xln\\,|\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\,{{-\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡xln\\,|\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x|}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=-\\cos x\\ln\\!\\left|\\tan x+\\sec x\\right|.\n$$" "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution $y_p$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $u_1$ and $u_2$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\nu_1(x)=-\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{f(x)y_2(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\n\\qquad\nu_2(x)=\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{f(x)y_1(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $W(y_1,y_2)$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)=\\tan x.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c(x)=A\\cos x + B\\sin x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\ny_1(x)=\\cos x,\n\\qquad\ny_2(x)=\\sin x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are linearly independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(x){y}_{2}^{′}(x)-{y}_{1}^{′}(x){y}_{2}(x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are independent.\n\nLet’s compute $u_1(x)$ and $u_2(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-ln\\,|\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x| \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-ln\\,|\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x|)+(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡xln\\,|\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\,\\underbrace{-\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡xln\\,|\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x|}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=-\\cos x\\ln\\!\\left|\\tan x+\\sec x\\right|.\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186228954 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$. Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution $y_p$ may be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $u_1$ and $u_2$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\nu_1(x) = -\\int \\dfrac{f(x)y_2(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,dx,\n\\qquad\nu_2(x) = \\int \\dfrac{f(x)y_1(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,dx,\n$$\n\nwhere $W(y_1,y_2)$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c(x) = A e^{x} + B x e^{x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = e^{x}, \\qquad y_2(x) = x e^{x}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(x{e}^{x})^{′}-({e}^{x})^{′}(x{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{x})({e}^{x}+x{e}^{x})-({e}^{x})(x{e}^{x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}+x{e}^{2x}-x{e}^{2x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are independent.\n\nNow compute $u_1(x)$ and $u_2(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{18x{e}^{x}\\cdot x{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =-18\\int {x}^{2}\\,dx \\\\  & =-6{x}^{3} \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{18x{e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =18\\int x\\,dx \\\\  & =9{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(-6{x}^{3}){e}^{x}+(9{x}^{2})(x{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =-6{x}^{3}{e}^{x}+9{x}^{3}{e}^{x} \\\\  & =3{x}^{3}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x}+{{3{x}^{3}{e}^{x}}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x) = 3x^3 e^{x}.\n$$" "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$. Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution $y_p$ may be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $u_1$ and $u_2$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\nu_1(x) = -\\int \\dfrac{f(x)y_2(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,dx,\n\\qquad\nu_2(x) = \\int \\dfrac{f(x)y_1(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,dx,\n$$\n\nwhere $W(y_1,y_2)$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c(x) = A e^{x} + B x e^{x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = e^{x}, \\qquad y_2(x) = x e^{x}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(x{e}^{x})^{′}-({e}^{x})^{′}(x{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{x})({e}^{x}+x{e}^{x})-({e}^{x})(x{e}^{x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}+x{e}^{2x}-x{e}^{2x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are independent.\n\nNow compute $u_1(x)$ and $u_2(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{18x{e}^{x}\\cdot x{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =-18\\int {x}^{2}\\,dx \\\\  & =-6{x}^{3} \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{18x{e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =18\\int x\\,dx \\\\  & =9{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(-6{x}^{3}){e}^{x}+(9{x}^{2})(x{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =-6{x}^{3}{e}^{x}+9{x}^{3}{e}^{x} \\\\  & =3{x}^{3}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x}+\\underbrace{3{x}^{3}{e}^{x}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x) = 3x^3 e^{x}.\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186229527 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2})\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =0.25({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g({y}_{\\text{new}}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+0.25({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2} \\\\ {{0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-0.25({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}}_{⏟}}_{g({y}_{\\text{new}})} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.25$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-0.25({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\n$$" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2})\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =0.25({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g({y}_{\\text{new}}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+0.25({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2} \\\\ \\underbrace{0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-0.25({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}}_{g({y}_{\\text{new}})} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.25$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-0.25({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186229528 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{x}_{\\text{new}}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{x}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g({y}_{\\text{new}}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {{{y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-y+{x}_{\\text{new}}}_{⏟}}_{g({y}_{\\text{new}})} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=1$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-y+{x}_{\\text{new}}.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+1=2.$ Therefore, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-1+2 \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{x}_{\\text{new}}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{x}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g({y}_{\\text{new}}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ \\underbrace{{y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-y+{x}_{\\text{new}}}_{g({y}_{\\text{new}})} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=1$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-y+{x}_{\\text{new}}.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+1=2.$ Therefore, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-1+2 \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186229656 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn this example, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}+4({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}})\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}+({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}+({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand moving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n{{{y}_{\\text{new}}-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-y-{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}_{⏟}}_{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.5$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-y-{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}.\n$$" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn this example, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}+4({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}})\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}+({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}+({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand moving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}_{\\text{new}}-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-y-{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}_{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.5$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-y-{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}.\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186229661 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+g(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and $g(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that \n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince the initial conditions are given at $t={t}_{0}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})} & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{bmatrix}\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{bmatrix}}_{s=0}^{s=t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+g(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and $g(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\underbrace{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that \n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince the initial conditions are given at $t={t}_{0}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})} & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{bmatrix}\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{bmatrix}}_{s=0}^{s=t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
