 [:db/cas 17592186247001 :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A-2I:$\n\n$$\nA-2I=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)w=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A-2I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{