rbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1.6 \\\\ 3.0 \\\\ 4.6 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 7.6\\end{bmatrix}}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}22.8 \\\\ 83.4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.06 \\\\ 1.5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=0.06+1.5x.$\n\nFinally, to predict the height of the balloon after $6$ minutes, we substitute $x=6$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.06+1.5(6) \\\\  & =9.06\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the balloon's height after $6$ minutes will be $906$ meters.  \n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=6$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable."]
