 [:db/cas 17592186278349 :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c3/c3333ded3e00e7d7408f0dbd37807f1adec0301691cc6311871bfea6d7b9fa15.png)\n\n \n\nLet's find a type III representation of our region.\n\n**Step 1.** We start by finding the surfaces ${f}_{1}(x,z)$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z).$ Since all $y$ in the region are bounded *below* by $y={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and *above* by $y=2x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{1}(x,z)}\\le y\\le {{2x}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{2}(x,z)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true.\n\n**Step 2.** We find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $y={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and the plane $y=2x$ onto the $xz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $y.$ Substituting this expression for $y$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & ={x}^{2}+{z}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x+1)+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle $(x-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ centered at $(1,0)$ in the $xz$-plane. \n\n![](images/0b/0bcadeca7ede6f709bf06391e10da66afd1b514fd0e041bbf108e6e4229b0da6.png)\n\nThe region is bounded *below* by $z=-\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^{2}}$ and bounded *above* by $z=\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^{2}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{-\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{1}(x)}\\le z\\le {{\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{2}(x)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false.\n\n**Step 3.** We find the bounds for $x.$ From the diagram, $x$ is bounded *below* by $x=0$ and bounded *above* by $x=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{0}_{⏟}}_{a}\\le x\\le {{2}_{⏟}}_{b}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement III is true.\n\nFinally, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "A typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c3/c3333ded3e00e7d7408f0dbd37807f1adec0301691cc6311871bfea6d7b9fa15.png)\n\n \n\nLet's find a type III representation of our region.\n\n**Step 1.** We start by finding the surfaces ${f}_{1}(x,z)$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z).$ Since all $y$ in the region are bounded *below* by $y={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and *above* by $y=2x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}_{{f}_{1}(x,z)}\\le y\\le \\underbrace{2x}_{{f}_{2}(x,z)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true.\n\n**Step 2.** We find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $y={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and the plane $y=2x$ onto the $xz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $y.$ Substituting this expression for $y$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & ={x}^{2}+{z}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x+1)+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle $(x-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ centered at $(1,0)$ in the $xz$-plane. \n\n![](images/0b/0bcadeca7ede6f709bf06391e10da66afd1b514fd0e041bbf108e6e4229b0da6.png)\n\nThe region is bounded *below* by $z=-\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^{2}}$ and bounded *above* by $z=\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^{2}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{-\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^{2}}}_{{g}_{1}(x)}\\le z\\le \\underbrace{\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^{2}}}_{{g}_{2}(x)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false.\n\n**Step 3.** We find the bounds for $x.$ From the diagram, $x$ is bounded *below* by $x=0$ and bounded *above* by $x=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{0}_{a}\\le x\\le \\underbrace{2}_{b}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement III is true.\n\nFinally, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""]
 [:db/cas 17592186278399 :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/05/05f940e247c6cd5d862d81fc3a34658353bef5513dfe862bbc67a120c0345838.png)\n\n \n\nLet's find a type I representation of our region.\n\n**Step 1.** We start by finding the surfaces ${f}_{1}(x,y)$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y).$ Since all $z$ in the region are bounded *below* by $z=2y+1$ and *above* by $z=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},$ we have \n\n$$\n{{2y+1}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{1}(x,y)}\\le z\\le {{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{2}(x,y)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is false.\n\n**Step 2.** We find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $z=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=2y+1$ onto the $xy$-plane. \n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $z.$ Substituting this expression for $z$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+1 & =4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2y & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2}+({y}^{2}+2y+1) & =4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(y+1)}^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle ${x}^{2}+{(y+1)}^{2}=4$ centered at $(0,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.    \n\n![](images/39/39d2daceb171d26d3396e5aaeab4d8b8da2e9b8324e4042f2b7ed048f4a5fdeb.png)\n\nThe region is bounded on the *left* by $x=-\\sqrt{4-(y+1)^{2}}$ and bounded on the *right* by $x=\\sqrt{4-(y+1)^{2}}.$ Therefore,      \n\n$$\n{{-\\sqrt{4-(y+1)^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{1}(y)}\\le x\\le {{\\sqrt{4-(y+1)^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{2}(y)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is true.\n\n**Step 3.** We find the bounds for $y.$ From the diagram, $y$ is bounded *below* by $y=-3$ and bounded *above* by $y=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{-3}_{⏟}}_{a}\\le y\\le {{1}_{⏟}}_{b}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement III is false.\n\nFinally, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "A typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/05/05f940e247c6cd5d862d81fc3a34658353bef5513dfe862bbc67a120c0345838.png)\n\n \n\nLet's find a type I representation of our region.\n\n**Step 1.** We start by finding the surfaces ${f}_{1}(x,y)$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y).$ Since all $z$ in the region are bounded *below* by $z=2y+1$ and *above* by $z=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{2y+1}_{{f}_{1}(x,y)}\\le z\\le \\underbrace{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}_{{f}_{2}(x,y)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is false.\n\n**Step 2.** We find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $z=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=2y+1$ onto the $xy$-plane. \n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $z.$ Substituting this expression for $z$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+1 & =4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2y & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2}+({y}^{2}+2y+1) & =4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(y+1)}^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle ${x}^{2}+{(y+1)}^{2}=4$ centered at $(0,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.    \n\n![](images/39/39d2daceb171d26d3396e5aaeab4d8b8da2e9b8324e4042f2b7ed048f4a5fdeb.png)\n\nThe region is bounded on the *left* by $x=-\\sqrt{4-(y+1)^{2}}$ and bounded on the *right* by $x=\\sqrt{4-(y+1)^{2}}.$ Therefore,      \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{-\\sqrt{4-(y+1)^{2}}}_{{g}_{1}(y)}\\le x\\le \\underbrace{\\sqrt{4-(y+1)^{2}}}_{{g}_{2}(y)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is true.\n\n**Step 3.** We find the bounds for $y.$ From the diagram, $y$ is bounded *below* by $y=-3$ and bounded *above* by $y=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{-3}_{a}\\le y\\le \\underbrace{1}_{b}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement III is false.\n\nFinally, the correct answer is \"II only.\""]
 [:db/cas 17592186278417 :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=2y-x-5$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=-5,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-1,\\,2,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=5.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2y-x-5$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨-2y,\\,\\frac{x}{2},\\,z⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨-2y,\\,\\frac{x}{2},\\,2y-x-5⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(-2y-x+2y-x-5) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(-2x-5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(-2x-5)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(2x+5)\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(2x+5)\\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{6}}}(2x+5)\\cdot \\cancel{\\sqrt{6}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-(2x+5)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+5)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+5)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-[y]_{-1}^{0}\\cdot [{x}^{2}+5x]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-(0+1)\\cdot (1+5-0) \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=2y-x-5$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\underbrace{⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=-5,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\underbrace{⟨-1,\\,2,\\,-1⟩}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=5.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2y-x-5$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨-2y,\\,\\frac{x}{2},\\,z⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨-2y,\\,\\frac{x}{2},\\,2y-x-5⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(-2y-x+2y-x-5) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(-2x-5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(-2x-5)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(2x+5)\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(2x+5)\\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{6}}}(2x+5)\\cdot \\cancel{\\sqrt{6}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-(2x+5)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+5)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+5)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-[y]_{-1}^{0}\\cdot [{x}^{2}+5x]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-(0+1)\\cdot (1+5-0) \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186278418 :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=2x-2y+1$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-2,\\,2,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=1,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨2,\\,-2,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨-2,\\,2,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-2,\\,2,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-2,\\,2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2x-2y+1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨-\\frac{{y}^{2}+1}{2},\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{2},\\,z-1⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨-2,\\,2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-\\frac{{y}^{2}+1}{2},\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{2},\\,2x-2y⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,\\,2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({y}^{2}+1+{x}^{2}+1+2x-2y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[({y}^{2}-2y+1)+({x}^{2}+2x+1)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{3}[(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}]\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{3}[(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}]\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{3}[(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}]\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\cancel{3}}[(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}]\\cdot \\cancel{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}]\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}]\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{[(x+1)^{2}\\cdot y+\\frac{(y-1)^{3}}{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}[(x+1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{(x+1)^{3}}{3}+\\frac{x}{3}]}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=2x-2y+1$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\underbrace{⟨-2,\\,2,\\,1⟩}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=1,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\underbrace{⟨2,\\,-2,\\,-1⟩}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨-2,\\,2,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-2,\\,2,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-2,\\,2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2x-2y+1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨-\\frac{{y}^{2}+1}{2},\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{2},\\,z-1⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨-2,\\,2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-\\frac{{y}^{2}+1}{2},\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{2},\\,2x-2y⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,\\,2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({y}^{2}+1+{x}^{2}+1+2x-2y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[({y}^{2}-2y+1)+({x}^{2}+2x+1)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{3}[(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}]\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{3}[(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}]\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{3}[(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}]\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\cancel{3}}[(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}]\\cdot \\cancel{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}]\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}]\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{[(x+1)^{2}\\cdot y+\\frac{(y-1)^{3}}{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}[(x+1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{(x+1)^{3}}{3}+\\frac{x}{3}]}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186278434 :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c3/c3333ded3e00e7d7408f0dbd37807f1adec0301691cc6311871bfea6d7b9fa15.png)\n\n \n\nLet's find a type III representation of our region.\n\n**Step 1.** We start by finding the surfaces ${f}_{1}(x,z)$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z).$ Since all $y$ in the region are bounded *below* by $y=2{x}^{2}+2z-1$ and *above* by $y=-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{{2{x}^{2}+2z-1}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{1}(x,z)}\\le y\\le {{-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{2}(x,z)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true.\n\n**Step 2.** We find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $y=-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1$ and the parabolic cylinder $y=2{x}^{2}+2z-1$ onto $xz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equations are already solved for $y.$ Equating the two expressions, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1 & =2{x}^{2}+2z-1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+2z-2 & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+({z}^{2}+2z+1) & =3 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+(z+1)^{2} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $3{x}^{2}+(z+1)^{2}=3$ centered at $(0,-1)$ in the $xz$-plane. \n\n![](images/86/868fceefed2a426feebe2a9199279826aaee935963fec8fa3761b17b97542c34.png)\n\nThe region is bounded *below* by $z=-1-\\sqrt{3-3{x}^{2}}$ and bounded *above* by $z=-1+\\sqrt{3-3{x}^{2}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{-1-\\sqrt{3-3{x}^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{1}(x)}\\le z\\le {{-1+\\sqrt{3-3{x}^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{2}(x)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false.\n\n**Step 3.** We find the bounds for $x.$ From the diagram, $x$ is bounded *below* by $x=-1$ and bounded *above* by $x=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{-1}_{⏟}}_{a}\\le x\\le {{1}_{⏟}}_{b}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement III is false.\n\nFinally, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "A typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c3/c3333ded3e00e7d7408f0dbd37807f1adec0301691cc6311871bfea6d7b9fa15.png)\n\n \n\nLet's find a type III representation of our region.\n\n**Step 1.** We start by finding the surfaces ${f}_{1}(x,z)$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z).$ Since all $y$ in the region are bounded *below* by $y=2{x}^{2}+2z-1$ and *above* by $y=-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{2{x}^{2}+2z-1}_{{f}_{1}(x,z)}\\le y\\le \\underbrace{-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1}_{{f}_{2}(x,z)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true.\n\n**Step 2.** We find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $y=-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1$ and the parabolic cylinder $y=2{x}^{2}+2z-1$ onto $xz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equations are already solved for $y.$ Equating the two expressions, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1 & =2{x}^{2}+2z-1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+2z-2 & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+({z}^{2}+2z+1) & =3 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+(z+1)^{2} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $3{x}^{2}+(z+1)^{2}=3$ centered at $(0,-1)$ in the $xz$-plane. \n\n![](images/86/868fceefed2a426feebe2a9199279826aaee935963fec8fa3761b17b97542c34.png)\n\nThe region is bounded *below* by $z=-1-\\sqrt{3-3{x}^{2}}$ and bounded *above* by $z=-1+\\sqrt{3-3{x}^{2}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{-1-\\sqrt{3-3{x}^{2}}}_{{g}_{1}(x)}\\le z\\le \\underbrace{-1+\\sqrt{3-3{x}^{2}}}_{{g}_{2}(x)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false.\n\n**Step 3.** We find the bounds for $x.$ From the diagram, $x$ is bounded *below* by $x=-1$ and bounded *above* by $x=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{-1}_{a}\\le x\\le \\underbrace{1}_{b}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement III is false.\n\nFinally, the correct answer is \"I only.\""]
 [:db/cas 17592186278506 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+y)=2 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+y)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨-x,\\,-y,\\,xyz⟩{|}_{z=2x+y} \\\\  & =⟨-x,\\,-y,\\,xy(2x+y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{{-x}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{-y}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{xy(2x+y)}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨-x,\\,-y,\\,xyz⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(-x)\\cdot 2-(-y)\\cdot (1)+xy(2x+y))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+y+2{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+y+2{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[2xy+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{x{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+\\frac{1}{2}+{x}^{2}+\\frac{x}{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+\\frac{7x}{3}+\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{7{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{x}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{7}{6}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+y)=2 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+y)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨-x,\\,-y,\\,xyz⟩{|}_{z=2x+y} \\\\  & =⟨-x,\\,-y,\\,xy(2x+y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{-x}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{-y}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{xy(2x+y)}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨-x,\\,-y,\\,xyz⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(-x)\\cdot 2-(-y)\\cdot (1)+xy(2x+y))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+y+2{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+y+2{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[2xy+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{x{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+\\frac{1}{2}+{x}^{2}+\\frac{x}{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+\\frac{7x}{3}+\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{7{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{x}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{7}{6}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186278508 :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional region of type II can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/e4/e4c9cbbad2f13e9cc125ddbb89125ed2d734eee3f80737125961f4af1846a972.png)\n\n \n\nLet's find a type II representation of our region.\n\n**Step 1.** We start by finding the surfaces ${f}_{1}(y,z)$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z).$ Since all $x$ in the region are bounded *below* by $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ and *above* by $x=\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16},$ we have\n\n$$\n{{\\frac{1}{4}}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{1}(y,z)}\\le x\\le {{\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{2}(y,z)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true.\n\n**Step 2.** We find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $x=\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}$ and the plane $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ onto the $yz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $x.$ Substituting this expression for $x$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}=1$ centered at $(0,0)$ in the $yz$-plane. \n\n![](images/46/46757c2bf93af4916afbd019f0faed2eef25245e987acbeff7eafb3e0ca7cd8c.png)\n\nThe region is bounded *below* by $z=-4\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}}$ and bounded *above* by $z=4\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{-4\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{1}(y)}\\le z\\le {{4\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{2}(y)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false.\n\n**Step 3.** We find the bounds for $y.$ From the diagram, $y$ is bounded *below* by $y=-3$ and bounded *above* by $y=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{-3}_{⏟}}_{a}\\le y\\le {{3}_{⏟}}_{b}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement III is true.\n\nFinally, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "A typical three-dimensional region of type II can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/e4/e4c9cbbad2f13e9cc125ddbb89125ed2d734eee3f80737125961f4af1846a972.png)\n\n \n\nLet's find a type II representation of our region.\n\n**Step 1.** We start by finding the surfaces ${f}_{1}(y,z)$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z).$ Since all $x$ in the region are bounded *below* by $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ and *above* by $x=\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\frac{1}{4}}_{{f}_{1}(y,z)}\\le x\\le \\underbrace{\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}}_{{f}_{2}(y,z)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true.\n\n**Step 2.** We find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $x=\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}$ and the plane $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ onto the $yz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $x.$ Substituting this expression for $x$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}=1$ centered at $(0,0)$ in the $yz$-plane. \n\n![](images/46/46757c2bf93af4916afbd019f0faed2eef25245e987acbeff7eafb3e0ca7cd8c.png)\n\nThe region is bounded *below* by $z=-4\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}}$ and bounded *above* by $z=4\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{-4\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}}}_{{g}_{1}(y)}\\le z\\le \\underbrace{4\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}}}_{{g}_{2}(y)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false.\n\n**Step 3.** We find the bounds for $y.$ From the diagram, $y$ is bounded *below* by $y=-3$ and bounded *above* by $y=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{-3}_{a}\\le y\\le \\underbrace{3}_{b}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement III is true.\n\nFinally, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""]
 [:db/cas 17592186278509 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y)=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨xy,\\,x+y,\\,{e}^{z}⟩{|}_{z=x+y} \\\\  & =⟨xy,\\,x+y,\\,{e}^{x+y}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{{xy}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{x+y}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{{e}^{x+y}}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨xy,\\,x+y,\\,{e}^{z}⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(xy)\\cdot 1-(x+y)\\cdot 1+{e}^{x+y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-xy-x-y+{e}^{x+y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}(-xy-x-y+{e}^{x+y})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[-\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{2}-xy-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+{e}^{x+y}]}_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}-{x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{e}^{2x}-{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}+{e}^{2x}-{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{8}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}-{e}^{x}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}-e)-(0+\\frac{1}{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{2}-8e-1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y)=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨xy,\\,x+y,\\,{e}^{z}⟩{|}_{z=x+y} \\\\  & =⟨xy,\\,x+y,\\,{e}^{x+y}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{xy}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{x+y}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{{e}^{x+y}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨xy,\\,x+y,\\,{e}^{z}⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(xy)\\cdot 1-(x+y)\\cdot 1+{e}^{x+y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-xy-x-y+{e}^{x+y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}(-xy-x-y+{e}^{x+y})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[-\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{2}-xy-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+{e}^{x+y}]}_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}-{x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{e}^{2x}-{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}+{e}^{2x}-{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{8}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}-{e}^{x}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}-e)-(0+\\frac{1}{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{2}-8e-1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186278522 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y) \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,y⟩{|}_{z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)} \\\\  & =⟨{{1}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{0}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{y}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨1,\\,0,\\,y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y))-0\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y))+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}{\\int }_{-x}^{0}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}{[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=-x}^{y=0}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+0)-(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}]}_{-\\pi }^{0} \\\\  & =0-(-0+(-\\pi )-\\frac{(-\\pi )^{3}}{6}) \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y) \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,y⟩{|}_{z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{1}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{0}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{y}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨1,\\,0,\\,y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y))-0\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y))+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}{\\int }_{-x}^{0}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}{[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=-x}^{y=0}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+0)-(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}]}_{-\\pi }^{0} \\\\  & =0-(-0+(-\\pi )-\\frac{(-\\pi )^{3}}{6}) \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186278545 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x})={e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x})=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the flux of $F$ through $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨y,\\,x+z,\\,0⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨y,\\,x+z,\\,0⟩{|}_{z={e}^{x}} \\\\  & =⟨{{y}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{x+{e}^{x}}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{0}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨y,\\,x+z,\\,0⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y\\cdot ({e}^{x})-(x+{e}^{x})\\cdot 0+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-y{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[-\\frac{{y}^{2}{e}^{x}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{[{e}^{x}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-1}-e}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x})={e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x})=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the flux of $F$ through $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨y,\\,x+z,\\,0⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨y,\\,x+z,\\,0⟩{|}_{z={e}^{x}} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{y}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{x+{e}^{x}}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{0}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨y,\\,x+z,\\,0⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y\\cdot ({e}^{x})-(x+{e}^{x})\\cdot 0+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-y{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[-\\frac{{y}^{2}{e}^{x}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{[{e}^{x}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-1}-e}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186278546 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(xy))=\\frac{y}{{cos}^{2}⁡(xy)} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(xy))=\\frac{x}{{cos}^{2}⁡(xy)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the flux of $F$ through $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨{cos}^{2}⁡(xy),\\,{cos}^{2}⁡(xy),\\,1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨{cos}^{2}⁡(xy),\\,{cos}^{2}⁡(xy),\\,1⟩{|}_{z=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(xy)} \\\\  & =⟨{{{cos}^{2}⁡(xy)}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{{cos}^{2}⁡(xy)}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{1}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨{cos}^{2}⁡(xy),\\,{cos}^{2}⁡(xy),\\,1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-({cos}^{2}⁡(xy))\\cdot (\\frac{y}{{cos}^{2}⁡(xy)})-({cos}^{2}⁡(xy))\\cdot (\\frac{x}{{cos}^{2}⁡(xy)})+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y-x+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-x}^{0}(-y-x+1)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-xy+y]}_{y=-x}^{y=0}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-{x}^{2}+x)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+x)\\,dx \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(xy))=\\frac{y}{{cos}^{2}⁡(xy)} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(xy))=\\frac{x}{{cos}^{2}⁡(xy)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the flux of $F$ through $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨{cos}^{2}⁡(xy),\\,{cos}^{2}⁡(xy),\\,1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨{cos}^{2}⁡(xy),\\,{cos}^{2}⁡(xy),\\,1⟩{|}_{z=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(xy)} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{{cos}^{2}⁡(xy)}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{{cos}^{2}⁡(xy)}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{1}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨{cos}^{2}⁡(xy),\\,{cos}^{2}⁡(xy),\\,1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-({cos}^{2}⁡(xy))\\cdot (\\frac{y}{{cos}^{2}⁡(xy)})-({cos}^{2}⁡(xy))\\cdot (\\frac{x}{{cos}^{2}⁡(xy)})+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y-x+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-x}^{0}(-y-x+1)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-xy+y]}_{y=-x}^{y=0}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-{x}^{2}+x)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+x)\\,dx \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186278547 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\sqrt{xy})=\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{x}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\\sqrt{xy})=\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the flux of $F$ through $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}},\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}},\\,{z}^{2}+1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}},\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}},\\,{z}^{2}+1⟩{|}_{z=2\\sqrt{xy}} \\\\  & =⟨{{\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}}}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}}}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{4xy+1}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}},\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}},\\,{z}^{2}+1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{y}{x}}-\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}}+4xy+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1-\\frac{x}{y}+4xy+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-\\frac{x}{y}+4xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(-\\frac{x}{y}+4xy)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}{[-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+2x{y}^{2}]}_{y=1}^{y=2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}(-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+8x+0-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}(6-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(6-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2){[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{3}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7(6-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\sqrt{xy})=\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{x}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\\sqrt{xy})=\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the flux of $F$ through $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}},\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}},\\,{z}^{2}+1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}},\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}},\\,{z}^{2}+1⟩{|}_{z=2\\sqrt{xy}} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}}}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}}}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{4xy+1}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}},\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}},\\,{z}^{2}+1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{y}{x}}-\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}}+4xy+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1-\\frac{x}{y}+4xy+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-\\frac{x}{y}+4xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(-\\frac{x}{y}+4xy)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}{[-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+2x{y}^{2}]}_{y=1}^{y=2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}(-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+8x+0-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}(6-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(6-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2){[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{3}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7(6-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186278548 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the flux of $F$ through $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨x+y,\\,y-x,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨x+y,\\,y-x,\\,z⟩{|}_{z=1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨{{x+y}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{y-x}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨x+y,\\,y-x,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(x+y)\\cdot (2x)-(y-x)\\cdot (2y)+1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2{x}^{2}-2xy-2{y}^{2}+2xy+1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[y-{x}^{2}y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{y=-1}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[(1-{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3})-(-1+{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3})]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(2-2{x}^{2}-\\frac{2}{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[2x-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}x]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(2-\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{2}{3})-(-2+\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the flux of $F$ through $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨x+y,\\,y-x,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨x+y,\\,y-x,\\,z⟩{|}_{z=1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{x+y}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{y-x}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨x+y,\\,y-x,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(x+y)\\cdot (2x)-(y-x)\\cdot (2y)+1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2{x}^{2}-2xy-2{y}^{2}+2xy+1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[y-{x}^{2}y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{y=-1}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[(1-{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3})-(-1+{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3})]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(2-2{x}^{2}-\\frac{2}{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[2x-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}x]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(2-\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{2}{3})-(-2+\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186278551 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+y)=2 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+y)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the flux of $F$ through $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨3{x}^{3}y,\\,2yz,\\,2x(y+z)⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨3{x}^{3}y,\\,2yz,\\,2xz⟩{|}_{z=2x+y} \\\\ F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨3{x}^{3}y,\\,2y(2x+y),\\,2x(y+2x+y)⟩{|}_{z=2x+y} \\\\  & =⟨{{3{x}^{3}y}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{4xy+2{y}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{4xy+4{x}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨3{x}^{3}y,\\,2yz,\\,2x(y+z)⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(3{x}^{3}y)\\cdot (2)-(4xy+2{y}^{2})\\cdot (1)+4xy+4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-6{x}^{3}y-2{y}^{2}+4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-6{x}^{3}y-2{y}^{2}+4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[-3{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-\\frac{2{y}^{3}}{3}+4{x}^{2}y]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(-3{x}^{3}-\\frac{2}{3}+4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{2x}{3}+\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-12-\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{32}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+y)=2 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+y)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the flux of $F$ through $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨3{x}^{3}y,\\,2yz,\\,2x(y+z)⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨3{x}^{3}y,\\,2yz,\\,2xz⟩{|}_{z=2x+y} \\\\ F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨3{x}^{3}y,\\,2y(2x+y),\\,2x(y+2x+y)⟩{|}_{z=2x+y} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{3{x}^{3}y}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{4xy+2{y}^{2}}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{4xy+4{x}^{2}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨3{x}^{3}y,\\,2yz,\\,2x(y+z)⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(3{x}^{3}y)\\cdot (2)-(4xy+2{y}^{2})\\cdot (1)+4xy+4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-6{x}^{3}y-2{y}^{2}+4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-6{x}^{3}y-2{y}^{2}+4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[-3{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-\\frac{2{y}^{3}}{3}+4{x}^{2}y]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(-3{x}^{3}-\\frac{2}{3}+4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{2x}{3}+\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-12-\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{32}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186281348 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot {{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{⏟}}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}v{e}^{u} & {e}^{u} \\\\ {e}^{v} & u{e}^{v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =v{e}^{u}\\cdot u{e}^{v}-{e}^{v}\\cdot {e}^{u} \\\\  & =uv{e}^{u+v}-{e}^{u+v} \\\\  & =(uv-1){e}^{u+v} \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(-1,1)} & =(uv-1){e}^{u+v}\\,{|}_{u=-1\\,v=1} \\\\  & =(-1\\cdot 1-1){e}^{-1+1} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|-2|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =2\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot \\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}v{e}^{u} & {e}^{u} \\\\ {e}^{v} & u{e}^{v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =v{e}^{u}\\cdot u{e}^{v}-{e}^{v}\\cdot {e}^{u} \\\\  & =uv{e}^{u+v}-{e}^{u+v} \\\\  & =(uv-1){e}^{u+v} \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(-1,1)} & =(uv-1){e}^{u+v}\\,{|}_{u=-1\\,v=1} \\\\  & =(-1\\cdot 1-1){e}^{-1+1} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|-2|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =2\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186281366 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot {{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{⏟}}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}} & -1 \\\\ 2uv & {u}^{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}})\\cdot ({u}^{2})-2uv\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-1+2uv \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(2,2)} & =(-1+2uv)\\,{|}_{u=2\\,v=2} \\\\  & =-1+2(2)(2) \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|7|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =7\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot \\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}} & -1 \\\\ 2uv & {u}^{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}})\\cdot ({u}^{2})-2uv\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-1+2uv \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(2,2)} & =(-1+2uv)\\,{|}_{u=2\\,v=2} \\\\  & =-1+2(2)(2) \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|7|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =7\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186281367 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot {{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{⏟}}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}} & 2v \\\\ v & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot u-v\\cdot 2v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{u}-2{v}^{2} \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(4,2)} & ={\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{u}-2{v}^{2}\\,|}_{u=4\\,v=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{4}-2(2)^{2} \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|-7|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =7\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot \\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}} & 2v \\\\ v & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot u-v\\cdot 2v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{u}-2{v}^{2} \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(4,2)} & ={\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{u}-2{v}^{2}\\,|}_{u=4\\,v=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{4}-2(2)^{2} \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|-7|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =7\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186281368 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot {{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{⏟}}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & -2v \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u)\\cdot 1-1\\cdot (-2v) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u+2v \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(0,3)} & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u+2v)\\,{|}_{u=0\\,v=3} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+2(3) \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|6|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =6\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot \\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & -2v \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u)\\cdot 1-1\\cdot (-2v) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u+2v \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(0,3)} & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u+2v)\\,{|}_{u=0\\,v=3} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+2(3) \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|6|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =6\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186281371 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot {{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{⏟}}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2u}{v} & -\\frac{{u}^{2}}{{v}^{2}} \\\\ v & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{2u}{v}\\cdot u-v\\cdot (-\\frac{{u}^{2}}{{v}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2{u}^{2}}{v}+\\frac{{u}^{2}}{v} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{u}^{2}}{v} \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(2,4)} & =(\\frac{3{u}^{2}}{v})\\,{|}_{u=2\\,v=4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(2)^{2}}{4} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|3|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =3\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot \\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2u}{v} & -\\frac{{u}^{2}}{{v}^{2}} \\\\ v & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{2u}{v}\\cdot u-v\\cdot (-\\frac{{u}^{2}}{{v}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2{u}^{2}}{v}+\\frac{{u}^{2}}{v} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{u}^{2}}{v} \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(2,4)} & =(\\frac{3{u}^{2}}{v})\\,{|}_{u=2\\,v=4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(2)^{2}}{4} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|3|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =3\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186281372 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot {{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{⏟}}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{v}}{2\\sqrt{u}} & \\frac{\\sqrt{u}}{2\\sqrt{v}} \\\\ {v}^{2} & 2uv\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{v}}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot 2uv-{v}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{u}}{2\\sqrt{v}} \\\\  & =v\\sqrt{uv}-\\frac{1}{2}v\\sqrt{uv} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}v\\sqrt{uv} \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(1,4)} & ={(\\frac{1}{2}v\\sqrt{uv})\\,|}_{u=1\\,v=4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4)\\sqrt{(1)(4)} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|4|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =4\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot \\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{v}}{2\\sqrt{u}} & \\frac{\\sqrt{u}}{2\\sqrt{v}} \\\\ {v}^{2} & 2uv\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{v}}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot 2uv-{v}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{u}}{2\\sqrt{v}} \\\\  & =v\\sqrt{uv}-\\frac{1}{2}v\\sqrt{uv} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}v\\sqrt{uv} \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(1,4)} & ={(\\frac{1}{2}v\\sqrt{uv})\\,|}_{u=1\\,v=4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4)\\sqrt{(1)(4)} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|4|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =4\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186281387 :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=\\frac{1}{2}x-y-1$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-\\frac{1}{2},\\,1,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=-1,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\,-1,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=1.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨-\\frac{1}{2},\\,1,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-\\frac{1}{2},\\,1,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}⟨-\\frac{1}{2},\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-1,\\,2,\\,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=\\frac{1}{2}x-y-1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x,\\,z,\\,y⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨-1,\\,2,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨x,\\,\\frac{1}{2}x-y-1,\\,y⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,2,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(-x+(x-2y-2)+2y) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}(-\\frac{2}{3})\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-\\frac{2}{3})\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-\\frac{2}{3})\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-\\frac{\\cancel{2}}{\\cancel{3}})\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{3}}{\\cancel{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-1\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=\\frac{1}{2}x-y-1$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\underbrace{⟨-\\frac{1}{2},\\,1,\\,1⟩}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=-1,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\underbrace{⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\,-1,\\,-1⟩}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=1.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨-\\frac{1}{2},\\,1,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-\\frac{1}{2},\\,1,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}⟨-\\frac{1}{2},\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-1,\\,2,\\,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=\\frac{1}{2}x-y-1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x,\\,z,\\,y⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨-1,\\,2,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨x,\\,\\frac{1}{2}x-y-1,\\,y⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,2,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(-x+(x-2y-2)+2y) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}(-\\frac{2}{3})\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-\\frac{2}{3})\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-\\frac{2}{3})\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-\\frac{\\cancel{2}}{\\cancel{3}})\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{3}}{\\cancel{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-1\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186281388 :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=x-y$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=0,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨1,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=0.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=x-y$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x+3,\\,x+y,\\,z-x+6⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨x+3,\\,x+y,\\,6-y⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}(-(x+3)+(x+y)+(6-y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{6} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=x-y$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\underbrace{⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=0,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\underbrace{⟨1,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=0.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=x-y$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x+3,\\,x+y,\\,z-x+6⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨x+3,\\,x+y,\\,6-y⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}(-(x+3)+(x+y)+(6-y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{6} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186281399 :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}u+v-2 \\\\ u-3v+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}u+v \\\\ u-3v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{vmatrix}=1\\cdot (-3)-1\\cdot 1=-4.\n$$\n\nSo, the area scale factor of the transformation is\n\n$$\n|det(A)|=|-4|=4.\n$$" "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}u+v-2 \\\\ u-3v+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}u+v \\\\ u-3v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{vmatrix}=1\\cdot (-3)-1\\cdot 1=-4.\n$$\n\nSo, the area scale factor of the transformation is\n\n$$\n|det(A)|=|-4|=4.\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186281401 :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u-v \\\\ 2v-u-5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u-v \\\\ -u+2v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}=2\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot (-1)=3.\n$$\n\nSo, the area scale factor of the transformation is\n\n$$\n|det(A)|=|3|=3.\n$$" "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u-v \\\\ 2v-u-5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u-v \\\\ -u+2v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}=2\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot (-1)=3.\n$$\n\nSo, the area scale factor of the transformation is\n\n$$\n|det(A)|=|3|=3.\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186281403 :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}u+5v+2 \\\\ 1-u\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}u+5v \\\\ -u\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}=1\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot 5=5.\n$$\n\nSo, the area scale factor of the transformation is\n\n$$\n|det(A)|=|5|=5.\n$$" "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}u+5v+2 \\\\ 1-u\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}u+5v \\\\ -u\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}=1\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot 5=5.\n$$\n\nSo, the area scale factor of the transformation is\n\n$$\n|det(A)|=|5|=5.\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186281404 :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u-2v-3 \\\\ u-v+2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u-2v \\\\ u-v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}=2\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot (-2)=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the area scale factor of the transformation is\n\n$$\n|det(A)|=|0|=0.\n$$" "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u-2v-3 \\\\ u-v+2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u-2v \\\\ u-v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}=2\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot (-2)=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the area scale factor of the transformation is\n\n$$\n|det(A)|=|0|=0.\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186281453 :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the paraboloid $z=1+{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}$ and the boundary of our infinite vertical column. One such surface is our paraboloid $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=1+{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1+{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1+{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})=4y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨y,\\,-x,\\,1⟩{|}_{z=1+{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨y,\\,-x,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{{y}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{-x}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{1}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y\\cdot (2x)-(-x)\\cdot (4y)+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy+1)\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/d8/d84b3ecc79c58a128d2f7ea262692d9f66dd9882ff244fb7c3dadd2b2571b550.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2xy+1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-1}^{0}\\,2xy+1\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the paraboloid $z=1+{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}$ and the boundary of our infinite vertical column. One such surface is our paraboloid $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=1+{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1+{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1+{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})=4y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨y,\\,-x,\\,1⟩{|}_{z=1+{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨y,\\,-x,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{y}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{-x}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{1}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y\\cdot (2x)-(-x)\\cdot (4y)+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy+1)\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/d8/d84b3ecc79c58a128d2f7ea262692d9f66dd9882ff244fb7c3dadd2b2571b550.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2xy+1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-1}^{0}\\,2xy+1\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186281454 :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=4.$ One such surface would be the plane $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=4.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4)=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨-x{e}^{z},\\,0,\\,{e}^{z}⟩{|}_{z=4} \\\\  & =⟨{{-x{e}^{4}}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{0}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{{e}^{4}}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot (-x{e}^{4})-0\\cdot 0+{e}^{4})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{e}^{4}\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ with radius $2$ centered at the origin. Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{e}^{4}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{4}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={e}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}r\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & ={e}^{4}[\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi }\\cdot {[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{r=0}^{r=2} \\\\  & ={e}^{4}(2\\pi -0)\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =4\\pi {e}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=4.$ One such surface would be the plane $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=4.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4)=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨-x{e}^{z},\\,0,\\,{e}^{z}⟩{|}_{z=4} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{-x{e}^{4}}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{0}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{{e}^{4}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot (-x{e}^{4})-0\\cdot 0+{e}^{4})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{e}^{4}\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ with radius $2$ centered at the origin. Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{e}^{4}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{4}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={e}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}r\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & ={e}^{4}[\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi }\\cdot {[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{r=0}^{r=2} \\\\  & ={e}^{4}(2\\pi -0)\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =4\\pi {e}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186281457 :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the plane $z=x.$ One such surface would be the plane $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=x.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨0,\\,z,\\,x⟩{|}_{z=x} \\\\  & =⟨{{0}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{x}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{x}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot 1-x\\cdot 0+x))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ with radius $1$ centered at the origin. Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the plane $z=x.$ One such surface would be the plane $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=x.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨0,\\,z,\\,x⟩{|}_{z=x} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{0}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{x}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{x}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot 1-x\\cdot 0+x))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ with radius $1$ centered at the origin. Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186281487 :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional region of type II can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/e4/e4c9cbbad2f13e9cc125ddbb89125ed2d734eee3f80737125961f4af1846a972.png)\n\n \n\nLet's find a type II representation of our region.\n\n**Step 1.** We start by finding the surfaces ${f}_{1}(y,z)$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z).$ Since all $x$ in the region are bounded *below* by $x=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}$ and *above* by $x=\\sqrt{2y},$ we have\n\n$$\n{{\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{1}(y,z)}\\le x\\le {{\\sqrt{2y}}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{2}(y,z)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is false.\n\n**Step 2.** We find the projection of the intersection between the cone $x=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}$ and the paraboloid $x=\\sqrt{2y}$ onto the $yz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the cone is already solved for $x.$ Substituting this expression for $x$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{2y} \\\\ {y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =2y \\\\ {y}^{2}-2y+{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-2y+1+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle $(y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ centered at $(1,0)$ in the $yz$-plane. \n\n![](images/d6/d6db5f2d27787c5414f92c2525398b3810d0b0d192ea6837f7cdf483531f48e9.png)\n\nThe region is bounded *below* by $z=-\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}$ and bounded *above* by $z=\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{-\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{1}(y)}\\le z\\le {{\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{2}(y)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is true.\n\n**Step 3.** We find the bounds for $y.$ From the diagram, $y$ is bounded *below* by $y=0$ and bounded *above* by $y=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{0}_{⏟}}_{a}\\le y\\le {{2}_{⏟}}_{b}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement III is false.\n\nFinally, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "A typical three-dimensional region of type II can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/e4/e4c9cbbad2f13e9cc125ddbb89125ed2d734eee3f80737125961f4af1846a972.png)\n\n \n\nLet's find a type II representation of our region.\n\n**Step 1.** We start by finding the surfaces ${f}_{1}(y,z)$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z).$ Since all $x$ in the region are bounded *below* by $x=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}$ and *above* by $x=\\sqrt{2y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}_{{f}_{1}(y,z)}\\le x\\le \\underbrace{\\sqrt{2y}}_{{f}_{2}(y,z)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is false.\n\n**Step 2.** We find the projection of the intersection between the cone $x=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}$ and the paraboloid $x=\\sqrt{2y}$ onto the $yz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the cone is already solved for $x.$ Substituting this expression for $x$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{2y} \\\\ {y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =2y \\\\ {y}^{2}-2y+{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-2y+1+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle $(y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ centered at $(1,0)$ in the $yz$-plane. \n\n![](images/d6/d6db5f2d27787c5414f92c2525398b3810d0b0d192ea6837f7cdf483531f48e9.png)\n\nThe region is bounded *below* by $z=-\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}$ and bounded *above* by $z=\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{-\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}}_{{g}_{1}(y)}\\le z\\le \\underbrace{\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}}_{{g}_{2}(y)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is true.\n\n**Step 3.** We find the bounds for $y.$ From the diagram, $y$ is bounded *below* by $y=0$ and bounded *above* by $y=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{0}_{a}\\le y\\le \\underbrace{2}_{b}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement III is false.\n\nFinally, the correct answer is \"II only.\""]
 [:db/cas 17592186281492 :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=x+2y+1$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=1,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨1,\\,2,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨-1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨-1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=x+2y+1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,-y,\\,4x+3⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨-1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨x+2y+1,\\,-y,\\,4x+3⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(-x-2y-1+2y+4x+3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(3x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(3x+2)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(3x+2)\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(3x+2)\\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{6}}}(3x+2)\\cdot \\cancel{\\sqrt{6}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3x+2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(3x+2)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}(3x+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[y]_{0}^{1}\\cdot {[\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+2x]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=x+2y+1$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\underbrace{⟨-1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=1,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\underbrace{⟨1,\\,2,\\,-1⟩}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨-1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨-1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=x+2y+1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,-y,\\,4x+3⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨-1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨x+2y+1,\\,-y,\\,4x+3⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(-x-2y-1+2y+4x+3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(3x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(3x+2)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(3x+2)\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(3x+2)\\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{6}}}(3x+2)\\cdot \\cancel{\\sqrt{6}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3x+2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(3x+2)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}(3x+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[y]_{0}^{1}\\cdot {[\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+2x]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186281494 :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=3x-y$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-3,\\,1,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=0,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨3,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=0.\n$$\n\nSince we require a downward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is negative. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨3,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨3,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}⟨3,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=3x-y$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨y,\\,x,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}⟨3,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}⟨y,\\,x,\\,3x-y⟩\\cdot ⟨3,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}(3y-x-(3x-y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}(4y-4x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}(4y-4x)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}(4y-4x)\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}(4y-4x)\\cdot \\sqrt{11}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{11}}}(4y-4x)\\cdot \\cancel{\\sqrt{11}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4y-4x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4y-4x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[4xy-2{x}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(4y-2)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[2{y}^{2}-2y]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=3x-y$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\underbrace{⟨-3,\\,1,\\,1⟩}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=0,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\underbrace{⟨3,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=0.\n$$\n\nSince we require a downward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is negative. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨3,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨3,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}⟨3,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=3x-y$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨y,\\,x,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}⟨3,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}⟨y,\\,x,\\,3x-y⟩\\cdot ⟨3,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}(3y-x-(3x-y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}(4y-4x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}(4y-4x)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}(4y-4x)\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}(4y-4x)\\cdot \\sqrt{11}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{11}}}(4y-4x)\\cdot \\cancel{\\sqrt{11}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4y-4x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4y-4x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[4xy-2{x}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(4y-2)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[2{y}^{2}-2y]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186281496 :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=2x-y+1$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-2,\\,1,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=1,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince we require a downward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is negative. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2x-y+1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨3x,\\,2z,\\,y-2⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨3x,\\,4x-2y+2,\\,y-2⟩\\cdot ⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(6x-(4x-2y+2)-(y-2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(2x+y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(2x+y)\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(2x+y)\\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{6}}}(2x+y)\\cdot \\cancel{\\sqrt{6}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{2}+xy]}_{x=0}^{x=4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4y+16)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[2{y}^{2}+16y]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=2x-y+1$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\underbrace{⟨-2,\\,1,\\,1⟩}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=1,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\underbrace{⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince we require a downward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is negative. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2x-y+1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨3x,\\,2z,\\,y-2⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨3x,\\,4x-2y+2,\\,y-2⟩\\cdot ⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(6x-(4x-2y+2)-(y-2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(2x+y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(2x+y)\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(2x+y)\\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{6}}}(2x+y)\\cdot \\cancel{\\sqrt{6}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{2}+xy]}_{x=0}^{x=4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4y+16)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[2{y}^{2}+16y]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186281594 :question/worked-solution "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =y{e}^{xy}, & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =x{e}^{xy} \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =0, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & ={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}, & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =0 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =(1+xy){e}^{xy}, & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =1 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & ={x}^{2}{e}^{xy}, & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈1+0\\cdot x+0\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(0\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(1)\\cdot xy+0\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1+1xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b=1+1=2.\n$$" "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overbrace{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}^{\\text{Linear approximation}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+\\underbrace{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =y{e}^{xy}, & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =x{e}^{xy} \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =0, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & ={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}, & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =0 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =(1+xy){e}^{xy}, & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =1 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & ={x}^{2}{e}^{xy}, & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈1+0\\cdot x+0\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(0\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(1)\\cdot xy+0\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1+1xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b=1+1=2.\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186281595 :question/worked-solution "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =2{e}^{x-y}, & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =-2{e}^{x-y} \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =2, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =2{e}^{x-y}, & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =2 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =-2{e}^{x-y}, & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =-2 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =2{e}^{x-y}, & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈2+2\\cdot x+(-2)\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(-2)\\cdot xy+2\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2+2x-2y+1{x}^{2}+(-2)xy+{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b=2+1+(-2)=1.\n$$" "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overbrace{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}^{\\text{Linear approximation}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+\\underbrace{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =2{e}^{x-y}, & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =-2{e}^{x-y} \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =2, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =2{e}^{x-y}, & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =2 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =-2{e}^{x-y}, & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =-2 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =2{e}^{x-y}, & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈2+2\\cdot x+(-2)\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(-2)\\cdot xy+2\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2+2x-2y+1{x}^{2}+(-2)xy+{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b=2+1+(-2)=1.\n$$"]
