 [:db/cas 17592186364606 :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ \\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖} & \\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖} & \\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above, where ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is an orthogonal set of vectors. You're given that $‖{v}_{1}‖=6,$ $‖{v}_{2}‖=3,$ $‖{v}_{3}‖=2,$ and that the system of equations below describes the relationship between the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$ \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} &={a}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} &={a}_{2}+{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{3} &={a}_{3}-2{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of ${r}_{22}+{r}_{23}+{r}_{33}?$" "$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ \\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖} & \\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖} & \\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above, where ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is an orthogonal set of vectors. You're given that $‖{v}_{1}‖=6,$ $‖{v}_{2}‖=3,$ $‖{v}_{3}‖=2,$ and that the system of equations below describes the relationship between the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$ \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} &={a}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} &={a}_{2}+{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{3} &={a}_{3}-2{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of ${r}_{22}+{r}_{23}+{r}_{33}?$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186364606 :question/worked-solution "First, we normalize the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{6}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{1}=6{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{3}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{2}=3{q}_{2} \\\\ {q}_{3} & =\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{3}}{2}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{3}=2{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the set ${{q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}}$ will be both orthogonal and normalized.\n\nNext, let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} &={a}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} &={a}_{2}+{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{3} &={a}_{3}-2{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} &={v}_{1}=6{q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} &=-{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}=-6{q}_{1}+3{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3} &=2{v}_{1}-{v}_{2}+{v}_{3}=12{q}_{1}-3{q}_{2}+2{q}_{3}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe final system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -6 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{22}+{r}_{23}+{r}_{33}=3+(-3)+2=2.\n$$" "First, we normalize the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{6}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{1}=6{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{3}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{2}=3{q}_{2} \\\\ {q}_{3} & =\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{3}}{2}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{3}=2{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the set ${{q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}}$ will be both orthogonal and normalized.\n\nNext, let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} &={a}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} &={a}_{2}+{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{3} &={a}_{3}-2{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} &={v}_{1}=6{q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} &=-{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}=-6{q}_{1}+3{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3} &=2{v}_{1}-{v}_{2}+{v}_{3}=12{q}_{1}-3{q}_{2}+2{q}_{3}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe final system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -6 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{22}+{r}_{23}+{r}_{33}=3+(-3)+2=2.\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186364613 :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ of $A$ are orthogonal, and that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained by normalizing the columns of $A,$ what is the value of ${q}_{21}\\cdot {r}_{12}?$" "$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ of $A$ are orthogonal, and that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained by normalizing the columns of $A,$ what is the value of ${q}_{21}\\cdot {r}_{12}?$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186364613 :question/worked-solution "Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct an orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we simply normalize ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{14} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{14} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{21}\\cdot {r}_{12}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}}\\cdot 0=0.$" "Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct an orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we simply normalize ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{14} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{14} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{21}\\cdot {r}_{12}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}}\\cdot 0=0.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186364620 :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of ${q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{22}.$" "$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of ${q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{22}.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186364620 :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}-(-\\frac{6}{18})\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\sqrt{2} & -\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}3\\sqrt{2} & -\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{22}=(\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}})\\cdot (3\\sqrt{2})=4.$" "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}-(-\\frac{6}{18})\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\sqrt{2} & -\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3\\sqrt{2} & -\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{22}=(\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}})\\cdot (3\\sqrt{2})=4.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186364627 :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{22}.$" "$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{22}.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186364627 :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{6}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{6}\\end{bmatrix}}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{22}=(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}})\\cdot (\\sqrt{6})=-2.$" "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{6}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{6}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{22}=(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}})\\cdot (\\sqrt{6})=-2.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186365066 :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖$ is attained when $x=\\hat{x},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $Ax=b.$ In this case, $A\\hat{x}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $‖A\\hat{x}-b‖$ is the distance between the vector $b$ and the column space. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\hat{x}-b & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n‖A\\hat{x}-b‖=\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-2)^{2}}=\\sqrt{21}.\n$$" "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{x} & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{b} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖$ is attained when $x=\\hat{x},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $Ax=b.$ In this case, $A\\hat{x}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $‖A\\hat{x}-b‖$ is the distance between the vector $b$ and the column space. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\hat{x}-b & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n‖A\\hat{x}-b‖=\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-2)^{2}}=\\sqrt{21}.\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186365087 :question/worked-solution "Solving $x-y+2z=0$ for $y,$ we get $y=x+2z.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ x+2z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=x{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}+z{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=2.$ \n\nFinally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Solving $x-y+2z=0$ for $y,$ we get $y=x+2z.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ x+2z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=x\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{1}}+z\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=2.$ \n\nFinally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186365094 :question/worked-solution "Solving $x-3z=0$ for $x,$ we get $x=3z.$ Solving $y+w=0$ for $w,$ we get $w=-y.$  Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Ker}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}3z \\\\ y \\\\ z \\\\ -y\\end{bmatrix}=z{{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}+y{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=2.$ \n\nFinally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T) & =4-\\text{nullity}(T) \\\\  & =4-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Solving $x-3z=0$ for $x,$ we get $x=3z.$ Solving $y+w=0$ for $w,$ we get $w=-y.$  Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Ker}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}3z \\\\ y \\\\ z \\\\ -y\\end{bmatrix}=z\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{1}}+y\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=2.$ \n\nFinally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T) & =4-\\text{nullity}(T) \\\\  & =4-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
