 [:db/cas 17592186378995 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following facts about the \"and\" $(∧)$ and \"or\" $(∨)$ logical connectives:\n\n- The statement $A∧B$ is true if *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\n- The statement $A∨B$ is true if either $A$ is true, or $B$ is true, or *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\nIn our case, $A$ is false, and $B$ is false. Therefore, the result is false:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{4\\text{ is prime}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{\\frac{2}{3}\\text{ is irrational}}_{⏟}}_{B} & \\,\\,≡\\,\\text{false}∨\\text{false}≡\\text{false}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the following facts about the \"and\" $(∧)$ and \"or\" $(∨)$ logical connectives:\n\n- The statement $A∧B$ is true if *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\n- The statement $A∨B$ is true if either $A$ is true, or $B$ is true, or *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\nIn our case, $A$ is false, and $B$ is false. Therefore, the result is false:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{4\\text{ is prime}}_{A}\\,\\underbrace{\\text{or}}_{∨}\\,\\underbrace{\\frac{2}{3}\\text{ is irrational}}_{B} & \\,\\,≡\\,\\text{false}∨\\text{false}≡\\text{false}\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
