 [:db/cas 17592186379084 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following facts about the \"and\" $(∧)$ and \"or\" $(∨)$ logical connectives:\n\n- The statement $A∧B$ is true if *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\n- The statement $A∨B$ is true if either $A$ is true, or $B$ is true, or *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\nIn our case, $P$ is false, $Q$ is true, and $R$ is false. Therefore, $Q∧R$ is false, and the result is false:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P∧(Q∧R) & ≡\\text{false}∧\\overset{\\text{false}}{\\overset{⏞}{(\\text{true}∧\\text{false})}} \\\\  & ≡\\text{false}∧\\text{false} \\\\  & ≡\\text{false}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the following facts about the \"and\" $(∧)$ and \"or\" $(∨)$ logical connectives:\n\n- The statement $A∧B$ is true if *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\n- The statement $A∨B$ is true if either $A$ is true, or $B$ is true, or *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\nIn our case, $P$ is false, $Q$ is true, and $R$ is false. Therefore, $Q∧R$ is false, and the result is false:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P∧(Q∧R) & ≡\\text{false}∧\\overbrace{(\\text{true}∧\\text{false})}^{\\text{false}} \\\\  & ≡\\text{false}∧\\text{false} \\\\  & ≡\\text{false}\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
