 [:db/cas 17592186379386 :question/worked-solution "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+1\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-5)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $C$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot C=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}C=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}C & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}C & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ C & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ C & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+1\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-5)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $C$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot C=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}C=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}C & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}C & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ C & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ C & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186379411 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)+⋯+{c}_{k}{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},…,{c}_{k}\\in R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=1\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-4\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{{x}_{1}(t)-4\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\n- Option II is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=-7\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{-7\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $2$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)+⋯+{c}_{k}{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},…,{c}_{k}\\in R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=1\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-4\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}_{1}(t)-4\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\n- Option II is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=-7\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{-7\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $2$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."]
 [:db/cas 17592186379418 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)+⋯+{c}_{k}{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},…,{c}_{k}\\in R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option I might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that ${x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $2$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $-5\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $-1$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option III is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=-6\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+9\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{-6\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+9\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)+⋯+{c}_{k}{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},…,{c}_{k}\\in R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option I might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that ${x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $2$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $-5\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $-1$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option III is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=-6\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+9\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{-6\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+9\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."]
 [:db/cas 17592186379803 :question/worked-solution "We have been given the following diagonalization of $M.$\n\n$$\nM={{\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[32]{3}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSo, since $M=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${M}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{M}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the inverse of the matrix $P.$ We get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}^{32} & =P{D}^{32}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[32]{3}\\end{bmatrix}}^{32}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{1}^{32} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (\\sqrt[32]{3})^{32}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 9 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-29 & 48 \\\\ -20 & 33\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We have been given the following diagonalization of $M.$\n\n$$\nM=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{P}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[32]{3}\\end{bmatrix}}_{D}\\underbrace{{\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSo, since $M=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${M}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{M}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the inverse of the matrix $P.$ We get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}^{32} & =P{D}^{32}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[32]{3}\\end{bmatrix}}^{32}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{1}^{32} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (\\sqrt[32]{3})^{32}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 9 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-29 & 48 \\\\ -20 & 33\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186380318 :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ of $A$ are orthogonal, and that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained by normalizing the columns of $A,$ what is the value of ${q}_{32}\\cdot {r}_{11}?$" "$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ of $A$ are orthogonal, and that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained by normalizing the columns of $A,$ what is the value of ${q}_{32}\\cdot {r}_{11}?$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186380318 :question/worked-solution "Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct an orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we simply normalize ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{32}\\cdot {r}_{11}=-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 6=-4.$" "Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct an orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we simply normalize ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{32}\\cdot {r}_{11}=-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 6=-4.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186380573 :question/worked-solution "Pre-multiplying a vector $x\\in {R}^{5}$ by $A$ gives a linear combination of the columns of $A:$\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} & {a}_{4} & {a}_{5} \\\\ | & | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{5}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}={x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ {a}_{1} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ {a}_{3} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{4}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ {a}_{4} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{5}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ {a}_{5} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${R}^{5}$ under the action of $A$ must be $\\text{Col}(A),$ the column space of $A.$" "Pre-multiplying a vector $x\\in {R}^{5}$ by $A$ gives a linear combination of the columns of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} & {a}_{4} & {a}_{5} \\\\ | & | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{5}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}={x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ {a}_{1} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ {a}_{3} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{4}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ {a}_{4} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{5}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ {a}_{5} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${R}^{5}$ under the action of $A$ must be $\\text{Col}(A),$ the column space of $A.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186381104 :question/worked-solution "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,{{11{x}_{1}^{2}-24{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}+12{x}_{1}+16{x}_{2}=20\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=\\frac{3}{5}{y}_{1}+\\frac{4}{5}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} &=\\frac{4}{5}{y}_{1}-\\frac{3}{5}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11{x}_{1}^{2}-24{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2}+12{x}_{1}+16{x}_{2} & =20 \\\\ 20{y}_{2}^{2}-5{y}_{1}^{2}+12(\\frac{3}{5}{y}_{1}+\\frac{4}{5}{y}_{2})+16(\\frac{4}{5}{y}_{1}-\\frac{3}{5}{y}_{2}) & =20 \\\\ 20{y}_{2}^{2}-5{y}_{1}^{2}+20{y}_{1} & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{1}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20{y}_{2}^{2}-5{y}_{1}^{2}+20{y}_{1} & =20 \\\\ 4{y}_{2}^{2}-{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{1} & =4 \\\\ 4{y}_{2}^{2}-({y}_{1}^{2}-4{y}_{1}+4-4) & =4 \\\\ 4{y}_{2}^{2}-({y}_{1}-2)^{2}+4 & =4 \\\\ 4{y}_{2}^{2}-({y}_{1}-2)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1} &={y}_{1}-2, \\\\ {z}_{2} &={y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}_{2}^{2}-({y}_{1}-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ 4{z}_{2}^{2}-{z}_{1}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis represents the pair of intersecting lines ${z}_{1}=2{z}_{2}$ and ${z}_{1}=-2{z}_{2}.$" "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,\\underbrace{11{x}_{1}^{2}-24{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2}}_{Q(x)}+12{x}_{1}+16{x}_{2}=20\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=\\frac{3}{5}{y}_{1}+\\frac{4}{5}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} &=\\frac{4}{5}{y}_{1}-\\frac{3}{5}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11{x}_{1}^{2}-24{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2}+12{x}_{1}+16{x}_{2} & =20 \\\\ 20{y}_{2}^{2}-5{y}_{1}^{2}+12(\\frac{3}{5}{y}_{1}+\\frac{4}{5}{y}_{2})+16(\\frac{4}{5}{y}_{1}-\\frac{3}{5}{y}_{2}) & =20 \\\\ 20{y}_{2}^{2}-5{y}_{1}^{2}+20{y}_{1} & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{1}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20{y}_{2}^{2}-5{y}_{1}^{2}+20{y}_{1} & =20 \\\\ 4{y}_{2}^{2}-{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{1} & =4 \\\\ 4{y}_{2}^{2}-({y}_{1}^{2}-4{y}_{1}+4-4) & =4 \\\\ 4{y}_{2}^{2}-({y}_{1}-2)^{2}+4 & =4 \\\\ 4{y}_{2}^{2}-({y}_{1}-2)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1} &={y}_{1}-2, \\\\ {z}_{2} &={y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}_{2}^{2}-({y}_{1}-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ 4{z}_{2}^{2}-{z}_{1}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis represents the pair of intersecting lines ${z}_{1}=2{z}_{2}$ and ${z}_{1}=-2{z}_{2}.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186381368 :question/worked-solution "The word \"or\" is represented by the symbol \"$∨.$\"\n\nSo, the sentence reads\n\n$$\n{{x\\text{ is rational}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{x>10}_{⏟}}_{B},\n$$\n\nthat is, $A∨B.$" "The word \"or\" is represented by the symbol \"$∨.$\"\n\nSo, the sentence reads\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x\\text{ is rational}}_{A}\\,\\underbrace{\\text{or}}_{∨}\\,\\underbrace{x>10}_{B},\n$$\n\nthat is, $A∨B.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186381375 :question/worked-solution "The word \"and\" is represented by the symbol \"$∧,$\" while the word \"or\" is represented by the symbol \"$∨.$\"\n\nNote that this sentence consists of two parts: \n\n- **Part 1:** $x$ is irrational or rational. This corresponds to\n\n$$\n{{x\\text{ is irrational}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{x\\text{ is rational }}_{⏟}}_{B},\n$$\n\nthat is, $A∨B.$\n\n- **Part 2:** $x$ is positive. This corresponds to $C.$\n\nSo, we can express the sentence as\n\n$$\n{{x\\text{ is irrational }\\text{ or }\\text{ rational }}_{⏟}}_{A\\,∨\\,B}\\,{{\\text{and}}_{⏟}}_{∧}\\,{{x\\text{ is positive}}_{⏟}}_{C},\n$$\n\nthat is, $(A∨B)∧C.$" "The word \"and\" is represented by the symbol \"$∧,$\" while the word \"or\" is represented by the symbol \"$∨.$\"\n\nNote that this sentence consists of two parts: \n\n- **Part 1:** $x$ is irrational or rational. This corresponds to\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x\\text{ is irrational}}_{A}\\,\\underbrace{\\text{or}}_{∨}\\,\\underbrace{x\\text{ is rational }}_{B},\n$$\n\nthat is, $A∨B.$\n\n- **Part 2:** $x$ is positive. This corresponds to $C.$\n\nSo, we can express the sentence as\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x\\text{ is irrational }\\text{ or }\\text{ rational }}_{A\\,∨\\,B}\\,\\underbrace{\\text{and}}_{∧}\\,\\underbrace{x\\text{ is positive}}_{C},\n$$\n\nthat is, $(A∨B)∧C.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186381382 :question/worked-solution "The word \"and\" is represented by the symbol \"$∧,$\" while the word \"or\" is represented by the symbol \"$∨.$\"\n\nNote that this sentence consists of two parts:\n\n- **Part 1:** $x$ is positive. This corresponds to $P.$\n\n- **Part 2:** $x$ is irrational or integer. This corresponds to\n\n$$\n{{x\\text{ is irrational}}_{⏟}}_{Q}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{x\\text{ is integer}}_{⏟}}_{R},\n$$\n\nthat is, $Q∨R.$\n\nSo, we can express the sentence as\n\n$$\n{{x\\text{ is positive}}_{⏟}}_{P}\\,{{\\text{and}}_{⏟}}_{∧}\\,{{x\\text{ is irrational}\\text{ or integer}}_{⏟}}_{Q\\,∨\\,R},\n$$\n\nthat is, $P∧(Q∨R).$" "The word \"and\" is represented by the symbol \"$∧,$\" while the word \"or\" is represented by the symbol \"$∨.$\"\n\nNote that this sentence consists of two parts:\n\n- **Part 1:** $x$ is positive. This corresponds to $P.$\n\n- **Part 2:** $x$ is irrational or integer. This corresponds to\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x\\text{ is irrational}}_{Q}\\,\\underbrace{\\text{or}}_{∨}\\,\\underbrace{x\\text{ is integer}}_{R},\n$$\n\nthat is, $Q∨R.$\n\nSo, we can express the sentence as\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x\\text{ is positive}}_{P}\\,\\underbrace{\\text{and}}_{∧}\\,\\underbrace{x\\text{ is irrational}\\text{ or integer}}_{Q\\,∨\\,R},\n$$\n\nthat is, $P∧(Q∨R).$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186381454 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the statement \n\n$$\n∀\\,m\\in N,\\,∃n\\in N,\\,m\\le n\n$$\n\nfrom option III reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ For every natural $m$ there exists natural $n$ such that $m\\le n.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true since $5\\le n$ for some natural number $n$ (e.g., for $n=6$).\n\n- Statement II is true. The predicate\n\n$$\nP(m):\\,∃\\,n\\in N,\\,m\\le n\n$$\n\nis true for any natural $m.$ For example,\n\n$$\nP(1):\\,∃\\,n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\n$$\n\nis true since we can take $n=2.$ Similarly,\n\n$$\nP(2):\\,∃\\,n\\in N,\\,2\\le n\n$$\n\nis true since we can take $n=3.$ And so on. In general, it's sufficient to take $n=m+1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The sentence \n\n$$\n(\\,∀\\,m\\in N,\\,∃n\\in N,\\,m\\le n\\,)\n$$\n\ncan be written as\n\n$$\n∀\\,m\\in N\\,(\\,{{∃n\\in N,\\,m\\le n}_{⏟}}_{P(m)}\\,)\n$$\n\nwhich is true whenever the inner predicate $P(m)$ is true over the universal set $N.$ And, according to statement II, this is the case.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "First, notice that the statement \n\n$$\n∀\\,m\\in N,\\,∃n\\in N,\\,m\\le n\n$$\n\nfrom option III reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ For every natural $m$ there exists natural $n$ such that $m\\le n.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true since $5\\le n$ for some natural number $n$ (e.g., for $n=6$).\n\n- Statement II is true. The predicate\n\n$$\nP(m):\\,∃\\,n\\in N,\\,m\\le n\n$$\n\nis true for any natural $m.$ For example,\n\n$$\nP(1):\\,∃\\,n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\n$$\n\nis true since we can take $n=2.$ Similarly,\n\n$$\nP(2):\\,∃\\,n\\in N,\\,2\\le n\n$$\n\nis true since we can take $n=3.$ And so on. In general, it's sufficient to take $n=m+1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The sentence \n\n$$\n(\\,∀\\,m\\in N,\\,∃n\\in N,\\,m\\le n\\,)\n$$\n\ncan be written as\n\n$$\n∀\\,m\\in N\\,(\\,\\underbrace{∃n\\in N,\\,m\\le n}_{P(m)}\\,)\n$$\n\nwhich is true whenever the inner predicate $P(m)$ is true over the universal set $N.$ And, according to statement II, this is the case.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""]
 [:db/cas 17592186381495 :question/worked-solution "The word \"and\" is represented by the symbol \"$∧.$\"\n\nSo, the sentence reads\n\n$$\n{{x\\text{ is a multiple of }3}_{⏟}}_{P}\\,{{\\text{and}}_{⏟}}_{∧}\\,{{x\\text{ is a multiple of }5}_{⏟}}_{Q},\n$$\n\nthat is, $P∧Q.$" "The word \"and\" is represented by the symbol \"$∧.$\"\n\nSo, the sentence reads\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x\\text{ is a multiple of }3}_{P}\\,\\underbrace{\\text{and}}_{∧}\\,\\underbrace{x\\text{ is a multiple of }5}_{Q},\n$$\n\nthat is, $P∧Q.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186381502 :question/worked-solution "The word \"and\" is represented by the symbol \"$∧$,\" while the word \"or\" is represented by the symbol \"$∨.$\"\n\nNote that this sentence consists of two parts:\n\n- **Part 1:** $x$ is negative **or** rational. This corresponds to\n\n$$\n{{x\\text{ is negative }}_{⏟}}_{N}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{x\\text{ is rational }}_{⏟}}_{R},\n$$\n\nthat is, $N∨R.$\n\n- **Part 2:** $x$ is positive **or** an integer. This corresponds to\n\n$$\n{{x\\text{ is positive }}_{⏟}}_{P}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{x\\text{ is an integer }}_{⏟}}_{I},\n$$\n\nthat is, $P∨I.$\n\nSo, we can express the sentence as\n\n$$\n{{x\\text{ is negative or rational}}_{⏟}}_{N\\,∨\\,R}\\,{{\\text{and}}_{⏟}}_{∧}\\,{{x\\text{ is positive or an integer }}_{⏟}}_{P\\,∨\\,I},\n$$\n\nthat is, $(N∨R)∧(P∨I).$" "The word \"and\" is represented by the symbol \"$∧$,\" while the word \"or\" is represented by the symbol \"$∨.$\"\n\nNote that this sentence consists of two parts:\n\n- **Part 1:** $x$ is negative **or** rational. This corresponds to\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x\\text{ is negative }}_{N}\\,\\underbrace{\\text{or}}_{∨}\\,\\underbrace{x\\text{ is rational }}_{R},\n$$\n\nthat is, $N∨R.$\n\n- **Part 2:** $x$ is positive **or** an integer. This corresponds to\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x\\text{ is positive }}_{P}\\,\\underbrace{\\text{or}}_{∨}\\,\\underbrace{x\\text{ is an integer }}_{I},\n$$\n\nthat is, $P∨I.$\n\nSo, we can express the sentence as\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x\\text{ is negative or rational}}_{N\\,∨\\,R}\\,\\underbrace{\\text{and}}_{∧}\\,\\underbrace{x\\text{ is positive or an integer }}_{P\\,∨\\,I},\n$$\n\nthat is, $(N∨R)∧(P∨I).$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186381791 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the statement \n\n$$\n∀\\,x,y\\in R,\\,\\sqrt{xy}\\in R\n$$\n\nfrom option III reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ For all reals $x$ and $y,$ we have $\\sqrt{xy}\\in R.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false since $\\sqrt{2y}\\in R$ is not true for some real $y.$ Say, for $y=-1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For the predicate\n\n$$\nP(x):\\,∀\\,y\\in R,\\,\\sqrt{xy}\\in R\n$$\n\nwe note that $P(x)$ is false for all values $x\\in R.$ For example, \n\n$$\nP(1):\\,∀\\,y\\in R,\\,\\sqrt{y}\\in R\n$$\n\nis false, since we can take $y=-1.$ Similarly,\n\n$$\nP(2):\\,∀\\,y\\in R,\\,\\sqrt{2y}\\in R\n$$\n\nis false, since we can take $y=-2.$ In general, it's sufficient to take $y=-x$ for any value of $x.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The sentence \n\n$$\n(\\,∀\\,x,y\\in R,\\,\\sqrt{xy}\\in R\\,)\n$$\n\n \ncan be written as\n\n$$\n∀x\\in R\\,(\\,{{∀y\\in R,\\,\\sqrt{xy}\\in R}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\\,)\n$$\n\nwhich is true whenever the inner predicate $P(x)$ is true over the universal set $R.$ But, according to statement II, this is not the case.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" "First, notice that the statement \n\n$$\n∀\\,x,y\\in R,\\,\\sqrt{xy}\\in R\n$$\n\nfrom option III reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ For all reals $x$ and $y,$ we have $\\sqrt{xy}\\in R.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false since $\\sqrt{2y}\\in R$ is not true for some real $y.$ Say, for $y=-1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For the predicate\n\n$$\nP(x):\\,∀\\,y\\in R,\\,\\sqrt{xy}\\in R\n$$\n\nwe note that $P(x)$ is false for all values $x\\in R.$ For example, \n\n$$\nP(1):\\,∀\\,y\\in R,\\,\\sqrt{y}\\in R\n$$\n\nis false, since we can take $y=-1.$ Similarly,\n\n$$\nP(2):\\,∀\\,y\\in R,\\,\\sqrt{2y}\\in R\n$$\n\nis false, since we can take $y=-2.$ In general, it's sufficient to take $y=-x$ for any value of $x.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The sentence \n\n$$\n(\\,∀\\,x,y\\in R,\\,\\sqrt{xy}\\in R\\,)\n$$\n\n \ncan be written as\n\n$$\n∀x\\in R\\,(\\,\\underbrace{∀y\\in R,\\,\\sqrt{xy}\\in R}_{P(x)}\\,)\n$$\n\nwhich is true whenever the inner predicate $P(x)$ is true over the universal set $R.$ But, according to statement II, this is not the case.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""]
 [:db/cas 17592186381805 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the statement \n\n$$\n∃\\,y\\in Z,\\,∀x\\in Z,\\,x+y=0\n$$\n\nfrom option III reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ There exists a integer $y$ such that, for every integer $x,$ we have $x+y=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false since $x+1=0$ is not true for some integer $x.$ Say, for $x=1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The predicate\n\n$$\nP(y):\\,∀x\\in Z,\\,x+y=0\n$$\n\nis false for every value of $y.$ For example,\n\n$$\nP(0):∀\\,x\\in Z,\\,x=0\n$$\n\nis false for any $x\\ne 0.$ In general, it's sufficient to take $x\\ne -y$ for any $y\\in Z.$ Therefore, its truth set is empty.\n\n- Statement III is false. The sentence \n\n$$\n(\\,∃\\,y\\in Z,\\,∀x\\in Z,\\,x+y=0\\,)\n$$\n\ncan be written as\n\n$$\n∃\\,y\\in Z\\,(\\,{{∀x\\in Z,\\,x+y=0}_{⏟}}_{P(y)}\\,)\n$$\n\nwhich is true whenever the inner predicate $P(y)$ has a nonempty truth set. And according to statement II, this is not the case.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "First, notice that the statement \n\n$$\n∃\\,y\\in Z,\\,∀x\\in Z,\\,x+y=0\n$$\n\nfrom option III reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ There exists a integer $y$ such that, for every integer $x,$ we have $x+y=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false since $x+1=0$ is not true for some integer $x.$ Say, for $x=1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The predicate\n\n$$\nP(y):\\,∀x\\in Z,\\,x+y=0\n$$\n\nis false for every value of $y.$ For example,\n\n$$\nP(0):∀\\,x\\in Z,\\,x=0\n$$\n\nis false for any $x\\ne 0.$ In general, it's sufficient to take $x\\ne -y$ for any $y\\in Z.$ Therefore, its truth set is empty.\n\n- Statement III is false. The sentence \n\n$$\n(\\,∃\\,y\\in Z,\\,∀x\\in Z,\\,x+y=0\\,)\n$$\n\ncan be written as\n\n$$\n∃\\,y\\in Z\\,(\\,\\underbrace{∀x\\in Z,\\,x+y=0}_{P(y)}\\,)\n$$\n\nwhich is true whenever the inner predicate $P(y)$ has a nonempty truth set. And according to statement II, this is not the case.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""]
 [:db/cas 17592186381812 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the statement \n\n$$\n∃\\,x\\in R,\\,∀y\\in R,\\,xy=0\n$$\n\nfrom option III reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ There exists a real $x$ such that, for every real $y,$ we have $xy=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false since $2y=0$ is not true for some real $y.$ Say, for $y=1.$\n\n- Statement II is false.  The predicate\n\n$$\nP(x):\\,∀\\,y\\in R,\\,xy=0\n$$\n\nis true for some $x\\in R.$ For example, \n\n$$\nP(0):\\,∀\\,y\\in R,\\,0\\cdot y=0\n$$\n\nis true. Therefore, its truth set is not empty.\n\n- Statement III is true. The sentence \n\n$$\n(\\,∃\\,x\\in R,\\,∀y\\in R,\\,xy=0\\,)\n$$\n\ncan be written as\n\n$$\n∃\\,x\\in R\\,(\\,{{∀y\\in R,\\,xy=0}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\\,)\n$$\n\nwhich is true whenever the inner predicate $P(x)$ has a nonempty truth set. And according to statement II, this is the case.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "First, notice that the statement \n\n$$\n∃\\,x\\in R,\\,∀y\\in R,\\,xy=0\n$$\n\nfrom option III reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ There exists a real $x$ such that, for every real $y,$ we have $xy=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false since $2y=0$ is not true for some real $y.$ Say, for $y=1.$\n\n- Statement II is false.  The predicate\n\n$$\nP(x):\\,∀\\,y\\in R,\\,xy=0\n$$\n\nis true for some $x\\in R.$ For example, \n\n$$\nP(0):\\,∀\\,y\\in R,\\,0\\cdot y=0\n$$\n\nis true. Therefore, its truth set is not empty.\n\n- Statement III is true. The sentence \n\n$$\n(\\,∃\\,x\\in R,\\,∀y\\in R,\\,xy=0\\,)\n$$\n\ncan be written as\n\n$$\n∃\\,x\\in R\\,(\\,\\underbrace{∀y\\in R,\\,xy=0}_{P(x)}\\,)\n$$\n\nwhich is true whenever the inner predicate $P(x)$ has a nonempty truth set. And according to statement II, this is the case.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""]
 [:db/cas 17592186381826 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our expression and mark the occurrences of the variables as bound $(\\text{b})$ or free $(\\text{f}).$\n\n- Occurrence I of the variable $x$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $x.$\n\n- Occurrence II of the variable $z$ is *bound* since it is placed right next to the quantifier $∀:$\n\n$$\n(\\,∃y\\in (0,1),\\,xy=1\\,)⇒(\\,∀{z}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in (1,2),\\,xz>3\\,)\n$$\n\n- Occurrence III of the variable $z$ is *bound* since it lies within the scope of the quantifier $∀$ that uses $z:$\n\n$$\n(\\,∃y\\in (0,1),\\,xy=1\\,)⇒(\\,∀z\\in (1,2),\\,\\overset{\\text{scope of }∀z}{\\overset{⏞}{x{z}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}>3}})\n$$\n\n \n\nThe occurrences of variables in the expression are as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\,∃{y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in (0,1),\\,{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}{y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}=1\\,)⇒(\\,∀{z}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in (1,2),\\,{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}{z}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}>3\\,)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the free occurrence is \"I only.\"" "Let's examine our expression and mark the occurrences of the variables as bound $(\\text{b})$ or free $(\\text{f}).$\n\n- Occurrence I of the variable $x$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $x.$\n\n- Occurrence II of the variable $z$ is *bound* since it is placed right next to the quantifier $∀:$\n\n$$\n(\\,∃y\\in (0,1),\\,xy=1\\,)⇒(\\,∀{z}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in (1,2),\\,xz>3\\,)\n$$\n\n- Occurrence III of the variable $z$ is *bound* since it lies within the scope of the quantifier $∀$ that uses $z:$\n\n$$\n(\\,∃y\\in (0,1),\\,xy=1\\,)⇒(\\,∀z\\in (1,2),\\,\\overbrace{x{z}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}>3}^{\\text{scope of }∀z})\n$$\n\n \n\nThe occurrences of variables in the expression are as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\,∃{y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in (0,1),\\,{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}{y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}=1\\,)⇒(\\,∀{z}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in (1,2),\\,{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}{z}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}>3\\,)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the free occurrence is \"I only.\""]
 [:db/cas 17592186381874 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our expression and mark the occurrences of the variables as bound $(\\text{b})$ or free $(\\text{f}).$\n\n- Occurrence I of the variable $n$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $n.$\n\n- Occurrence II of the variable $z$ is *bound* since it lies within the scope of the quantifier $∃z$ that uses $z:$ \n\n$$\n(\\,∀x\\in N,\\,x>n+1\\,)⇒(\\,∃z\\in R,\\,\\overset{\\text{scope of }∃z}{\\overset{⏞}{{z}^{3}-1={y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}}}\\,)\n$$\n\n- Occurrence III of the variable $y$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $y.$\n\nThe occurrences of variables in the expression are as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\,∀{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in N,\\,{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}>{n}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}+1\\,)⇒(\\,∃{z}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in R,\\,{{z}^{3}}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}-1={y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}\\,)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the free occurrence is \"I and III only.\"" "Let's examine our expression and mark the occurrences of the variables as bound $(\\text{b})$ or free $(\\text{f}).$\n\n- Occurrence I of the variable $n$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $n.$\n\n- Occurrence II of the variable $z$ is *bound* since it lies within the scope of the quantifier $∃z$ that uses $z:$ \n\n$$\n(\\,∀x\\in N,\\,x>n+1\\,)⇒(\\,∃z\\in R,\\,\\overbrace{{z}^{3}-1={y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}}^{\\text{scope of }∃z}\\,)\n$$\n\n- Occurrence III of the variable $y$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $y.$\n\nThe occurrences of variables in the expression are as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\,∀{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in N,\\,{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}>{n}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}+1\\,)⇒(\\,∃{z}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in R,\\,{{z}^{3}}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}-1={y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}\\,)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the free occurrence is \"I and III only.\""]
 [:db/cas 17592186381942 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{10}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{2}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{3}{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables.\n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we only need to normalize them. This gives the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{10}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{2}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{3}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables.\n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we only need to normalize them. This gives the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186381985 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=5$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{1}+\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,4{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{2}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{3}{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables.\n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we only need to normalize them. This gives the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} & -\\frac{3}{5} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=5$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{1}+\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,4{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{2}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{3}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables.\n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we only need to normalize them. This gives the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} & -\\frac{3}{5} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186382559 :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}36 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 36 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 36\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -36 \\\\ 36\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{36}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -36 \\\\ 36\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}^{x}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}}^{b}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}36 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 36 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 36\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -36 \\\\ 36\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{36}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -36 \\\\ 36\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186382566 :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\,\\,\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖$ is attained when $x=\\hat{x},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $Ax=b.$ In this case, $A\\hat{x}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $‖A\\hat{x}-b‖$ is the distance between the vector $b$ and the column space. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\hat{x}-b & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n‖A\\hat{x}-b‖=\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-8)^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-2)^{2}}=2\\sqrt{26}.\n$$" "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{x} & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{b} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\,\\,\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖$ is attained when $x=\\hat{x},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $Ax=b.$ In this case, $A\\hat{x}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $‖A\\hat{x}-b‖$ is the distance between the vector $b$ and the column space. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\hat{x}-b & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n‖A\\hat{x}-b‖=\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-8)^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-2)^{2}}=2\\sqrt{26}.\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186382573 :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\,\\,\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 10\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{36}\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖$ is attained when $x=\\hat{x},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $Ax=b.$ In this case, $A\\hat{x}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $‖A\\hat{x}-b‖$ is the distance between the vector $b$ and the column space. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\hat{x}-b & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n‖A\\hat{x}-b‖=\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-5)^{2}+(-9)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-8)^{2}}=3\\sqrt{19}.\n$$" "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{x} & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}^{b} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\,\\,\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 10\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{36}\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖$ is attained when $x=\\hat{x},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $Ax=b.$ In this case, $A\\hat{x}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $‖A\\hat{x}-b‖$ is the distance between the vector $b$ and the column space. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\hat{x}-b & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n‖A\\hat{x}-b‖=\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-5)^{2}+(-9)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-8)^{2}}=3\\sqrt{19}.\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186383568 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot {{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{⏟}}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{u-v} & -\\frac{1}{u-v} \\\\ -2u & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u-v}\\cdot 1-(-2u)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{u-v}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2u}{u-v} \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(2,1)} & ={(\\frac{1-2u}{u-v})\\,|}_{u=2\\,v=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2(2)}{2-1} \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|-3|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =3\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot \\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{u-v} & -\\frac{1}{u-v} \\\\ -2u & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u-v}\\cdot 1-(-2u)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{u-v}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2u}{u-v} \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(2,1)} & ={(\\frac{1-2u}{u-v})\\,|}_{u=2\\,v=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2(2)}{2-1} \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|-3|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =3\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
