 [:db/cas 17592186389825 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following facts about the \"and\" $(∧)$ and \"or\" $(∨)$ logical connectives:\n\n- The statement $A∧B$ is true if *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\n- The statement $A∨B$ is true if either $A$ is true, or $B$ is true, or *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\nIn our case, $P$ is true, $Q$ is false, and $R$ is false. Therefore, $Q∨R$ is false, and the result is false:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P∧(Q∨R) & ≡\\text{true}∧\\overset{\\text{false}}{\\overset{⏞}{(\\text{false}∨\\text{false})}} \\\\  & ≡\\text{true}∧\\text{false} \\\\  & ≡\\text{false}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the following facts about the \"and\" $(∧)$ and \"or\" $(∨)$ logical connectives:\n\n- The statement $A∧B$ is true if *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\n- The statement $A∨B$ is true if either $A$ is true, or $B$ is true, or *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\nIn our case, $P$ is true, $Q$ is false, and $R$ is false. Therefore, $Q∨R$ is false, and the result is false:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P∧(Q∨R) & ≡\\text{true}∧\\overbrace{(\\text{false}∨\\text{false})}^{\\text{false}} \\\\  & ≡\\text{true}∧\\text{false} \\\\  & ≡\\text{false}\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186389841 :question/worked-solution "The word \"and\" is represented by the symbol \"$∧$\", while the word \"or\" is represented by the symbol \"$∨$\".\n\nNote that this sentence consists of two parts:\n\n- **Part 1:** $5$ is less than $2$ or odd. This corresponds to\n\n$$\n{{5\\text{ is less than }2}_{⏟}}_{A}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{5\\text{ is odd}}_{⏟}}_{B},\n$$\n\nthat is, $A∨B.$\n\n- **Part 2:** $9$ is composite. This corresponds to $C.$\n\nSo, we can express the sentence as\n\n$$\n{{5\\text{ is less than }2\\text{ or }\\text{odd}}_{⏟}}_{A\\,∨\\,B},\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{9\\text{ is composite}}_{⏟}}_{C}\n$$\n\nthat is, $(A∨B)∨C.$" "The word \"and\" is represented by the symbol \"$∧$\", while the word \"or\" is represented by the symbol \"$∨$\".\n\nNote that this sentence consists of two parts:\n\n- **Part 1:** $5$ is less than $2$ or odd. This corresponds to\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{5\\text{ is less than }2}_{A}\\,\\underbrace{\\text{or}}_{∨}\\,\\underbrace{5\\text{ is odd}}_{B},\n$$\n\nthat is, $A∨B.$\n\n- **Part 2:** $9$ is composite. This corresponds to $C.$\n\nSo, we can express the sentence as\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{5\\text{ is less than }2\\text{ or }\\text{odd}}_{A\\,∨\\,B},\\,\\underbrace{\\text{or}}_{∨}\\,\\underbrace{9\\text{ is composite}}_{C}\n$$\n\nthat is, $(A∨B)∨C.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186389848 :question/worked-solution "The word \"and\" is represented by the symbol \"$∧$\", while the word \"or\" is represented by the symbol \"$∨$\".\n\nNote that this sentence consists of two parts:\n\n- **Part 1:** $\\pi $ is rational. This corresponds to $M.$\n\n- **Part 2:** $2$ is less than $4$ and $2\\in Z.$ This corresponds to\n\n$$\n{{2\\text{ is less than }4}_{⏟}}_{N}\\,{{\\text{and}}_{⏟}}_{∧}\\,{{2\\in Z}_{⏟}}_{P},\n$$\n\nthat is, $N∧P.$\n\nSo, we can express the sentence as\n\n$$\n{{\\pi \\text{ is rational}}_{⏟}}_{M}\\,{{\\text{and}}_{⏟}}_{∧}\\,{{2\\text{ is less than }4\\text{ and }2\\in Z}_{⏟}}_{N\\,∧\\,P},\n$$\n\nthat is, $M∧(N∧P).$" "The word \"and\" is represented by the symbol \"$∧$\", while the word \"or\" is represented by the symbol \"$∨$\".\n\nNote that this sentence consists of two parts:\n\n- **Part 1:** $\\pi $ is rational. This corresponds to $M.$\n\n- **Part 2:** $2$ is less than $4$ and $2\\in Z.$ This corresponds to\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{2\\text{ is less than }4}_{N}\\,\\underbrace{\\text{and}}_{∧}\\,\\underbrace{2\\in Z}_{P},\n$$\n\nthat is, $N∧P.$\n\nSo, we can express the sentence as\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\pi \\text{ is rational}}_{M}\\,\\underbrace{\\text{and}}_{∧}\\,\\underbrace{2\\text{ is less than }4\\text{ and }2\\in Z}_{N\\,∧\\,P},\n$$\n\nthat is, $M∧(N∧P).$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186390205 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our expression and mark the occurrences of the variables as bound $(\\text{b})$ or free $(\\text{f}).$\n\n- Occurrence I of the variable $x$ is *bound* since it's placed right next to the quantifier $∃:$\n\n$$\n∃\\,{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in R,\\,2-x=w\n$$\n\n- Occurrence II of the variable $x$ is *bound* since it lies within the scope of the quantifier $∃x$ that uses $x:$\n\n$$\n∃\\,x\\in R,\\,\\overset{\\text{scope of }∃x}{\\overset{⏞}{2-{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}=w}}\n$$\n\n- Occurrence III of the variable $w$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $w.$\n\nThe occurrences of variables in the expression are as follows:\n\n$$\n∃{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in R,\\,2-{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}={w}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the free occurrence is \"III only.\"" "Let's examine our expression and mark the occurrences of the variables as bound $(\\text{b})$ or free $(\\text{f}).$\n\n- Occurrence I of the variable $x$ is *bound* since it's placed right next to the quantifier $∃:$\n\n$$\n∃\\,{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in R,\\,2-x=w\n$$\n\n- Occurrence II of the variable $x$ is *bound* since it lies within the scope of the quantifier $∃x$ that uses $x:$\n\n$$\n∃\\,x\\in R,\\,\\overbrace{2-{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}=w}^{\\text{scope of }∃x}\n$$\n\n- Occurrence III of the variable $w$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $w.$\n\nThe occurrences of variables in the expression are as follows:\n\n$$\n∃{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in R,\\,2-{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}={w}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the free occurrence is \"III only.\""]
