ix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7t)\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7(t-s)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7(t-s))\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7(t-s)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7(t-s))\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7(t-s)) \\\\ 7\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7(t-s))\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}7\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7(t-s)) \\\\ 7\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7(t-s))\\end{bmatrix}\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7(t-s)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7(t-s))\\end{bmatrix}}_{s=0}^{s=t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7t)-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7t)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7t)-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7t)-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7t)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186396792 :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x),g(x),h(x)$ and $k(x)$ are functions and $c$ is a nonzero constant. Then, we have the following properties of Big-O notation. \n\n- The sum and product properties. If\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(k)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf+g=O(h+k)\\,\\text{and}\\,fg=O(hk)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The constant multiple property. If\n\n$$\nf=O(cg)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The reflexive property.\n\n$$\nf=O(f)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The transitive property. If\n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The absorption property. If\n\n$$\nf=O(g+h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let us justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L1},$ which states that\n\n$$\n3x=O({{3x}_{⏟}}_{f}).\n$$\n\nThis is true by $\\text{the reflexive property}.$\n\n- Next, we consider line $\\text{L4}.$ From line $\\text{L1}$ we have\n\n$$\n3x=O(3x),\n$$\n\nand from line $\\text{L3}$ we have\n\n$$\n-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=O(2x)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThus, by lines $\\text{L1 and L3}$ and $\\text{the sum property},$ we obtain\n\n$$\nf=O(5x)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$\n\n- Finally, we consider line $\\text{L5}.$ From line  $\\text{L4}$   we have\n\n$$\nf=O(5x)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0,\n$$\n\nand by the $\\text{constant multiple property}$ with $c=5,$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O(x)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$" "Suppose $f(x),g(x),h(x)$ and $k(x)$ are functions and $c$ is a nonzero constant. Then, we have the following properties of Big-O notation. \n\n- The sum and product properties. If\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(k)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf+g=O(h+k)\\,\\text{and}\\,fg=O(hk)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The constant multiple property. If\n\n$$\nf=O(cg)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The reflexive property.\n\n$$\nf=O(f)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The transitive property. If\n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The absorption property. If\n\n$$\nf=O(g+h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let us justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L1},$ which states that\n\n$$\n3x=O(\\underbrace{3x}_{f}).\n$$\n\nThis is true by $\\text{the reflexive property}.$\n\n- Next, we consider line $\\text{L4}.$ From line $\\text{L1}$ we have\n\n$$\n3x=O(3x),\n$$\n\nand from line $\\text{L3}$ we have\n\n$$\n-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=O(2x)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThus, by lines $\\text{L1 and L3}$ and $\\text{the sum property},$ we obtain\n\n$$\nf=O(5x)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$\n\n- Finally, we consider line $\\text{L5}.$ From line  $\\text{L4}$   we have\n\n$$\nf=O(5x)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0,\n$$\n\nand by the $\\text{constant multiple property}$ with $c=5,$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O(x)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186396818 :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x),g(x),h(x)$ and $k(x)$ are functions and $c$ is a nonzero constant. Then, we have the following properties of Big-O notation. \n\n- The sum and product properties. If\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(k)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n \nthen\n\n$$\nf+g=O(h+k)\\,\\text{and}\\,fg=O(hk)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The constant multiple property. If \n\n$$\nf=O(cg)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n \nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The reflexive property. \n\n$$\nf=O(f)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n- The transitive property. If \n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The absorption property. If\n\n$$\nf=O(g+h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n \nthen \n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L1},$ which states that\n\n$$\nf=O({{2+{e}^{-x}}_{⏟}}_{f}),\n$$\n\nand this is true by the $\\text{reflexive property}.$\n\n- Next, we consider line $\\text{L4}.$ From line $\\text{L1}$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O(2+{e}^{-x}),\n$$\n\nand from line $\\text{L3}$ we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-x}=O(2).\n$$\n\nThus, by lines   $\\text{L1 and L3}$ and the  $\\text{absorption property},$ we obtain\n\n$$\nf=O(2).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we consider line $\\text{L5}.$ From line $\\text{L4},$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O(2)\n$$\n\nand by the $\\text{constant multiple property}$ with $c=2,$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O(1).\n$$" "Suppose $f(x),g(x),h(x)$ and $k(x)$ are functions and $c$ is a nonzero constant. Then, we have the following properties of Big-O notation. \n\n- The sum and product properties. If\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(k)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n \nthen\n\n$$\nf+g=O(h+k)\\,\\text{and}\\,fg=O(hk)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The constant multiple property. If \n\n$$\nf=O(cg)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n \nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The reflexive property. \n\n$$\nf=O(f)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n- The transitive property. If \n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The absorption property. If\n\n$$\nf=O(g+h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n \nthen \n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L1},$ which states that\n\n$$\nf=O(\\underbrace{2+{e}^{-x}}_{f}),\n$$\n\nand this is true by the $\\text{reflexive property}.$\n\n- Next, we consider line $\\text{L4}.$ From line $\\text{L1}$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O(2+{e}^{-x}),\n$$\n\nand from line $\\text{L3}$ we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-x}=O(2).\n$$\n\nThus, by lines   $\\text{L1 and L3}$ and the  $\\text{absorption property},$ we obtain\n\n$$\nf=O(2).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we consider line $\\text{L5}.$ From line $\\text{L4},$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O(2)\n$$\n\nand by the $\\text{constant multiple property}$ with $c=2,$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O(1).\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186397950 :question/worked-solution "Pre-multiplying a vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ by $A+B$ gives a linear combination of the columns of $A+B:$\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & ⋯ & | \\\\ (a+b)_{1} & (a+b)_{2} & ⋯ & (a+b)_{n} \\\\ | & | & ⋯ & |\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A+B}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}={x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ (a+b)_{1} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ (a+b)_{2} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}+⋯+{x}_{n}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ (a+b)_{n} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${R}^{n}$ under the action of $A+B$ must be $\\text{Col}(A+B),$ the column space of $A+B.$\n\nFurthermore, since $\\text{Null}(A+B)$ is mapped to the zero vector $0$ by the definition of the null subspace, the image of its orthogonal complement $\\text{Col}((A+B)^{T})=\\text{Col}({A}^{T}+{B}^{T})$ must be $\\text{Col}(A+B).$\n\nThis can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/02/02a739d8888f2b46b81075218693e6b2cc87e3c5b81b522abfc9246dcd7c870d.png)" "Pre-multiplying a vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ by $A+B$ gives a linear combination of the columns of $A+B:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & ⋯ & | \\\\ (a+b)_{1} & (a+b)_{2} & ⋯ & (a+b)_{n} \\\\ | & | & ⋯ & |\\end{bmatrix}}_{A+B}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}={x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ (a+b)_{1} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ (a+b)_{2} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}+⋯+{x}_{n}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ (a+b)_{n} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${R}^{n}$ under the action of $A+B$ must be $\\text{Col}(A+B),$ the column space of $A+B.$\n\nFurthermore, since $\\text{Null}(A+B)$ is mapped to the zero vector $0$ by the definition of the null subspace, the image of its orthogonal complement $\\text{Col}((A+B)^{T})=\\text{Col}({A}^{T}+{B}^{T})$ must be $\\text{Col}(A+B).$\n\nThis can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/02/02a739d8888f2b46b81075218693e6b2cc87e3c5b81b522abfc9246dcd7c870d.png)"]
 [:db/cas 17592186399037 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & \\,\\,\\,-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-\\frac{1}{2},$ ${u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & \\,\\,\\,-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-\\frac{1}{2},$ ${u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186399552 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 9 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 9 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}}_{U}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186399559 :question/worked-solution "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $H$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot H=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}H=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}H & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}H & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ H & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ H & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $H$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot H=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}H=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}H & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}H & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ H & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ H & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186399566 :question/worked-solution "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+1\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-4)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $H$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot H=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}H=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}H & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}H & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ H & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ H & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+1\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-4)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $H$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot H=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}H=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}H & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}H & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ H & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ H & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186399573 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186399594 :question/problem "$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -10 & 7 & 9 \\\\ 40 & -13 & 8\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 0 \\\\ \\boxed{} & \\boxed{} & \\boxed{} \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}.\n$$\n\nConsider the LU factorization of the matrix $A$ above. From left to right, what are the missing elements from the second row of the matrix $U?$" "$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -10 & 7 & 9 \\\\ 40 & -13 & 8\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 0 \\\\ \\boxed{} & \\boxed{} & \\boxed{} \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{U}.\n$$\n\nConsider the LU factorization of the matrix $A$ above. From left to right, what are the missing elements from the second row of the matrix $U?$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186399713 :question/worked-solution "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${a}_{3}$ is a linear combination of vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{3}=1\\cdot {a}_{1}+1\\cdot {a}_{2}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${a}_{3}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is spanned by just ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB={{{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}},{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}}.\n$$" "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${a}_{3}$ is a linear combination of vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{3}=1\\cdot {a}_{1}+1\\cdot {a}_{2}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${a}_{3}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is spanned by just ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB={\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{1}},\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{2}}}.\n$$"]
