 [:db/cas 17592186419394 :question/worked-solution "By denoting $P(a)$ and $Q(a)$ as\n\n- $P(a):\\,15∣6a$\n\n- $Q(a):\\,15∣a$\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∀a\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q.\n$$\n\nWe want to disprove this statement. In other words, we must show that its negation is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∀a\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q) & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,¬(P⇒Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,¬(¬P∨Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,P∧¬Q \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,(15∣6a)\\,∧\\,(15∤a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we begin as follows:\n\nTo disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. \nIn other words, we need to show that there exists an integer $a$ such that \n\n$$\n15∣6a\\,\\text{and}\\,15∤a.\n$$\n\nFinding a counterexample is sometimes a nontrivial task, but we only need one. In this case, substituting $a=5$ suffices. \n\nLet's choose $a=5.$\nThen, we have that \n\n$$\n15∣{{30}_{⏟}}_{6a}\\,\\text{and}\\,15∤{{5}_{⏟}}_{a}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we state our conclusion:\n\nSo, $a=5$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false." "By denoting $P(a)$ and $Q(a)$ as\n\n- $P(a):\\,15∣6a$\n\n- $Q(a):\\,15∣a$\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∀a\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q.\n$$\n\nWe want to disprove this statement. In other words, we must show that its negation is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∀a\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q) & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,¬(P⇒Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,¬(¬P∨Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,P∧¬Q \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,(15∣6a)\\,∧\\,(15∤a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we begin as follows:\n\nTo disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. \nIn other words, we need to show that there exists an integer $a$ such that \n\n$$\n15∣6a\\,\\text{and}\\,15∤a.\n$$\n\nFinding a counterexample is sometimes a nontrivial task, but we only need one. In this case, substituting $a=5$ suffices. \n\nLet's choose $a=5.$\nThen, we have that \n\n$$\n15∣\\underbrace{30}_{6a}\\,\\text{and}\\,15∤\\underbrace{5}_{a}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we state our conclusion:\n\nSo, $a=5$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false."]
 [:db/cas 17592186420695 :question/worked-solution "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{{(\\,m>0\\,∧n>0\\,)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{sufficient}}\\,⇒\\,{{(\\,m\\cdot n>0\\,)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{necessary}}\n$$\n\nis a true statement. Therefore:\n\n- Statement II is true. $m>0\\,∧n>0$ is a **sufficient** condition for $m\\cdot n>0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. $m\\cdot n>0$ is a **necessary** condition for $m>0\\,∧n>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(\\,m>0\\,∧n>0\\,)}_{\\text{sufficient}}\\,⇒\\,\\underbrace{(\\,m\\cdot n>0\\,)}_{\\text{necessary}}\n$$\n\nis a true statement. Therefore:\n\n- Statement II is true. $m>0\\,∧n>0$ is a **sufficient** condition for $m\\cdot n>0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. $m\\cdot n>0$ is a **necessary** condition for $m>0\\,∧n>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""]
