d columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=2$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{2}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{3}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables.\n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we only need to normalize them. This gives the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186424110 :question/worked-solution "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(A),$ we need to check if the equation $Ax=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[A\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,b] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 12 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-6){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -12 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3} &=-2 \\\\ -{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3} &=2 \\\\ -12{x}_{3} &=-6.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Substituting it into the second equation gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2}+4(\\frac{1}{2}) & =2 \\\\ -{x}_{2}+2 & =2 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, from the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3(0)-8(\\frac{1}{2}) & =-2 \\\\ {x}_{1}-4 & =-2 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we have a solution, the equation $Ax=b$ is consistent, which implies that $b\\in \\text{Col}(A).$ \n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${a}_{1},$ ${a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}=2\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+0\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=2\\cdot 0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=0.$" "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(A),$ we need to check if the equation $Ax=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[A\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,b] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 12 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-6){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -12 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3} &=-2 \\\\ -{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3} &=2 \\\\ -12{x}_{3} &=-6.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Substituting it into the second equation gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2}+4(\\frac{1}{2}) & =2 \\\\ -{x}_{2}+2 & =2 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, from the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3(0)-8(\\frac{1}{2}) & =-2 \\\\ {x}_{1}-4 & =-2 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we have a solution, the equation $Ax=b$ is consistent, which implies that $b\\in \\text{Col}(A).$ \n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${a}_{1},$ ${a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}=2\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{1}}+0\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{2}}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{3}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=2\\cdot 0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=0.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186424810 :question/worked-solution "Here, ${53}^{4}$ is in exponent form. The base is $53$ and the exponent is $4.$ \n\nThis means that $53$ is multiplied by itself $4$ times:\n\n$$\n{{53\\times 53\\times 53\\times 53}_{⏟}}_{4\\text{ times}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{53}^{4}=53\\times 53\\times 53\\times 53.\n$$" "Here, ${53}^{4}$ is in exponent form. The base is $53$ and the exponent is $4.$ \n\nThis means that $53$ is multiplied by itself $4$ times:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{53\\times 53\\times 53\\times 53}_{4\\text{ times}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{53}^{4}=53\\times 53\\times 53\\times 53.\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186424811 :question/worked-solution "First, by writing the expression with multiplication signs, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(7.2)}^{2} & ={{7.2\\times 7.2}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ times}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe proceed by multiplying the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{1} \\\\ \\boxed{}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned} & \\,\\, & \\begin{aligned}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now count the number of decimal places in the $2$ factors.\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in $7.2.$\nTherefore, the exponent $(7.2)^{2}$ will have $1\\times 2=2$ decimal places.\n\nSo, we take our value of $5184$ and add a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n51\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{84}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $(7.2)^{2}=51.84.$" "First, by writing the expression with multiplication signs, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(7.2)}^{2} & =\\underbrace{7.2\\times 7.2}_{2\\text{ times}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe proceed by multiplying the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{1} \\\\ \\boxed{}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned} & \\,\\, & \\begin{aligned}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now count the number of decimal places in the $2$ factors.\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in $7.2.$\nTherefore, the exponent $(7.2)^{2}$ will have $1\\times 2=2$ decimal places.\n\nSo, we take our value of $5184$ and add a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n51\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\underbrace{84}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $(7.2)^{2}=51.84.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186424812 :question/worked-solution "Interpreting the fractions as division, we get\n\n$$\n(32\\,÷\\,5)\\times (25\\,÷\\,8).\n$$\n\n \n\nSince our expression contains multiplication and division only, we can create an equivalent expression by swapping the $÷\\,5$ and $÷\\,8:$\n\n$$\n(32\\,÷\\,8)\\times (25\\,÷\\,5)\n$$\n\n \n\nInterpreting the divisions as fractions, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{32}{8}\\times \\frac{25}{5}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the divisions, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\frac{32}{8}}_{⏟}}_{4}\\times {{\\frac{25}{5}}_{⏟}}_{5}=4\\times 5=20.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Interpreting the fractions as division, we get\n\n$$\n(32\\,÷\\,5)\\times (25\\,÷\\,8).\n$$\n\n \n\nSince our expression contains multiplication and division only, we can create an equivalent expression by swapping the $÷\\,5$ and $÷\\,8:$\n\n$$\n(32\\,÷\\,8)\\times (25\\,÷\\,5)\n$$\n\n \n\nInterpreting the divisions as fractions, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{32}{8}\\times \\frac{25}{5}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the divisions, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{\\frac{32}{8}}_{4}\\times \\underbrace{\\frac{25}{5}}_{5}=4\\times 5=20.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186424826 :question/worked-solution "Interpreting the fractions as division, we get\n\n$$\n(15\\,÷\\,8)\\times (24\\,÷\\,5).\n$$\n\n \n\nSince our expression contains multiplication and division only, we can create an equivalent expression by swapping the $÷\\,8$ and $÷\\,5:$\n\n$$\n(15\\,÷\\,5)\\times (24\\,÷\\,8)\n$$\n\n \n\nInterpreting the divisions as fractions, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{15}{5}\\times \\frac{24}{8}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the divisions, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\frac{15}{5}}_{⏟}}_{3}\\times {{\\frac{24}{8}}_{⏟}}_{3}=3\\times 3=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Interpreting the fractions as division, we get\n\n$$\n(15\\,÷\\,8)\\times (24\\,÷\\,5).\n$$\n\n \n\nSince our expression contains multiplication and division only, we can create an equivalent expression by swapping the $÷\\,8$ and $÷\\,5:$\n\n$$\n(15\\,÷\\,5)\\times (24\\,÷\\,8)\n$$\n\n \n\nInterpreting the divisions as fractions, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{15}{5}\\times \\frac{24}{8}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the divisions, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{\\frac{15}{5}}_{3}\\times \\underbrace{\\frac{24}{8}}_{3}=3\\times 3=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
