 [:db/cas 17592186426700 :question/worked-solution "Interpreting the fraction as division, we can write our expression as:\n\n$$\n7÷3\\times 15\n$$\n\nWe can write an equivalent expression by swapping the $÷3$ and the $\\times 15:$\n\n$$\n7\\times 15÷3\n$$\n\nExpressing the division as a fraction, we arrive at:\n\n$$\n7\\times \\frac{15}{3}\n$$\n\nWe now solve:\n\n$$\n7\\times {{\\frac{15}{3}}_{⏟}}_{5}=7\\times 5=35\n$$" "Interpreting the fraction as division, we can write our expression as:\n\n$$\n7÷3\\times 15\n$$\n\nWe can write an equivalent expression by swapping the $÷3$ and the $\\times 15:$\n\n$$\n7\\times 15÷3\n$$\n\nExpressing the division as a fraction, we arrive at:\n\n$$\n7\\times \\frac{15}{3}\n$$\n\nWe now solve:\n\n$$\n7\\times \\underbrace{\\frac{15}{3}}_{5}=7\\times 5=35\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186426839 :question/worked-solution "The word \"and\" is represented by the symbol \"$∧,$\" while the word \"or\" is represented by the symbol \"$∨.$\"\n\nNote that this sentence consists of two parts:  \n\n- **Part 1:** $n$ is less than $10$ or $13.$ This corresponds to\n\n$$\n{{n\\text{ is less than }10}_{⏟}}_{X}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{n\\text{ is less than }13}_{⏟}}_{Y},\n$$\n\nthat is, $X∨Y.$\n\n- **Part 2:** $n$ is an integer. This corresponds to $Z.$\n\nSo, we can express the sentence as\n\n$$\n{{n\\text{ is less than }10\\text{ or }13}_{⏟}}_{X∨Y}\\,{{\\text{and}}_{⏟}}_{∧}\\,{{n\\text{ is an integer}}_{⏟}}_{Z},\n$$\n\nthat is, $(X∨Y)∧Z.$" "The word \"and\" is represented by the symbol \"$∧,$\" while the word \"or\" is represented by the symbol \"$∨.$\"\n\nNote that this sentence consists of two parts:  \n\n- **Part 1:** $n$ is less than $10$ or $13.$ This corresponds to\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{n\\text{ is less than }10}_{X}\\,\\underbrace{\\text{or}}_{∨}\\,\\underbrace{n\\text{ is less than }13}_{Y},\n$$\n\nthat is, $X∨Y.$\n\n- **Part 2:** $n$ is an integer. This corresponds to $Z.$\n\nSo, we can express the sentence as\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{n\\text{ is less than }10\\text{ or }13}_{X∨Y}\\,\\underbrace{\\text{and}}_{∧}\\,\\underbrace{n\\text{ is an integer}}_{Z},\n$$\n\nthat is, $(X∨Y)∧Z.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186427560 :question/worked-solution "We want to determine if $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$ So, we want to find ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}={{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 17\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=5 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2} &=17\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the augmented matrix of the system to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 7 & 17\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -8\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{8}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the RREF matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So,  $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$\nAlso, the matrix tells us that ${x}_{1}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=1,$ meaning that\n\n$$\n2\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+1\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}={{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 17\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2\\cdot 1=2.$" "We want to determine if $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$ So, we want to find ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{1}}+{x}_{2}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{2}}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 17\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=5 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2} &=17\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the augmented matrix of the system to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 7 & 17\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -8\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{8}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the RREF matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So,  $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$\nAlso, the matrix tells us that ${x}_{1}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=1,$ meaning that\n\n$$\n2\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{1}}+1\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{2}}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 17\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2\\cdot 1=2.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186427863 :question/worked-solution "Since $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $S,$ then $S$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $S=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $S,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nS={{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -7 & 8\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -7 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -7 & 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}40 & -2 \\\\ 35 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -7 & 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}54 & -56 \\\\ 49 & -51\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $S,$ then $S$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $S=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $S,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nS=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}_{P}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{D}\\underbrace{{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -7 & 8\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -7 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -7 & 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}40 & -2 \\\\ 35 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -7 & 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}54 & -56 \\\\ 49 & -51\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186427864 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), left-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $B:$ \n\n$$\nB={{{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $B$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P.$ Computing $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the corresponding eigenvectors are: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}$" "Notice that the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), left-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $B:$ \n\n$$\nB=\\underbrace{{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{P}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{D}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $B$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P.$ Computing $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the corresponding eigenvectors are: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186429769 :question/worked-solution "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${a}_{1}$ is a linear combination of vectors ${a}_{2}$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=1\\cdot {a}_{2}+1\\cdot {a}_{3}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${a}_{1}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is spanned by just $\\{{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${a}_{2}$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set $\\{{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is $\\{{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}},{{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}\\}.\n$$" "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${a}_{1}$ is a linear combination of vectors ${a}_{2}$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=1\\cdot {a}_{2}+1\\cdot {a}_{3}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${a}_{1}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is spanned by just $\\{{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${a}_{2}$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set $\\{{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is $\\{{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{2}},\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{3}}\\}.\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186429890 :question/worked-solution "Here, ${1}^{7}$ is in exponent form. The base is $1$ and the exponent is $7.$ \n\nThis means that $1$ is taken as a factor $7$ times. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{1}^{7}={{1\\times 1\\times 1\\times 1\\times 1\\times 1\\times 1}_{⏟}}_{7\\text{ times}}=1.\n$$" "Here, ${1}^{7}$ is in exponent form. The base is $1$ and the exponent is $7.$ \n\nThis means that $1$ is taken as a factor $7$ times. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{1}^{7}=\\underbrace{1\\times 1\\times 1\\times 1\\times 1\\times 1\\times 1}_{7\\text{ times}}=1.\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186429892 :question/worked-solution "We need to evaluate ${7}^{3}.$\n\nTo evaluate ${7}^{3},$ we start by writing it out with multiplication signs:\n\n$$\n{7}^{3}={{7\\times 7\\times 7}_{⏟}}_{3\\text{ times}}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the product. Since $7\\times 7=49,$ we have\n\n$$\n{{7\\times 7}_{⏟}}_{49}\\times 7=49\\times 7.\n$$\n\nFinally, we work out $49\\times 7.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,{}_{6}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $49\\times 7=343,$ we have ${7}^{3}=343.$" "We need to evaluate ${7}^{3}.$\n\nTo evaluate ${7}^{3},$ we start by writing it out with multiplication signs:\n\n$$\n{7}^{3}=\\underbrace{7\\times 7\\times 7}_{3\\text{ times}}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the product. Since $7\\times 7=49,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{7\\times 7}_{49}\\times 7=49\\times 7.\n$$\n\nFinally, we work out $49\\times 7.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,{}_{6}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $49\\times 7=343,$ we have ${7}^{3}=343.$"]
