 [:db/cas 17592186429906 :question/worked-solution "We need to evaluate ${4}^{3}.$\n\nTo evaluate ${4}^{3},$ we start by writing it out with multiplication signs:\n\n$$\n{4}^{3}={{4\\times 4\\times 4}_{⏟}}_{3\\text{ times}}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the product. Since $4\\times 4=16,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{4\\times 4}_{⏟}}_{16}\\times 4 & =16\\times 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we work out $16\\times 4.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,{}_{2}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $16\\times 4=64,$ we have ${4}^{3}=64.$" "We need to evaluate ${4}^{3}.$\n\nTo evaluate ${4}^{3},$ we start by writing it out with multiplication signs:\n\n$$\n{4}^{3}=\\underbrace{4\\times 4\\times 4}_{3\\text{ times}}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the product. Since $4\\times 4=16,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{4\\times 4}_{16}\\times 4 & =16\\times 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we work out $16\\times 4.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,{}_{2}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $16\\times 4=64,$ we have ${4}^{3}=64.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186429907 :question/worked-solution "We need to evaluate ${9}^{2}.$\n\nTo evaluate ${9}^{2},$ we write it out with multiplication signs and then compute the product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{9}^{2}={{9\\times 9}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ times}}=81\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We need to evaluate ${9}^{2}.$\n\nTo evaluate ${9}^{2},$ we write it out with multiplication signs and then compute the product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{9}^{2}=\\underbrace{9\\times 9}_{2\\text{ times}}=81\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186429908 :question/worked-solution "To evaluate ${9}^{3},$ we start by writing it out with multiplication signs:\n\n$$\n{9}^{3}={{9\\times 9\\times 9}_{⏟}}_{3\\text{ times}}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the product. Since $9\\times 9=81,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{9\\times 9}_{⏟}}_{81}\\times 9 & =81\\times 9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we work out $81\\times 9.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,{}_{}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $81\\times 9=729,$ we have ${9}^{3}=729.$" "To evaluate ${9}^{3},$ we start by writing it out with multiplication signs:\n\n$$\n{9}^{3}=\\underbrace{9\\times 9\\times 9}_{3\\text{ times}}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the product. Since $9\\times 9=81,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{9\\times 9}_{81}\\times 9 & =81\\times 9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we work out $81\\times 9.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,{}_{}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $81\\times 9=729,$ we have ${9}^{3}=729.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186430001 :question/worked-solution "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(C),$ we need to check if the equation $Cx=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[C\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[C\\,|\\,b] & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -4 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & -8 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & ↔{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & -8 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -4 & 9\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -4 & 9\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -7 & -16 & -11\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(\\frac{7}{3}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -9 & -18\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=5 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=-3 \\\\ -9{x}_{3} &=-18.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=2.$ Substituting it into the second equation gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2}+3(2) & =-3 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, from the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =5-2{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} \\\\  & =5-2(-3)-3(2) \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we have a solution, the equation $Cx=b$ is consistent, which implies that $b\\in \\text{Col}(C).$ \n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${c}_{1},$ ${c}_{2},$ and ${c}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}=5\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{c}_{1}}+(-3)\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{c}_{2}}+2\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{c}_{3}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=5\\cdot (-3)\\cdot 2=-30.$" "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(C),$ we need to check if the equation $Cx=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[C\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[C\\,|\\,b] & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -4 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & -8 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & ↔{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & -8 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -4 & 9\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -4 & 9\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -7 & -16 & -11\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(\\frac{7}{3}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -9 & -18\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=5 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=-3 \\\\ -9{x}_{3} &=-18.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=2.$ Substituting it into the second equation gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2}+3(2) & =-3 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, from the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =5-2{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} \\\\  & =5-2(-3)-3(2) \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we have a solution, the equation $Cx=b$ is consistent, which implies that $b\\in \\text{Col}(C).$ \n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${c}_{1},$ ${c}_{2},$ and ${c}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}=5\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{c}_{1}}+(-3)\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{c}_{2}}+2\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{{c}_{3}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=5\\cdot (-3)\\cdot 2=-30.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186430359 :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$  select the correct options in the computations of the matrix $U$ below.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}=2\\sqrt{3}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\n\nNow, we compute the $1$st column of $U.$ It is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${u}_{1}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n|  |  | $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}$ |  |  |\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal.\n\nHence, we must find vectors $u=[{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}]^{T}$ such that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nChoosing ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ as free variables, the general solution is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | ${x}_{2}$ |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-5}} |  |  |\n\nthat we can rewrite as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{1}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nNext, we orthogonalize ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process to get the corresponding orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nLet ${c}_{1}={b}_{1},$ then:\n\n|  |  | {{field-6}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${c}_{2}=\\,\\,$ |  | $1$ |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-7}} |  |  |\n\nFinally, normalizing orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n                ${u}_{2}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                {{field-8}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-9}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                $-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n                ${u}_{3}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                {{field-10}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-11}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$  select the correct options in the computations of the matrix $U$ below.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}=2\\sqrt{3}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\n\nNow, we compute the $1$st column of $U.$ It is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${u}_{1}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n|  |  | $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}$ |  |  |\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal.\n\nHence, we must find vectors $u=[{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}]^{T}$ such that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nChoosing ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ as free variables, the general solution is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | ${x}_{2}$ |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-5}} |  |  |\n\nthat we can rewrite as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{1}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{2}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{b}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nNext, we orthogonalize ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process to get the corresponding orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nLet ${c}_{1}={b}_{1},$ then:\n\n|  |  | {{field-6}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${c}_{2}=\\,\\,$ |  | $1$ |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-7}} |  |  |\n\nFinally, normalizing orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n                ${u}_{2}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                {{field-8}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-9}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                $-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n                ${u}_{3}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                {{field-10}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-11}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"]
 [:db/cas 17592186430359 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=2\\sqrt{3}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{1}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are free variables. \n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are *not* mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we have to orthogonalize them using the Gram-Schmidt process. So, let \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}={b}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & ={b}_{2}-\\frac{{b}_{2}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{{c}_{1}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{c}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, normalizing orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=2\\sqrt{3}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{1}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{2}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are free variables. \n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are *not* mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we have to orthogonalize them using the Gram-Schmidt process. So, let \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}={b}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & ={b}_{2}-\\frac{{b}_{2}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{{c}_{1}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{c}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, normalizing orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186430360 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=2\\sqrt{5}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,-{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{2}{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{3}{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables.\n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we only need to normalize them. This gives the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=2\\sqrt{5}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,-{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{2}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{3}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables.\n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we only need to normalize them. This gives the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186430444 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2} & -2y\\end{bmatrix},$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-2y.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})=2x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2y)=0 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2})=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2x & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2} & -2y\\end{bmatrix},$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-2y.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})=2x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2y)=0 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2})=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2x & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186430445 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-y) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-y)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & \\frac{x}{y}-1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{y}-1)=\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=\\frac{1}{y}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y}-1)=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & -\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-y) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-y)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & \\frac{x}{y}-1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{y}-1)=\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=\\frac{1}{y}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y}-1)=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & -\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186430446 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate this matrix at $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},1).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate this matrix at $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},1).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186430447 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2xy+{y}^{2} & {x}^{2}+2xy\\end{bmatrix},$ \n we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2xy+{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}+2xy.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy+{y}^{2})=2y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2xy)=2x+2y \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy+{y}^{2})=2x+2y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2xy)=2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2y & 2x+2y \\\\ 2x+2y & 2x\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate this matrix at $(-1,2).$ This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(-1,2)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2xy+{y}^{2} & {x}^{2}+2xy\\end{bmatrix},$ \n we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2xy+{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}+2xy.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy+{y}^{2})=2y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2xy)=2x+2y \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy+{y}^{2})=2x+2y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2xy)=2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2y & 2x+2y \\\\ 2x+2y & 2x\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate this matrix at $(-1,2).$ This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(-1,2)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186430448 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det({f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since\n${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x} & 2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{bmatrix},$\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x})=-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x})=\\frac{2y}{x}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{2y}{x} & 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,1)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(1,1)=(-1)\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 2=-4.\n$$" "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det({f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since\n${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x} & 2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{bmatrix},$\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x})=-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x})=\\frac{2y}{x}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{2y}{x} & 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,1)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(1,1)=(-1)\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 2=-4.\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186430655 :question/worked-solution "Interpreting the fractions as division, we get\n\n$$\n(15\\,÷\\,4)\\times (16\\,÷\\,3).\n$$\n\n \n\nSince our expression contains multiplication and division only, we can create an equivalent expression by swapping the $÷\\,4$ and $÷\\,3$:\n\n \n\n$$\n(15\\,÷\\,3)\\times (16\\,÷\\,4)\n$$\n\n \n\nInterpreting the divisions as fractions, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{15}{3}\\times \\frac{16}{4}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the divisions, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\frac{15}{3}}_{⏟}}_{5}\\times {{\\frac{16}{4}}_{⏟}}_{4}=5\\times 4=20.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Interpreting the fractions as division, we get\n\n$$\n(15\\,÷\\,4)\\times (16\\,÷\\,3).\n$$\n\n \n\nSince our expression contains multiplication and division only, we can create an equivalent expression by swapping the $÷\\,4$ and $÷\\,3$:\n\n \n\n$$\n(15\\,÷\\,3)\\times (16\\,÷\\,4)\n$$\n\n \n\nInterpreting the divisions as fractions, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{15}{3}\\times \\frac{16}{4}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the divisions, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{\\frac{15}{3}}_{5}\\times \\underbrace{\\frac{16}{4}}_{4}=5\\times 4=20.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186430656 :question/worked-solution "Interpreting the fractions as division, we get\n\n$$\n(35\\,÷\\,6)\\times (18\\,÷\\,7).\n$$\n\n \n\nSince our expression contains multiplication and division only, we can create an equivalent expression by swapping the $÷\\,6$ and $÷\\,7$:\n\n$$\n(35\\,÷\\,7)\\times (18\\,÷\\,6)\n$$\n\n \n\nInterpreting the divisions as fractions, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{35}{7}\\times \\frac{18}{6}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the divisions, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\frac{35}{7}}_{⏟}}_{5}\\times {{\\frac{18}{6}}_{⏟}}_{3}=5\\times 3=15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{35}{6}\\times \\frac{18}{7}=15.$" "Interpreting the fractions as division, we get\n\n$$\n(35\\,÷\\,6)\\times (18\\,÷\\,7).\n$$\n\n \n\nSince our expression contains multiplication and division only, we can create an equivalent expression by swapping the $÷\\,6$ and $÷\\,7$:\n\n$$\n(35\\,÷\\,7)\\times (18\\,÷\\,6)\n$$\n\n \n\nInterpreting the divisions as fractions, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{35}{7}\\times \\frac{18}{6}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the divisions, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{\\frac{35}{7}}_{5}\\times \\underbrace{\\frac{18}{6}}_{3}=5\\times 3=15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{35}{6}\\times \\frac{18}{7}=15.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186430658 :question/worked-solution "Interpreting the fractions as division, we get\n\n$$\n(9\\,÷\\,4)\\times (8\\,÷\\,3).\n$$\n\n \n\nSince our expression contains multiplication and division only, we can create an equivalent expression by swapping the $÷\\,4$ and $÷\\,3$:\n \n\n$$\n(9\\,÷\\,3)\\times (8\\,÷\\,4)\n$$\n\n \n\nInterpreting the divisions as fractions, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{3}\\times \\frac{8}{4}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the divisions, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\frac{9}{3}}_{⏟}}_{3}\\times {{\\frac{8}{4}}_{⏟}}_{2}=3\\times 2=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{9}{4}\\times \\frac{8}{3}=6.$" "Interpreting the fractions as division, we get\n\n$$\n(9\\,÷\\,4)\\times (8\\,÷\\,3).\n$$\n\n \n\nSince our expression contains multiplication and division only, we can create an equivalent expression by swapping the $÷\\,4$ and $÷\\,3$:\n \n\n$$\n(9\\,÷\\,3)\\times (8\\,÷\\,4)\n$$\n\n \n\nInterpreting the divisions as fractions, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{3}\\times \\frac{8}{4}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the divisions, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{\\frac{9}{3}}_{3}\\times \\underbrace{\\frac{8}{4}}_{2}=3\\times 2=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{9}{4}\\times \\frac{8}{3}=6.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186430660 :question/worked-solution "Interpreting the fractions as division, we get\n\n$$\n(15\\,÷\\,2)\\times (10\\,÷\\,3).\n$$\n\n \n\nSince our expression contains multiplication and division only, we can create an equivalent expression by swapping the $÷\\,2$ and $÷\\,3$:\n\n \n\n$$\n(15\\,÷\\,3)\\times (10\\,÷\\,2)\n$$\n\n \n\nInterpreting the divisions as fractions, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{15}{3}\\times \\frac{10}{2}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the divisions, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\frac{15}{3}}_{⏟}}_{5}\\times {{\\frac{10}{2}}_{⏟}}_{5}=5\\times 5=25.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Interpreting the fractions as division, we get\n\n$$\n(15\\,÷\\,2)\\times (10\\,÷\\,3).\n$$\n\n \n\nSince our expression contains multiplication and division only, we can create an equivalent expression by swapping the $÷\\,2$ and $÷\\,3$:\n\n \n\n$$\n(15\\,÷\\,3)\\times (10\\,÷\\,2)\n$$\n\n \n\nInterpreting the divisions as fractions, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{15}{3}\\times \\frac{10}{2}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the divisions, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{\\frac{15}{3}}_{5}\\times \\underbrace{\\frac{10}{2}}_{5}=5\\times 5=25.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186430666 :question/worked-solution "Interpreting the fractions as division, we get\n\n$$\n(9\\,÷\\,2)\\times (8\\,÷\\,3).\n$$\n\n \n\nSince our expression contains multiplication and division only, we can create an equivalent expression by swapping the $÷\\,2$ and $÷\\,3$:\n\n \n\n$$\n(9\\,÷\\,3)\\times (8\\,÷\\,2)\n$$\n\n \n\nInterpreting the divisions as fractions, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{3}\\times \\frac{8}{2}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the divisions, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\frac{9}{3}}_{⏟}}_{3}\\times {{\\frac{8}{2}}_{⏟}}_{4}=3\\times 4=12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Interpreting the fractions as division, we get\n\n$$\n(9\\,÷\\,2)\\times (8\\,÷\\,3).\n$$\n\n \n\nSince our expression contains multiplication and division only, we can create an equivalent expression by swapping the $÷\\,2$ and $÷\\,3$:\n\n \n\n$$\n(9\\,÷\\,3)\\times (8\\,÷\\,2)\n$$\n\n \n\nInterpreting the divisions as fractions, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{3}\\times \\frac{8}{2}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the divisions, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{\\frac{9}{3}}_{3}\\times \\underbrace{\\frac{8}{2}}_{4}=3\\times 4=12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186430705 :question/worked-solution "To evaluate ${160}^{2},$ we start by writing it out with a multiplication sign:\n\n$$\n{160}^{2}={{160\\times 160}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ times}}\n$$\n\nThen, we work out $160\\times 160.$\n\nFirst, we figure out the non-zero part and the block of zeros for both numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}160 & \\,⟶\\,160 \\\\ 160 & \\,⟶\\,160\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that there are $2$ zeros in total.\n\nNext, we multiply the non-zero parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{} \\\\ \\boxed{3}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned} & \\,\\, & \\begin{aligned}\\end{aligned} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we join the above with the block of zeros: \n\n$$\n25600=25,600\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${160}^{2}=25,600.$" "To evaluate ${160}^{2},$ we start by writing it out with a multiplication sign:\n\n$$\n{160}^{2}=\\underbrace{160\\times 160}_{2\\text{ times}}\n$$\n\nThen, we work out $160\\times 160.$\n\nFirst, we figure out the non-zero part and the block of zeros for both numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}160 & \\,⟶\\,160 \\\\ 160 & \\,⟶\\,160\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that there are $2$ zeros in total.\n\nNext, we multiply the non-zero parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{} \\\\ \\boxed{3}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned} & \\,\\, & \\begin{aligned}\\end{aligned} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we join the above with the block of zeros: \n\n$$\n25600=25,600\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${160}^{2}=25,600.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186430706 :question/worked-solution "To evaluate ${21}^{2},$ we start by writing it out with a multiplication sign:\n\n$$\n{21}^{2}={{21\\times 21}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ times}}\n$$\n\nThen, we work out $21\\times 21.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${21}^{2}=441.$" "To evaluate ${21}^{2},$ we start by writing it out with a multiplication sign:\n\n$$\n{21}^{2}=\\underbrace{21\\times 21}_{2\\text{ times}}\n$$\n\nThen, we work out $21\\times 21.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${21}^{2}=441.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186430707 :question/worked-solution "To evaluate ${15}^{4},$ we start by writing it out with multiplication signs:\n\n$$\n{15}^{4}={{15\\times 15\\times 15\\times 15}_{⏟}}_{4\\text{ times}}\n$$\n\nSince ${15}^{2}=15\\times 15=225,$ we have\n\n$$\n{{15\\times 15}_{⏟}}_{225}\\times {{15\\times 15}_{⏟}}_{225}=225\\times 225.\n$$\n\nFinally, we work out $225\\times 225.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\cancel{0} \\\\ \\cancel{1}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ \\cancel{1} \\\\ \\cancel{2}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned} & \\,\\, & \\begin{aligned}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${15}^{4}=50,625.$" "To evaluate ${15}^{4},$ we start by writing it out with multiplication signs:\n\n$$\n{15}^{4}=\\underbrace{15\\times 15\\times 15\\times 15}_{4\\text{ times}}\n$$\n\nSince ${15}^{2}=15\\times 15=225,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{15\\times 15}_{225}\\times \\underbrace{15\\times 15}_{225}=225\\times 225.\n$$\n\nFinally, we work out $225\\times 225.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\cancel{0} \\\\ \\cancel{1}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ \\cancel{1} \\\\ \\cancel{2}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned} & \\,\\, & \\begin{aligned}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${15}^{4}=50,625.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186430708 :question/worked-solution "To evaluate ${700}^{2},$ we start by writing it out with a multiplication sign:\n\n$$\n{700}^{2}={{700\\times 700}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ times}}\n$$\n\nThen, we work out $700\\times 700.$\n\nFirst, we figure out the non-zero part and the block of zeros for both numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}700 & \\,⟶\\,700 \\\\ 700 & \\,⟶\\,700\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that there are $4$ zeros in total.\n\nNext, we multiply the non-zero parts:\n\n$$\n7\\times 7=49\n$$\n\nFinally, we join the above with the block of zeros: \n\n$$\n490000=490,000\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${700}^{2}=490,000.$" "To evaluate ${700}^{2},$ we start by writing it out with a multiplication sign:\n\n$$\n{700}^{2}=\\underbrace{700\\times 700}_{2\\text{ times}}\n$$\n\nThen, we work out $700\\times 700.$\n\nFirst, we figure out the non-zero part and the block of zeros for both numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}700 & \\,⟶\\,700 \\\\ 700 & \\,⟶\\,700\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that there are $4$ zeros in total.\n\nNext, we multiply the non-zero parts:\n\n$$\n7\\times 7=49\n$$\n\nFinally, we join the above with the block of zeros: \n\n$$\n490000=490,000\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${700}^{2}=490,000.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186430709 :question/worked-solution "To evaluate ${14}^{2},$ we start by writing it out with a multiplication sign:\n\n$$\n{14}^{2}={{14\\times 14}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ times}}\n$$\n\nThen, we work out $14\\times 14.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{} \\\\ \\boxed{1}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${14}^{2}=196.$" "To evaluate ${14}^{2},$ we start by writing it out with a multiplication sign:\n\n$$\n{14}^{2}=\\underbrace{14\\times 14}_{2\\text{ times}}\n$$\n\nThen, we work out $14\\times 14.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{} \\\\ \\boxed{1}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${14}^{2}=196.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186430710 :question/worked-solution "To evaluate ${26}^{4},$ we start by writing it out with multiplication signs:\n\n$$\n{26}^{4}={{26\\times 26\\times 26\\times 26}_{⏟}}_{4\\text{ times}}\n$$\n\nSince ${26}^{2}=26\\times 26=676,$ we have\n\n$$\n{{26\\times 26}_{⏟}}_{676}\\times {{26\\times 26}_{⏟}}_{676}=676\\times 676.\n$$\n\nFinally, we work out $676\\times 676.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ \\cancel{5} \\\\ \\cancel{4}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\cancel{4} \\\\ \\cancel{3}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned} & \\,\\, & \\begin{aligned}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${26}^{2}=456,976.$" "To evaluate ${26}^{4},$ we start by writing it out with multiplication signs:\n\n$$\n{26}^{4}=\\underbrace{26\\times 26\\times 26\\times 26}_{4\\text{ times}}\n$$\n\nSince ${26}^{2}=26\\times 26=676,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{26\\times 26}_{676}\\times \\underbrace{26\\times 26}_{676}=676\\times 676.\n$$\n\nFinally, we work out $676\\times 676.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ \\cancel{5} \\\\ \\cancel{4}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\cancel{4} \\\\ \\cancel{3}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned} & \\,\\, & \\begin{aligned}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${26}^{2}=456,976.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186430732 :question/worked-solution "We know that any vector in  $\\text{Span}\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ has the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the coordinates relative to the basis $\\,B.$ Since we are told that $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={{(\\,3\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{{(\\,-3\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -24 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -12 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -36 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We know that any vector in  $\\text{Span}\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ has the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the coordinates relative to the basis $\\,B.$ Since we are told that $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\underbrace{(\\,3\\,)}_{{x}_{1}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\underbrace{(\\,-3\\,)}_{{x}_{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -24 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -12 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -36 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186431184 :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the paraboloid $z=(x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}.$ One such surface would be the plane $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=(x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2})=2(x-1) \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2})=2(y+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨x+y,\\,-x+y,\\,-2z⟩{|}_{z=(x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨{{x+y}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{-x+y}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{-2(x-1)^{2}-2(y+1)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(x+y)\\cdot 2(x-1)-(-x+y)\\cdot 2(y+1)-2((x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2((x+y)(1-x)+(x-y)(y+1)-(x-1)^{2}-(y+1)^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4(-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+2x-y-1)\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ with radius $1$ centered at the origin. Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4(-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+2x-y-1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4(-{r}^{2}+2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-{r}^{3}+2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -r)\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[-{r}^{3}\\theta +2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -r\\theta ]}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-2\\pi {r}^{3}-2\\pi r)\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =-8\\pi {[-\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-8\\pi (-\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-6\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the paraboloid $z=(x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}.$ One such surface would be the plane $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=(x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2})=2(x-1) \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2})=2(y+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨x+y,\\,-x+y,\\,-2z⟩{|}_{z=(x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{x+y}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{-x+y}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{-2(x-1)^{2}-2(y+1)^{2}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(x+y)\\cdot 2(x-1)-(-x+y)\\cdot 2(y+1)-2((x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2((x+y)(1-x)+(x-y)(y+1)-(x-1)^{2}-(y+1)^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4(-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+2x-y-1)\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ with radius $1$ centered at the origin. Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4(-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+2x-y-1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4(-{r}^{2}+2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-{r}^{3}+2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -r)\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[-{r}^{3}\\theta +2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -r\\theta ]}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-2\\pi {r}^{3}-2\\pi r)\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =-8\\pi {[-\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-8\\pi (-\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-6\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186431187 :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the half-cone $z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and the boundary of our infinite vertical column. One such surface is our paraboloid $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨0\\,-{z}^{3},\\,0⟩{|}_{z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/2},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{{0}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/2}}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{0}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/2}\\cdot \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/ab/ab155520a21d38483e8b0978a04972e1a96c8d273d23ca7aa36bb1846189b4c8.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1+x}\\,y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the half-cone $z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and the boundary of our infinite vertical column. One such surface is our paraboloid $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨0\\,-{z}^{3},\\,0⟩{|}_{z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/2},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{0}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/2}}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{0}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/2}\\cdot \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/ab/ab155520a21d38483e8b0978a04972e1a96c8d273d23ca7aa36bb1846189b4c8.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1+x}\\,y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186432326 :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. You're given that the system of equations given below connects the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$  \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} &=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2} &=\\frac{1}{5}{a}_{2}+6{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3} &=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{3}-2{q}_{1}+{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of ${r}_{11}+{r}_{13}+{r}_{23}?$" "$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. You're given that the system of equations given below connects the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$  \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} &=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2} &=\\frac{1}{5}{a}_{2}+6{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3} &=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{3}-2{q}_{1}+{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of ${r}_{11}+{r}_{13}+{r}_{23}?$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186432326 :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} &=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2} &=\\frac{1}{5}{a}_{2}+6{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3} &=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{3}-2{q}_{1}+{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} &=3{q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} &=-30{q}_{1}+5{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3} &=8{q}_{1}-4{q}_{2}+4{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, this system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -30 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{11}+{r}_{13}+{r}_{23}=3+8+(-4)=7.\n$$" "Let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} &=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2} &=\\frac{1}{5}{a}_{2}+6{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3} &=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{3}-2{q}_{1}+{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} &=3{q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} &=-30{q}_{1}+5{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3} &=8{q}_{1}-4{q}_{2}+4{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, this system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -30 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.}^{R}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{11}+{r}_{13}+{r}_{23}=3+8+(-4)=7.\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186432329 :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{11}.$" "$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{11}.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186432329 :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}-(-\\frac{9}{9})\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 6\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 6\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{11}=(\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}})\\cdot (3)=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}}=2\\sqrt{2}.$" "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}-(-\\frac{9}{9})\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 6\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 6\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{11}=(\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}})\\cdot (3)=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}}=2\\sqrt{2}.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186432330 :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ of $A$ are orthogonal, and that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained by normalizing the columns of $A,$ what is the value of ${q}_{21}\\cdot {r}_{11}?$" "$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ of $A$ are orthogonal, and that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained by normalizing the columns of $A,$ what is the value of ${q}_{21}\\cdot {r}_{11}?$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186432330 :question/worked-solution "Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct an orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we simply normalize ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{14}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{14}\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{21}\\cdot {r}_{11}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}=1.$" "Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct an orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we simply normalize ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{14}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{14}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{21}\\cdot {r}_{11}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}=1.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186432334 :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ \\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖} & \\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖} & \\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above, where $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is an orthogonal set of vectors. You're given that $‖{v}_{1}‖=1,$ $‖{v}_{2}‖=2,$ $‖{v}_{3}‖=3,$ and that the system of equations below describes the relationship between the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$ \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} &={a}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} &={a}_{2}-{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{3} &={a}_{3}-2{v}_{2}+{v}_{1}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of ${r}_{11}+{r}_{13}+{r}_{33}?$" "$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ \\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖} & \\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖} & \\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above, where $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is an orthogonal set of vectors. You're given that $‖{v}_{1}‖=1,$ $‖{v}_{2}‖=2,$ $‖{v}_{3}‖=3,$ and that the system of equations below describes the relationship between the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$ \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} &={a}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} &={a}_{2}-{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{3} &={a}_{3}-2{v}_{2}+{v}_{1}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of ${r}_{11}+{r}_{13}+{r}_{33}?$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186432334 :question/worked-solution "First, we normalize the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{1}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{1}={q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{2}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{2}=2{q}_{2} \\\\ {q}_{3} & =\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{3}}{3}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{3}=3{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the set $\\{{q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}\\}$ will be both orthogonal and normalized.\n\nNext, let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} &={a}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} &={a}_{2}-{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{3} &={a}_{3}-2{v}_{2}+{v}_{1}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} &={v}_{1}={q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} &={v}_{1}+{v}_{2}={q}_{1}+2{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3} &=-{v}_{1}+2{v}_{2}+{v}_{3}=-{q}_{1}+4{q}_{2}+3{q}_{3}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe final system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{11}+{r}_{13}+{r}_{33}=1+(-1)+3=3.\n$$" "First, we normalize the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{1}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{1}={q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{2}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{2}=2{q}_{2} \\\\ {q}_{3} & =\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{3}}{3}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{3}=3{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the set $\\{{q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}\\}$ will be both orthogonal and normalized.\n\nNext, let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} &={a}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} &={a}_{2}-{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{3} &={a}_{3}-2{v}_{2}+{v}_{1}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} &={v}_{1}={q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} &={v}_{1}+{v}_{2}={q}_{1}+2{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3} &=-{v}_{1}+2{v}_{2}+{v}_{3}=-{q}_{1}+4{q}_{2}+3{q}_{3}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe final system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{11}+{r}_{13}+{r}_{33}=1+(-1)+3=3.\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186432370 :question/worked-solution "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+y+1}}, & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+y+1}} \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =1, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{(x+y+1)^{3}}}, & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{(x+y+1)^{3}}}, & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{(x+y+1)^{3}}}, & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈2+1\\cdot x+1\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot xy+(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2+1x+1y-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2})xy-\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=1+1-\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$" "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overbrace{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}^{\\text{Linear approximation}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+\\underbrace{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+y+1}}, & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+y+1}} \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =1, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{(x+y+1)^{3}}}, & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{(x+y+1)^{3}}}, & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{(x+y+1)^{3}}}, & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈2+1\\cdot x+1\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot xy+(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2+1x+1y-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2})xy-\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=1+1-\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186432374 :question/worked-solution "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{(x+y+1)^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{(x+y+1)^{2}} \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =-1, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =\\frac{2}{(x+y+1)^{3}}, & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =2 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{2}{(x+y+1)^{3}}, & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =2 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =\\frac{2}{(x+y+1)^{3}}, & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈1+(-1)\\cdot x+(-1)\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(2)\\cdot xy+2\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1+(-1)x-y+{x}^{2}+2xy+1{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=-1+2+1=2.\n$$" "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overbrace{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}^{\\text{Linear approximation}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+\\underbrace{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{(x+y+1)^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{(x+y+1)^{2}} \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =-1, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =\\frac{2}{(x+y+1)^{3}}, & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =2 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{2}{(x+y+1)^{3}}, & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =2 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =\\frac{2}{(x+y+1)^{3}}, & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈1+(-1)\\cdot x+(-1)\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(2)\\cdot xy+2\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1+(-1)x-y+{x}^{2}+2xy+1{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=-1+2+1=2.\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186433715 :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A-2I:$\n\n$$\nA-2I=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)w=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Again, since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $x$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)x=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }{{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A-2I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $3$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A-2I:$\n\n$$\nA-2I=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)w=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Again, since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $x$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)x=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{v}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{w}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A-2I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $3$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\""]
 [:db/cas 17592186433716 :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A-I:$\n\n$$\nA-I=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-I:$\n\n$$\n(A-I)v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A-I:$\n\n$$\n(A-I)w=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }{{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A-I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A-I:$\n\n$$\nA-I=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-I:$\n\n$$\n(A-I)v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A-I:$\n\n$$\n(A-I)w=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{v}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{w}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A-I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\""]
 [:db/cas 17592186433717 :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A+I:$\n\n$$\nA+I=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A+I:$\n\n$$\n(A+I)v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A+I:$\n\n$$\n(A+I)w=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A+I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A+I:$\n\n$$\nA+I=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A+I:$\n\n$$\n(A+I)v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A+I:$\n\n$$\n(A+I)w=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{v}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{w}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A+I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""]
 [:db/cas 17592186433718 :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A+I:$\n\n$$\nA+I=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -8 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A+I:$\n\n$$\n(A+I)v=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A+I:$\n\n$$\n(A+I)w=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }{{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A+I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A+I:$\n\n$$\nA+I=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -8 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A+I:$\n\n$$\n(A+I)v=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A+I:$\n\n$$\n(A+I)w=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{v}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{w}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A+I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\""]
 [:db/cas 17592186435082 :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFirst, notice that we can express the given transformation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6u+4v+2 \\\\ 5v-1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the constants and expressing the linear part as a product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}6u+4v+2 \\\\ 5v-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6u+4v \\\\ 5v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=4+5+(-1)=8.\n$$" "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFirst, notice that we can express the given transformation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6u+4v+2 \\\\ 5v-1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the constants and expressing the linear part as a product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}6u+4v+2 \\\\ 5v-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6u+4v \\\\ 5v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=4+5+(-1)=8.\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186435169 :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFirst, notice that we can express the given transformation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2u+5v \\\\ u-3v\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the constants and expressing the linear part as a product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u+5v \\\\ u-3v\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u+5v \\\\ u-3v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=1+(-3)+0=-2.\n$$" "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFirst, notice that we can express the given transformation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2u+5v \\\\ u-3v\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the constants and expressing the linear part as a product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u+5v \\\\ u-3v\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u+5v \\\\ u-3v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=1+(-3)+0=-2.\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186435170 :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nWe can carry out the matrix operations using the matrix form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot \\overset{u}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}}+\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4u \\\\ 6u+2v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4u \\\\ 6u+2v-1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding elements, we obtain\n\n$$\nx=4u+0,\\,y=6u+2v+(-1)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=0+2+(-1)=1.\n$$" "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nWe can carry out the matrix operations using the matrix form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}\\cdot \\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}^{u}+\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}^{b} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4u \\\\ 6u+2v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4u \\\\ 6u+2v-1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding elements, we obtain\n\n$$\nx=4u+0,\\,y=6u+2v+(-1)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=0+2+(-1)=1.\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186435171 :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFirst, notice that we can express the given transformation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}v-7u \\\\ 2v-u\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the constants and expressing the linear part as a product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}v-7u \\\\ 2v-u\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7u+v \\\\ -u+2v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-7+2+0=-5.\n$$" "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFirst, notice that we can express the given transformation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}v-7u \\\\ 2v-u\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the constants and expressing the linear part as a product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}v-7u \\\\ 2v-u\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7u+v \\\\ -u+2v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-7+2+0=-5.\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186435356 :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $Q$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 24\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{8}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(Q)$ is $\\{{q}_{1},{q}_{2}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $3$rd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${q}_{3}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{q}_{3}}={{(2)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{q}_{1}}+{{(3)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{q}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2\\cdot 3=6.$" "First, we reduce the matrix $Q$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 24\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{8}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(Q)$ is $\\{{q}_{1},{q}_{2}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $3$rd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${q}_{3}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{q}_{3}}=\\underbrace{(2)}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{{q}_{1}}+\\underbrace{(3)}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{q}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2\\cdot 3=6.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186435357 :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $Q$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & -\\frac{5}{2} & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -\\frac{5}{8} & \\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(Q)$ is $\\{{q}_{1},{q}_{2}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $4$th column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${q}_{4}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{q}_{4}}={{(\\frac{3}{4})}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{q}_{1}}+{{(1)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{q}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot 1=\\frac{3}{4}.$" "First, we reduce the matrix $Q$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & -\\frac{5}{2} & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -\\frac{5}{8} & \\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(Q)$ is $\\{{q}_{1},{q}_{2}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $4$th column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${q}_{4}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{q}_{4}}=\\underbrace{(\\frac{3}{4})}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{q}_{1}}+\\underbrace{(1)}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{q}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot 1=\\frac{3}{4}.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186435358 :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $W$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -9 & 6 \\\\ -5 & 15 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 15 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 20\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{20}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $3$rd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(W)$ is $\\{{w}_{1},{w}_{3}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $2$nd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${w}_{2}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{w}_{2}}={{(-3)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{w}_{1}}+{{(0)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{3}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{w}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}=(-3)\\cdot 0=0.$" "First, we reduce the matrix $W$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -9 & 6 \\\\ -5 & 15 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 15 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 20\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{20}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $3$rd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(W)$ is $\\{{w}_{1},{w}_{3}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $2$nd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${w}_{2}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}}_{{w}_{2}}=\\underbrace{(-3)}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}}_{{w}_{1}}+\\underbrace{(0)}_{{x}_{3}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}}_{{w}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}=(-3)\\cdot 0=0.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186435393 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & x+2y\\end{bmatrix},$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x+2y.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+2y)=1 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=1\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+2y)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & x+2y\\end{bmatrix},$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x+2y.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+2y)=1 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=1\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+2y)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186435394 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2xy & 2y-{x}^{2}\\end{bmatrix},$ \n we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-2xy,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y-{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2xy)=-2y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y-{x}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2xy)=-2x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y-{x}^{2})=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2y & -2x \\\\ -2x & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate this matrix at $(2,1).$ This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(2,1)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2xy & 2y-{x}^{2}\\end{bmatrix},$ \n we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-2xy,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y-{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2xy)=-2y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y-{x}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2xy)=-2x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y-{x}^{2})=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2y & -2x \\\\ -2x & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate this matrix at $(2,1).$ This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(2,1)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186435395 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det({f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since\n${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x{y}^{2} & 2y{x}^{2}-1\\end{bmatrix},$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y{x}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x{y}^{2})=2{y}^{2}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y{x}^{2}-1)=4xy \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{y}^{2})=4xy\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y{x}^{2}-1)=2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2{y}^{2} & 4xy \\\\ 4xy & 2{x}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,-1)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(1,-1)=2\\cdot 2-(-4)\\cdot (-4)=-12.\n$$" "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det({f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since\n${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x{y}^{2} & 2y{x}^{2}-1\\end{bmatrix},$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y{x}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x{y}^{2})=2{y}^{2}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y{x}^{2}-1)=4xy \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{y}^{2})=4xy\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y{x}^{2}-1)=2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2{y}^{2} & 4xy \\\\ 4xy & 2{x}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,-1)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(1,-1)=2\\cdot 2-(-4)\\cdot (-4)=-12.\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186435768 :question/worked-solution "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a-2 & b & c \\\\ -3 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}a-2 & b+0 & c+0 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants and expanding the second determinant across the $1$st row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a-2 & b+0 & c+0 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & ={{|\\begin{aligned}a & b & c \\\\ -3 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|}_{⏟}}_{3}+|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3-2(-1)^{1+1}|\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3-2\\cdot (5+1) \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a-2 & b & c \\\\ -3 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}a-2 & b+0 & c+0 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants and expanding the second determinant across the $1$st row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a-2 & b+0 & c+0 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =\\underbrace{|\\begin{aligned}a & b & c \\\\ -3 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|}_{3}+|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3-2(-1)^{1+1}|\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3-2\\cdot (5+1) \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186440392 :question/worked-solution "We must write and evaluate an expression representing the total amount of peanuts.\n\nSince each of them bought $6$ ounces of salted peanuts and $4$ ounces of unsalted peanuts, the amount of peanuts they purchased, in ounces, is:\n\n$$\n{{6}_{⏟}}_{\\text{salted peanuts}}+{{4}_{⏟}}_{\\text{unsalted peanuts}}\n$$\n\nSince *each* person purchased this amount of peanuts, and there are $2$ people, we must multiply the amount of peanuts by $2.$ Therefore, the total amount of peanuts is:\n\n$$\n{{(6+4)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{each person}}\\times {{2}_{⏟}}_{\\text{people}}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the expression, we follow the order of operations with parentheses.\n\nFirst, we perform the operations inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6+4)\\times 2 & =(6+4)\\times 2 \\\\  & =10\\times 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply:\n\n$$\n10\\times 2=20\n$$" "We must write and evaluate an expression representing the total amount of peanuts.\n\nSince each of them bought $6$ ounces of salted peanuts and $4$ ounces of unsalted peanuts, the amount of peanuts they purchased, in ounces, is:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{6}_{\\text{salted peanuts}}+\\underbrace{4}_{\\text{unsalted peanuts}}\n$$\n\nSince *each* person purchased this amount of peanuts, and there are $2$ people, we must multiply the amount of peanuts by $2.$ Therefore, the total amount of peanuts is:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(6+4)}_{\\text{each person}}\\times \\underbrace{2}_{\\text{people}}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the expression, we follow the order of operations with parentheses.\n\nFirst, we perform the operations inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6+4)\\times 2 & =(6+4)\\times 2 \\\\  & =10\\times 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply:\n\n$$\n10\\times 2=20\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186441352 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 8 & -12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=4,{u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=4.$" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 8 & -12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{U}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=4,{u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=4.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186441353 :question/worked-solution "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $A,$ we write $A=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nA=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although we have a product of an upper triangular matrix on the left and a unit lower triangular matrix on the right, the order of multiplication is not correct (instead, we need a unit lower triangular matrix on the left and an upper triangular matrix on the right).\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the right is not an upper triangular matrix, as it has a non-zero entry below the diagonal.\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $A,$ we write $A=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nA=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{L}\\cdot \\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}}^{U}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although we have a product of an upper triangular matrix on the left and a unit lower triangular matrix on the right, the order of multiplication is not correct (instead, we need a unit lower triangular matrix on the left and an upper triangular matrix on the right).\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the right is not an upper triangular matrix, as it has a non-zero entry below the diagonal.\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."]
 [:db/cas 17592186441354 :question/worked-solution "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $B,$ we write $B=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nB=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the right is not an upper triangular matrix, as it has a non-zero entry below the diagonal.\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 20 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although we have a product of an upper triangular matrix on the left and a unit lower triangular matrix on the right, the order of multiplication is not correct (instead, we need a unit lower triangular matrix on the left and an upper triangular matrix on the right).\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $B,$ we write $B=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nB=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{L}\\cdot \\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}}^{U}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the right is not an upper triangular matrix, as it has a non-zero entry below the diagonal.\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 20 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although we have a product of an upper triangular matrix on the left and a unit lower triangular matrix on the right, the order of multiplication is not correct (instead, we need a unit lower triangular matrix on the left and an upper triangular matrix on the right).\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."]
 [:db/cas 17592186441355 :question/worked-solution "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $B,$ we write $B=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nB=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form because  the matrix on the left is not a unit lower triangular matrix, as its main diagonal contains numbers different from $1.$\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 12 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although we have a product of an upper triangular matrix on the left and a unit lower triangular matrix on the right, the order of multiplication is not correct (instead, we need a unit lower triangular matrix on the left and an upper triangular matrix on the right).\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $B,$ we write $B=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nB=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{L}\\cdot \\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}}^{U}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form because  the matrix on the left is not a unit lower triangular matrix, as its main diagonal contains numbers different from $1.$\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 12 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although we have a product of an upper triangular matrix on the left and a unit lower triangular matrix on the right, the order of multiplication is not correct (instead, we need a unit lower triangular matrix on the left and an upper triangular matrix on the right).\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."]
 [:db/cas 17592186441356 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ -20 & 15\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=5,$ ${u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=5.$" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ -20 & 15\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=5,$ ${u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=5.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186442429 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by multiplying the first two numbers. We have the product of two negatives, which gives a positive:\n\n$$\n(-6)\\cdot (-4)=24\n$$\n\nSo we now have\n\n$$\n{{(-6)\\cdot (-4)}_{⏟}}_{24}\\cdot 2=24\\cdot 2.\n$$\n\nThen, we work out this remaining product using the fact that the product of two positives gives a positive:\n\n$$\n24\\cdot 2=48\n$$" "Let's start by multiplying the first two numbers. We have the product of two negatives, which gives a positive:\n\n$$\n(-6)\\cdot (-4)=24\n$$\n\nSo we now have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(-6)\\cdot (-4)}_{24}\\cdot 2=24\\cdot 2.\n$$\n\nThen, we work out this remaining product using the fact that the product of two positives gives a positive:\n\n$$\n24\\cdot 2=48\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186442430 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by multiplying the first two numbers. We have the product of a positive and a negative, which gives a negative:\n\n$$\n7\\cdot (-2)=-14\n$$\n\nSo we now have\n\n$$\n{{7\\cdot (-2)}_{⏟}}_{-14}\\cdot (-5)=(-14)\\cdot (-5).\n$$\n\nThen, we work out this remaining product using the fact that the product of two negatives gives a positive:\n\n$$\n(-14)\\cdot (-5)=14\\cdot 5=70\n$$" "Let's start by multiplying the first two numbers. We have the product of a positive and a negative, which gives a negative:\n\n$$\n7\\cdot (-2)=-14\n$$\n\nSo we now have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{7\\cdot (-2)}_{-14}\\cdot (-5)=(-14)\\cdot (-5).\n$$\n\nThen, we work out this remaining product using the fact that the product of two negatives gives a positive:\n\n$$\n(-14)\\cdot (-5)=14\\cdot 5=70\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186442433 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by multiplying the first two numbers. We have the product of a negative and a positive, which gives a negative:\n\n$$\n(-3)\\cdot 4=-12\n$$\n\nSo we now have\n\n$$\n{{(-3)\\cdot 4}_{⏟}}_{-12}\\cdot (-2)=(-12)\\cdot (-2).\n$$\n\nThen, we work out this remaining product using the fact that the product of two negatives gives a positive:\n\n$$\n(-12)\\cdot (-2)=12\\cdot 2=24\n$$" "Let's start by multiplying the first two numbers. We have the product of a negative and a positive, which gives a negative:\n\n$$\n(-3)\\cdot 4=-12\n$$\n\nSo we now have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(-3)\\cdot 4}_{-12}\\cdot (-2)=(-12)\\cdot (-2).\n$$\n\nThen, we work out this remaining product using the fact that the product of two negatives gives a positive:\n\n$$\n(-12)\\cdot (-2)=12\\cdot 2=24\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186442438 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by multiplying the first two numbers. We have the product of a positive and a negative, which gives a negative:\n\n$$\n5\\cdot (-3)=-15\n$$\n\nSo we now have\n\n$$\n{{5\\cdot (-3)}_{⏟}}_{-15}\\cdot 2=(-15)\\cdot 2.\n$$\n\nThen, we work out this remaining product using the fact that a negative times a positive gives a negative:\n\n$$\n(-15)\\cdot 2=-(15\\cdot 2)=-30\n$$" "Let's start by multiplying the first two numbers. We have the product of a positive and a negative, which gives a negative:\n\n$$\n5\\cdot (-3)=-15\n$$\n\nSo we now have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{5\\cdot (-3)}_{-15}\\cdot 2=(-15)\\cdot 2.\n$$\n\nThen, we work out this remaining product using the fact that a negative times a positive gives a negative:\n\n$$\n(-15)\\cdot 2=-(15\\cdot 2)=-30\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186442439 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by multiplying the first two numbers. We have the product of two negatives, which gives a positive: \n\n$$\n(-4)\\cdot (-4)=16\n$$\n\nSo we now have\n\n$$\n{{(-4)\\cdot (-4)}_{⏟}}_{16}\\cdot (-3)=16\\cdot (-3).\n$$\n\nThen, we work out this remaining product using the fact that a positive times a negative gives a negative:\n\n$$\n16\\cdot (-3)=-(16\\cdot 3)=-48\n$$" "Let's start by multiplying the first two numbers. We have the product of two negatives, which gives a positive: \n\n$$\n(-4)\\cdot (-4)=16\n$$\n\nSo we now have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(-4)\\cdot (-4)}_{16}\\cdot (-3)=16\\cdot (-3).\n$$\n\nThen, we work out this remaining product using the fact that a positive times a negative gives a negative:\n\n$$\n16\\cdot (-3)=-(16\\cdot 3)=-48\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186442810 :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of ${q}_{32}\\cdot {r}_{12}.$" "$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of ${q}_{32}\\cdot {r}_{12}.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186442810 :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{6}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{6}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{6}\\end{bmatrix}}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{32}\\cdot {r}_{12}=(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}})\\cdot (2\\sqrt{3})=\\sqrt{2}.$" "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{6}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{6}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{6}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{32}\\cdot {r}_{12}=(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}})\\cdot (2\\sqrt{3})=\\sqrt{2}.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186442958 :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$P$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to the usual component-wise addition and scalar multiplication in ${R}^{2}$) because **addition of ordered pairs in $P$ is not closed.** *  \n\nFor example, $(1,0)\\in P$ because $1\\cdot 0=0$ and $(0,1)\\in P$ because $0\\cdot 1=0.$ However, if we add the two pairs component-wise, we get\n\n$$\n(1,0)+(0,1)=(1+0,0+1)=(1,1).\n$$\n\nThe product of the components of this pair is $1\\cdot 1=1\\ne 0.$ So, $(1,0)+(0,1)\\notin P.$\n\nOn the other hand:\n\n- Multiplication of $(x,y)\\in P$ by a scalar is closed. Indeed, if $\\alpha \\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\alpha \\cdot (x,y)=(\\alpha x,\\alpha y)\\in P\n$$\n\nsince $(\\alpha x)\\cdot (\\alpha y)={\\alpha }^{2}{{(xy)}_{⏟}}_{=0}=0.$\n$\\,✓$\n\n- The zero element in $P$ is $(0,0).$ $✓$\n\n- For an ordered pair $(x,y)\\in P,$ the ordered pair $-(x,y)=(-x,-y)$ is also an element of $P$ since $(-x)\\cdot (-y)=xy=0.$\n$\\,✓$\n\n- $1\\cdot (x,y)=(x,y)$ for all $(x,y)\\in P.$ $\\,✓$" "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$P$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to the usual component-wise addition and scalar multiplication in ${R}^{2}$) because **addition of ordered pairs in $P$ is not closed.** *  \n\nFor example, $(1,0)\\in P$ because $1\\cdot 0=0$ and $(0,1)\\in P$ because $0\\cdot 1=0.$ However, if we add the two pairs component-wise, we get\n\n$$\n(1,0)+(0,1)=(1+0,0+1)=(1,1).\n$$\n\nThe product of the components of this pair is $1\\cdot 1=1\\ne 0.$ So, $(1,0)+(0,1)\\notin P.$\n\nOn the other hand:\n\n- Multiplication of $(x,y)\\in P$ by a scalar is closed. Indeed, if $\\alpha \\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\alpha \\cdot (x,y)=(\\alpha x,\\alpha y)\\in P\n$$\n\nsince $(\\alpha x)\\cdot (\\alpha y)={\\alpha }^{2}\\underbrace{(xy)}_{=0}=0.$\n$\\,✓$\n\n- The zero element in $P$ is $(0,0).$ $✓$\n\n- For an ordered pair $(x,y)\\in P,$ the ordered pair $-(x,y)=(-x,-y)$ is also an element of $P$ since $(-x)\\cdot (-y)=xy=0.$\n$\\,✓$\n\n- $1\\cdot (x,y)=(x,y)$ for all $(x,y)\\in P.$ $\\,✓$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186443075 :question/worked-solution "We need to calculate the product of $-3.2$ with itself:\n\n$$\n(-3.2)^{2}=(-3.2)\\cdot (-3.2)=(3.2)\\cdot (3.2)\n$$\n\nFirst, we ignore the decimal points and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{} \\\\ \\boxed{}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $32\\times 32=1\\,024.$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in the first factor ($3.2$) and $1$ decimal place in the second factor ($3.2$), so their product will have $1+1=2$ decimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $1\\,024$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n10\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,{{24}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${3.2}^{2}=10.24.$" "We need to calculate the product of $-3.2$ with itself:\n\n$$\n(-3.2)^{2}=(-3.2)\\cdot (-3.2)=(3.2)\\cdot (3.2)\n$$\n\nFirst, we ignore the decimal points and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{} \\\\ \\boxed{}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $32\\times 32=1\\,024.$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in the first factor ($3.2$) and $1$ decimal place in the second factor ($3.2$), so their product will have $1+1=2$ decimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $1\\,024$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n10\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,\\underbrace{24}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${3.2}^{2}=10.24.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186443078 :question/worked-solution "We need to calculate the product of $(-1.4)$ with itself:\n\n$$\n(-1.4)^{2}=(-1.4)\\cdot (-1.4)=(1.4)\\cdot (1.4)\n$$\n\nFirst, we ignore the decimal points and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{} \\\\ \\boxed{1}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $14\\times 14=196.$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in the first factor ($1.4$) and $1$ decimal place in the second factor ($1.4$), so their product will have $1+1=2$ decimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $196$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,{{96}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $(-1.4)^{2}=1.96\\,.$" "We need to calculate the product of $(-1.4)$ with itself:\n\n$$\n(-1.4)^{2}=(-1.4)\\cdot (-1.4)=(1.4)\\cdot (1.4)\n$$\n\nFirst, we ignore the decimal points and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{} \\\\ \\boxed{1}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $14\\times 14=196.$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in the first factor ($1.4$) and $1$ decimal place in the second factor ($1.4$), so their product will have $1+1=2$ decimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $196$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,\\underbrace{96}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $(-1.4)^{2}=1.96\\,.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186443086 :question/worked-solution "We need to calculate the product of $5.3$ with itself:\n\n$$\n{5.3}^{2}=(5.3)\\cdot (5.3)\n$$\n\nFirst, we ignore the decimal points and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{1} \\\\ \\boxed{}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $53\\times 53=2\\,809.$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in the first factor ($5.3$) and $1$ decimal place in the second factor ($5.3$), so their product will have $1+1=2$ decimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $2\\,809$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n28\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,{{09}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${5.3}^{2}=28.09\\,.$" "We need to calculate the product of $5.3$ with itself:\n\n$$\n{5.3}^{2}=(5.3)\\cdot (5.3)\n$$\n\nFirst, we ignore the decimal points and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{1} \\\\ \\boxed{}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $53\\times 53=2\\,809.$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in the first factor ($5.3$) and $1$ decimal place in the second factor ($5.3$), so their product will have $1+1=2$ decimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $2\\,809$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n28\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,\\underbrace{09}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${5.3}^{2}=28.09\\,.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186443087 :question/worked-solution "We need to calculate the product of $-0.3$ with itself:\n\n$$\n(-0.3)^{2}=(-0.3)\\cdot (-0.3)=(0.3)\\cdot (0.3)\n$$\n\nFirst, we ignore the decimal points and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n3\\times 3=9.\n$$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in the first factor ($0.3$) and $1$ decimal place in the second factor ($0.3$), so their product will have $1+1=2$ decimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $9$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n0\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,{{09}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $(-0.3)^{2}=0.09\\,.$" "We need to calculate the product of $-0.3$ with itself:\n\n$$\n(-0.3)^{2}=(-0.3)\\cdot (-0.3)=(0.3)\\cdot (0.3)\n$$\n\nFirst, we ignore the decimal points and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n3\\times 3=9.\n$$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in the first factor ($0.3$) and $1$ decimal place in the second factor ($0.3$), so their product will have $1+1=2$ decimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $9$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n0\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,\\underbrace{09}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $(-0.3)^{2}=0.09\\,.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186443089 :question/worked-solution "We need to calculate the product of $1.6$ with itself:\n\n$$\n{1.6}^{2}=(1.6)\\cdot (1.6)\n$$\n\nFirst, we ignore the decimal points and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{} \\\\ \\boxed{3}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $16\\times 16=256.$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in the first factor ($1.6$) and $1$ decimal place in the second factor ($1.6$), so their product will have $1+1=2$ decimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $256$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,{{56}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${1.6}^{2}=2.56\\,.$" "We need to calculate the product of $1.6$ with itself:\n\n$$\n{1.6}^{2}=(1.6)\\cdot (1.6)\n$$\n\nFirst, we ignore the decimal points and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{} \\\\ \\boxed{3}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $16\\times 16=256.$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in the first factor ($1.6$) and $1$ decimal place in the second factor ($1.6$), so their product will have $1+1=2$ decimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $256$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,\\underbrace{56}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${1.6}^{2}=2.56\\,.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186443407 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{5}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{2}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{3}{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables.\n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we only need to normalize them. This gives the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{5}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{2}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{3}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables.\n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we only need to normalize them. This gives the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186443408 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=2\\sqrt{5}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{6}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ -3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{1}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{3}{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables.\n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we only need to normalize them. This gives the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & 0 & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=2\\sqrt{5}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{6}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ -3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{1}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{3}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables.\n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we only need to normalize them. This gives the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & 0 & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186444618 :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=x+2y+1$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=1,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨1,\\,2,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince we require a downward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is negative. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨1,\\,2,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨1,\\,2,\\,-1⟩}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨1,\\,2,\\,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=x+2y+1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z+5,\\,x-2y,\\,3x-2y⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨1,\\,2,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨x+2y+6,\\,x-2y,\\,3x-2y⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,2,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}((x+2y+6)+2(x-2y)-(3x-2y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(x+2y+6+2x-4y-3x+2y) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=x+2y+1$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\underbrace{⟨-1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=1,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\underbrace{⟨1,\\,2,\\,-1⟩}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince we require a downward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is negative. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨1,\\,2,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨1,\\,2,\\,-1⟩}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨1,\\,2,\\,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=x+2y+1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z+5,\\,x-2y,\\,3x-2y⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨1,\\,2,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨x+2y+6,\\,x-2y,\\,3x-2y⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,2,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}((x+2y+6)+2(x-2y)-(3x-2y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(x+2y+6+2x-4y-3x+2y) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186444619 :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=x+y$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=0,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=0.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=x+y$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,y,\\,x⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨x+y,\\,y,\\,x⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}(-(x+y)-y+x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}(-2y) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\,y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\,y)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\,y)\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}y\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{2}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{3}}}y\\cdot \\cancel{\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-2y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}-2y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}2y\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-[{y}^{2}]_{0}^{1}\\cdot [x]_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=x+y$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\underbrace{⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=0,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\underbrace{⟨1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=0.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=x+y$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,y,\\,x⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨x+y,\\,y,\\,x⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}(-(x+y)-y+x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}(-2y) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\,y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\,y)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\,y)\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}y\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{2}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{3}}}y\\cdot \\cancel{\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-2y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}-2y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}2y\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-[{y}^{2}]_{0}^{1}\\cdot [x]_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186445316 :question/worked-solution "To find out the amount of money that Carmen must pay, we need to multiply $15$ by $3.48.$\n\nWe proceed by multiplying the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{} \\\\ \\boxed{2}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{} \\\\ \\boxed{4}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,.4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere are $2$ decimal places in $3.48.$ \nTherefore, the product will also have $2$ decimal places.\n\nSo, we take our value of $5220$ and add a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places.\n\n$$\n52\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,{{20}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Carmen must pay $$3.48\\times 15=$52.20.$" "To find out the amount of money that Carmen must pay, we need to multiply $15$ by $3.48.$\n\nWe proceed by multiplying the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{} \\\\ \\boxed{2}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{} \\\\ \\boxed{4}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,.4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere are $2$ decimal places in $3.48.$ \nTherefore, the product will also have $2$ decimal places.\n\nSo, we take our value of $5220$ and add a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places.\n\n$$\n52\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,\\underbrace{20}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Carmen must pay $$3.48\\times 15=$52.20.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186445532 :question/worked-solution "First, we group the like terms.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+2b+2a-b & = \\\\ {{a+2a}_{⏟}}_{a\\text{ terms}}+{{2b-b}_{⏟}}_{b\\text{ terms}} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we combine the like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a+2a}_{⏟}+{2b-b}_{⏟} & = \\\\ (1+2)a+(2-1)b & = \\\\ 3a+b\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we group the like terms.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+2b+2a-b & = \\\\ \\underbrace{a+2a}_{a\\text{ terms}}+\\underbrace{2b-b}_{b\\text{ terms}} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we combine the like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{a+2a}+\\underbrace{2b-b} & = \\\\ (1+2)a+(2-1)b & = \\\\ 3a+b\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186445534 :question/worked-solution "We rearrange the terms so that the like terms, $8a$ and $11a$, are together.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8a+3b+11a & = \\\\ {{8a+11a}_{⏟}}_{a\\text{ terms}}+3b & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we combine the like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{8a+11a}_{⏟}\\,+\\,3b & = \\\\ (8+11)a+3b & = \\\\ 19\\,a+3\\,b\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We rearrange the terms so that the like terms, $8a$ and $11a$, are together.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8a+3b+11a & = \\\\ \\underbrace{8a+11a}_{a\\text{ terms}}+3b & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we combine the like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{8a+11a}\\,+\\,3b & = \\\\ (8+11)a+3b & = \\\\ 19\\,a+3\\,b\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186445535 :question/worked-solution "First, we group the like terms.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+4y+5x-2y-4 & = \\\\ {{2x+5x}_{⏟}}_{x\\text{ terms}}+{{4y-2y}_{⏟}}_{y\\text{ terms}}-4 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we combine the like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2x+5x}_{⏟}\\,+\\,{4y-2y}_{⏟}\\,-\\,4 & = \\\\ (2+5)x+(4-2)y-4 & = \\\\ 7\\,x+2\\,y-4 & \\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we group the like terms.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+4y+5x-2y-4 & = \\\\ \\underbrace{2x+5x}_{x\\text{ terms}}+\\underbrace{4y-2y}_{y\\text{ terms}}-4 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we combine the like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{2x+5x}\\,+\\,\\underbrace{4y-2y}\\,-\\,4 & = \\\\ (2+5)x+(4-2)y-4 & = \\\\ 7\\,x+2\\,y-4 & \\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186446984 :question/worked-solution "We must write and evaluate an expression representing the total number of pounds of seeds.\n\nSince each of them bought a pound of canary seed and $3$ pounds of sunflower seeds, the number of pounds each person bought is:\n\n$$\n{{1}_{⏟}}_{\\text{canary seeds}}+{{3}_{⏟}}_{\\text{sunflower seeds}}\n$$\n\nSince *each* person purchased this amount of seeds, and there are $3$ people, we must multiply the number of pounds of seeds by $3.$ Therefore, the total amount of seeds is:\n\n$$\n{{(1+3)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{each person}}\\times {{3}_{⏟}}_{\\text{people}}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the expression, we follow the order of operations with parentheses.\n\nFirst, we perform the operations inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1+3)\\times 3 & =(1+3)\\times 3 \\\\  & =4\\times 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply:\n\n$$\n4\\times 3=12\n$$" "We must write and evaluate an expression representing the total number of pounds of seeds.\n\nSince each of them bought a pound of canary seed and $3$ pounds of sunflower seeds, the number of pounds each person bought is:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{1}_{\\text{canary seeds}}+\\underbrace{3}_{\\text{sunflower seeds}}\n$$\n\nSince *each* person purchased this amount of seeds, and there are $3$ people, we must multiply the number of pounds of seeds by $3.$ Therefore, the total amount of seeds is:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(1+3)}_{\\text{each person}}\\times \\underbrace{3}_{\\text{people}}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the expression, we follow the order of operations with parentheses.\n\nFirst, we perform the operations inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1+3)\\times 3 & =(1+3)\\times 3 \\\\  & =4\\times 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply:\n\n$$\n4\\times 3=12\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186459287 :question/worked-solution "The average value of a function on a given interval $[a,b]$ is \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{avg}}=\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the additive property of the definite integral, we write\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe 1st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,0].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{1}=-4.\n$$\n\nThe 2nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,4].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-6.\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{1}-{A}_{2} \\\\  & =-4-6 \\\\  & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the formula for the average value, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4-(-4)}\\cdot (-10) \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The average value of a function on a given interval $[a,b]$ is \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{avg}}=\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the additive property of the definite integral, we write\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\underbrace{{\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{-{A}_{1}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{-{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe 1st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,0].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{1}=-4.\n$$\n\nThe 2nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,4].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-6.\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{1}-{A}_{2} \\\\  & =-4-6 \\\\  & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the formula for the average value, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4-(-4)}\\cdot (-10) \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186459291 :question/worked-solution "The average value of a function on a given interval $[a,b]$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{avg}}=\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the additive property of the definite integral, we write\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-2}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe 1st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,1].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe 2nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,4].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-3.\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2} \\\\  & =3-3 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the formula for the average value, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4-(-2)}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The average value of a function on a given interval $[a,b]$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{avg}}=\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the additive property of the definite integral, we write\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\underbrace{{\\int }_{-2}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{{A}_{1}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{-{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe 1st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,1].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe 2nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,4].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-3.\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2} \\\\  & =3-3 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the formula for the average value, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4-(-2)}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
