 [:db/cas 17592186475958 :question/worked-solution "To work out $(-1.6)^{3},$ we multiply $-1.6$ by itself and then multiply the result by $-1.6$ once more:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1.6)^{3} & =\\overset{2.56}{\\overset{⏞}{(-1.6)\\cdot (-1.6)}}\\cdot (-1.6) \\\\  & =2.56\\cdot (-1.6) \\\\  & =-4.096\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To work out $(-1.6)^{3},$ we multiply $-1.6$ by itself and then multiply the result by $-1.6$ once more:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1.6)^{3} & =\\overbrace{(-1.6)\\cdot (-1.6)}^{2.56}\\cdot (-1.6) \\\\  & =2.56\\cdot (-1.6) \\\\  & =-4.096\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186475968 :question/worked-solution "To work out $(0.01)^{3},$ we multiply $0.01$ by itself and then multiply the result by $0.01$ once more:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0.01}^{3} & =\\overset{0.000\\,1}{\\overset{⏞}{0.01\\cdot 0.01}}\\cdot 0.01 \\\\  & =0.000\\,1\\cdot 0.01 \\\\  & =0.000\\,001\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To work out $(0.01)^{3},$ we multiply $0.01$ by itself and then multiply the result by $0.01$ once more:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0.01}^{3} & =\\overbrace{0.01\\cdot 0.01}^{0.000\\,1}\\cdot 0.01 \\\\  & =0.000\\,1\\cdot 0.01 \\\\  & =0.000\\,001\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186475977 :question/worked-solution "To work out ${(-4)}^{3},$ we multiply $-4$ by itself and then multiply the result by $-4$ again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4)^{3} & =\\overset{16}{\\overset{⏞}{(-4)\\cdot (-4)}}\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =16\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =-64\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To work out ${(-4)}^{3},$ we multiply $-4$ by itself and then multiply the result by $-4$ again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4)^{3} & =\\overbrace{(-4)\\cdot (-4)}^{16}\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =16\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =-64\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186476920 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${A}^{T}+{B}^{T}$ is a $n\\times m$ matrix.\n\nNow, pre-multiplying a vector $x\\in {R}^{m}$ by ${A}^{T}+{B}^{T}$ gives a linear combination of the columns of ${A}^{T}+{B}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & ⋯ & | \\\\ ({a}^{T}+{b}^{T})_{1} & ({a}^{T}+{b}^{T})_{2} & ⋯ & ({a}^{T}+{b}^{T})_{m} \\\\ | & | & ⋯ & |\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{T}+{B}^{T}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{m}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}={x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ ({a}^{T}+{b}^{T})_{1} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ ({a}^{T}+{b}^{T})_{2} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}+⋯+{x}_{m}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ ({a}^{T}+{b}^{T})_{m} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${R}^{m}$ under the action of ${A}^{T}+{B}^{T}$ must be $\\text{Col}({A}^{T}+{B}^{T}),$ the row space of $A+B.$" "First, notice that ${A}^{T}+{B}^{T}$ is a $n\\times m$ matrix.\n\nNow, pre-multiplying a vector $x\\in {R}^{m}$ by ${A}^{T}+{B}^{T}$ gives a linear combination of the columns of ${A}^{T}+{B}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & ⋯ & | \\\\ ({a}^{T}+{b}^{T})_{1} & ({a}^{T}+{b}^{T})_{2} & ⋯ & ({a}^{T}+{b}^{T})_{m} \\\\ | & | & ⋯ & |\\end{bmatrix}}_{{A}^{T}+{B}^{T}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{m}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}={x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ ({a}^{T}+{b}^{T})_{1} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ ({a}^{T}+{b}^{T})_{2} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}+⋯+{x}_{m}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ ({a}^{T}+{b}^{T})_{m} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${R}^{m}$ under the action of ${A}^{T}+{B}^{T}$ must be $\\text{Col}({A}^{T}+{B}^{T}),$ the row space of $A+B.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186478379 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=2,{u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=2.$" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=2,{u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=2.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186478385 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{32}=-\\frac{1}{2},{u}_{33}=-2,$ and ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=1.$" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{32}=-\\frac{1}{2},{u}_{33}=-2,$ and ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=1.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186478389 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -8 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 15 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 15 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+5{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{31}=-5,{u}_{22}=4,$ and ${l}_{31}\\cdot {u}_{22}=-20.$" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -8 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 15 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 15 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+5{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{31}=-5,{u}_{22}=4,$ and ${l}_{31}\\cdot {u}_{22}=-20.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186478390 :question/worked-solution "We must write and evaluate an expression representing the total number of muffins.\n\nSince each of them bought $3$ blackberry muffins and $5$ strawberry muffins, the number of muffins each of them bought is:\n\n$$\n{{3}_{⏟}}_{\\text{blackberry muffins}}+{{5}_{⏟}}_{\\text{strawberry muffins}}\n$$\n\nSince *each* person bought this number of muffins, and there are $2$ people, we must multiply the number of muffins by $2.$ Therefore, the total number of muffins is:\n\n$$\n{{(3+5)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{each person}}\\times {{2}_{⏟}}_{\\text{people}}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the expression, we follow the order of operations with parentheses.\n\nFirst, we perform the operations inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3+5)\\times 2 & =(3+5)\\times 2 \\\\  & =8\\times 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply:\n\n$$\n8\\times 2=16\n$$" "We must write and evaluate an expression representing the total number of muffins.\n\nSince each of them bought $3$ blackberry muffins and $5$ strawberry muffins, the number of muffins each of them bought is:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{3}_{\\text{blackberry muffins}}+\\underbrace{5}_{\\text{strawberry muffins}}\n$$\n\nSince *each* person bought this number of muffins, and there are $2$ people, we must multiply the number of muffins by $2.$ Therefore, the total number of muffins is:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(3+5)}_{\\text{each person}}\\times \\underbrace{2}_{\\text{people}}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the expression, we follow the order of operations with parentheses.\n\nFirst, we perform the operations inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3+5)\\times 2 & =(3+5)\\times 2 \\\\  & =8\\times 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply:\n\n$$\n8\\times 2=16\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186478391 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -4 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{31}=4,{u}_{23}=3,$ and ${l}_{31}\\cdot {u}_{23}=12.$" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -4 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{31}=4,{u}_{23}=3,$ and ${l}_{31}\\cdot {u}_{23}=12.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186478398 :question/worked-solution "According to PEMDAS, we should first calculate the expression inside the **P**arentheses: $(1+{2}^{3}).$\n\n To evaluate $(1+{2}^{3})$ we should also apply PEMDAS. We start with the **E**xponents:\n\n$$\n1\\,+\\overset{8}{\\overset{⏞}{{2}^{3}}}=1+8\n$$\n\nThen, we perform the **A**ddition:\n\n$$\n1+8=9\n$$\n\nSo we have:\n\n$$\n5\\times {{(1+{2}^{3})}_{⏟}}_{9}=5\\times 9\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we perform the **M**ultiplication:\n\n$$\n5\\times 9=45\n$$" "According to PEMDAS, we should first calculate the expression inside the **P**arentheses: $(1+{2}^{3}).$\n\n To evaluate $(1+{2}^{3})$ we should also apply PEMDAS. We start with the **E**xponents:\n\n$$\n1\\,+\\overbrace{{2}^{3}}^{8}=1+8\n$$\n\nThen, we perform the **A**ddition:\n\n$$\n1+8=9\n$$\n\nSo we have:\n\n$$\n5\\times \\underbrace{(1+{2}^{3})}_{9}=5\\times 9\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we perform the **M**ultiplication:\n\n$$\n5\\times 9=45\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186478403 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -8 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -8 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0