 [:db/cas 17592186478404 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 9 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 9 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 9 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{31}=-\\frac{1}{3},{u}_{33}=2,$ and ${l}_{31}\\cdot {u}_{33}=-\\frac{2}{3}.$" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 9 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 9 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}