\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-3,$ ${u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=-3.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186478421 :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot [y]_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }[\\operatorname{sin}⁡x]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi },\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}].$ So, when $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$ and $y\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}],$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n{{\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}_{⏟}}_{f(x,y)}={{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{X}(x)}\\cdot {{(\\frac{4}{\\pi })}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot [y]_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }[\\operatorname{sin}⁡x]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi },\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}].$ So, when $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$ and $y\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}],$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}_{f(x,y)}=\\underbrace{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}_{{f}_{X}(x)}\\cdot \\underbrace{(\\frac{4}{\\pi })}_{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""]
 [:db/cas 17592186478422 :question/worked-solution "According to PEMDAS, we should first calculate the expression inside the **P**arentheses: $({5}^{2}-1).$\n\n To evaluate $({5}^{2}-1)$ we should also apply PEMDAS. We start with the **E**xponents:\n\n$$\n\\overset{25}{\\overset{⏞}{{5}^{2}}}-1=25-1\n$$\n\nThen, we perform the **S**ubtraction:\n\n$$\n25-1=24\n$$\n\nSo we have:\n\n$$\n{{({5}^{2}-1)}_{⏟}}_{24}\\,÷8=24÷\\,8\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we perform the **D**ivision:\n\n$$\n24÷8=3\n$$" "According to PEMDAS, we should first calculate the expression inside the **P**arentheses: $({5}^{2}-1).$\n\n To evaluate $({5}^{2}-1)$ we should also apply PEMDAS. We start with the **E**xponents:\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{{5}^{2}}^{25}-1=25-1\n$$\n\nThen, we perform the **S**ubtraction:\n\n$$\n25-1=24\n$$\n\nSo we have:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{({5}^{2}-1)}_{24}\\,÷8=24÷\\,8\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we perform the **D**ivision:\n\n$$\n24÷8=3\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186478426 :question/worked-solution "We use PEMDAS. We start with the **P**arentheses:\n\n$$\n{{(1+5)}_{⏟}}_{6}{}^{2}+{4}^{3}={6}^{2}+{4}^{3}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we find the **E**xponents:\n\n$$\n\\overset{36}{\\overset{⏞}{{6}^{2}}}+\\overset{64}{\\overset{⏞}{{4}^{3}}}=36+64\n$$\n\nFinally, we perform the **A**ddition:\n\n$$\n36+64=100\n$$" "We use PEMDAS. We start with the **P**arentheses:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(1+5)}_{6}{}^{2}+{4}^{3}={6}^{2}+{4}^{3}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we find the **E**xponents:\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{{6}^{2}}^{36}+\\overbrace{{4}^{3}}^{64}=36+64\n$$\n\nFinally, we perform the **A**ddition:\n\n$$\n36+64=100\n$$"]
