 [:db/cas 17592186479088 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly &=b \\\\ Ux &=y.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=-2 \\\\ 7{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=-9.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${y}_{1}=-2$ and ${y}_{2}=5.$ \n\nTherefore, $y=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.$" "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,\\underbrace{(LU)}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL\\underbrace{(Ux)}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly &=b \\\\ Ux &=y.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=-2 \\\\ 7{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=-9.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${y}_{1}=-2$ and ${y}_{2}=5.$ \n\nTherefore, $y=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186479090 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly &=b \\\\ Ux &=y.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=6 \\\\ -{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=-2 \\\\ {y}_{1}-{y}_{2}+{y}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the solution to this system using forward substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =6, \\\\ {y}_{2} & =-2+{y}_{1} \\\\  & =-2+6 \\\\  & =4, \\\\ {y}_{3} & =-{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} \\\\  & =-(6)+4 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$y=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$" "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,\\underbrace{(LU)}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL\\underbrace{(Ux)}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly &=b \\\\ Ux &=y.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=6 \\\\ -{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=-2 \\\\ {y}_{1}-{y}_{2}+{y}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the solution to this system using forward substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =6, \\\\ {y}_{2} & =-2+{y}_{1} \\\\  & =-2+6 \\\\  & =4, \\\\ {y}_{3} & =-{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} \\\\  & =-(6)+4 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$y=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186479095 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly &=b \\\\ Ux &=y.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=-4 \\\\ 2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=-5 \\\\ -4{y}_{1}-3{y}_{2}+{y}_{3} &=9.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the solution to this system using forward substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =-4, \\\\ {y}_{2} & =-5-2{y}_{1} \\\\  & =-5-2(-4) \\\\  & =3, \\\\ {y}_{3} & =9+4{y}_{1}+3{y}_{2} \\\\  & =9+4(-4)+3(3) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$y=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$" "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,\\underbrace{(LU)}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL\\underbrace{(Ux)}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly &=b \\\\ Ux &=y.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=-4 \\\\ 2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=-5 \\\\ -4{y}_{1}-3{y}_{2}+{y}_{3} &=9.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the solution to this system using forward substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =-4, \\\\ {y}_{2} & =-5-2{y}_{1} \\\\  & =-5-2(-4) \\\\  & =3, \\\\ {y}_{3} & =9+4{y}_{1}+3{y}_{2} \\\\  & =9+4(-4)+3(3) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$y=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$"]
