 [:db/cas 17592186496303 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following facts about the \"and\" $(∧)$ and \"or\" $(∨)$ logical connectives:\n\n- The statement $A∧B$ is true if *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\n- The statement $A∨B$ is true if either $A$ is true, or $B$ is true, or *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\nIn our case, $P$ is true, $Q$ is true, and $R$ is false. Therefore, $P∨Q$ is true, but the result is false:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(P∨Q)∧R & ≡\\overset{\\text{true}}{\\overset{⏞}{(\\text{true}∨\\text{true})}}∧\\text{false} \\\\  & ≡\\text{true}∧\\text{false} \\\\  & ≡\\text{false}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the following facts about the \"and\" $(∧)$ and \"or\" $(∨)$ logical connectives:\n\n- The statement $A∧B$ is true if *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\n- The statement $A∨B$ is true if either $A$ is true, or $B$ is true, or *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\nIn our case, $P$ is true, $Q$ is true, and $R$ is false. Therefore, $P∨Q$ is true, but the result is false:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(P∨Q)∧R & ≡\\overbrace{(\\text{true}∨\\text{true})}^{\\text{true}}∧\\text{false} \\\\  & ≡\\text{true}∧\\text{false} \\\\  & ≡\\text{false}\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
