 [:db/cas 17592186512943 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t)+g(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $g(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that \n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince the initial conditions are given at $t={t}_{0}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})} & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{2(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2(t-s)}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{2(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2(t-s)}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2(t-s)} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2(t-s)} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2(t-s)} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{s=0}^{s=t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{2t}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{2t}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t)+g(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $g(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\underbrace{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that \n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince the initial conditions are given at $t={t}_{0}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})} & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{2(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2(t-s)}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{2(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2(t-s)}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2(t-s)} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2(t-s)} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2(t-s)} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{s=0}^{s=t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{2t}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{2t}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186512945 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t)+g(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $g(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that \n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & t{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince the initial conditions are given at $t={t}_{0}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})} & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & t{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{-(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-(t-s)}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{-(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-(t-s)}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(t-s){e}^{s-t} \\\\ {e}^{s-t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}(t-s){e}^{s-t} \\\\ {e}^{s-t}\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}(-s+t+1){e}^{s-t} \\\\ {e}^{s-t}\\end{aligned}]}_{s=0}^{s=t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1-(t+1){e}^{-t} \\\\ 1-{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1-(t+1){e}^{-t} \\\\ 1-{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1-{e}^{-t}-t{e}^{-t} \\\\ 1-{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t)+g(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $g(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\underbrace{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that \n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & t{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince the initial conditions are given at $t={t}_{0}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})} & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & t{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{-(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-(t-s)}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{-(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-(t-s)}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(t-s){e}^{s-t} \\\\ {e}^{s-t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}(t-s){e}^{s-t} \\\\ {e}^{s-t}\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}(-s+t+1){e}^{s-t} \\\\ {e}^{s-t}\\end{aligned}]}_{s=0}^{s=t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1-(t+1){e}^{-t} \\\\ 1-{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1-(t+1){e}^{-t} \\\\ 1-{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1-{e}^{-t}-t{e}^{-t} \\\\ 1-{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186514417 :question/worked-solution "Splitting the fraction into separate terms and then simplifying the fractions in the individual terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8x-12y}{4} & = \\\\ \\frac{8x}{4}-\\frac{12y}{4} & = \\\\ \\frac{8}{4}\\cdot x-\\frac{12}{4}\\cdot y & = \\\\ 2x-3y & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above in the form $ax+by,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{(2)}_{⏟}}_{a}x+{{(-3)}_{⏟}}_{b}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=2$ and $b=-3.$" "Splitting the fraction into separate terms and then simplifying the fractions in the individual terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8x-12y}{4} & = \\\\ \\frac{8x}{4}-\\frac{12y}{4} & = \\\\ \\frac{8}{4}\\cdot x-\\frac{12}{4}\\cdot y & = \\\\ 2x-3y & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above in the form $ax+by,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{(2)}_{a}x+\\underbrace{(-3)}_{b}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=2$ and $b=-3.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186514470 :question/worked-solution "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a+4 & 5 \\\\ b+2 & -3\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}(a+5)-1 & 5 \\\\ b+2 & -3\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}(a+5)-1 & 5 \\\\ b+2 & -3\\end{aligned}| & ={{|\\begin{aligned}a+5 & 5 \\\\ b & -3\\end{aligned}|}_{⏟}}_{-34}+|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-34+(3-10) \\\\  & =-41.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a+4 & 5 \\\\ b+2 & -3\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}(a+5)-1 & 5 \\\\ b+2 & -3\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}(a+5)-1 & 5 \\\\ b+2 & -3\\end{aligned}| & =\\underbrace{|\\begin{aligned}a+5 & 5 \\\\ b & -3\\end{aligned}|}_{-34}+|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-34+(3-10) \\\\  & =-41.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186515518 :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)={x}^{2}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{csch}⁡4x.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={({x}^{2})}^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{csch}⁡4x+{x}^{2}\\cdot {(\\operatorname{csch}⁡4x)}^{′} \\\\  & =2x\\cdot \\operatorname{csch}⁡4x+{x}^{2}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{csch}⁡4x\\operatorname{coth}⁡4x) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{csch}⁡4x-4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{csch}⁡4x\\operatorname{coth}⁡4x \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{csch}⁡4x\\cdot {{(1-2x\\operatorname{coth}⁡4x)}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=1-2x\\operatorname{coth}⁡4x.$" "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)={x}^{2}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{csch}⁡4x.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={({x}^{2})}^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{csch}⁡4x+{x}^{2}\\cdot {(\\operatorname{csch}⁡4x)}^{′} \\\\  & =2x\\cdot \\operatorname{csch}⁡4x+{x}^{2}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{csch}⁡4x\\operatorname{coth}⁡4x) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{csch}⁡4x-4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{csch}⁡4x\\operatorname{coth}⁡4x \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{csch}⁡4x\\cdot \\underbrace{(1-2x\\operatorname{coth}⁡4x)}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=1-2x\\operatorname{coth}⁡4x.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186515519 :question/worked-solution "The given points are ${{(-5,3)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1},\\,{y}_{1})},$ ${{(a,b)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2},\\,{y}_{2})}$ and ${{(1,4)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{m},\\,{y}_{m})}\\,.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\ (1,4) & =(\\frac{-5+a}{2},\\frac{3+b}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding $x$- and $y$-coordinates in the equation above gives us\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=\\frac{-5+a}{2} \\\\ 4 &=\\frac{3+b}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}a &=7 \\\\ b &=5.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $(a,b)=(7,5)$ and $a+b=12.$" "The given points are $\\underbrace{(-5,3)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1},\\,{y}_{1})},$ $\\underbrace{(a,b)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2},\\,{y}_{2})}$ and $\\underbrace{(1,4)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{m},\\,{y}_{m})}\\,.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\ (1,4) & =(\\frac{-5+a}{2},\\frac{3+b}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding $x$- and $y$-coordinates in the equation above gives us\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=\\frac{-5+a}{2} \\\\ 4 &=\\frac{3+b}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}a &=7 \\\\ b &=5.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $(a,b)=(7,5)$ and $a+b=12.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186515559 :question/worked-solution "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=\\operatorname{csch}⁡x$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\operatorname{csch}⁡x)}^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′}}{{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{x})}{{ln}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\overset{g(x)}{\\overset{⏞}{\\operatorname{csch}⁡x(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+1)}}}{x{ln}^{2}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=\\operatorname{csch}⁡x(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+1).$" "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=\\operatorname{csch}⁡x$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\operatorname{csch}⁡x)}^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′}}{{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{x})}{{ln}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\overbrace{\\operatorname{csch}⁡x(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+1)}^{g(x)}}{x{ln}^{2}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=\\operatorname{csch}⁡x(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+1).$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186515569 :question/worked-solution "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=\\operatorname{sech}⁡x$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\operatorname{sech}⁡x)}^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\cdot {(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}^{′}}{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\overset{g(x)}{\\overset{⏞}{\\operatorname{sech}⁡x(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}}}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=\\operatorname{sech}⁡x(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).$" "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=\\operatorname{sech}⁡x$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\operatorname{sech}⁡x)}^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\cdot {(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}^{′}}{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\overbrace{\\operatorname{sech}⁡x(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{g(x)}}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=\\operatorname{sech}⁡x(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186515597 :question/worked-solution "The given points are ${{(a,b)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1},\\,{y}_{1})}$, ${{(0,1)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2},\\,{y}_{2})}$ and ${{(-3,3)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{m},\\,{y}_{m})}.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\ (-3,3) & =(\\frac{a+0}{2},\\frac{b+1}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding $x$- and $y$-coordinates in the equation above gives us\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3 &=\\frac{a}{2} \\\\ 3 &=\\frac{b+1}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}a &=-6 \\\\ b &=5.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $a=-6$ and $b=5.$" "The given points are $\\underbrace{(a,b)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1},\\,{y}_{1})}$, $\\underbrace{(0,1)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2},\\,{y}_{2})}$ and $\\underbrace{(-3,3)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{m},\\,{y}_{m})}.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\ (-3,3) & =(\\frac{a+0}{2},\\frac{b+1}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding $x$- and $y$-coordinates in the equation above gives us\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3 &=\\frac{a}{2} \\\\ 3 &=\\frac{b+1}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}a &=-6 \\\\ b &=5.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $a=-6$ and $b=5.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186518123 :question/worked-solution "From the fundamental theorem of calculus, we find that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =f(6)-f(-3) \\\\ {\\int }_{-3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =f(6)-(-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}) \\\\ f(6) & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}+{\\int }_{-3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ on $[-3,6]$ consists of two parts: \n\n* an arc of a quarter circle for $x\\in [-3,3],$ which lies above the $x$-axis, and \n\n* a line segment for $x\\in [3,6],$ which lies below the $x$-axis. \n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe first integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,3].$ Earlier, we found that\n\n$$\n{A}_{1}=\\frac{9\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe second integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [3,6].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}ab \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of the original integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{2}-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can find $f(6):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(6) & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}+{\\int }_{-3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}+(\\frac{9\\pi }{2}-6) \\\\  & =2\\pi -6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "From the fundamental theorem of calculus, we find that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =f(6)-f(-3) \\\\ {\\int }_{-3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =f(6)-(-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}) \\\\ f(6) & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}+{\\int }_{-3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ on $[-3,6]$ consists of two parts: \n\n* an arc of a quarter circle for $x\\in [-3,3],$ which lies above the $x$-axis, and \n\n* a line segment for $x\\in [3,6],$ which lies below the $x$-axis. \n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\underbrace{{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{{A}_{1}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{-{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe first integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,3].$ Earlier, we found that\n\n$$\n{A}_{1}=\\frac{9\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe second integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [3,6].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}ab \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of the original integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{2}-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can find $f(6):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(6) & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}+{\\int }_{-3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}+(\\frac{9\\pi }{2}-6) \\\\  & =2\\pi -6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186519537 :question/worked-solution "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{3,6,9,12,15\\}\\times \\{2,4,6,8,10\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support.\n\n![](images/71/715408bbd41d8551b8eadce9df6259f763ec34a702d48f4d116c4164ae5fda50.png)\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/5c/5c50878b85d26a87d6220a99f6fbe896925a6802801117239a5ba56dcba46906.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(12,6).$\n\n2\\. Subtract the infinite rectangles that have their top-right corners at $(12,4)$ and $(6,6).$\n\n3\\. Finally, we need to add back the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(6,4).$ This is because we subtracted this region twice in step 2.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(6<X\\le 12,4<Y\\le 6) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(12,6)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 12,Y\\le 6)}} \\\\  & \\,-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(12,4)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 12,Y\\le 4)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(6,6)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 6,Y\\le 6)}} \\\\  & \\,+\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(6,4)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 6,Y\\le 4)}} \\\\  & =F(12,6)-F(12,4)-F(6,6)+F(6,4) \\\\  & =0.81-0.3-0.17+0.1 \\\\  & =0.44.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{3,6,9,12,15\\}\\times \\{2,4,6,8,10\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support.\n\n![](images/71/715408bbd41d8551b8eadce9df6259f763ec34a702d48f4d116c4164ae5fda50.png)\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/5c/5c50878b85d26a87d6220a99f6fbe896925a6802801117239a5ba56dcba46906.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(12,6).$\n\n2\\. Subtract the infinite rectangles that have their top-right corners at $(12,4)$ and $(6,6).$\n\n3\\. Finally, we need to add back the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(6,4).$ This is because we subtracted this region twice in step 2.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(6<X\\le 12,4<Y\\le 6) & =\\overbrace{P(X\\le 12,Y\\le 6)}^{\\text{top-right corner at }(12,6)} \\\\  & \\,-\\overbrace{P(X\\le 12,Y\\le 4)}^{\\text{top-right corner at }(12,4)}-\\overbrace{P(X\\le 6,Y\\le 6)}^{\\text{top-right corner at }(6,6)} \\\\  & \\,+\\overbrace{P(X\\le 6,Y\\le 4)}^{\\text{top-right corner at }(6,4)} \\\\  & =F(12,6)-F(12,4)-F(6,6)+F(6,4) \\\\  & =0.81-0.3-0.17+0.1 \\\\  & =0.44.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186519541 :question/worked-solution "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{1,3,5,7,9\\}\\times \\{2,4,6,8,10\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support.\n\n![](images/9e/9e7509e29912cc54dde9a86786f1d171a49b89e7b5f7305ef025582b3402350a.png)\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/78/78a130ca07be1acecc4655a557abb8d3d037da28f143d0696272d12898b6b4cd.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(7,10)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(7,4)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](images/e4/e4ff1e7cd8259fa9bcaae75210a7a3f2aeeb547ceba46b2fb411f94133372fa2.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 7,4<Y\\le 10) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(7,10)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 7,Y\\le 10)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(7,4)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 7,Y\\le 4)}} \\\\  & =F(7,10)-F(7,4) \\\\  & =0.82-0.11 \\\\  & =0.71.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{1,3,5,7,9\\}\\times \\{2,4,6,8,10\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support.\n\n![](images/9e/9e7509e29912cc54dde9a86786f1d171a49b89e7b5f7305ef025582b3402350a.png)\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/78/78a130ca07be1acecc4655a557abb8d3d037da28f143d0696272d12898b6b4cd.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(7,10)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(7,4)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](images/e4/e4ff1e7cd8259fa9bcaae75210a7a3f2aeeb547ceba46b2fb411f94133372fa2.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 7,4<Y\\le 10) & =\\overbrace{P(X\\le 7,Y\\le 10)}^{\\text{top-right corner at }(7,10)}-\\overbrace{P(X\\le 7,Y\\le 4)}^{\\text{top-right corner at }(7,4)} \\\\  & =F(7,10)-F(7,4) \\\\  & =0.82-0.11 \\\\  & =0.71.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186519599 :question/worked-solution "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{3,6,9,12\\}\\times \\{2,4,6,8\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's plot the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support, and sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/a7/a782578ef55726c98ed23d89cf3de531ede60fe5db3fa9062c05507c5c2f60af.png)\n\nNotice that this region encompasses the same possible support points as the infinite rectangle with its top-right corner at $(12,4)$ and top-left corner at $(3,4),$ where the top and right boundaries are included, and the left boundary is excluded.\n\n![](images/7b/7ba99957a743b5cf283c2668e81b6c1aeb06730d47fc157aec90fe8bc880ced4.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(12,4)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(3,4)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](images/3b/3bd652b8d918e26cd330ad43555f039845d0b5405b70c96828bb2d652b7f872c.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 6,Y\\le 5) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(12,4)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 12,Y\\le 4)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(3,4)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 3,Y\\le 4)}} \\\\  & =F(12,4)-F(3,4) \\\\  & =0.6-0.15 \\\\  & =0.45.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{3,6,9,12\\}\\times \\{2,4,6,8\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's plot the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support, and sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/a7/a782578ef55726c98ed23d89cf3de531ede60fe5db3fa9062c05507c5c2f60af.png)\n\nNotice that this region encompasses the same possible support points as the infinite rectangle with its top-right corner at $(12,4)$ and top-left corner at $(3,4),$ where the top and right boundaries are included, and the left boundary is excluded.\n\n![](images/7b/7ba99957a743b5cf283c2668e81b6c1aeb06730d47fc157aec90fe8bc880ced4.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(12,4)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(3,4)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](images/3b/3bd652b8d918e26cd330ad43555f039845d0b5405b70c96828bb2d652b7f872c.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 6,Y\\le 5) & =\\overbrace{P(X\\le 12,Y\\le 4)}^{\\text{top-right corner at }(12,4)}-\\overbrace{P(X\\le 3,Y\\le 4)}^{\\text{top-right corner at }(3,4)} \\\\  & =F(12,4)-F(3,4) \\\\  & =0.6-0.15 \\\\  & =0.45.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186520486 :question/worked-solution "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{x+{e}^{y}}, & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{{e}^{y}}{x+{e}^{y}} \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =1, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{(x+{e}^{y})^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =-\\frac{{e}^{y}}{(x+{e}^{y})^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =\\frac{x{e}^{y}}{(x+{e}^{y})^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈0+1\\cdot x+1\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(-1\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(-1)\\cdot xy+0\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1x+1y-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+(-1)xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=1+1+(-1)=1.\n$$" "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overbrace{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}^{\\text{Linear approximation}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+\\underbrace{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{x+{e}^{y}}, & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{{e}^{y}}{x+{e}^{y}} \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =1, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{(x+{e}^{y})^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =-\\frac{{e}^{y}}{(x+{e}^{y})^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =\\frac{x{e}^{y}}{(x+{e}^{y})^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈0+1\\cdot x+1\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(-1\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(-1)\\cdot xy+0\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1x+1y-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+(-1)xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=1+1+(-1)=1.\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186520510 :question/worked-solution "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+y+4}}, & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+y+4}} \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =\\frac{1}{4}, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{(x+y+4)^{3}}}, & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =-\\frac{1}{32} \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{(x+y+4)^{3}}}, & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =-\\frac{1}{32} \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{(x+y+4)^{3}}}, & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =-\\frac{1}{32}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈2+\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot x+\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{32}\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(-\\frac{1}{32})\\cdot xy+(-\\frac{1}{32})\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2+\\frac{1}{4}x+\\frac{1}{4}y-\\frac{1}{64}{x}^{2}+(-\\frac{1}{32})xy+(-\\frac{1}{64}){y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=\\frac{1}{4}+(-\\frac{1}{32})+(-\\frac{1}{64})=\\frac{13}{64}.\n$$" "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overbrace{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}^{\\text{Linear approximation}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+\\underbrace{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+y+4}}, & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+y+4}} \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =\\frac{1}{4}, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{(x+y+4)^{3}}}, & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =-\\frac{1}{32} \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{(x+y+4)^{3}}}, & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =-\\frac{1}{32} \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{(x+y+4)^{3}}}, & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =-\\frac{1}{32}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈2+\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot x+\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{32}\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(-\\frac{1}{32})\\cdot xy+(-\\frac{1}{32})\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2+\\frac{1}{4}x+\\frac{1}{4}y-\\frac{1}{64}{x}^{2}+(-\\frac{1}{32})xy+(-\\frac{1}{64}){y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=\\frac{1}{4}+(-\\frac{1}{32})+(-\\frac{1}{64})=\\frac{13}{64}.\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186521469 :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the boundary of our infinite vertical column. One such surface is our paraboloid $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨y,\\,x,\\,2⟩{|}_{z={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨y,\\,x,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{{y}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{x}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{2}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y\\cdot (2x)-x\\cdot (-2y)+2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/5f/5f2f4cb03b3b0232c9debd1f4ed64bd683fe788ccba16cfbe395d9c3b629972d.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\,2\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the boundary of our infinite vertical column. One such surface is our paraboloid $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨y,\\,x,\\,2⟩{|}_{z={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨y,\\,x,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{y}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{x}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{2}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y\\cdot (2x)-x\\cdot (-2y)+2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/5f/5f2f4cb03b3b0232c9debd1f4ed64bd683fe788ccba16cfbe395d9c3b629972d.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\,2\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186523930 :question/worked-solution "From the figure, we note that the points $A$ and $B$ have coordinates ${{(-2,3)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(2,7)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})},$ respectively.\n\nTo find the length of $\\overset{―}{AB},$ we use the distance formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2-(-2))^{2}+(7-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{32} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "From the figure, we note that the points $A$ and $B$ have coordinates $\\underbrace{(-2,3)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and $\\underbrace{(2,7)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})},$ respectively.\n\nTo find the length of $\\overset{―}{AB},$ we use the distance formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2-(-2))^{2}+(7-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{32} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186523944 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the probability that the pair $(X,Y)$ will lie inside the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/13/137e3d2e3344f5d1f57f36d470564a518c359bb46d5278382b7242e73999e80e.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(5,4).$\n\n2\\. Subtract the infinite rectangles that have their top-right corners at $(5,1)$ and $(4,4).$\n\n3\\. Finally, we need to *add back* the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(4,1).$ This is because we subtracted this region twice in step 2.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(4\\le X\\le 5,1\\le Y\\le 4) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(5,4)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 5,Y\\le 4)}} \\\\  & \\,-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(5,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 5,Y\\le 1)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(4,4)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 4,Y\\le 4)}} \\\\  & \\,+\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(4,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 4,Y\\le 1)}} \\\\  & =F(5,4)-F(5,1)-F(4,4)+F(4,1) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{2}{9}-\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{9}-\\frac{2}{9}-\\frac{3}{9}+\\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the probability that the pair $(X,Y)$ will lie inside the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/13/137e3d2e3344f5d1f57f36d470564a518c359bb46d5278382b7242e73999e80e.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(5,4).$\n\n2\\. Subtract the infinite rectangles that have their top-right corners at $(5,1)$ and $(4,4).$\n\n3\\. Finally, we need to *add back* the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(4,1).$ This is because we subtracted this region twice in step 2.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(4\\le X\\le 5,1\\le Y\\le 4) & =\\overbrace{P(X\\le 5,Y\\le 4)}^{\\text{top-right corner at }(5,4)} \\\\  & \\,-\\overbrace{P(X\\le 5,Y\\le 1)}^{\\text{top-right corner at }(5,1)}-\\overbrace{P(X\\le 4,Y\\le 4)}^{\\text{top-right corner at }(4,4)} \\\\  & \\,+\\overbrace{P(X\\le 4,Y\\le 1)}^{\\text{top-right corner at }(4,1)} \\\\  & =F(5,4)-F(5,1)-F(4,4)+F(4,1) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{2}{9}-\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{9}-\\frac{2}{9}-\\frac{3}{9}+\\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186523947 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the probability that the pair $(X,Y)$ will lie inside the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/03/0378964a9f167554e4faeba228ebae485de94dda83fbd6704a864a06b8abe77a.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(1,5)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(-2,5)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](images/90/9079baa6464df773cacb820a6bb4a8e2b8f4ecc19755619a9b5c191a0c48507f.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-2\\le X\\le 1,Y\\le 5) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(1,5)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 1,Y\\le 5)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(-2,5)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le -2,Y\\le 5)}} \\\\  & =F(1,5)-F(-2,5) \\\\  & =0.63-0.27 \\\\  & =0.36.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the probability that the pair $(X,Y)$ will lie inside the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/03/0378964a9f167554e4faeba228ebae485de94dda83fbd6704a864a06b8abe77a.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(1,5)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(-2,5)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](images/90/9079baa6464df773cacb820a6bb4a8e2b8f4ecc19755619a9b5c191a0c48507f.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-2\\le X\\le 1,Y\\le 5) & =\\overbrace{P(X\\le 1,Y\\le 5)}^{\\text{top-right corner at }(1,5)}-\\overbrace{P(X\\le -2,Y\\le 5)}^{\\text{top-right corner at }(-2,5)} \\\\  & =F(1,5)-F(-2,5) \\\\  & =0.63-0.27 \\\\  & =0.36.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186523992 :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have ${{(-4\\sqrt{5},-2\\sqrt{5})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(\\sqrt{5},-12\\sqrt{5})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})}.$\nSo, the distance $d$ can be calculated using the distance formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{5}-(-4\\sqrt{5}))}^{2}+{(-12\\sqrt{5}-(-2\\sqrt{5}))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(5\\sqrt{5})^{2}+(-10\\sqrt{5})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25(5)+100(5)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{125+500} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{625} \\\\  & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$" "In this case, we have $\\underbrace{(-4\\sqrt{5},-2\\sqrt{5})}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and $\\underbrace{(\\sqrt{5},-12\\sqrt{5})}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})}.$\nSo, the distance $d$ can be calculated using the distance formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{5}-(-4\\sqrt{5}))}^{2}+{(-12\\sqrt{5}-(-2\\sqrt{5}))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(5\\sqrt{5})^{2}+(-10\\sqrt{5})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25(5)+100(5)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{125+500} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{625} \\\\  & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186524026 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the probability that the pair $(X,Y)$ will lie inside the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/d3/d31cdb5e3f201d9dd5b3b622d883f67bb2c8bc0a5036905695a5cc5ceb3bda07.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(8,8)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(8,4)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](images/62/626d5c4cba7b41d6b6f2da77ca1d62c5bcd442990ce31cf630e69882dd6580b8.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 8,4\\le Y\\le 8) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(8,8)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 8,Y\\le 8)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(8,4)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 8,Y\\le 4)}} \\\\  & =F(8,8)-F(8,4) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the probability that the pair $(X,Y)$ will lie inside the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/d3/d31cdb5e3f201d9dd5b3b622d883f67bb2c8bc0a5036905695a5cc5ceb3bda07.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(8,8)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(8,4)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](images/62/626d5c4cba7b41d6b6f2da77ca1d62c5bcd442990ce31cf630e69882dd6580b8.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 8,4\\le Y\\le 8) & =\\overbrace{P(X\\le 8,Y\\le 8)}^{\\text{top-right corner at }(8,8)}-\\overbrace{P(X\\le 8,Y\\le 4)}^{\\text{top-right corner at }(8,4)} \\\\  & =F(8,8)-F(8,4) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186524958 :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}y{[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y\\in [1,2].$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3}(2-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =4{x}^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x\\in [0,1].$ So, when $x\\in [0,1]$ and $y\\in [1,2],$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n{{\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3}y}_{⏟}}_{f(x,y)}={{(4{x}^{3})}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{X}(x)}\\cdot {{(\\frac{2}{3}y)}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"" "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}y{[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y\\in [1,2].$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3}(2-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =4{x}^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x\\in [0,1].$ So, when $x\\in [0,1]$ and $y\\in [1,2],$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3}y}_{f(x,y)}=\\underbrace{(4{x}^{3})}_{{f}_{X}(x)}\\cdot \\underbrace{(\\frac{2}{3}y)}_{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\""]
 [:db/cas 17592186524962 :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{4}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+yx]}_{x=1}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(2+2y-\\frac{1}{2}-y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(y+\\frac{3}{2}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y\\in [2,3].$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{4}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[xy+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=2}^{y=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(3x+\\frac{9}{2}-2x-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+\\frac{5}{2}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x\\in [1,2].$ So, when $x\\in [1,2]$ and $y\\in [2,3],$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n{{\\frac{1}{4}(x+y)}_{⏟}}_{f(x,y)}\\ne {{\\frac{1}{4}(x+\\frac{5}{2})}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{X}(x)}\\cdot {{\\frac{1}{4}(y+\\frac{3}{2})}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is *not* true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{4}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+yx]}_{x=1}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(2+2y-\\frac{1}{2}-y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(y+\\frac{3}{2}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y\\in [2,3].$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{4}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[xy+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=2}^{y=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(3x+\\frac{9}{2}-2x-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+\\frac{5}{2}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x\\in [1,2].$ So, when $x\\in [1,2]$ and $y\\in [2,3],$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\frac{1}{4}(x+y)}_{f(x,y)}\\ne \\underbrace{\\frac{1}{4}(x+\\frac{5}{2})}_{{f}_{X}(x)}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\frac{1}{4}(y+\\frac{3}{2})}_{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is *not* true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""]
 [:db/cas 17592186525015 :question/worked-solution "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x.$\n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x)^{′}}{{arcoth}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x}{{arcoth}^{2}⁡x}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x)^{′}}{{arcoth}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}}{\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x)^{′}}{{arcoth}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{arcoth}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x}+{{(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{(1-{x}^{2}){arcoth}^{2}⁡x})}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{(1-{x}^{2}){arcoth}^{2}⁡x}.$" "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x.$\n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x)^{′}}{{arcoth}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x}{{arcoth}^{2}⁡x}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x)^{′}}{{arcoth}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}}{\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x)^{′}}{{arcoth}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{arcoth}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x}+\\underbrace{(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{(1-{x}^{2}){arcoth}^{2}⁡x})}_{g(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{(1-{x}^{2}){arcoth}^{2}⁡x}.$"]
