{field-7}} |  |  |\n\nFinally, normalizing orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n                ${u}_{2}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                {{field-8}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-9}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n                ${u}_{3}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                {{field-10}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-11}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{30} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$  select the correct options in the computations of the matrix $U$ below.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{30}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\n\nNow, we compute the $1$st column of $U.$ It is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${u}_{1}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n|  |  | $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}$ |  |  |\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal.\n\nHence, we must find vectors $u=[{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}]^{T}$ such that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nChoosing ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ as free variables, the general solution is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | {{field-5}} |  |  |\n|  |  | ${x}_{3}$ |  |  |\n\nthat we can rewrite as:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{{b}_{1}}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{{b}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nNext, we orthogonalize ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process to get the corresponding orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nLet ${c}_{1}={b}_{1},$ then:\n\n|  |  | {{field-6}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${c}_{2}=\\frac{2}{5}\\,\\,$ |  | $1$ |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-7}} |  |  |\n\nFinally, normalizing orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n                ${u}_{2}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                {{field-8}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-9}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n                ${u}_{3}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                {{field-10}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-11}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"]
 [:db/cas 17592186525985 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{30}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}}[\\begin{aligned}-2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ -\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}{x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,-2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables. \n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are *not* mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we have to orthogonalize them using the Gram-Schmidt process. So, let \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}={b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & ={b}_{2}-\\frac{{b}_{2}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{{c}_{1}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{c}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{(-1)}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{2}{5}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, normalizing orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & 0 & \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{30}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}}[\\begin{aligned}-2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ -\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}{x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,-2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{{b}_{1}}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables. \n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are *not* mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we have to orthogonalize them using the Gram-Schmidt process. So, let \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}={b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & ={b}_{2}-\\frac{{b}_{2}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{{c}_{1}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{c}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{(-1)}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{2}{5}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, normalizing orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & 0 & \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186527148 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det({f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since\n${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2x{y}^{2}-2x & 2y{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}],$\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x{y}^{2}-2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x{y}^{2}-2x)=2{y}^{2}-2\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y{x}^{2})=4xy \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{y}^{2}-2x)=4xy\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y{x}^{2})=2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}-2 & 4xy \\\\ 4xy & 2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,-1)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(1,-1)=0\\cdot 2-(-4)\\cdot (-4)=-16.\n$$" "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det({f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since\n${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2x{y}^{2}-2x & 2y{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}],$\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x{y}^{2}-2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x{y}^{2}-2x)=2{y}^{2}-2\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y{x}^{2})=4xy \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{y}^{2}-2x)=4xy\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y{x}^{2})=2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}-2 & 4xy \\\\ 4xy & 2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,-1)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(1,-1)=0\\cdot 2-(-4)\\cdot (-4)=-16.\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186527179 :question/worked-solution "We need to calculate the product of $7.3$ with itself:\n\n$$\n{7.3}^{2}=(7.3)\\cdot (7.3)\n$$\n\nFirst, we ignore the decimal points and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{2} \\\\ \\boxed{}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $73\\times 73=5\\,329.$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in the first factor ($7.3$) and $1$ decimal place in the second factor ($7.3$), so their product will have $1+1=2$ decimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $5\\,329$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n53\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,{{29}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${7.3}^{2}=53.29\\,.$" "We need to calculate the product of $7.3$ with itself:\n\n$$\n{7.3}^{2}=(7.3)\\cdot (7.3)\n$$\n\nFirst, we ignore the decimal points and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{2} \\\\ \\boxed{}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $73\\times 73=5\\,329.$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in the first factor ($7.3$) and $1$ decimal place in the second factor ($7.3$), so their product will have $1+1=2$ decimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $5\\,329$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n53\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,\\underbrace{29}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${7.3}^{2}=53.29\\,.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186527182 :question/worked-solution "We need to calculate the product of $(-4.5)$ with itself:\n\n$$\n(-4.5)^{2}=(-4.5)\\cdot (-4.5)=(4.5)\\cdot (4.5)\n$$\n\nFirst, we ignore the decimal points and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{2} \\\\ \\boxed{2}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $45\\times 45=2\\,025.$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in the first factor ($4.5$) and $1$ decimal place in the second factor ($4.5$), so their product will have $1+1=2$ decimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $2\\,025$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n20\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,{{25}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $(-4.5)^{2}=20.25\\,.$" "We need to calculate the product of $(-4.5)$ with itself:\n\n$$\n(-4.5)^{2}=(-4.5)\\cdot (-4.5)=(4.5)\\cdot (4.5)\n$$\n\nFirst, we ignore the decimal points and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{2} \\\\ \\boxed{2}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $45\\times 45=2\\,025.$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in the first factor ($4.5$) and $1$ decimal place in the second factor ($4.5$), so their product will have $1+1=2$ decimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $2\\,025$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n20\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,\\underbrace{25}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $(-4.5)^{2}=20.25\\,.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186527933 :question/worked-solution "Writing the terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=2x-\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2}+⋯+{{(-1)^{n+1}\\frac{6}{n(n+2)}{x}^{n}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above series term-by-term gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=2-\\frac{3}{2}x+⋯+{{(-1)^{n+1}\\frac{6}{n+2}{x}^{n-1}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{n+2}(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n-1}\n$$" "Writing the terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=2x-\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2}+⋯+\\underbrace{(-1)^{n+1}\\frac{6}{n(n+2)}{x}^{n}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above series term-by-term gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=2-\\frac{3}{2}x+⋯+\\underbrace{(-1)^{n+1}\\frac{6}{n+2}{x}^{n-1}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{n+2}(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n-1}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186527952 :question/worked-solution "Writing the terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=3(x+2)-\\frac{9}{2}(x+2)^{2}+⋯+{{(-1)^{n+1}\\frac{{3}^{n}}{n}(x+2)^{n}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above series term-by-term gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=3-9(x+2)+⋯+{{(-1)^{n+1}{3}^{n}(x+2)^{n-1}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the Taylor series for ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{3}^{n}(-1)^{n+1}(x+2)^{n-1}\n$$" "Writing the terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=3(x+2)-\\frac{9}{2}(x+2)^{2}+⋯+\\underbrace{(-1)^{n+1}\\frac{{3}^{n}}{n}(x+2)^{n}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above series term-by-term gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=3-9(x+2)+⋯+\\underbrace{(-1)^{n+1}{3}^{n}(x+2)^{n-1}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the Taylor series for ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{3}^{n}(-1)^{n+1}(x+2)^{n-1}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186527961 :question/worked-solution "Remember, the order of the vectors in a basis is important.\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}={{(\\,6\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{{(\\,-3\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx=6\\cdot {b}_{1}+(-3)\\cdot {b}_{2}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that ${x}_{2}=-3.$" "Remember, the order of the vectors in a basis is important.\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{x}=\\underbrace{(\\,6\\,)}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot \\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{{b}_{1}}+\\underbrace{(\\,-3\\,)}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot \\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{{b}_{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx=6\\cdot {b}_{1}+(-3)\\cdot {b}_{2}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that ${x}_{2}=-3.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186527999 :question/worked-solution "Writing the terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{3}+\\frac{{x}^{5}}{2}+⋯+{{\\frac{{x}^{2n+1}}{n}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above series term-by-term gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}+\\frac{5{x}^{4}}{2}+⋯+{{\\frac{(2n+1){x}^{2n}}{n}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the Taylor series for ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(2n+1){x}^{2n}}{n}\n$$\n\nComparing the series above with the given series, we can deduce that\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(2n+1){x}^{2n}}{n}.\n$$" "Writing the terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{3}+\\frac{{x}^{5}}{2}+⋯+\\underbrace{\\frac{{x}^{2n+1}}{n}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above series term-by-term gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}+\\frac{5{x}^{4}}{2}+⋯+\\underbrace{\\frac{(2n+1){x}^{2n}}{n}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the Taylor series for ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(2n+1){x}^{2n}}{n}\n$$\n\nComparing the series above with the given series, we can deduce that\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(2n+1){x}^{2n}}{n}.\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186528007 :question/worked-solution "Writing the terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x-2}{7}-\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{10}+⋯+{{\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(x-2)^{n}}{3n+4}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above series term-by-term gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{7}-\\frac{2(x-2)}{10}+⋯+{{\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}n(x-2)^{n-1}}{3n+4}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the Taylor series for ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n(-1)^{n+1}(x-2)^{n-1}}{3n+4} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n}{3n+4}(-1)^{n+1}(x-2)^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Writing the terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x-2}{7}-\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{10}+⋯+\\underbrace{\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(x-2)^{n}}{3n+4}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above series term-by-term gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{7}-\\frac{2(x-2)}{10}+⋯+\\underbrace{\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}n(x-2)^{n-1}}{3n+4}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the Taylor series for ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n(-1)^{n+1}(x-2)^{n-1}}{3n+4} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n}{3n+4}(-1)^{n+1}(x-2)^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186530453 :question/worked-solution "The given repeating decimal can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3.\\overset{―}{03} & =3.030\\,303… \\\\  & =3+0.03+0.000\\,3+0.000\\,003+… \\\\  & ={{3+\\frac{3}{100}+\\frac{3}{10\\,000}+\\frac{3}{1\\,000\\,000}+⋯.}_{⏟}}_{\\text{geometric series}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that we have a geometric series where the first term is ${a}_{1}=3$ and the common ratio is $\\frac{1}{100}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3.\\overset{―}{03} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }300(\\frac{1}{100}){(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }300{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The given repeating decimal can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3.\\overset{―}{03} & =3.030\\,303… \\\\  & =3+0.03+0.000\\,3+0.000\\,003+… \\\\  & =\\underbrace{3+\\frac{3}{100}+\\frac{3}{10\\,000}+\\frac{3}{1\\,000\\,000}+⋯.}_{\\text{geometric series}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that we have a geometric series where the first term is ${a}_{1}=3$ and the common ratio is $\\frac{1}{100}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3.\\overset{―}{03} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }300(\\frac{1}{100}){(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }300{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186530477 :question/worked-solution "The given repeating decimal can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.0\\overset{―}{28} & =0.028\\,282\\,8...… \\\\  & =0.028+0.000\\,28+0.000\\,002\\,8+… \\\\  & ={{\\frac{28}{1\\,000}+\\frac{28}{100\\,000}+\\frac{28}{10\\,000\\,000}+⋯.}_{⏟}}_{\\text{geometric series}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that we have a geometric series where the first term ${a}_{1}=\\frac{28}{1\\,000}$ and the common ratio is $r=\\frac{1}{100}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.0\\overset{―}{28} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{28}{1\\,000}{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{28}{10}(\\frac{1}{100}){(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2.8{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The given repeating decimal can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.0\\overset{―}{28} & =0.028\\,282\\,8...… \\\\  & =0.028+0.000\\,28+0.000\\,002\\,8+… \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\frac{28}{1\\,000}+\\frac{28}{100\\,000}+\\frac{28}{10\\,000\\,000}+⋯.}_{\\text{geometric series}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that we have a geometric series where the first term ${a}_{1}=\\frac{28}{1\\,000}$ and the common ratio is $r=\\frac{1}{100}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.0\\overset{―}{28} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{28}{1\\,000}{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{28}{10}(\\frac{1}{100}){(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2.8{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186530485 :question/worked-solution "The given repeating decimal can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.\\overset{―}{75} & =0.757\\,575… \\\\  & =0.75+0.007\\,5+0.000\\,075+… \\\\  & ={{\\frac{75}{100}+\\frac{75}{10\\,000}+\\frac{75}{1\\,000\\,000}+⋯.}_{⏟}}_{\\text{geometric series}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that we have a geometric series where the first term ${a}_{1}=\\frac{75}{100}$ and the common ratio is $r=\\frac{1}{100}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.\\overset{―}{75} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{75}{100}{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }75(\\frac{1}{100}){(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }75{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The given repeating decimal can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.\\overset{―}{75} & =0.757\\,575… \\\\  & =0.75+0.007\\,5+0.000\\,075+… \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\frac{75}{100}+\\frac{75}{10\\,000}+\\frac{75}{1\\,000\\,000}+⋯.}_{\\text{geometric series}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that we have a geometric series where the first term ${a}_{1}=\\frac{75}{100}$ and the common ratio is $r=\\frac{1}{100}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.\\overset{―}{75} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{75}{100}{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }75(\\frac{1}{100}){(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }75{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
