 [:db/cas 17592186545827 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=2\\sqrt{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{2}{2\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,\\sqrt{3}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ -\\sqrt{3}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{1}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are free variables. \n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are *not* mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we have to orthogonalize them using the Gram-Schmidt process. So, let \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}={b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & ={b}_{2}-\\frac{{b}_{2}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{{c}_{1}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{c}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{4}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, normalizing orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{2\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{2}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=2\\sqrt{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{2}{2\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,\\sqrt{3}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ -\\sqrt{3}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{1}\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{2}\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are free variables. \n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are *not* mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we have to orthogonalize them using the Gram-Schmidt process. So, let \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}={b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & ={b}_{2}-\\frac{{b}_{2}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{{c}_{1}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{c}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{4}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, normalizing orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{2\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{2}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"]
