 [:db/cas 17592186549020 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=3\\sqrt{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{1}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{1}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are free variables. \n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are *not* mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we have to orthogonalize them using the Gram-Schmidt process. So, let \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}={b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & ={b}_{2}-\\frac{{b}_{2}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{{c}_{1}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{c}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{(-4)}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, normalizing orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} & 0 & \\frac{5}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=3\\sqrt{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{1}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{1}\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{2}\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are free variables. \n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are *not* mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we have to orthogonalize them using the Gram-Schmidt process. So, let \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}={b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & ={b}_{2}-\\frac{{b}_{2}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{{c}_{1}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{c}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{(-4)}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, normalizing orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} & 0 & \\frac{5}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186550002 :question/worked-solution "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,{{9{x}_{1}^{2}-24{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+16{x}_{2}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}-50{x}_{1}+25{x}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=\\frac{4}{5}{y}_{2}-\\frac{3}{5}{y}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}=\\frac{4}{5}{y}_{1}+\\frac{3}{5}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}_{1}^{2}-24{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+16{x}_{2}^{2}-50{x}_{1}+25{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 25{y}_{1}^{2}-50(\\frac{4}{5}{y}_{2}-\\frac{3}{5}{y}_{1})+25(\\frac{4}{5}{y}_{1}+\\frac{3}{5}{y}_{2}) & =0 \\\\ 25{y}_{1}^{2}+50{y}_{1}-25{y}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{1}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{y}_{1}^{2}+50{y}_{1}-25{y}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{1}-{y}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{1}+1-1-{y}_{2} & =0 \\\\ ({y}_{1}+1)^{2}-1-{y}_{2} & =0 \\\\ ({y}_{1}+1)^{2} & ={y}_{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1}={y}_{1}+1, \\\\ {z}_{2}={y}_{2}+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{1}+1)^{2} & ={y}_{2}+1 \\\\ {z}_{1}^{2} & ={z}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis represents a parabola that opens in the direction of the positive ${z}_{2}$-axis." "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,\\underbrace{9{x}_{1}^{2}-24{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+16{x}_{2}^{2}}_{Q(x)}-50{x}_{1}+25{x}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=\\frac{4}{5}{y}_{2}-\\frac{3}{5}{y}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}=\\frac{4}{5}{y}_{1}+\\frac{3}{5}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}_{1}^{2}-24{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+16{x}_{2}^{2}-50{x}_{1}+25{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 25{y}_{1}^{2}-50(\\frac{4}{5}{y}_{2}-\\frac{3}{5}{y}_{1})+25(\\frac{4}{5}{y}_{1}+\\frac{3}{5}{y}_{2}) & =0 \\\\ 25{y}_{1}^{2}+50{y}_{1}-25{y}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{1}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{y}_{1}^{2}+50{y}_{1}-25{y}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{1}-{y}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{1}+1-1-{y}_{2} & =0 \\\\ ({y}_{1}+1)^{2}-1-{y}_{2} & =0 \\\\ ({y}_{1}+1)^{2} & ={y}_{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1}={y}_{1}+1, \\\\ {z}_{2}={y}_{2}+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{1}+1)^{2} & ={y}_{2}+1 \\\\ {z}_{1}^{2} & ={z}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis represents a parabola that opens in the direction of the positive ${z}_{2}$-axis."]
 [:db/cas 17592186551239 :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}8.6 \\\\ 13.1 \\\\ 24.6 \\\\ 28.1 \\\\ 38.6\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}11 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}113 \\\\ 414\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.1 \\\\ 7.5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=0.1+7.5x.$\n\nFinally, to predict the distance the object will cover after $6$ seconds, we substitute $x=6$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.1+7.5(6) \\\\  & =45.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the object will cover a distance of $45.1$ inches in $6$ seconds.  \n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=6$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable." "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{X}\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}8.6 \\\\ 13.1 \\\\ 24.6 \\\\ 28.1 \\\\ 38.6\\end{aligned}]}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}11 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}113 \\\\ 414\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.1 \\\\ 7.5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=0.1+7.5x.$\n\nFinally, to predict the distance the object will cover after $6$ seconds, we substitute $x=6$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.1+7.5(6) \\\\  & =45.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the object will cover a distance of $45.1$ inches in $6$ seconds.  \n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=6$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable."]
