 [:db/cas 17592186554563 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -10 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-4,{u}_{23}=3,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{23}=-12.$" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -10 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{L}\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-4,{u}_{23}=3,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{23}=-12.$"]
