 [:db/cas 17592186555769 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -8 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=\\frac{1}{2},$ ${u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=\\frac{1}{2}.$" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -8 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{L}\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=\\frac{1}{2},$ ${u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=\\frac{1}{2}.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186555795 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-7){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=7,{u}_{22}=-5,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{12}=-35.$" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-7){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{L}\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=7,{u}_{22}=-5,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{12}=-35.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186555807 :question/worked-solution "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $A,$ we write $A=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nA=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The second product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although we have a product of an upper triangular matrix on the left and a unit lower triangular matrix on the right, the order of multiplication is not correct (instead, we need a unit lower triangular matrix on the left and an upper triangular matrix on the right).\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $A,$ we write $A=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nA=\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{L}\\cdot \\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}]}^{U}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The second product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although we have a product of an upper triangular matrix on the left and a unit lower triangular matrix on the right, the order of multiplication is not correct (instead, we need a unit lower triangular matrix on the left and an upper triangular matrix on the right).\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."]
 [:db/cas 17592186555856 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ -12 & -9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-3,{u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=-3.$" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ -12 & -9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{L}\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-3,{u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=-3.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186557211 :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each property in turn.\n\n- The domain of $f(x)=a\\sqrt{2x+b}+c\\,$ is determined by the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+b & \\ge 0 \\\\ 2x & \\ge -b \\\\ x & \\ge -\\frac{b}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that the domain is $x\\in [4,\\infty ),$ which is equivalent to $x\\ge 4.$ Hence, we obtain\n\n$$\n-\\frac{b}{2}=4\\,⟹\\,b=-8.\n$$\n\n- Since the range is unbounded on the right-hand side, we know that $a>0.$ Furthermore, since we are given that the lowest point in the range is $-1,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{a\\sqrt{2x-8}}_{⏟}}_{\\ge 0}+c\\ge -1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $c=-1.$\n\n- Finally, since the point $(6,3)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(6) & =3 \\\\ a\\sqrt{2(6)-8}-1 & =3 \\\\ 2a-1 & =3 \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our function is $f(x)=2\\sqrt{2x-8}-1.$" "Let's consider each property in turn.\n\n- The domain of $f(x)=a\\sqrt{2x+b}+c\\,$ is determined by the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+b & \\ge 0 \\\\ 2x & \\ge -b \\\\ x & \\ge -\\frac{b}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that the domain is $x\\in [4,\\infty ),$ which is equivalent to $x\\ge 4.$ Hence, we obtain\n\n$$\n-\\frac{b}{2}=4\\,⟹\\,b=-8.\n$$\n\n- Since the range is unbounded on the right-hand side, we know that $a>0.$ Furthermore, since we are given that the lowest point in the range is $-1,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{a\\sqrt{2x-8}}_{\\ge 0}+c\\ge -1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $c=-1.$\n\n- Finally, since the point $(6,3)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(6) & =3 \\\\ a\\sqrt{2(6)-8}-1 & =3 \\\\ 2a-1 & =3 \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our function is $f(x)=2\\sqrt{2x-8}-1.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186557216 :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. You're given that the system of equations given below connects the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{6}{a}_{2}-3{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3}=\\frac{1}{5}{a}_{3}-{q}_{1}-2{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of ${r}_{11}+{r}_{13}+{r}_{33}?$" "$$\n\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}^{A}=\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}^{Q}\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{aligned}]}^{R}\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. You're given that the system of equations given below connects the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{6}{a}_{2}-3{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3}=\\frac{1}{5}{a}_{3}-{q}_{1}-2{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of ${r}_{11}+{r}_{13}+{r}_{33}?$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186557216 :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{6}{a}_{2}-3{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3}=\\frac{1}{5}{a}_{3}-{q}_{1}-2{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}=4{q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}=18{q}_{1}+6{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3}=5{q}_{1}+10{q}_{2}+5{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, this system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 18 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{11}+{r}_{13}+{r}_{33}=4+5+5=14.\n$$" "Let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{6}{a}_{2}-3{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3}=\\frac{1}{5}{a}_{3}-{q}_{1}-2{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}=4{q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}=18{q}_{1}+6{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3}=5{q}_{1}+10{q}_{2}+5{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, this system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}^{A}=\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}^{Q}\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 18 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{R}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{11}+{r}_{13}+{r}_{33}=4+5+5=14.\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186557222 :question/worked-solution "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,{{24{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+21{x}_{2}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}-\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2}=-\\frac{5}{16}\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+21{x}_{2}^{2}-\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2} & =-\\frac{5}{16} \\\\ 25{y}_{1}^{2}+20{y}_{2}^{2}-\\sqrt{5}(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2})+2\\sqrt{5}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2}) & =-\\frac{5}{16} \\\\ 25{y}_{1}^{2}+20{y}_{2}^{2}+5{y}_{2} & =-\\frac{5}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{2}^{2}+{y}_{2} & =-\\frac{1}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{2}^{2}+{y}_{2} & =-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4({y}_{2}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{2}) & =-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4({y}_{2}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{2}+\\frac{1}{64}-\\frac{1}{64}) & =-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4({y}_{2}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{2}+\\frac{1}{64})-\\frac{4}{64} & =-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4({y}_{2}+\\frac{1}{8})^{2}-\\frac{1}{16} & =-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4({y}_{2}+\\frac{1}{8})^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1}={y}_{1}, \\\\ {z}_{2}={y}_{2}+\\frac{1}{8},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{z}_{1}^{2}+4{z}_{2}^{2} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{\\frac{1}{5}}+\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{\\frac{1}{4}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}})^{2}}+\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{(\\frac{1}{2})^{2}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nA sum of squares is equal to zero if and only if each summand is zero, so the given quadratic equation represents a single point." "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,\\underbrace{24{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+21{x}_{2}^{2}}_{Q(x)}-\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2}=-\\frac{5}{16}\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+21{x}_{2}^{2}-\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2} & =-\\frac{5}{16} \\\\ 25{y}_{1}^{2}+20{y}_{2}^{2}-\\sqrt{5}(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2})+2\\sqrt{5}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2}) & =-\\frac{5}{16} \\\\ 25{y}_{1}^{2}+20{y}_{2}^{2}+5{y}_{2} & =-\\frac{5}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{2}^{2}+{y}_{2} & =-\\frac{1}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{2}^{2}+{y}_{2} & =-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4({y}_{2}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{2}) & =-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4({y}_{2}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{2}+\\frac{1}{64}-\\frac{1}{64}) & =-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4({y}_{2}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{2}+\\frac{1}{64})-\\frac{4}{64} & =-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4({y}_{2}+\\frac{1}{8})^{2}-\\frac{1}{16} & =-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4({y}_{2}+\\frac{1}{8})^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1}={y}_{1}, \\\\ {z}_{2}={y}_{2}+\\frac{1}{8},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{z}_{1}^{2}+4{z}_{2}^{2} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{\\frac{1}{5}}+\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{\\frac{1}{4}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}})^{2}}+\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{(\\frac{1}{2})^{2}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nA sum of squares is equal to zero if and only if each summand is zero, so the given quadratic equation represents a single point."]
 [:db/cas 17592186557247 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{32}=-\\frac{3}{2},{u}_{33}=-1,$ and ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=\\frac{3}{2}.$" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{L}\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{32}=-\\frac{3}{2},{u}_{33}=-1,$ and ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=\\frac{3}{2}.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186557253 :question/worked-solution "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,{{{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}+2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2}=\\frac{119}{8}\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly: \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{119}{8} \\\\ 2{y}_{1}^{2}-3{y}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{5}(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2})+\\sqrt{5}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2}) & =\\frac{119}{8} \\\\ 2{y}_{1}^{2}-3{y}_{2}^{2}+5{y}_{1} & =\\frac{119}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1}^{2}-3{y}_{2}^{2}+5{y}_{1} & =\\frac{119}{8} \\\\ 2({y}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}{y}_{1})-3{y}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{119}{8} \\\\ 2({y}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}{y}_{1}+\\frac{25}{16}-\\frac{25}{16})-3{y}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{119}{8} \\\\ 2{({y}_{1}+\\frac{5}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{25}{8}-3{y}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{119}{8} \\\\ 2{({y}_{1}+\\frac{5}{4})}^{2}-3{y}_{2}^{2} & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1}={y}_{1}+\\frac{5}{4}, \\\\ {z}_{2}={y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{z}_{1}^{2}-3{z}_{2}^{2} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}-\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{6})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents a hyperbola with semiaxes $3$ and $\\sqrt{6}.$" "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,\\underbrace{{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}}_{Q(x)}+2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2}=\\frac{119}{8}\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly: \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{119}{8} \\\\ 2{y}_{1}^{2}-3{y}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{5}(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2})+\\sqrt{5}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2}) & =\\frac{119}{8} \\\\ 2{y}_{1}^{2}-3{y}_{2}^{2}+5{y}_{1} & =\\frac{119}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1}^{2}-3{y}_{2}^{2}+5{y}_{1} & =\\frac{119}{8} \\\\ 2({y}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}{y}_{1})-3{y}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{119}{8} \\\\ 2({y}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}{y}_{1}+\\frac{25}{16}-\\frac{25}{16})-3{y}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{119}{8} \\\\ 2{({y}_{1}+\\frac{5}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{25}{8}-3{y}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{119}{8} \\\\ 2{({y}_{1}+\\frac{5}{4})}^{2}-3{y}_{2}^{2} & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1}={y}_{1}+\\frac{5}{4}, \\\\ {z}_{2}={y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{z}_{1}^{2}-3{z}_{2}^{2} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}-\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{6})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents a hyperbola with semiaxes $3$ and $\\sqrt{6}.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186557260 :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{104}[\\begin{aligned}26 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ 26\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3.5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $y=3.5+x.$" "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{X}\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{104}[\\begin{aligned}26 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ 26\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3.5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $y=3.5+x.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186557275 :question/worked-solution "| $x$ | **$-15$** | **$-10$** | **$-5$** | **$0$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-6$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -15 \\\\ 1 & -10 \\\\ 1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector." "| $x$ | **$-15$** | **$-10$** | **$-5$** | **$0$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-6$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -15 \\\\ 1 & -10 \\\\ 1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{X}\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."]
 [:db/cas 17592186557281 :question/worked-solution "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}].$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-1)}\\,x(1) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1)) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1))+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-s))\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-s))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{6s}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{6s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s)) \\\\ {e}^{6s}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-s))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1)) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1))+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))\\end{aligned}]+{\\int }_{1}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{6s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s)) \\\\ {e}^{6s}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-s))\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\underbrace{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}].$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-1)}\\,x(1) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1)) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1))+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-s))\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-s))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{6s}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{6s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s)) \\\\ {e}^{6s}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-s))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1)) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1))+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))\\end{aligned}]+{\\int }_{1}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{6s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s)) \\\\ {e}^{6s}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-s))\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186557283 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $M$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $M$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{5}){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot M \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot M \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}M=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}M & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}M & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ M & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ M & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ M & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ M & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $M$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $M$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{5}){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot M \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot M \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}M=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}M & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}M & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ M & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ M & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ M & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ M & =\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{L}\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186557289 :question/worked-solution "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}].$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At}\\,x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{4(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4(t-s)}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{4(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4(t-s)}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{4s}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(t-s){e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]+{\\int }_{0}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}(t-s){e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\underbrace{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}].$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At}\\,x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{4(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4(t-s)}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{4(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4(t-s)}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{4s}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(t-s){e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]+{\\int }_{0}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}(t-s){e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186557294 :question/worked-solution "| $x$ | **$-1$** | **$1$** | **$3$** | **$5$** | **$7$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $4$ | $8$ | $11$ | $14$ | $20$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 11 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector." "| $x$ | **$-1$** | **$1$** | **$3$** | **$5$** | **$7$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $4$ | $8$ | $11$ | $14$ | $20$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{aligned}]}^{X}\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 11 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}]}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."]
 [:db/cas 17592186557314 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-6){R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-6){R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{L}\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]}_{U}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186557317 :question/worked-solution "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+1\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $H$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot H=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}H=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}H & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}H & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ H & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ H & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+1\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $H$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot \\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot H=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}H=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}H & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}H & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ H & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ H & =\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186557325 :question/worked-solution "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At}\\,x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]+{\\int }_{0}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\underbrace{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At}\\,x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]+{\\int }_{0}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186558485 :question/worked-solution "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y), & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y) \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =0, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y), & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =0 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y), & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =1 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y), & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈0+0\\cdot x+0\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(0\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(1)\\cdot xy+2\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1xy+1{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=1+1=2.\n$$" "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overbrace{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}^{\\text{Linear approximation}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+\\underbrace{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y), & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y) \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =0, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y), & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =0 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y), & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =1 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y), & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈0+0\\cdot x+0\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(0\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(1)\\cdot xy+2\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1xy+1{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=1+1=2.\n$$"]
