 [:db/cas 17592186558528 :question/problem "Consider the LU decomposition of the matrix $A$ and the vector $b,$ given by\n\n$$\nA={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U},\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $⟨{x}_{1},{x}_{2}⟩$ and $⟨{y}_{1},{y}_{2}⟩$ are the solutions to the systems $Ax=b$ and $Ly=b$ respectively, what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{y}_{2}?$" "Consider the LU decomposition of the matrix $A$ and the vector $b,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{L}\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{U},\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $⟨{x}_{1},{x}_{2}⟩$ and $⟨{y}_{1},{y}_{2}⟩$ are the solutions to the systems $Ax=b$ and $Ly=b$ respectively, what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{y}_{2}?$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186558528 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the two systems in turn.\n\n- First, we solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=b=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=4 \\\\ -{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${y}_{1}=4$ and ${y}_{2}=5.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\ny=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $Ux=y.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=y=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}8{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=4 \\\\ {x}_{2}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${x}_{2}=5$ and ${x}_{1}=3.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{y}_{2}=3+5=8.$" "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,\\underbrace{(LU)}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL\\underbrace{(Ux)}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the two systems in turn.\n\n- First, we solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=b=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=4 \\\\ -{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${y}_{1}=4$ and ${y}_{2}=5.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\ny=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $Ux=y.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=y=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}8{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=4 \\\\ {x}_{2}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${x}_{2}=5$ and ${x}_{1}=3.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{y}_{2}=3+5=8.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186558529 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -5 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=-1 \\\\ -2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=-3 \\\\ 8{y}_{1}-5{y}_{2}+{y}_{3}=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the solution to this system using forward substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =-1, \\\\ {y}_{2} & =-3+2{y}_{1} \\\\  & =-3+2(-1) \\\\  & =-5, \\\\ {y}_{3} & =8-8{y}_{1}+5{y}_{2} \\\\  & =8-8(-1)+5(-5) \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$y=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}].$" "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,\\underbrace{(LU)}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL\\underbrace{(Ux)}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -5 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=-1 \\\\ -2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=-3 \\\\ 8{y}_{1}-5{y}_{2}+{y}_{3}=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the solution to this system using forward substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =-1, \\\\ {y}_{2} & =-3+2{y}_{1} \\\\  & =-3+2(-1) \\\\  & =-5, \\\\ {y}_{3} & =8-8{y}_{1}+5{y}_{2} \\\\  & =8-8(-1)+5(-5) \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$y=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}].$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186558544 :question/worked-solution "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} & t{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}].$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-1)}\\,x(1) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3(t-1)} & (t-1){e}^{3(t-1)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3(t-1)}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(t-2){e}^{3(t-1)} \\\\ {e}^{3(t-1)}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{3(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3(t-s)}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{3(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3(t-s)}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3s} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}(t-2){e}^{3(t-1)} \\\\ {e}^{3(t-1)}\\end{aligned}]+{\\int }_{1}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\underbrace{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} & t{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}].$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-1)}\\,x(1) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3(t-1)} & (t-1){e}^{3(t-1)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3(t-1)}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(t-2){e}^{3(t-1)} \\\\ {e}^{3(t-1)}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{3(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3(t-s)}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{3(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3(t-s)}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3s} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}(t-2){e}^{3(t-1)} \\\\ {e}^{3(t-1)}\\end{aligned}]+{\\int }_{1}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
