tion vector."]
 [:db/cas 17592186558563 :question/worked-solution "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At}\\,x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t)+2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t)+2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t-s) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t-s) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{s} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{s}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t-s) \\\\ {e}^{s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t)+2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t)+2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}]+{\\int }_{0}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{s}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t-s) \\\\ {e}^{s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\underbrace{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At}\\,x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t)+2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t)+2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t-s) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t-s) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{s} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{s}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t-s) \\\\ {e}^{s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t)+2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t)+2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}]+{\\int }_{0}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{s}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t-s) \\\\ {e}^{s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186558570 :question/problem "$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 28 & -8 & -3 \\\\ -49 & 37 & 7\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\boxed{}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}.\n$$\n\nConsider the LU factorization of the matrix $A$ above. What is the value of ${u}_{33}$ in the matrix $U?$" "$$\n\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 28 & -8 & -3 \\\\ -49 & 37 & 7\\end{aligned}]}_{A}=\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{L}\\cdot \\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\boxed{}\\end{aligned}]}_{U}.\n$$\n\nConsider the LU factorization of the matrix $A$ above. What is the value of ${u}_{33}$ in the matrix $U?$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186558571 :question/problem "Consider the LU decomposition of the matrix $A$ and the vector $b,$ given by\n\n$$\nA={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U},\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $⟨{x}_{1},{x}_{2}⟩$ and $⟨{y}_{1},{y}_{2}⟩$ are the solutions to the systems $Ax=b$ and $Ly=b$ respectively, what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {y}_{2}?$" "Consider the LU decomposition of the matrix $A$ and the vector $b,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{L}\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{U},\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $⟨{x}_{1},{x}_{2}⟩$ and $⟨{y}_{1},{y}_{2}⟩$ are the solutions to the systems $Ax=b$ and $Ly=b$ respectively, what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {y}_{2}?$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186558571 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the two systems in turn.\n\n- First, we solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=b=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=-3 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${y}_{1}=-3$ and ${y}_{2}=2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\ny=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $Ux=y.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=y=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-3 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${x}_{2}=1$ and ${x}_{1}=-1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {y}_{2}=(-1)\\cdot 2=-2.$" "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,\\underbrace{(LU)}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL\\underbrace{(Ux)}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the two systems in turn.\n\n- First, we solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=b=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=-3 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${y}_{1}=-3$ and ${y}_{2}=2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\ny=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $Ux=y.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=y=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-3 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${x}_{2}=1$ and ${x}_{1}=-1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {y}_{2}=(-1)\\cdot 2=-2.$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186558577 :question/worked-solution "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$50$** | **$58$** | **$62$** | **$65$** | **$70$** |\n| $y$ | $10$ | $12$ | $15$ | $20$ | $17$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the plane \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 50 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 58 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 62 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 65 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 70\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 20 \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector." "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$50$** | **$58$** | **$62$** | **$65$** | **$70$** |\n| $y$ | $10$ | $12$ | $15$ | $20$ | $17$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the plane \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 50 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 58 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 62 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 65 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 70\\end{aligned}]}^{X}\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 20 \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}]}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."]
 [:db/cas 17592186561038 :question/worked-solution "Using the definition of factorial, we have \n\n$$\n(k-1)!=(k-1)\\cdot (k-2)\\cdot (k-3)\\cdot (k-4)\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1.\n$$\n\nNow, applying the factorial definition to all but the first two factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(k-1)! & =(k-1)\\cdot (k-2)\\cdot \\overset{(k-3)!}{\\overset{⏞}{(k-3)\\cdot (k-4)\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1}} \\\\  & =(k-1)\\cdot (k-2)\\cdot (k-3)!\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Using the definition of factorial, we have \n\n$$\n(k-1)!=(k-1)\\cdot (k-2)\\cdot (k-3)\\cdot (k-4)\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1.\n$$\n\nNow, applying the factorial definition to all but the first two factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(k-1)! & =(k-1)\\cdot (k-2)\\cdot \\overbrace{(k-3)\\cdot (k-4)\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1}^{(k-3)!} \\\\  & =(k-1)\\cdot (k-2)\\cdot (k-3)!\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186562518 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]=C.\n$$\n\nNext, we can find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =[\\begin{aligned}-4+3\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(v)}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(v)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]=C.\n$$\n\nNext, we can find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =[\\begin{aligned}-4+3\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{\\text{Re}(v)}+\\text{i}\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{\\text{Im}(v)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186563647 :question/worked-solution "Using the  addition rule for limits, and the fact that both functions are continuous, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 5}[f(t)+3g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 5}f(t)+{lim}_{t\\to 5}[3g(t)] \\\\  & ={lim}_{t\\to 5}f(t)+3{lim}_{t\\to 5}g(t) \\\\  & =f(5)+3g(5) \\\\  & =f(5)+3{{⟨-1,1,-2⟩}_{⏟}}_{g(5)} \\\\  & =f(5)+⟨-3,3,-6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we are told that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 5}[f(t)+3g(t)]=⟨1,3,1⟩.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain the following equation which we can solve for $f(5):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 5}[f(t)+3g(t)] & =⟨1,3,1⟩ \\\\ f(5)+⟨-3,3,-6⟩ & =⟨1,3,1⟩ \\\\ f(5) & =⟨1,3,1⟩-⟨-3,3,-6⟩ \\\\ f(5) & =⟨4,0,7⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Using the  addition rule for limits, and the fact that both functions are continuous, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 5}[f(t)+3g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 5}f(t)+{lim}_{t\\to 5}[3g(t)] \\\\  & ={lim}_{t\\to 5}f(t)+3{lim}_{t\\to 5}g(t) \\\\  & =f(5)+3g(5) \\\\  & =f(5)+3\\underbrace{⟨-1,1,-2⟩}_{g(5)} \\\\  & =f(5)+⟨-3,3,-6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we are told that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 5}[f(t)+3g(t)]=⟨1,3,1⟩.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain the following equation which we can solve for $f(5):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 5}[f(t)+3g(t)] & =⟨1,3,1⟩ \\\\ f(5)+⟨-3,3,-6⟩ & =⟨1,3,1⟩ \\\\ f(5) & =⟨1,3,1⟩-⟨-3,3,-6⟩ \\\\ f(5) & =⟨4,0,7⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186563690 :question/worked-solution "Using the  addition rule for limits, and the fact that both functions are continuous, we get:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)-g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)+{lim}_{t\\to 0}[-g(t)] \\\\  & ={lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)-{lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) \\\\  & =f(0)-g(0) \\\\  & =f(0)-{{⟨-1,0,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{g(0)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we are told that:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)-g(t)]=⟨-2,-3,0⟩\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain the following equation which we can solve for $f(0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)-g(t)] & =⟨-2,-3,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨-2,-3,0⟩ & =f(0)-⟨-1,0,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨-2,-3,0⟩+⟨-1,0,1⟩ & =f(0) \\\\ f(0) & =⟨-3,-3,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Using the  addition rule for limits, and the fact that both functions are continuous, we get:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)-g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)+{lim}_{t\\to 0}[-g(t)] \\\\  & ={lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)-{lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) \\\\  & =f(0)-g(0) \\\\  & =f(0)-\\underbrace{⟨-1,0,1⟩}_{g(0)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we are told that:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)-g(t)]=⟨-2,-3,0⟩\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain the following equation which we can solve for $f(0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)-g(t)] & =⟨-2,-3,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨-2,-3,0⟩ & =f(0)-⟨-1,0,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨-2,-3,0⟩+⟨-1,0,1⟩ & =f(0) \\\\ f(0) & =⟨-3,-3,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186564473 :question/worked-solution "Let $({x}_{m},{y}_{m})$ be the midpoint between ${{(8p,-q)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(-2p,-9q)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})}.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{8p+(-2p)}{2},\\frac{-q+(-9q)}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{8p-2p}{2},\\frac{-q-9q}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{6p}{2},\\frac{-10q}{2}) \\\\  & =(3p,-5q).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $({x}_{m},{y}_{m})$ be the midpoint between $\\underbrace{(8p,-q)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and $\\underbrace{(-2p,-9q)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})}.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{8p+(-2p)}{2},\\frac{-q+(-9q)}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{8p-2p}{2},\\frac{-q-9q}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{6p}{2},\\frac{-10q}{2}) \\\\  & =(3p,-5q).\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186564531 :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}9 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 8\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}9 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]x & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}9{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}=-9 \\\\ {x}_{2}=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=9.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}_{1}+8(9) & =-9 \\\\ 9{x}_{1} & =-81 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}].$" "The least-squares solution $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}9 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{R}x & =\\overbrace{\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 8\\end{aligned}]}^{{Q}^{T}}\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}^{b} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}9 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]x & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}9{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}=-9 \\\\ {x}_{2}=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=9.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}_{1}+8(9) & =-9 \\\\ 9{x}_{1} & =-81 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}].$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186564537 :question/worked-solution "Let $({x}_{m},{y}_{m})$ be the midpoint between ${{(-w,7z)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(5w,-3z)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})}.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{-w+5w}{2},\\frac{7z+(-3z)}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{-w+5w}{2},\\frac{7z-3z}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{4w}{2},\\frac{4z}{2}) \\\\  & =(2w,2z).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $({x}_{m},{y}_{m})$ be the midpoint between $\\underbrace{(-w,7z)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and $\\underbrace{(5w,-3z)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})}.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{-w+5w}{2},\\frac{7z+(-3z)}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{-w+5w}{2},\\frac{7z-3z}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{4w}{2},\\frac{4z}{2}) \\\\  & =(2w,2z).\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186565446 :question/worked-solution "The word \"and\" is represented by the symbol \"$∧$\", while the word \"or\" is represented by the symbol \"$∨$\".\n\nNote that this sentence consists of two parts:\n\n- **Part 1:** $\\sqrt{5}$ is rational. This corresponds to $P.$\n\n- **Part 2:** $3|12$ and $3|18.$ This corresponds to\n\n$$\n{{3|12}_{⏟}}_{Q}\\,{{\\text{and}}_{⏟}}_{∧}\\,{{3|18}_{⏟}}_{R},\n$$\n\nthat is, $Q∧R.$\n\nSo, we can express the sentence as\n\n$$\n{{\\sqrt{5}\\text{ is rational}}_{⏟}}_{P}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{3|12\\text{ and }3|18}_{⏟}}_{Q\\,∧\\,R},\n$$\n\nthat is, $P∨(Q∧R).$" "The word \"and\" is represented by the symbol \"$∧$\", while the word \"or\" is represented by the symbol \"$∨$\".\n\nNote that this sentence consists of two parts:\n\n- **Part 1:** $\\sqrt{5}$ is rational. This corresponds to $P.$\n\n- **Part 2:** $3|12$ and $3|18.$ This corresponds to\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{3|12}_{Q}\\,\\underbrace{\\text{and}}_{∧}\\,\\underbrace{3|18}_{R},\n$$\n\nthat is, $Q∧R.$\n\nSo, we can express the sentence as\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\sqrt{5}\\text{ is rational}}_{P}\\,\\underbrace{\\text{or}}_{∨}\\,\\underbrace{3|12\\text{ and }3|18}_{Q\\,∧\\,R},\n$$\n\nthat is, $P∨(Q∧R).$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186565456 :question/problem "$$\n{{0\\text{ is even}}_{⏟}}_{P}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\{1,2\\}\\cap \\{1\\}=\\{1,2\\}}}\\,\\overset{∨}{\\overset{⏞}{\\text{or}}}\\,\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{2}{3}\\text{ is irrational}}}}_{⏟}}_{Q\\,∨\\,R}\n$$\n\nFor the compound statement above, we have that\n\n$\\,$ $P$ is {{field-1}},\n$\\,$ $Q∨R$ is {{field-2}},\n$\\,$ $P∨(Q∨R)$ is {{field-3}}." "$$\n\\underbrace{0\\text{ is even}}_{P}\\,\\underbrace{\\text{or}}_{∨}\\,\\underbrace{\\overbrace{\\{1,2\\}\\cap \\{1\\}=\\{1,2\\}}^{Q}\\,\\overbrace{\\text{or}}^{∨}\\,\\overbrace{\\frac{2}{3}\\text{ is irrational}}^{R}}_{Q\\,∨\\,R}\n$$\n\nFor the compound statement above, we have that\n\n$\\,$ $P$ is {{field-1}},\n$\\,$ $Q∨R$ is {{field-2}},\n$\\,$ $P∨(Q∨R)$ is {{field-3}}."]
 [:db/cas 17592186565456 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following facts about the \"and\" $(∧)$ and \"or\" $(∨)$ logical connectives:\n\n- The statement $A∧B$ is true if *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\n- The statement $A∨B$ is true if either $A$ is true, or $B$ is true, or *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\nIn our case, $P$ is true, $Q$ is false, and $R$ is false. Therefore, $Q∨R$ is false, but the result is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P∨(Q∨R) & ≡\\text{true}∨\\overset{\\text{false}}{\\overset{⏞}{(\\text{false}∨\\text{false})}} \\\\  & ≡\\text{true}∨\\text{false} \\\\  & ≡\\text{true}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the following facts about the \"and\" $(∧)$ and \"or\" $(∨)$ logical connectives:\n\n- The statement $A∧B$ is true if *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\n- The statement $A∨B$ is true if either $A$ is true, or $B$ is true, or *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\nIn our case, $P$ is true, $Q$ is false, and $R$ is false. Therefore, $Q∨R$ is false, but the result is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P∨(Q∨R) & ≡\\text{true}∨\\overbrace{(\\text{false}∨\\text{false})}^{\\text{false}} \\\\  & ≡\\text{true}∨\\text{false} \\\\  & ≡\\text{true}\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186569965 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -16 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & \\,{R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-8){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -16 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & \\,{R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-8){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186569998 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{4}{3}{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{4}{3} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{4}{3}{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{4}{3} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186570009 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186572113 :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have ${{(5,1)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(1,-2)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})}.$ \nSo, the distance $d$ can be calculated using the distance formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(1-5)^{2}+(-2-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$" "In this case, we have $\\underbrace{(5,1)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and $\\underbrace{(1,-2)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})}.$ \nSo, the distance $d$ can be calculated using the distance formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(1-5)^{2}+(-2-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
