 [:db/cas 17592186572149 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\ne C,\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda })\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{―}{\\lambda }=2+5\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{―}{v},$ the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1-4\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\ne C,\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda })\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{―}{\\lambda }=2+5\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{―}{v},$ the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1-4\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]
 [:db/cas 17592186572671 :question/worked-solution "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,{{{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}+2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=2\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} &=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} & =2 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}+2(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2})-2(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2}) & =2 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2} & =2 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}-2\\sqrt{2}{y}_{2} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}-2\\sqrt{2}{y}_{2} & =2 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}-({y}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{2}{y}_{2}) & =2 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}-({y}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{2}{y}_{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}-(\\sqrt{2})^{2}) & =2 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}-({y}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{2}{y}_{2}+2-2) & =2 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}-{({y}_{2}+\\sqrt{2})}^{2}+2 & =2 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}-{({y}_{2}+\\sqrt{2})}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1} &={y}_{1}, \\\\ {z}_{2} &={y}_{2}+\\sqrt{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{z}_{1}^{2}-{z}_{2}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents the pair of intersecting lines ${z}_{2}=\\sqrt{3}{z}_{1}$ and ${z}_{2}=-\\sqrt{3}{z}_{1}.$" "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,\\underbrace{{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}}_{Q(x)}+2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=2\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} &=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} & =2 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}+2(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2})-2(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2}) & =2 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2} & =2 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}-2\\sqrt{2}{y}_{2} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}-2\\sqrt{2}{y}_{2} & =2 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}-({y}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{2}{y}_{2}) & =2 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}-({y}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{2}{y}_{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}-(\\sqrt{2})^{2}) & =2 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}-({y}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{2}{y}_{2}+2-2) & =2 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}-{({y}_{2}+\\sqrt{2})}^{2}+2 & =2 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}-{({y}_{2}+\\sqrt{2})}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1} &={y}_{1}, \\\\ {z}_{2} &={y}_{2}+\\sqrt{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{z}_{1}^{2}-{z}_{2}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents the pair of intersecting lines ${z}_{2}=\\sqrt{3}{z}_{1}$ and ${z}_{2}=-\\sqrt{3}{z}_{1}.$"]
